window.NEXUS_SEARCH_DATA = {"records": {"assets/diagrams/README.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This atlas delivers 15 required views and two supplementary diagrams. Main views are registered in the diagram index; maintenance follows the diagram standards. Each view has an exact current/reference identity. Source existence, successful rendering or a software flow does not prove hardware execution or external publication. Code references are pinned to 0111c8d3b9c7b37d258b9c401070305b13b6f3a1; documentation tooling implemented after that commit is bounded by its final execution records. Rendering did not change firmware source relationships. Editable DOT sources produce local SVG. Dark labels, line styles and text jointly express relationships rather than depending on color alone. Each of the 17 sources derives Chinese and English views; pages use the local SVG for their selected language. One canonical DOT still owns nodes, edges, attributes and scope; labels.json contains complete label translations only and cannot change topology. Checks pair all 34 localized DOT/SVG sets with their originals; missing translations, stale output or illegal resources fail the gate. Graphviz Cairo emits SVG glyph paths so offline readers need no Chinese fonts. Each view includes a title, description and textual alt/caption. The generation host requires locked Graphviz, Cairo and Noto Sans CJK SC; delivery records bind final tool identities and automated results. Source semantics, actual desktop/narrow-screen/zoom/print browser checks and offline acceptance have separate records. The 15 main and two supplementary sources have independent edge-by-edge and PNG review: current is source_reviewed, reference is reference_reviewed, and the supplementary target is design_reviewed. Actual records establish browser and formal-package acceptance separately; these states establish neither HIL nor public-deployment qualification. After changing labels, explicitly use the prepared locked tools to generate language views, then run the complete check: localized/en/ and localized/zh_CN/ are derived assets. Successful generation proves rendering only. Label semantics, actual browser behavior and offline resource closure are reviewed separately; original diagram code references and historical execution identities remain unchanged. Identity: current. Scope: Products remain external; components and OS are optional; runtime access and build facts have distinct edges. FIG-01: Products remain external; components and OS are optional; runtime access and build facts have distinct edges. Applications own tasks, scheduling, recovery and shutdown coordination. Board contributes build-time authorization. After factory lookup obtains an identity, daily I/O enters the provider through its face. Arch is selected by the compiled target; there is no CPU factory or mandatory OS forwarding. Dashed edges denote explicit optional relationships or build-time facts; solid edge labels identify calls/contracts. Sources: current architecture contract, root CMake, static assembly generation, runtime supplement. editable DOT; Implementation item: DOC-43. Identity: reference. Scope: H743 baremetal IRQ UART; exact PUBLIC/PRIVATE/INTERFACE/OBJECT relationships. FIG-02: H743 baremetal IRQ UART; exact PUBLIC/PRIVATE/INTERFACE/OBJECT relationships. This reference selects the targets of tools/configure/assemblies/nucleo-h743-baremetal-uart.toml. Arrows run from the dependent to its dependency; dashed edges explicitly identify PRIVATE includes or OBJECT injection. Shared warnings, configuration-option edges and GC policy are omitted and remain managed by real CMake. Vendor headers enter only private implementation/startup scope; generated bindings also consume SDKs through private includes. Optional components are not selected in this assembly. Sources: root target graph, firmware helper, IO targets, Arch targets, OS targets, H743 target, ABI/Config targets. editable DOT; Implementation item: DOC-44. Identity: current. Scope: Build-input/artifact chain; generation failure invalidates the owned bundle. FIG-03: Build-input/artifact chain; generation failure invalidates the owned bundle. Edges represent build-time input and artifact flow, not runtime calls. Strict parsing cannot repair invalid fields, conflicts or stale inputs. Failure withdraws only owned outputs: it neither clears unowned directories nor exposes partial artifacts. ELF/resource reports do not establish physical-board qualification. Sources: configurator, immutable IR, bundle transaction, build contract. editable DOT; Implementation item: DOC-45. Identity: reference. Scope: Illustrative two selected instances of one class; same-mode ops are shared; controller/endpoint identities remain separate. FIG-04: Illustrative two selected instances of one class; same-mode ops are shared; controller/endpoint identities remain separate. The structure shows configuration-selected layouts supported by the generator; it does not claim every PCB authorizes those combinations. Same-provider, same-mode instances share read-only ops while each context/state/storage remains independent. Different providers or modes can have different ops; a method receives the current context. Controllers and endpoints have distinct factory namespaces, and an endpoint cannot impersonate its controller. IDs are valid only in the current assembly; the default uint16_t typedef does not provide language-level protection against mixing classes. Sources: typed factory generation, face/state assembly, public SPI interface, Native provider. editable DOT; Implementation item: DOC-46. Identity: current. Scope: Generated lifecycle skeleton; each SoC retains its own private startup/cleanup contract. FIG-05: Generated lifecycle skeleton; each SoC retains its own private startup/cleanup contract. One external executor serializes start/stop. start does not start a scheduler; failures retain primary, cleanup and remaining_effects separately. Owners of acquired effects participate in reverse-order cleanup. Stop can release some instances successfully before returning BUSY/IO. BUSY cannot imply the platform is still fully running, and timeout does not permit storage reclamation. A concrete start_system failure may clean up inside its private path or retain the failed phase. Retry must satisfy that provider contract; the diagram is not a universal recovery API. Sources: generated start/stop, cold GPIO lease, H743 cold lifecycle, current platform contract. editable DOT; Implementation item: DOC-47. Identity: current. Scope: Transitions and preconditions allowed by Core helpers; no promise of a recovery API for every provider. FIG-06: Transitions and preconditions allowed by Core helpers; no promise of a recovery API for every provider. REJECTED/ACCEPTED are admission results, not request states. After ACCEPTED, request/buffer borrowing continues; cancel/deadline only starts termination progress. The provider first drains every required reference, then release-publishes SETTLED as its final request access; the caller observes with acquire semantics. Related adapters/observers must exit separately. QUARANTINED to SETTLED is legal only after actual recovery and proof of drain. initialize cannot forcibly reset a borrowed object. Sources: public request contract, actual Core transitions, final owner publication. editable DOT; Implementation item: DOC-48. Identity: reference. Scope: Exact finite F407 USART1/SPI1 DMA; Task/IRQ/DMA completion and failure paths. FIG-07: Exact finite F407 USART1/SPI1 DMA; Task/IRQ/DMA completion and failure paths. Top-to-bottom order distinguishes Task, DMA IRQ and UART IRQ. UART DMA TC cannot replace TC for the final serial bit; completion of both SPI DMA streams cannot replace BSY=0/TXE=1. Final drain, error observation and reference withdrawal all precede SETTLED. CS nodes describe software commands and do not prove electrical levels or physical timing. Cancel/error paths require the same actual drain conditions; failure retains QUARANTINED borrowing. NDTR is not a reliable wire-byte count, and this view does not extend to GD32 reset semantics. Sources: F407 UART DMA, F407 SPI DMA, DMA drain contract, multiple-controller contract. editable DOT; Implementation item: DOC-49. Identity: current. Scope: ready-arm-ready-wait-ready and actual execution/join responsibilities. FIG-08: ready-arm-ready-wait-ready and actual execution/join responsibilities. nx_wait_until only checks a predicate and waits for hints; it neither services hardware nor creates a worker. Rechecking after arm prevents a lost wake. After TIMEOUT, a final true predicate still returns SUCCESS. The notification port requires one waiter. External shutdown first closes admission and requests cancel while keeping owner/IRQ drain progress alive. Join producers after their loans settle; confirm owner idle includes publication exit, then stop and join the executor. Next stop hardware RX/IRQ publishers, detach wake and join other publishers; only then destroy OS synchronization storage. Related observers need their final access to exit independently. A running kernel must retain its clock/Tick, and failed storage remains retained. Sources: wait contract, production wait algorithm, owner interface, OS lifecycle. editable DOT; Implementation item: DOC-50. Identity: reference. Scope: Finite H743ZIT6 USART3 TX; complete 32-byte AXI SRAM lines. FIG-09: Finite H743ZIT6 USART3 TX; complete 32-byte AXI SRAM lines. Selected TX DMA authorizes only 512 KiB AXI SRAM; other physical domains and dma_engines reachability facts do not automatically authorize placement or power. Cache-on requires exclusive complete 32-byte lines, with a maximum of 65504 bytes; Cache-off accepts 1..65535 bytes. PREPARING establishes the borrow first; clean executes under the caller\u2019s normal masks, and cancellation cannot reclaim storage before it finishes. This diagram neither implements nor claims H743 RX DMA; future RX needs separate pre- and post-drain maintenance contracts. Trusted reset or a prior successful owned clean/disable establishes cold caches; clear CCR bits cannot claim responsibility for foreign dirty state. Sources: exact physical domains, H743 provider scope, TX DMA implementation, cache range contract, cold-cache contract. editable DOT; Implementation item: DOC-51. Identity: current. Scope: Actual public entry points and consumers; relocate the complete prefix; Native execution and ARM linking remain separate. FIG-10: Actual public entry points and consumers; relocate the complete prefix; Native execution and ARM linking remain separate. Packaging, relocation, verify and consumer execution are separate steps. Move the complete installed prefix; an SDK subtree cannot replace package closure. find_package and resolver boundaries read actual byte identities; fixture opt-in does not bypass checksums. Native consumers actually run; MCU consumers establish compile/link/resource evidence, not physical execution. Platform macros or vendor includes must not contaminate parent targets. Sources: Source SDK packaging and verify, real independent consumers, resolver SDK verification boundary, CMake SDK entry. editable DOT; Implementation item: DOC-52. Identity: current. Scope: Each scope obtains actual evidence separately; HIL tooling is not a physical measurement. FIG-11: Each scope obtains actual evidence separately; HIL tooling is not a physical measurement. Host/model, ARM/link/resources, SDK consumption, two network-disabled reproductions and HIL tooling each need actual execution records bound to the same source/config/artifact. Candidate sealing revalidates scope proofs and raw bytes. Physical station measurements additionally bind PCB, silicon, fixture and the same image; absent equipment means not_executed. A software candidate does not automatically become a product release, safety certification or LTS promise. Sources: qualification contract, candidate scope validation, actual proof verification, network-disabled reproduction, HIL tooling. editable DOT; Implementat", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Nexus platform diagram atlas", "type": "reference"}, "delivery/README.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The default implementation is Core / Arch / SoC / Board / static factory / polymorphic I/O / optional OS / optional components. One TOML is strictly validated and resolved through immutable IR to generate exact instances. The factory returns stable read-only interface objects with shared read-only operation tables; independent state remains inside each provider. Getting an interface does not start hardware. External projects own applications, product tasks, private PCBs, Flash partitions, recovery and security policy. Further documentation organization is described in architecture and delivery planning. The documentation tools, prose and figure atlas are implemented; documentation acceptance and firmware qualification are recorded separately. The current entry point is documentation. The CPU software layer covers M0/M0+/M3/M4/M7/M23/M33/M55/M85. External projects can use nexus_add_runtime or the installed Runtime component with one strict CPU-runtime TOML/facts package, reusing Core/Arch/I/O faces and the selected baremetal/FreeRTOS adapter. Runtime assembly generates no SoC, Board, startup or factory; external projects still own hardware, product tasks and security policy. The three F407/F470 Boards maintain launch modes; G474/H743/U575 add exact GPIO/IRQ-UART/timebase reference assemblies. G474 additionally has selected EXTI, H743 fixed Cache configuration and limited TX DMA, and U575 an independent OS-free synchronous split-domain reference. These vertical slices do not represent all peripherals or complete security-domain integration; all physical HIL remains unexecuted. The historical SoC-extension baseline at fixed source 98270512 completed all Native contracts, tool and configuration tests, 121 ARM images, 104 CPU-runtime variants, independent SDK consumption and full GitHub CI. Eight public H743/U575 reference configurations each completed two independent network-disabled builds with byte-identical ELF/BIN and were sealed as unsigned software candidates. Exact scope and raw evidence are in the results index. Later documentation commits only record that source and inherit no new code-execution qualification. Hardware is not connected; all physical HIL is not_executed. Runtime handover, DVFS and continuous deep-STOP timing remain unimplemented; the 55 original exit conditions are unchanged. This build-system delivery is bound to cc6a065c; complete results are separately recorded in its build results index and cannot inherit the historical SoC execution identity above. The eight current candidates use H743 HSI64/Cache off and U575 MSIS4/TZEN=0 empty/UART baremetal/FreeRTOS reference combinations; this expands no physical-board support. The 72-stage software master plan was not executed as one complete plan invocation. OCI recipe rebuild, CP311 offline installation and physical HIL remain unexecuted. Later documentation commits only record the exact code source. docs/archive/ retains historical designs, results and old Sphinx tutorials; these confer no current support or acceptance. Current public API documentation is generated only from existing public include trees. Code formatting and backslash Doxygen style retain the root .clang-format, .editorconfig and existing standards. Behavior changes follow TDD. GoogleTest/GoogleMock enter host test targets only; production firmware remains C11. Commit hooks actually run the complete testing/configure/ci/workflows tool suites and all GoogleTest contracts, rejecting incomplete, filtered or skipped reports. Execution results prove current contracts; actual RED\u2192GREEN history is recorded in PRs and raw logs. SoC refinement and physical acceptance", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Current platform delivery", "type": "status"}, "delivery/architecture.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "SoC translation units are organized by controller kind/mode; one implementation serves multiple independent instances. Selecting one UART does not retain all peripherals, RTOS queues or maximum object pools. Startup/system sources are explicit firmware inputs; correct reset/vectors do not depend on unreferenced weak symbols in static archives. Strong provider IRQs enter the image only with selected implementations; section GC removes unused implementations. CMake reads selected controller kind/mode from the same resolved result, querying selection facts only; each family\u2019s CMake still owns source sets. Unselected drivers are not compiled, and endpoint base drivers are supplied by composition. Empty STM configurations compile no peripheral drivers; GD retains base SDK sources required by system/timebase. Final section GC removes unreferenced functions. Compilation trimming and image trimming are verified separately. The G474 HSE24/170 MHz reference maintains actual generated factory, clock/GPIO/LPUART/EXTI algorithms and baremetal/FreeRTOS ARM fixtures for empty/GPIO-UART/selected-EXTI. PA0/PA1 EXTI use separate caller-sized event rings and own interrupt-source withdrawal. Empty configurations have no GPIO/UART provider. Selected LPUART IRQ91 and system TIM2 IRQ28 bind actual vector bytes. FreeRTOS retains real SVC/PendSV/SysTick; 170 MHz and 1 kHz Tick correspond to reload169999. Start rejects occupied LPUART control registers before changing clocks. Failed construction cleans in reverse order; failed withdrawal retains clock/pin/state responsibility. These are independent development evidence, not reference-PCB, physical-timing or ThreadX-G474 support. H743/U575 add concrete M7/M33 SoC assemblies. H743 maintains LDO, fixed HSI32/HSI64 \u00d7 Cache off/on, TIM2, PB0 and USART3 PD8/PD9; RX remains IRQ bytes while TX selects IRQ or bounded DMA. Default U575 Platform maintains externally confirmed TZEN=0/SWAP_BANK=0, MSIS4 MHz, Cache off, TIM2, PC7 and USART1 PA9/PA10. Both Platforms maintain baremetal/FreeRTOS and derive no other peripherals automatically from chip facts. H743 runs an independent bounded power entry before main RAM is writable. U575 separately has an OS-free TZEN=1 reference: two Runtime images, cold-ownership owner and synchronous CMSE GPIO gateway, independent of TZEN=0 factory. Neither path changes option bytes; split-domain startup requires external preconfiguration. Exact scope is in SoC refinement contracts and U575 split-domain contract. Initial execution retains historical qualification; extension execution records later evidence separately. Boards do not duplicate SoC drivers. GD32 I2C/PWM/ADC/EXTI routes and STM32 advanced-controller routes may compile/link in external Board packages explicitly marked software fixtures; this does not mean the three reference PCBs have matching verified connectors/electrical facts. Default Boards bind only routes with existing provenance. Current configuration chain: strict parsing and an atomic bundle enter the sole CMake target graph Input/artifact relations for the current figure are in FIG-03 and source basis. Software evidence and physical qualification are obtained separately; see FIG-11. The sketch source below is retained only as historical migration input; its arrows cannot represent current HIL admission or completed verification. Historical build sketch source: One assembly selects Board, clock profile, OS backend, controller instances, bounded modes, capacities, IRQ priorities, endpoints and budgets. The generator creates only static hardware assembly, not tasks, business code or software dependency graphs. Unknown keys, invalid inputs/modes, pin/IRQ/timer conflicts and out-of-range capacities are rejected. Failed reconfiguration invalidates the old bundle instead of silently reusing a previous success. SoC/Board facts use JSON and consumers author schema-2 TOML. A strict resolver produces immutable typed IR consumed by every generator. resolved.json serializes that result and is not hand-authored configuration input. Migration schema-1 JSON fixtures have explicit entry points and are not overlaid with TOML. IR identity, budgets and controller options use frozen records; IRQ is nested, ADC channels/sample times are tuples. Full JSON-fact projections retain read-only mappings, so not every field has nominal strong typing. tools/configure/providers/\u003cfamily\u003e/ owns ABI, mode\u2192implementation, DMA routes, triggers, shared IRQ/CS contracts and constructor/rollback/IRQ/system-clock generation. The common resolver owns schema/facts/resources/input identity/output; common binding code orchestrates Nexus faces and lifecycle progress. New SoCs require explicit actual provider registration and maintained CMake/SDK payloads. Unknown families/modes are rejected rather than inheriting Native/Cortex-M4 defaults. Ordinary Boards reuse implementations without duplicating drivers; documentation matrices cannot determine configuration backward. Immutable family descriptors unify configuration entry, dependency identity and required SDK payloads. Controller descriptors reuse exact register bases, IRQ, DMA and pin facts; production-C SDK static assertions independently check them. clock.source/input_hz express real HSE/HSI/MSIS sources; legacy hse_hz is normalized only at parsing, without inventing external crystals for internal oscillators. Complex chips use dma_engines for actual per-master reachability rather than one boolean hiding differences. These are build-time facts, not a runtime registry/query layer or second source graph. CMake Nexus::Platform exports configuration, typed factory and immutable interfaces. SDK headers/provider storage are visible only to implementation/generated binding TUs. Public consumption has no vendor-SDK ABI. External firmware explicitly adds startup/system/linker/resource checks through nexus_add_firmware(). CPU primitives, privilege, interrupt masks and FreeRTOS startup contracts are in Cortex-M integration. Arch selects implementation at compile time without a runtime CPU factory; device typed factories/shared polymorphic tables remain. F407/F470 launch modes and limited G474/H743/U575 vertical slices are maintained separately. Nine-core CPU models/compilation cannot replace concrete SoC integration or physical verification. Independent CPU runtime reads external runtime.toml/explicit cpu.json, reusing frozen CpuProfileIR and production CMake targets. nexus_add_runtime(ASSEMBLY ...) exports Nexus::Runtime with Config/ABI, Core, Arch, typed I/O faces and selected baremetal/FreeRTOS adapter. Installed find_package(Nexus REQUIRED COMPONENTS Runtime) exposes the same entry. There is no SoC/Board/startup/linker script/typed factory. External projects implement actual memory/clock/IRQ resources, device providers, security policy and final firmware. Nine-core FPU/MVE/cache/MPU/DWT/security/SAU are explicit facts; the parser selects legal combinations/exact kernel ports, never CMake-cache inference/override. Hardware/product qualification remains separate. Single-region MPU/SAU mechanisms alone provide neither user-task isolation nor split-world gateways. The U575 split-domain reference explicitly combines independent SoC ownership and external image partitioning; Runtime/default Platform does not generate it automatically. ThreadX is a separate actual-kernel backend, selecting locked Eclipse ThreadX Linux GNU/Cortex-M4 GNU ports without reusing FreeRTOS configuration fields. Initial scope maintains three F407/F470 Boards, Native Linux and explicit M4-integer/M4F CPU runtime; other Cortex-M ports are rejected. Products enter the kernel with nx_threadx_kernel_enter(). Only real tx_application_define permits prestart task creation/reclamation. Abnormal return after a start attempt retains all effects and fail-stops; it cannot restore prestart reclamation. Linux uses actual pthreads/CLOCK_MONOTONIC kernel timers, but caller stack parameters are not actual host-pthread stack budgets. See ThreadX integration. The factory/polymorphic skeleton is restored, with construction completed during generation. Each interface face has only const ops*/context*. Faces/operation tables are read-only; mutable provider state and selected per-instance buffers use independent static storage. Methods receive their own context rather than infer instances from operation-table addresses. Generated nexus_factory.h defines assembly-local IDs. Typed functions such as nx_factory_uart() return existing objects only: no heap, hardware start, string lookup or locks. Invalid IDs return NULL. Lookup, nx_platform_start(), request borrow and stop are separate actions. Static handles are not revocable sessions; shutdown must settle callers, IRQs, owners and notification publishers. Default factory IDs are class-named uint16_t; C typedefs do not prevent passing another class\u2019s numeric value. Optional class-specific struct references prevent ordinary ID mixing, or read-only capability queries may be selected. Both return the same static objects without runtime registration/construction. Capability queries describe selected bounded modes, not current state, PCB electrical facts or admission. IDs are local to the current assembly, not persistent cross-version numbers. One external executor serializes start/stop without hidden platform locks. Public I/O has no uniform global-started check; contracts govern business exit/cleanup after start failure. Stop may withdraw some instances before returning BUSY; BUSY does not restore complete running state. Cold startup first rejects observable external owners. GPIO assembly observes every selected bank gate, then every selected pin mode/lock, before saving leases/modifying pins; it cannot modify one bank then claim no effects on another-bank conflict. Controller routes and endpoint CS share this check. Unselected pins in shared banks remain externally owned. H743 establishes fixed clocks/Cache from trusted reset or successfully completed owned clean/disable, never claiming unknown bootloader state from disabled CCR bits alone. Arch cold-Cache control retains owned I/D Cache and selector-recovery responsibility; only M7 is maintained. Selected TX DMA accesses AXI SRAM only and requires exclusive complete 32-byte-line borrow with Cache on. Submission acquires PREPARING borrow before cleaning outside a long IRQ-masked section; cancellation cannot publish SETTLED before preparation ends. Platform stop drains providers, withdraws TIM2, cleans/disables Cache and restores the HSI divider in that order. Uncleared effects remain with original owners. This is cold lifecycle, not live frequency scaling. Driver GPIO stop preserves declared safe idle. Full Platform stop restores selected-pin leases/releases gates only after all IRQ/DMA/caller references drain; failed restoration readback retains cleanup responsibility. Full release therefore does not promise active product-safe levels indefinitely. Products needing continued drive retain the platform owner or first exit under external power/safety policy. This is resource ownership, not built-in product shutdown policy. Timebase publishes a valid epoch only after successful start. Startup excludes old UIF before observing a new UG update. Runtime ACK refusal invalidates time and boundedly withdraws interrupt sources instead of incrementing epoch. Regardless of isolation success, it retains terminal interrupt masking and calls fault observation; returned observers still halt, without zero-time deadline processing or releasing request/low-power borrows. Products may provide separately reviewed nonreturning recovery. Normal stop/restart/cleanup retries cannot recover this terminal path. Reads recheck epoch validity under the same mask to prevent entry-gap IRQ invalidation. Old deadlines cannot cross stop/failure/restart. SPI/I2C controller factories return actual controllers; device factories return CS/address endpoints. A controller arbitrates all children and owns", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Current system architecture", "type": "status"}, "delivery/build-system-verification.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This record covers the 48-item ledger. Its requirements and original exit conditions remain unchanged. The qualified code source is cc6a065c002041234a212a964be863a69198f9b4, tree de0e687f659a22badb55259b2e03646fc7b748d1. Later changes to these documents record this code source and do not claim a new code execution at their HEAD. The result index binds actual receipts, archive navigation and byte identities. The fixed primary execution index is /workspace/nexus-build-maintenance-20261012/final-qualification/formal-execution-evidence-index-cc6a065c00.json, SHA-256 b42e1857f6802cbabe35d10f515fd9033ee3bb0bbb40368a50222927c5a45e47. The implemented build path remains one authored TOML assembly, immutable typed IR and static typed factory, with one CMake target graph executed by Ninja. Configuration owns atomic publication and selected hardware facts; CMake owns software dependencies and scoped compile/link policy. Products continue to own application sources, composition, scheduling and deployment policy. The exact staged code tree passed ordinary commit hooks, including the complete TDD and commit-message gates. The index and behavioral source stayed unchanged through execution. The subsequent canonical GitHub commit contains the same tree. Final qualification separately executes the clean committed source with fresh unfiltered discovery and reports. No hook bypass, test filter, empty report or skipped-only execution establishes acceptance. All final-source suites in the table have zero failures, errors and skips. Expected nonzero child commands test rejection and retained failure evidence; they are not suite failures. Counts overlap and must not be added into a platform test total. GoogleTest and GoogleMock remain host-only. Production firmware remains C11 and contains no C++ test framework or its runtime. The real small CMake projects exercise protected inputs, output publication, identical reconfigure/no-op builds, derived artifact repair, configuration and resource dependencies, external LTO and C/C++ policy propagation, invalid helper arguments, mixed assembly rejection, private includes and process cancellation. Source SDK fixtures deliberately mutate packages and remain nonpublishable. The candidate path separately generates a clean committed SDK and verifies its whole manifest before consumption. The selected SDK result is attempt 2 in sdk-boundaries-attempt-2, with its matching explicit receipt. It executes all 62 cases in 673.078 seconds and retains 280 actual child commands and 13 consumer artifact directories. Attempt 1 remains failed in the original sdk-boundaries namespace: missing CMake on its PATH caused six errors and six skips, with zero test failures. No report or failed namespace was renamed or replaced to select attempt 2. The SDK suite runs serially with a one-job environment default; original consumer build argv explicitly request four or two jobs where specified. The offline candidate compiler uses its separately recorded bound; the SDK execution is not described as all compiler commands running with one job. The final-source push run 38294880491 completed with success: 29 successful jobs, zero failed jobs, and one expected main-only Documentation / Deploy to Pages skip. All job pages, actual executed job logs and the artifact metadata catalog are retained. The receipt binds the run, jobs and referenced workflow to the exact canonical code source. The four Native debug/release/ASan/TSan profiles each execute their complete reported CTest and GoogleTest scopes. ARM, Cortex-M, OS, provider, installed SDK, quality and security scopes retain their actual individual results. All 27 artifact ZIP bytes were downloaded, digest-checked and CRC-checked. Twenty-six Nexus job identities and 25 required selected-dependency closures were verified; the Pages ZIP retains GitHub artifact byte identity without a Nexus job identity. The sealed CI index covers 131 files / 104,572,091 bytes. The earlier acc70f30 run 38280227545 failed and remains failed evidence. Its successful Native jobs do not qualify the failed aggregate run or this later source. The fixes make scoped preparation receipts match actual selected dependency closures, separate canonical-budget byte tampering from measured authored-budget overruns, bind expected ThreadX link failure to the exact Ninja ELF edge, and execute all four Python tool domains through the maintained complete runner. Each fix retains real RED/GREEN execution and independent review. The subsequent 3e7c0d50 run also failed: three Tool Contract cases still addressed the removed GD32 helper. Its full raw 196-case execution remains failed evidence; cc6a065c restores the checked contract entry point and retains the helper semantics. The final passing run above must establish this correction separately. Eight exact H743/U575 reference profiles pass two fresh network-disabled builds per profile: H743 HSI64 with Cache off and U575 MSIS4 with TZEN=0, each with empty/UART selection and baremetal/FreeRTOS backend. The matrix records sixteen fresh builds and 32 actual network-none container commands. It does not qualify H743 Cache/DMA or U575 dual-domain firmware. The actual selected-image resource observations are below. Flash LOAD counts physical initialization bytes; footprint includes the occupied address span. Reserved RAM includes the 2,048-byte MSP once and zero heap, rather than measured stack high water. These are separate selections, not MCU cycle or latency results. The immutable Native/tool/SDK proof remains under cc6a065c; the new candidate execution uses the separate cc6a065c00 namespace. The same clean committed Source SDK bytes are fully reverified and copied into independent fresh build roots. No failed matrix output or compiled object is reused. The same source, clean Source SDK, authored configuration, recursive dependency pins, compiler/toolchain and OCI identities bind the executed commands and actual ELF/BIN/map/resource bytes. Each candidate passes the owned verifier and an actual fixed-checker dev candidate plan. Each complete archive is streamed and verified before only its owned live profile scratch is retired. Original live artifact paths then remain historical locators; the result index supplies the archive and member-relative navigation used for current verification. Two earlier matrices remain failed with zero qualified profiles: the first exhausted storage during reproduction, and the second passed two fresh builds but could not seal after temporary SDK reference files had been cleaned up. Independent review restored 102 exact original references from precaptured identities and verified source and original SDK proof records unchanged. The failed scratch was fully archived before retirement. These are storage operations; they do not add source behavior, a new SDK test execution or platform support. The new matrix uses the separate cc6a065c00 namespace and qualifies all eight profiles from its own fresh builds and actual executed candidate plans. Independent archive-only review passed for all eight archives after every original live profile was absent. It verified 168,468 members / 150,508 files and 3,356,224,498 uncompressed file bytes, executed the owned candidate verifier on each packed candidate, and independently reconstructed and fully verified each packed Source SDK. Private temporary copies were removed afterward. The same source and matrix remained unchanged. The main receipt is /workspace/nexus-build-maintenance-20261012/independent-review/candidate-archive-audit/final-eight-archive-only-independent-review.json, SHA-256 1c127cb2a348b62e07809cfa7ce7b784f57c45cac7cca3f58922c5e973817c12. Local archive retention does not establish off-site replication or publication. Candidates remain unsigned software candidates, with no product-release or hardware acceptance claim. The maintained tools/dev/plans/software.json declares 72 reviewed stages. The individual software gates and eight actual candidate plans are recorded separately. The complete 72-stage plan did not execute end-to-end through one qualification-plan invocation. Its plan contracts and genuine failed-checker fixtures establish orchestration behavior; no separate execution is invented. The final Linux x86_64 CP312 lock contains 15 hash-locked wheels. A fresh CP312 virtual environment executes a no-index, hash-required installation. Actual pre-commit install --install-hooks primes the Python hook with PEP517 build isolation enabled, and the isolated interpreter executes the complete tool domains. Setuptools is included for local PEP517 backend preparation; the hook has its exact PyYAML, pre-commit and clang-format runtime closure. Vendored nested wheel metadata remains covered by the whole-wheel digest, while top-level canonical distribution identity and duplicate metadata are checked strictly. CP311 wheel bytes are verified against the declared lock. CP311 installation is not_executed. The original 14-wheel runtime bundles remain historical artifacts and are not relabelled as the new hook closure. Linux is the executed host scope; Windows/macOS integration and non-Linux descendant cancellation remain untested. The recorded OCI and full ARM toolchain archives were actually transported, restored and byte-verified at their own recorded source scopes. The new candidate builds consume those same artifact identities. The OCI\u2019s Python/Git observations remain distinct from the development host. This proves artifact reuse, not recipe reconstruction. The recipe declares fixed base/frontend digests, Debian snapshot and five exact APT packages, plus the hash of the final CP312 requirements. Emitted Dockerfile and ephemeral proxy-CA contracts are verified. Reconstruction of the observed OCI image bytes has not executed successfully; recipe_reproducible stays false. implemented_software_validated records a completed mechanism with final-source software evidence. More specific states retain measured decisions, actual tool installation/transport, verified archive restoration and truthful scope limits. They do not turn every requirement into a physical or product exit condition. BLD-18 retains full SDK byte checks at each consuming boundary after measurement; no persistent verification bypass is introduced. BLD-31 retains measured compiler unit and selected linked-image costs without a speculative source split. Their decision_verified states are intentional results. Prior timing/cache/LTO measurements retain their original source and selected-profile identities. BLD-38 records actual candidate plans while explicitly retaining the absence of one complete 72-stage software-plan invocation. BLD-39 verifies the dependency upgrade validator\u2019s evidence contracts; a real vendor dependency upgrade was not performed. BLD-40 records actual CP312 installation and hook isolation, and preserves the missing CP311 installation. BLD-42 retains declared-recipe versus successful reconstruction as separate states. BLD-44 records actual preserved current archives and archive-only verification, without claiming off-site storage. Earlier normal-hook failures, the isolated-environment installation failure and the failed GitHub run remain immutable failed attempts. Later passing attempts use new receipts and never overwrite their historical outcomes. No physical station is connected: every HIL result remains not_executed. This iteration makes no new MCU cycle/WCET, electrical, power-loss, cache/DMA coherence, TrustZone isolation, external-example, certification or product-release claim.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Build system execution record", "type": "status"}, "delivery/capabilities.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "tools/maintenance/capabilities.py derives this table by reading maintained soc.json, routes.json and reference-Board board.json, then running the production resolver/binding emitter on software assemblies. The table is not another configuration input. Capability changes start with original facts, implementation and regressions, then --write refreshes derived prose and --check rejects stale content. Mode is a bounded SoC-declared mode; a mode without routes does not authorize connections. Reviewed SoC routes describe maintained chip routes, not PCB continuity/electrical qualification. Declared reference Boards means the Board has a binding, not that the default assembly enables it or physical acceptance passed. Resolver fixtures are TOML software fixtures actually accepted by the current resolver. Selected production source reads full ops identifiers from actual generated faces and matches initialized exported definitions in production C; comments, strings, declarations and similar prefixes are not definitions. This is bounded source-consistency evidence, not compilation/execution/physical measurement. Blank columns explicitly lack such evidence. The table inherits no old-candidate qualification for new HEAD and infers no physical results. The three actual Boards are disconnected; HIL remains not_executed. Exact mode limits/ownership are in support scope.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Derived capabilities and integration matrix", "type": "status"}, "delivery/cortex-m.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Nexus CPU software supports M0, M0+, M3, M4, M7, M23, M33, M55 and M85. One immutable CPU-profile parser drives Core/Arch/atomics/baremetal-FreeRTOS runtime configuration. DSP, FPU, MVE, cache, MPU, DWT, security and SAU are explicit facts. Core selection generates no new SoC/Board/vector/startup/driver/link layout/typed factory. The three STM32F407/GD32F470 Boards maintain launch modes. G474/H743/U575 have exact GPIO/IRQ-UART/timebase references; G474 adds selected EXTI, H743 fixed Cache and bounded UART TX DMA, U575 an independent OS-free synchronous split-domain GPIO reference. CPU selection alone establishes none of these SoC mechanisms or wider peripheral support. All physical HIL remains unexecuted. Detailed ownership/context/rejection semantics are in Arch contracts, OS lifecycle in OS adaptation, and exact whole-platform scope in support matrix. Previous-HEAD candidates/qualification are not inherited. The table lists capability formats accepted by the parser. Optional capabilities require concrete chip facts, not inference from CPU names. none/zero explicitly means undeclared, with no runtime discovery. Cortex-M uses short-enums ABI. Without FPU/MVE use soft; with FPU explicitly choose hard/softfp. M55/M85 MVE is none, integer or float: no MVE adds +nomve, no scalar FP adds +nofp; integer-only MVE uses softfp while FPU remains none. FP-MVE requires a declared available FPU. M55/M85 FPU choices are auto, fpv5-sp-d16 or fpv5-d16; auto is an explicit compiler option, not hardware detection. Security state is single, secure or nonsecure. Only security-extension-capable M23/M33/M55/M85 may declare the latter states; Secure adds -mcmse. SAU requires true Secure facts. Cache lines are zero/32 bytes; MPU is zero or maintained v7/v8 format. M0/M0+/M23 forbid DWT capability. Other cores also require explicit facts to read an already enabled cycle counter. IRQ priority width, external IRQ count and clock are chip facts. PRIMASK profiles fix two priority bits; BASEPRI supports 3\u20138. M0/M0+ support at most 32 external IRQs; M3/M4/M7/M23 240; M33/M55/M85 480. SoCs declare actual valid ranges. These limits do not replace vendor facts/electrical verification. Arch depends on no SoC/I/O/kernel/vendor SDK. Production algorithms execute through inline-instruction/MMIO boundaries; GoogleTest/GoogleMock replace only that hardware boundary. Native provides actual recursive thread exclusion, C11 fences/publication but models no NVIC, physical IRQ, privilege switching or actual cycles. Restore PRIMASK tokens once, in reverse order on the same CPU/thread, preserving input BASEPRI/FAULTMASK. Protected metadata permits privileged Thread/configurable IRQ only; NMI/HardFault, other CPUs/security worlds/DMA are outside exclusion. Context queries authorize no kernel-ISR calls. Barriers prove neither peripheral idle nor DMA drain. DWT enables/resets no counters and is not a monotonic deadline source. Aligned-word acquire/release loads/stores do not mask IRQs. M0/M0+ RMW uses bounded saved-PRIMASK exclusion without silent libatomic linkage. Other cores use compiler lock-free RMW; exclusive retries are not fixed cycles. Owners guarantee request/buffer/notification lifetimes; atomic publication reclaims no objects. cache.h maintains exclusive complete 32-byte lines only, without rounding/enabling; all relevant cache capability/enabled checks precede writes. Providers/products own pre-DMA-RX/post-drain maintenance, reachable addresses and buffer loans. cache_control.h separately maintains M7 cold enable/disable; other cores reject it. Callers supply owned control objects/exclude other memory users. Trusted reset or prior successful owned clean/disable is required; CCR=0 proves no foreign-dirty cleanliness. Actual set/way geometry is checked, failures retain cache/selector responsibility, and a stack-free leaf cleans all D-cache before disabling. This is neither ordinary live range maintenance nor DMA drain. MPU v7/v8 and SAU use distinct exact encoders. Actual single-region programming requires privileged Thread in this domain, input PRIMASK=1 and the unit already disabled. CTRL, other regions and input RNR are preserved; nothing implicitly enables the unit. Products own MPU/MAIR policy, SAU/IDAU, NSC veneers, secure boot, shared-resource protocols and recovery. CPU MPU programming alone establishes no user-task isolation. Explicit restricted-task runtime uses an actual FreeRTOS MPU port; software and physical security acceptance remain separate. nonsecure.h provides full-span CMSE permission observation from Secure privileged Thread, checking attribution/MPU as NS-unprivileged read/read-write; other images reject it. Observation neither copies/pins memory, identifies callers, excludes MMIO/DMA nor freezes mapping. The U575 reference adds cold ownership, fixed world principal, synchronous snapshots/output prevalidation for a limited gateway; no NS RTOS/asynchronous user DMA. See U575 split-domain contract. Every CPU profile selects exact pinned-kernel port sources; M0/v8-M also compile required assembly files. Non-FP M7 uses a reviewed build overlay derived from pinned CM3, adding erratum-837070 PRIMASK-preserving BASEPRI helpers/PendSV handling while vendor files remain locked. Mapping every core to CM4F for convenience is forbidden. Armv8-M NTZ, Secure-only and standalone NonSecure defaults are selected by explicit facts with configENABLE_TRUSTZONE=0. Optional split profiles use matched Secure/NS images, actual TZ-aware ports and caller Secure-context/Idle storage, without default Secure heaps/pools. External projects still own Secure startup, IDAU/SAU and NS coprocessor access. Optional MPU profiles provide restricted tasks/narrow delay-tick SVC; ordinary APIs remain privileged. MPU-only/split-only are separate; MPU+split is rejected. OS support records exact variants/executed software evidence, not physical-isolation acceptance. M55/M85 use actual MVE-aware context ports. configENABLE_FPU with FP/MVE enables shared register banks/extended-lazy frames; integer-only MVE gains no scalar FP. Upper-bank save paths are checked separately in source/objects. VPR/lower bank depend on hardware extended exception framing, a port-acceptance assumption requiring architectural/physical checks. Compilation/ELF proves neither complete payload retention nor real FP/MVE switching and cannot replace physical acceptance. Callers statically supply tasks, TCBs, stacks, queues and notification storage; kernels link no heap allocator. Applications own scheduler startup, workers, queue depths, fairness, shutdown progress and post-join reclaim. Platform start neither starts schedulers nor generates hidden tasks/daemons. Running APIs require privileged unmasked Thread. Only NOT_STARTED permits the pinned port\u2019s canonical bootstrap mask: PRIMASK=1 for Baseline, exact syscall BASEPRI for Mainline. Masks prove no provenance and confer no runtime/suspended permission. Fault/other masks are rejected before object changes. IRQ wake separately checks external exception range, priority width and actual-port syscall rules; notification only prompts request/sequence predicate rechecks. Complete SoC projects retain strict platform assembly. CPU/Core/OS-only reuse takes external runtime.toml plus chip-package cpu.json. The following is an M33 NonSecure software-configuration example, not new chip/Board support. Replace clock/IRQ-count/optional capabilities with reviewed real-chip facts. No Board file is needed and no SoC/device factory is emitted. runtime.toml must contain the following four entries; paths are relative to that TOML. It may also explicitly provide [os] policy matching the selected backend. Baremetal accepts no kernel policy; CPU-only configuration rejects low-power timebase selections requiring SoC timer facts: backend is baremetal, freertos or explicitly scoped threadx. ThreadX accepts Native/Cortex-M4/M4F only and inherits no nine-core FreeRTOS port scope from this page; see ThreadX integration. optimization accepts O0, Og, O2, Os or O3; Debug selections establish no timing or resource qualification. CPU-package/CPU/IRQ fields are complete and unique. Unknown fields, duplicate JSON keys, contradictory feature/ABI, invalid schema/clock and symlink paths are rejected; authored overrides cannot fabricate chip capabilities. Validate independently and emit the bundle atomically: Outputs are resolved-cpu.json, nexus_config.h, selection.cmake, input_paths.json and ownership marker, binding input/config SHA-256. No linker script/bindings/factory is emitted. Failed configuration invalidates existing owned bundles instead of reusing stale selection. Nonowned directories are never cleared; outputs cannot overwrite inputs/parents. clock_hz does not set clocks. Public CMake entry from a source checkout: Installed source SDK uses the entry below; the other two target operations are unchanged: Nexus::Runtime propagates Config/ABI, Core, Arch, typed I/O faces and selected OS adapter without SoC/Board/factory. A CMake build allows one resolved Nexus assembly and no conflicting ABIs; external projects explicitly link extra components. MCU projects select cmake/toolchains/arm-gcc.cmake on first configure. External projects still implement startup/vectors/link layout/clocks/hardware/final executable. The example shows a reusable static-library source graph, not runnable-board firmware. Installed SDK checks complete source identity before generation/compilation. Development-fixture SDK requires explicit opt-in and is not a publishable candidate; opt-in bypasses no checksum/closure checks. SDK/external consumers must rerun for this new HEAD. CPU-profile/Runtime-resolver tests cover immutable IR, strict schemas, ABI/feature conflicts, atomic publication, changed inputs and stale outputs. Google models execute actual CPU algorithms. ARM gates retain macros/objects/assembly-disassembly/commands/exits/raw hashes. Runtime gates reuse the nexus_add_runtime production graph to build Core/Arch/OS and inspect actual FP/MVE kernel context/ELF rather than maintain another handwritten source graph. These commands establish CPU-primitive/runtime software evidence within actually executed scope. Concrete F407/F470 platforms, G474/H743/U575 references and physical stations have their own gates; compilation provides no physical evidence. Optional-feature scope follows actual execution; one passed combination does not qualify every possible combination. Current development output is /workspace/nexus-all-cortex-evidence; actual reports establish counts/source identity instead of previous-version counts. This iteration adds no actual Board and runs no physical HIL. IRQ latency, FP/MVE switching, cache/DMA coherence, MPU/SAU behavior, security-world cooperation, stack high-water, clocks/waveforms remain unaccepted. Formal clean-source SDK, offline double-build, image hashes and candidate sealing must rerun per qualification. Software support does not automatically become production release, hardware qualification, safety certification or LTS.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Cortex-M software support and platform-integration boundaries", "type": "status"}, "delivery/index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "These records retain their original source, configuration and evidence identities. Rebuilding the website neither reruns them nor grants a new-source qualification.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Recorded delivery evidence", "type": "reference"}, "delivery/migration.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This iteration retains the overall factory and polymorphic-interface model while rebuilding configuration and static instance generation. Compared with the original factory, instances have exact independent state, shared read-only operation tables and explicit lifecycle. Compared with the previous direct typed binding, callers can now obtain interfaces by typed IDs and replace providers. First pin the new platform source version, then update callers and tests completely. Persistent formats and recovery for previously deployed products need separate contracts. External projects supply explicit NEXUS_ASSEMBLY_FILE, link Nexus::Platform, and use nexus_add_firmware() for startup/system/linker/resource gates. Applications include only public Nexus headers and generated nexus_factory.h/nexus_bindings.h; they do not inspect private provider storage or SDK register types. SPI/I2C controllers and child-device endpoints are obtained separately; a controller factory must not return an alias of the first child. Invalid IDs return NULL. Factory lookup neither initializes nor allocates memory. Maintained assemblies use TOML schema 2. Schema-1 JSON reading is only for explicit migration and tool-derived input, not a parallel authored copy of TOML configuration. Cold startup still explicitly calls nx_platform_start() and shutdown calls nx_platform_stop(). BUSY/QUARANTINED retain state and all borrowed storage. Faces may be retained before start. Successful stop preserves face identity while the provider rejects operations. Request migration must update ownership too: prepare does not mean accepted, and cancel does not mean settled. After acceptance, retain request and payload until acquire-observed SETTLED permits reuse. Shared-owner control handles retain stable slot+epoch; an old request pointer cannot cancel a new operation. Producers submit and wait; the sole executor continuously services requests. Stop closes admission before drain/join, and notification storage cannot be reclaimed before publishers exit. OS storage uses real per-object stack/TCB/depth. Task-entry return does not mean underlying deletion; storage is reclaimed only after successful join. Timeout retains handle, stack and TCB. Persistent instances own external workspace. After mutating-I/O failure, reopen to confirm generation; an error cannot prove that commit did not occur. Migration acceptance proceeds through strict-input negative paths, Native/model behavior, independent source-SDK consumption, six base ARM ELFs, exact budgets, offline double-build and candidate sealing; HIL follows on the actual PCB/wiring. External nexus-examples is an independent example repository. Platform CI does not add product applications to default platform targets.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "External-project migration", "type": "status"}, "delivery/next-iterations.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The highest priority is connecting the three actual launch Boards, recording PCB/crystal/power/wiring, and establishing probe, serial and waveform/power-cut stations. G474/H743/U575 source-reference configurations require separate actual-board checks. H743 additionally needs power-mode observation; U575 requires option-byte/start-state confirmation before running an image. Check safe initial outputs and clock first, then UART true TC, cancellation, RX loss, watchdog reset, Flash power cuts and EXTI/PWM/ADC. Qualify each mode only on actually executed target tuples. Keep trial outputs disconnected from product loads; station contracts govern field actions. Use the same candidate ELF to measure cycles, IRQ latency, maximum service interval, stack high-water, loss and sustained load. Source/assembly inference helps locate causes but is not a measured timing result. Budget or provider changes require regenerated source/config/artifact evidence. F407/F470 already have bounded UART/SPI DMA, continuous UART RX/bidirectional DMA, bounded/continuous ADC trigger blocks, I2C IRQ/DMA, Classic CAN, USB FS PIO and Ethernet/capture/complementary-PWM exact software fixtures. First refine these existing modes with actual throughput, IRQ latency, inter-block gap, line-drain and overload measurements. Continuous modes cannot promise losslessness under arbitrary IRQ latency or consumer speed. I2C remains 100 kHz, 7-bit, 1\u2013256 bytes per message, at most eight messages and exact routes; 10-bit or longer transactions are separate extensions. H743 currently maintains fixed HSI clocks, timebase, GPIO, IRQ UART and explicitly selected finite UART TX DMA. Cache-on TX accepts only AXI SRAM payloads whose complete cache lines are exclusively owned; RX remains IRQ bytes. U575 maintains its default TZEN0 Platform separately from the independent TZEN1 synchronous dual-world GPIO reference; the dual-world reference provides no RTOS, factory or asynchronous DMA. Chip facts authorize no other modes, routes or physical qualification. The sequence and explicit exit conditions are in SOC-28\u201336: Each extension first fixes owner, buffer, cancel/drain, ISR, deadline, alignment/cache and stop contracts. Then use GoogleTest/GoogleMock for an actual RED\u2192GREEN production-provider vertical slice, followed by generated factory, ARM ELF, SDK and physical-boundary verification. Unselected modes retain no resources; growing generic-object costs are not imposed on every product. Reuse existing narrow ports and static assembly first; allow explicitly versioned breaking changes when contracts cannot express hardware facts. Flash authentication, upgrades, manufacturing keys and industrial security policy belong to external products/independent mechanisms. Existing storage CRC or watchdog feed cannot establish their completion. Codex assumes all maintenance responsibility; .github/maintainer-roles.json uses agent_managed. Ten-person roles remain domain/capacity divisions without requiring named accounts. The same maintenance responsibility covers security, manufacturing and field interfaces; product permissions/processes remain in external application repositories. python tools/maintenance/ownership.py --require-assigned verifies responsible agents, path coverage, backup domains and required gates. GoogleTest/GoogleMock, configuration, ARM, budget, SDK and evidence-identity checks continue. This policy fabricates no CODEOWNERS and claims no independent human review. Actual GitHub branch-rule/required-check configuration still needs remote verification. Commands are in maintenance tools. Issues describe concrete triggers and observable results. PRs state source/config/dependency/tool identities and affected contracts. Each commit automatically checks staged content and Conventional Commit; CI uses the same whole-file standard. New behavior and bug fixes require TDD: GoogleTest/GoogleMock for host contracts, unittest for Python tools. New cases run RED first, then a minimal GREEN fix. Mechanical gates do not prove historical editing order; PRs record commands, failure reasons and raw logs. IRQ, lifecycle, persistence and concurrency changes need actual failure-path regressions. Nightly/formal-candidate measurements, offline builds and station results are sealed separately; ordinary platform CI does not automatically promote production releases.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Next iterations and team execution", "type": "status"}, "delivery/os.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The OS retains public Wait plus backend-specific explicit storage. The platform creates no default workers, starts no scheduler, implicitly occupies no task-notification index and creates no maximum object pools. Product composition/recovery policy live in independent examples/product projects. Full contracts are in OS contracts; per-item actual status is in OS01\u2013OS55 ledger. implemented means code exists. Software qualification requires actual execution bound to current clean Git/config/dependency/tool identities. nexus-examples/apps/uart_owner_runtime uses generated typed-factory UART faces. Native creates actual pthreads for two producers/one owner. FreeRTOS creates explicit static producer/owner/stop-coordinator tasks. Baremetal cooperatively services the same owner. Three Boards reuse existing console routes without defining new wiring/providers. Shutdown includes admission stop, active cancel/drain, producer join, owner join, IRQ-wake quiescence and notification destruction. GoogleTest/GoogleMock exercise actual BusOwner/application-shutdown modules, mocking only external join/hardware quiescence. Coverage includes stage order, timeout, errors, premature reclaim and retries. Partial startup failures reclaim acquired resources separately. Native closes submission, keeps the owner draining and joins running tasks. Pre-scheduler FreeRTOS uses nx_freertos_task_discard_before_scheduler: suspend removes kernel candidate-TCB references before synchronous task/completion-latch deletion. It requires privileged bootstrap, NOT_STARTED, an unexecuted wrapper and no unmatched scheduler suspension. Successful discard alone permits stack/TCB/context reuse; running tasks use cooperative stop/join. Actual POSIX-kernel regressions verify reverse reclaim, poisoned-storage reuse, actual scheduler execution and refusal to discard executed wrappers after scheduler end; they replace no MCU validation. Native explicitly provides three 64 KiB host-stack buffers, separately increasing application budgets; this is host-fixture cost, not MCU stacks. MCU references use four tasks of 256 StackType_t words each with 8-byte alignment; physical peak measurement remains required and is not inferred from startup minimums. After I/O/publisher stop, FreeRTOS retains scheduler clock/Tick and suspends the coordinator. Native/baremetal may continue platform stop. Products separately own reset/scheduler-stop policy. Software execution should cover continuously waking deadlines, final-completion races, startup-stack rejection, complete Baseline IRQ binding, close/drain, task reuse, direct-notification receiver/index, tickless failures and trace overflow. GoogleMock context models, actual POSIX kernel, Native multitasking, nine-core ABI links and installed SDK consumption each establish separate evidence. Twelve actual ARM MPU variants and V7/V8 host contracts were executed; M0/M0+, M7-kernel-MPU and MPU+split are rejected. Optional split has 31 Secure/NS pairs (62 ELFs), covering maintained M23/M33/M55/M85 CPU-authority combinations and checking seven real SG veneers/imports/context ASM/caller Idle callbacks without default Secure heaps/pools. Two host lease targets cover integer/shared-FP-MVE-bank software contracts. Both require explicit consumer linkers/storage/trusted lifecycles. Fixture addresses are synthetic; actual MPU faults, security attribution, cross-world startup/context switches remain unexecuted. See OS contracts. OS49\u201355 physical fixtures execute as prepare/validate tools, but disconnected hardware means no actual IRQ\u2192task latency, FP/MVE retention, MSP/task peaks, Tick/deadline error, full-load lifecycle, sleep recovery or exception-safe outputs. Plans remain prepared_unbound, physical status not_executed. Plans are not measurements, WCET, safety certification or product releases. After hardware connection, station owners set five budgets: os_irq_latency, os_deadline_lateness, os_low_power_lateness, os_lifecycle, os_fail_response, with us/us/us/count/us units. Lifecycle requires positive minimum; the other four use maximum. Records bind station admission and budgets cannot be relaxed inside acquisition context. Strict OS-context fields are schema_version=1/kind=os_hil_context, admission_sha256, workload/instrument file identities, actual load/executor/irq_priorities/cpu_clock_hz, features.fpu/mve, complete stack_names and requirements. Names include MSP/every selected workload task and raw stack traces must match exactly, not one example task. Features exactly match admitted CPU ABI. Each independent raw trace binds context SHA and retains per-sample/register/stack/lifecycle/fail-stop data. A self-authored status=passed summary cannot replace it. Acquisition time needs a fresh UTC offset. Reports require unused output targets and never overwrite old evidence/inputs. These commands read retained records only and never operate hardware automatically. Actual station/workload review owns measurement method, observed load, power, safe outputs and product budgets; host models/old commits confer no physical qualification. The 32-bit-ABI standard development resource probe measured the object sizes below. Changed profile/CPU port/enum ABI/compiler requires fresh cost gates. These are actual ELF object sizes, not runtime cycles/final software-candidate qualification. maximum_waiters bounds close traversal while the scheduler is paused; payload item size bounds each kernel-copy critical section. Entire close traversal is not IRQ-masked time and static instruction counts are not measured latency. Cost gates retain argv, ELF/nm/disassembly/sizes. Compare minimal-resource/complete-capability profiles separately; different-workload images are not one baseline.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "OS delivery scope and verification", "type": "status"}, "delivery/qualification.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Platform mechanisms are implemented; current development verification describes actual execution scope. Final clean-source SDK, offline double-build, candidate sealing and physical HIL are separate stages. Code completion, CI definitions or a hash cannot establish passed status. HIL fixtures and station templates are in tools/hil/fixtures/. Admission rejects empty stations, incorrect Board/PCB/chip/probe/image/config, invalid tool files or incomplete budgets. Preparing fixtures is not flashing, serial testing, waveform measurement or completed power-cut testing. Evidence for ThreadX, USB FS, shallow-sleep timebase, Ethernet, advanced timers, G474, the user I/O broker and H743/U575 GPIO/UART references is each bound to independent source scope. Real ThreadX Linux-kernel execution differs from IRQ/libc boundary models. Three-board BASEPRI/PRIMASK ELFs and exact negative links for absent external Tick differ from MCU execution. The installed SDK retention-only permanent-task program establishes linking only; a separately executed bounded-exit real Linux-kernel consumer establishes runtime evidence. A linked program does not imply scheduler execution, and two host cases do not imply complete MCU-backend qualification. The eight public single-image candidate configurations use H743 HSI64/Cache off and U575 MSIS4 MHz/TZEN=0/SWAP_BANK=0. H743 HSI32/Cache-on fixed configuration and limited TX DMA, and the independent U575 TZEN=1 split-domain reference, have separate software/ARM evidence and are outside those eight candidates. Models and ELFs do not observe actual power, option bytes or silicon revision. Per-engine DMA facts alone establish neither actual cache coherence nor security-domain isolation. The SDK must actually build H743/U575 public consumers from the current complete source closure; qualification of an old package that only added CMSIS dependencies cannot qualify new platforms. Exact results are in SoC extension execution. G474 has actual typed-factory host execution, system/provider/EXTI regressions and explicit TOML ARM images (empty/selected/EXTI fixture \u00d7 baremetal/FreeRTOS). EXTI-fixture PA0/PA1 are software wiring fixtures without PCB electrical qualification. Image budgets, operation-table retention, unselected-peripheral trimming and actual vector bytes are checked separately. SysTick 170 MHz/1 kHz/reload169999 is compilation/link evidence, not timing-precision measurement. Host sanitizers only check executed software paths. Reference crystal/PCB/solder bridges, hardware timing and final-clean-HEAD SDK/offline reproduction/new candidates have separate exit conditions; other G474 peripherals or ThreadX cannot be marked supported from this evidence. USB packet/clock/factory models and software-Board ELFs do not physically prove VBUS, connector, PHY, enumeration or flush cycles. The shallow-Sleep 1 MHz timer model establishes only the executed quantization-phase contract; actual clock/PLL/wake/IRQ/catch-up/power require measurement on the same image. Ethernet descriptor OWN/loan, capture/break and caller-memory-pin software models likewise establish no electrical, throughput, edge-aperture, power-stage safety or MPU/TZ userspace isolation. Public API, mode configuration, SoC provider, reviewed Board route, final candidate and physical execution have different exit conditions. Returning tasks release storage only after actual kernel detach. Prestart discard applies only to proven prestart scope. A scheduler-start attempt that returns is a retaining fail-stop; kernel return cannot mean never started or revoke tasks, DMA, clocks and external effects that may have run. A kernel still running after I/O stop retains clock/Tick. Product reset, safe device outputs and a complete scheduler-stop port are external responsibilities. The platform claims no automatic fault recovery or safety certification. Core nx_fault_observe is a best-effort observation narrow port with no default state. FreeRTOS/ThreadX retaining fail-stop paths supply actual domain/reason/static source position and retain all borrows. Fault context may be masked, ISR, unprivileged or stack-corrupted. Observers cannot assume a working kernel/clock/transport, release borrows, take OS locks or replay requests. Returning hooks still proceed with halt. Fault logging, external safe output and controlled reset are separately accepted; host death tests do not imply executed physical exception handling or safe outputs. Final candidate packages retain exact raw commands, exit codes, JUnit/model/budget and binary identities for revalidation. Adding introductory documentation does not inherit past test qualification; changed source/artifacts require applicable checks to run again. Ordinary CI and source-SDK workflows do not automatically promote products. After hardware connection, first check safe outputs, clock/reset, UART TC/cancel/RX loss, SPI/I2C, Flash power cuts, watchdog and EXTI/PWM/ADC; then measure cycles/latency/high-water and sustained load. External projects own private product policies, field deployment, keys and manufacturing permissions. Ledger implemented records that code/tools exist in source. qualification_record_authority means actual acceptance is determined by external delivery evidence for that source HEAD, not self-reported passed status in source. Formal execution writes to nexus-delivery/\u003cHEAD\u003e/; candidate candidate.json, six qualification classes and raw reports together determine final status. Post-commit acceptance can thus bind exact HEAD, and later documentation commits cannot inherit old qualification.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Software candidates and physical qualification", "type": "status"}, "delivery/security-review.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "CodeQL analyzes maintained production implementations, current tools and behavior tests in an actual Native build. Python extraction excludes only nonexecuted historical documentation tools under docs/archive/, not current tests, configurator, evidence tools or SDK packaging. Queries remain security-and-quality; ownership/path checks were not disabled to remove diagnostics. Successful scan execution and diagnostic review are separate results. The comment-check CLI explicitly terminates invalid input rather than printing an unestablished success report; regressions check failure exit code, stderr and empty stdout. Storage fault tests use their own 0700 temporary directory and fixed filename, validate/enter it through the same directory FD, then restore cwd. They do not check a path before resolving it again. Cross-process persistence reexecutes fixed Linux /proc/self/exe; the command line cannot select files/programs. Actual tests retain two groups of 315 write/power-cut boundaries and fresh-process reads rather than substituting same-process memory. STM32 GPIO register fixtures restore their borrowed global RCC pointer before exit and then actually verify rejection while unbound, avoiding escaped stack addresses. GD32 watchdog fixtures use static storage. Independent-watchdog enable is irreversible; stop returns unsupported and the owner lasts until process/platform reset, not ordinary scope exit. Unused maintenance-tool imports were removed; expected process-cleanup races and polling timeout are documented. Failure still cleans the entire process group and rethrows the exception. Relevant source/regressions are in scripts/ci/test_comment_style.py, tests/contracts/components_storage.c, stm32_io_test.c, gd32_io_test.c and tool contract tests. Current-commit CI and candidate evidence determine final execution status. Asynchronous interfaces necessarily borrow caller addresses stored in providers/owners. Stack-address-escape diagnostics require checking actual lifecycle; implicit heap allocation, removal of IRQ tables or removal of barriers cannot bypass them. Generated MCU-provider objects use static storage; private-model local storage follows the same responsibility. GD32 Flash program/erase enters unusable state and retains its static provider when relock readback fails. Ordinary stop cannot claim recovery. Controlled reset is required; the owner cannot be released or writing continued. Current GoogleTest register models inject relock failure, checking that later read/write, erase, geometry, stop and reacquisition cannot bypass isolation, and that later successful locking cannot clear the latched fault. This proves the software failure contract; actual Flash electrical behavior and reset recovery still need hardware verification. Long state-machine readability, comments in third-party headers and borrow diagnostics still require path-specific review. Vendor imports retain original bytes/license identity. Ordinary static scanning proves neither absence of vulnerabilities nor actual IRQ, electrical, power-cut, timing, sustained-load or product-safety qualification. This factory/DMA/block iteration also tests simultaneous complete block/IDLE, latched error prefix, late ADC TC after error, DMA EN drain refusal and NEVER-deadline tail timeout. The ADC zero-clock-divider path found by static analysis was fixed after an actual failing test. Resource parsing rejects declarations inconsistent with fixed provider DMA channels and aliases bypassing timer/timebase reservations. Same-clean-HEAD execution records determine final status.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Security scanning and lifecycle boundaries", "type": "status"}, "delivery/soc-extensions-verification.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This record continues the earlier SoC baseline. The 55-item ledger retains its original exit conditions. Implemented software, executed software evidence and physical acceptance are separate states. Hardware is not connected; every physical result remains not_executed. The qualified software source is commit 98270512ea2ff7eefe8ff79720018e582be3a949, tree 8d3e69d3179d7b0d2382c5fd074c970dfc9348d9. All formal runs use the clean canonical checkout and verify unchanged source inputs. Later documentation commits record this source; they do not claim a new software execution. These scopes overlap and must not be added into a unique-test total. The source SDK tests intentionally create a nonpublishable development fixture to exercise failure boundaries. The separately packaged clean SDK requires its own actual verification before candidate sealing. GitHub CI run 38256744920 completed successfully for this exact source: 29 successful jobs and one normal branch-conditioned documentation deployment skip. Debug, Release, ASan/UBSan and TSan each executed all 199 CTest entries and 3252 Google cases. The artifact catalog is an API observation; it is not a claim that every remote archive was downloaded and independently checked. Eight unsigned software candidates passed sealing and the platform\u2019s complete packed verification. Each has all six required qualification scopes bound to its actual run-a ELF and configuration. Sixteen independent builds used 32 network-disabled container commands; all eight ELF/BIN pairs are byte-identical. Both maps and original raw logs are retained. The source-bound result index records the precise candidate identities, independent review and durable archive references. The final closure check found 102 report references to cleaned SDK fixture paths. Their 1,871,287 bytes were restored only from already retained, hash-identical artifacts. Original reports and qualifications were not rewritten; the first failed closure check remains recorded. All eight complete closures subsequently passed the original validator. The archived Native executables and test sources also remain available for audit. Native binary hashes are explicitly bound at archive time, rather than presented as invented execution-time signatures. These are the recorded GNU Arm 14.3.rel1 reference images, measured from the linked ELF and configuration. RAM below includes the 2048-byte MSP reservation; all eight images reserve zero libc heap. These values describe the selected reference firmware, not a product workload, runtime stack high-water, interrupt latency or WCET. The result index retains loaded Flash bytes, address footprint, per-domain RAM and the original budget reports separately. The public device architecture remains static typed factory plus immutable polymorphic interfaces. Factory lookup does not allocate, start hardware or take a lock. New modes extend selected private providers and configuration facts; they do not add a runtime registry, implicit worker or mandatory OS layer. H743 DMA borrows request and payload storage until acquire-observed SETTLED. Cache-enabled transfers require the caller to own complete aligned cache lines; the provider does not round maintenance outward into neighboring allocations or allocate a bounce buffer. Cancellation and timeout do not revoke the borrow. Failure to withdraw hardware references retains storage in quarantine. The U575 reference admits a fixed TZEN=1 boot profile and an immutable unprivileged NonSecure thread without NonSecure interrupts, an RTOS or concurrent DMA. It clears and verifies future NonSecure RAM before changing attribution, checks final protection state, and enters through the actual compiler CMSE sequence. Its synchronous gateway copies bounded input and checks output before publication. This does not implement general asynchronous cross-domain loans, arbitrary userspace tasks, product authentication or secure update policy. See the complete contract. Normal commit hooks execute the complete GoogleTest/GoogleMock, configuration and tool suites for behavioral changes. Formal qualification additionally binds a clean canonical commit and tree, actual compiler inputs, raw command output, configuration and linked artifacts. Failed attempts remain failed evidence; changing the source requires new source-bound execution. The verification work exposed missing generated-header input in the H743 ARM startup test, a fixed virtual-environment path in CPU configuration tests, an uncontrolled scheduling assumption in a serial-noise test, and lost forced-include context in static analysis. Sanitizer jobs also exposed register-mask width conversions in host models and signal-dependent termination observers. Their regressions retain the actual compiler warnings and production nonreturning loops; test observers must work without a signal-delivery point inside those loops. U575 production clock and MPU masks also required explicit 32-bit operands before bit operations for GCC 13 on the LP64 sanitizer host. The independent comparison found identical allocated ARM sections for the tested old/new translation units after normalizing __FILE__; this is a scoped code and storage comparison, not measured cycles or a promise for every compiler. Regression evidence and final execution status belong with the delivery index. No suppression or skipped test can substitute for closing these findings. Offline candidates cover the eight maintained public H743/U575 combinations of empty/IRQ-UART assemblies and baremetal/FreeRTOS. Each requires two fresh, network-disabled builds and comparison of actual ELF/BIN bytes, with both maps and raw logs retained. The H743 cache/DMA test fixtures and U575 paired-image reference have their own ARM evidence; they are outside those eight single-image candidate configurations. An unsigned software candidate is not a product release or physical qualification. The local archive is /workspace/nexus-soc-delivery-98270512/platform-evidence.tar.gz: 1,269,027,807 bytes, SHA-256 3aae3a6b96701ce1a042cb4e43078d00d59ba5460ebc4816fa68e2fe4a8fe41c. Its embedded delivery-manifest.json and external manifest describe 216,230 entries, including 173,376 regular files. Original raw paths and record bytes remain unchanged. Resolve the recorded final execution root beneath platform/; additional roots and nested Native/source archives have explicit member maps. The result index binds the archive, manifests and verification receipts. An independent streaming review checked every outer member, all eight candidate payload closures, the three nested Native/source archives and the exact source identities from the retained archive bytes; no full unpack or original-root fallback was used. All eight candidates also passed actual verification after copying to separate fresh directories while access to original evidence, SDK, OCI and ARM tool roots was denied. Original-path read attempts were zero. This verifies their packed evidence closure; rebuilding still requires provision of the recorded external OCI image and GNU Arm toolchain, whose binaries are not embedded. The local archive is not a published GitHub Release asset or a signed product release. SOC-28 through SOC-32 and SOC-36 are exact partial references. Their original hardware, peripheral and isolation exits remain open. SOC-33 bootloader/running state handoff, SOC-34 dynamic frequency changes and SOC-35 deep STOP with continuous time are not implemented. SOC-37 through SOC-55 provide station, admission, capture and result-validation tooling; physical execution remains pending equipment. Fixed clock profiles do not establish DVFS. Ordinary WFI or tickless idle does not establish deep STOP. Host models and linked ARM images do not establish electrical behavior, DMA/cache coherence on silicon, TrustZone isolation, interrupt latency, measured power or WCET. Applications, PCB-specific policies and recovery decisions remain in external projects.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "SoC extension verification", "type": "status"}, "delivery/soc-refinement-verification.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This is the historical 3728b334 baseline. Later functionality and execution status are recorded separately in SoC extensions. The hashes and counts below do not qualify those later changes. This record covers the 55-item SoC ledger. It distinguishes implemented mechanisms from the exact source and execution scope of each result. Subsequent source changes do not inherit earlier results. All physical tests remain not_executed. The following commits passed ordinary staged hooks, including the unchanged-index TDD gate and commit-message checks. No test filter, skipped-only report or hook bypass establishes acceptance. Counts describe their individual execution scopes; they must not be added together as a final-platform test total. All numerical suites above completed without failures or skips. Their staged source-tree identities and raw hook outputs are retained in the external nexus-soc-20261012/evidence/batch-*/ and nexus-soc-qualification-20261012/batch-*-hook-passed/ records. An earlier failed hook remains a failed attempt; its output is not rewritten as a successful run. The dependency-only commit ae27e999f157fecd027db99bdfa99ce38582a940 additionally passed 47 actual source-SDK boundary tests with zero failures or skips. Source and dependency state were clean before and after execution. Relocated public consumers exercised Native C/C++, the existing platform ARM profiles and ThreadX within their declared scopes. This run did not qualify H743/U575 firmware. Its raw command receipts and consumer artifacts are recorded in nexus-soc-qualification-20261012/sdk-dependency-boundaries-v2/. The implementation qualification source is 3728b334b02eb2eb70bcf5d4caafbb9746837dd3, tree 48ba820e5c2d4c372bf7eb6e909c49850cc1fe76. Subsequent documentation commits describe that exact code source; they do not replace its qualification identity. The portable result index records exact report hashes and archive-relative paths without copying raw logs into Git. Normal hooks passed on that source. The same complete model execution binds 203 reviewed I/O properties to assertion anchors and current production inputs; this is bounded executed evidence, not a formal or exhaustive proof. All five maintained ARM gates passed, covering sixteen memory/reference images and 87 selected-provider images. The same code source passed all 58 installed SDK boundary tests. Its 236 actual commands include expected nonzero negative-path results; the test suite has zero failures, errors or skips. Earlier results, including 58 SDK tests and 22 legacy contract programs on 23cde7e, are retained as source-specific history and are not promoted to the new source. The H743 ELF gate inspects the actual reset-to-power-entry closure before RAM is usable, in addition to vector bytes, selected retention and resources. The U575 gate preserves the declared TZEN=0 single-state boot boundary. Its vector/count checks do not automatically prove the complete Reset/SystemInit/data/BSS/MSP call closure. Independent review has now checked all eight actual H743/U575 images, including the complete linked startup path and four-byte DATA load alignment; 2,297 identified inputs stayed unchanged before and after capture. The gate\u2019s source_and_call_closure_review_required boundary is closed by that separate source-bound report, not by relabelling the vector test. Neither ARM gate executes a scheduler on the MCU or establishes a measured electrical result. The actual selected-reference costs are below. Flash is the sum of LOAD file bytes, not the largest occupied address span. RAM includes reserved MSP exactly once; every row reserves 2,048 MSP bytes and zero heap. Static reservations are not measured stack high water or product application budgets. The selected H743 and U575 images expose 60 and 68 bytes respectively of GPIO lease/gate objects. These are not total Platform storage. An inlined or unretained helper without a separate function symbol is not assigned zero cost. These observations describe exact linked selections; different backends, capacities or routes are not comparable optimization percentages. No cycles, interrupt blackout time or WCET was measured. The same observer also inspected three existing images from the completed 87-fixture matrix without recompiling them: These RAM figures also include the 2,048-byte MSP reservation and zero heap. The supplementary report records exact symbol sizes, decoded instructions and static branch sites. Different routes and capacities make these separate baselines. The eleven measured profiles do not represent every maintained mode or establish cycle counts, blackout duration or a cross-chip optimization rate. External examples are qualified at code commit f02b91867bb64abfa31e3c1d4b6004bd1a38b091, pinned to the platform code above. The actual 46-command clean execution and its source record are indexed separately. The ten HIL preparations do not mean ten physical station executions. implemented records a mechanism in source. contract_implemented and documentation_updated record narrower contract/documentation work. qualification_record_authority delegates acceptance to the actual external record for the exact source/configuration/artifact tuple; it is not a source file\u2019s self-reported pass. final_source_pending means that execution is still required for the completed source scope. selected_profiles_observed records actual static cost measurements only for the profiles named above and in the index. tooling_software_validated qualifies the HIL software contracts; the corresponding physical test remains unexecuted. The partial-reference implementation states preserve their limited GPIO/UART scope even after host, ARM or installed-SDK checks pass. SOC-05 records versioned datasheet, manual and errata provenance. Actual silicon revision and full per-mode errata applicability remain unqualified. SOC-23 and SOC-27 require observed actual ELF costs; an observer\u2019s unit tests alone do not complete measurement. A changed capacity, backend, Board route or memory layout must be reported as a distinct selection rather than a like-for-like saving. SOC-28, SOC-30 and SOC-31 contain only the exact H743/U575 GPIO/UART/time reference subset. SOC-29 and SOC-32\u201336 retain explicit missing features. Their original exit conditions remain unchanged. A rejected unsupported mode is not a completed mode implementation. SOC-37\u201355 contain executable software preparation, admission, capture and validation tools; every physical exit remains open until real station records are captured and validated. No result here establishes MCU cycles, interrupt latency, Flash power-loss behavior, cache/DMA coherence, TrustZone isolation, product safety, certification or a product release. Exact support remains defined by the support matrix and SoC contracts.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "SoC refinement execution record", "type": "status"}, "delivery/support.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The derived capability matrix automatically checks SoC/route modes, reference-Board bindings and production sources selected by the actual resolver/emitter. Narrative tables describe runtime contracts; derived tables describe exact identities and bounded source consistency. Neither inherits old-HEAD qualification nor replaces physical execution. Baremetal/pinned FreeRTOS are explicitly selected thin adapters. Startup establishes platform clocks/interfaces only, without schedulers, product tasks or workflows. FreeRTOS POSIX host execution verifies software lifecycle, not MCU IRQ ceilings, FPU context or physical stack high-water. H743 uses official Cortex-M7 startup/bounded LDO entry and forbids RAM writes before ACTVOSRDY, establishing selected clocks/Cache from trusted HSI64/Cache-off cold state. Disabled Cache bits do not prove foreign dirty data clean; trusted reset/prior successful owned clean-disable is required. Default U575 Platform uses official Cortex-M33 startup/TZEN=0 single-address state. Independent TZEN=1 has separate entry/security owner. Reading option registers after startup cannot legalize incompatible startup. Neither changes option bytes or automatically takes over bootloaders, DMA or existing clock/IRQ owners. Original CMSIS Device H7/U5 bytes/licenses/versions are source-locked. Entries: H743, U575. ThreadX is an additional explicit backend pinned to official Eclipse ThreadX 6.5.2.202603. Configuration maintains Cortex-M4 GNU on three F407/F470 Boards and Native Linux GNU. Independent CPU runtime maintains M4 integer/M4F hard-softfp with BASEPRI/PRIMASK policies; other Cortex-M ThreadX ports reject. Production graphs, actual Linux task/queue/join and three-board ARM links are verified separately. Linux pthread stacks/internal host allocations are not MCU cost. ThreadX implements none of this project\u2019s FreeRTOS MPU/split-security/tickless modes. See ThreadX. Platform start enters no kernel; products enter explicitly. Running schedulers cannot lose clock/Tick through ordinary I/O shutdown. CPU software additionally supports M0/M0+/M3/M4/M7/M23/M33/M55/M85. One strict fact parser selects Core/Arch/atomics/baremetal-FreeRTOS runtime. M0/M0+ RMW uses bounded saved-PRIMASK. Optional cache/MPU/SAU execute real CPU algorithms; FPU/MVE/security choose exact kernel ports/ABI. CPU-only runtime generates no SoC/Board/factory. H743/U575 slices separately own assembly/acceptance. Compilation, register models and actual FP/MVE/cache/MPU/security-world acceptance are recorded separately. cache.h range maintenance and M7-only cold control are separate; CMSE permission observation alone provides no user-memory pin/copy/transport. Restricted-MPU-task/split Secure-NonSecure runtime have independent software implementations with separately verified narrow delay/tick SVC, Secure-context leases and caller static storage. Default remains privileged single-world; MPU+split rejects; no physical HIL added. See Cortex-M and Arch contracts. The full peripheral table below describes F407/F470 launch implementations. G474 additionally maintains selected EXTI; H743 fixed Cache/bounded UART TX DMA; default U575 GPIO/IRQ UART with separately maintained synchronous split-domain GPIO. Chip names/generic interfaces imply no additional peripherals, DMA, ThreadX or low-power modes. USB configuration/factory actually ARM-links on software-Board fixtures for both STM packages/GD470. Default reference Boards expose no automatic USB connector/VBUS electrical facts. vbus_sensing=true rejects absent reviewed electrical facts. PA11/PA12 AF10 silicon routes prove no actual jumpers/power/VBUS/ESD; CAN1/USART1-flow-control conflicts must reject. See USB packet contract. The following slices have independent production mechanisms/development evidence. Final configuration/SDK/full-image integration are accepted separately. One passed model cannot mean whole-platform/reference-PCB acceptance. Reference Boards declare no default Ethernet PHY/REFCLK/reset wiring. Qiming-ZG optional RMII provenance needs PG11/13/14, unauthorized on VE100. Sky Star/Liangshan Pi lack reviewed PHY routes. External projects own protocols/cables/PHY power-reset/product MAC/recovery. See Ethernet, advanced timers, user I/O broker and G474. G474 maintains actual generated-factory host contracts, independent system/provider regressions and empty/GPIO-UART/selected-EXTI baremetal-FreeRTOS ARM fixtures. Gates check budgets/table retention/unselected-driver trimming/vector bytes. Reviewed Board documents/pinned official CMSIS prove no actual MCU/X3 crystal/VCP-LD2 bridges. Initial SoC execution retains historical code revision; extensions independently bind later source/evidence. Offline reproduction/candidate sealing have separate exits and inherit no old F407/F470 qualification. H743 bounded TX DMA maintains only USART3, DMA1 Stream0, DMAMUX1 channel0/request46 and AXI SRAM reachability. Cache on requires caller ownership of complete 32-byte lines and clean before DMA. Neither incomplete preparation nor drain releases borrows. Cache lifecycle is fixed at platform start/stop; all providers drain before timebase withdrawal/cache clean-disable. No RX DMA, other DMA engines, optional RAM layout or live scaling. Independent U575 split partitions/fixed principal/synchronous-borrow premises are in TrustZone. Ethernet fixtures retain exact TX/RX descriptors/RX buffers and admit only STM main SRAM128 KiB/GD main SRAM192 KiB. Development budget/retention on four ZG ARM graphs authorize no GD ADDSRAM/TCM, STM CCM, VE100 PG routes or product PHY. Independent cold review/clean-source SDK/physical qualification remain separate. Default references select no ETH/MDIO. Exact bounded-DMA launch routes follow; the resolver rejects other controller/channel combinations. Reviewed software-Board configurations support STM32F407 I2C1/I2C2 and GD32F470 I2C0/I2C1 IRQ/DMA assembly/typed factories. Dual-controller fixtures have independent-instance isolation/real-SDK link evidence. Reference Boards expose declared bindings only; other silicon routes/actual wiring require separate review. See multicontroller ownership. SPI DMA requires nonempty/nonoverlapping actual TX/RX spans; fixed-address directions access caller one-byte storage only. No scratch/implicit copy pool is allocated. Providers recheck actual memory domains/permissions; STM CCM/GD TCM cannot be DMA buffers. DMA TC is a memory fact; UART TC/SPI idle are line facts. Both drain proofs precede SETTLED. Cancellation/timeout/EN-disable refusal retain borrows in QUARANTINED. UART irq-blocks keeps independent IRQ RX. rx-dma-idle uses bounded DMA/IDLE, rx-dma-continuous hardware double buffers; explicit dma-duplex/dma-duplex-continuous also exist. Caller blocks remain immutable after consumer acquire until matching-epoch release. Continuous modes reserve two hardware targets/replacement blocks. Replacement-address readback, bank/new-error checks precede old-block publication. No replacement means stop, never overwrite consumer loans. RX-only uses a separate read-only table without TX-DMA methods or corresponding linked code; the shared receive-state layout still contains TX-DMA base fields, so zero cold-field overhead is not claimed. RTS/CTS does not replace capacity/drain. External RS485 supplies DE pin/polarity/setup-hold; actual UART TC precedes hold/DE withdrawal. See UART DMA/RS485. ADC trigger-dma uses ordinary bounded DMA: stop trigger/conversion/memory writes per block before publishing complete blocks. Callers explicitly service stabilization/GD calibration before next trigger. No free loan means pause without overwriting/invented exact missed samples. Inter-block gaps are expected. trigger-dma-continuous explicitly selects hardware double buffers/preposted blocks, publishing after successful replacement; at least three caller slots are needed. Frequency must satisfy exact timer division/full scan time. GD continuous selects SDK sample-time codes; bounded mode retains 15-cycle samples. Optional timestamps are software publication observations, not sampling apertures. One latched TC/CT cannot count unobserved multiple rounds. Stop with outstanding loans returns BUSY. I2C IRQ/DMA share a V1 register algorithm checked against both SDK layouts/bits; MCU hot paths access registers directly. DMA TC is not wire completion. Final byte/BTF/STOP-MSL/DMA-EN/IRQ publishers drain before SETTLED. Separate bus-clear requires quiescent peripherals, exclusive open-drain GPIO and bounded waits; at most nine completed pulses. STOP/release failure retains leases for cleanup retries. No automatic pin switching occurs on unreviewed Boards. See I2C recovery. These are SoC implementation capabilities. Default reference bindings, software-fixture routes and field measurements are independent. STM defaults declare no qualifiable SPI CS; resource matrices measure only empty/GPIO/UART, so fixture SPI does not qualify reference-board SPI. Default GD SPI4 PF6/7/8/9 has public provenance; external NOR exact part remains unknown. Continuous modes promise no losslessness under arbitrary IRQ latency/consumer speed. I2C reads beyond 256 bytes, dynamic clocks, deep sleep/STOP, secure boot/update and unmaintained modes are unavailable. FreeRTOS lowpower_port=\"timebase\" uses shallow Sleep only on fixed F407/GD470 profiles, requiring SysTick/tickless/single-privileged/no-MPU. Default custom adds no sleep mechanism; CPU-only runtime without SoC timebase rejects the choice. Actual phase/clock continuity/wake latency/power are physically unexecuted; see shallow sleep. Unsupported configurations reject explicitly rather than claim support through successful stubs. Deadlines cannot cancel Flash pulses; uncleared BUSY retains responsibility. Products own Flash stalls/voltage/watchdog windows/power cuts/authentication/layout/persistent-format migration. Generic storage CRC/commit is not a secure-update protocol. GD32 Flash relock-readback failure retains static providers/rejects further operations and requires controlled reset; ordinary stop cannot recover it. tests/google/gd32_flash_fault_test.cpp checks postfault read/write/controller-reacquisition refusal, without physical qualification. See security review for lifecycle/scan diagnostics. Hardware is disconnected. Clock/reset/GPIO-safe-output/UART-TC-abort-RX/SPI-I2C-electrical/Flash-power-cut/watchdog-reset/EXTI-waveform/PWM/ADC-precision execution is absent for every MCU target. Current support cannot replace physical HIL or product-release promotion.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Exact support scope", "type": "status"}, "delivery/verification.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This nine-core CPU/Arch/Core/OS-runtime/platform iteration must rerun verification. Old candidates, historical test counts and development directories cannot qualify new HEAD. Ledger implemented means source/tools exist; acceptance reads the corresponding HEAD execution reports. Final execution is external in nexus-delivery/\u003cHEAD\u003e/; every report binds source, configuration, dependencies, tools, command exit codes and artifacts. Canonical SoC commit, actual complete gates, offline candidates and remaining acceptance are in SoC extension execution; early baseline records retain original-version evidence. Versioned datasheet/manual/errata indexes establish provenance only; actual silicon revision and mode-specific workaround applicability are separately accepted. soc_costs.py retains actual ELF/disassembly/tool calls and comparable profiles; size differences across backends/routes/capacities are not same-configuration optimization, and instruction counts are not measured cycles. tools/hil/soc_acceptance.py provides nineteen plan/capture/validate contracts for SOC-37\u201355. Plan yields only prepared_unbound; reference G474/H743/U575 cannot bypass reference-only admission through renaming or recomputed metadata hashes. Actual station/tools, protocols, instruments, continuous nonempty samples and reviewed limits determine results. Physical Flash power cuts additionally require external erase-aligned test layouts outside every firmware LOAD. Development directories build/controller-routes-factory/ and build/minimal-factory-resources/ retain separate attempts/raw commands without overwriting earlier runs. Resource numbers describe only corresponding ELFs and must be rerun after source changes. MSP is explicitly reserved, heap=0; unused memory regions are not automatically resident merely because the chip contains them. Assembly/ELF bytes cannot replace actual cycles/IRQ latency. CPU/runtime development execution is in /workspace/nexus-all-cortex-evidence, retaining separate raw CPU-facts/schema, atomic-publication/RMW, nine-core register-model, optional-feature compilation and real-kernel-context audit reports. Public reports record actual commands, exits, tools and hashes; pass counts/features are read from execution, not inherited from prior counts or one combination. Source/installed Runtime consumers and complete-platform consumers establish separate evidence. Runtime has no concrete startup/SoC/Board/factory and cannot replace full-platform consumption or candidate sealing. RED logs are in tools/testing/evidence/; GREEN is actual framework execution. Migration of already-correct contracts is explicitly marked; production behavior is not changed merely to manufacture RED. Commit hooks check staged snapshots/index trees at start/end, reject behavior changes during execution and publish no new success summary on failure. Formal software qualification requires clean source/dependencies, exact tools/configuration, nonzero fresh tests, matching ELF/BIN/map, current budgets, independent source-SDK consumption and offline double-build. Exact support scope is authoritative; software-fixture connectors are not inherited by reference Boards. All physical timing, precision, waveform, reset, power-cut and sustained-load items remain unexecuted. FP/MVE context switching, IRQ latency, cache/DMA coherence, MPU/SAU and security-world cooperation also lack physical acceptance. Optional restricted-MPU-task and split-runtime software execution cannot establish physical isolation. Actual HIL must record PCB, chip UID, probe/serial/power, station, budgets and the same candidate image.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Current evidence and acceptance boundaries", "type": "status"}, "design/README.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Current code uses unified physical directories, TOML static assembly, typed factories and shared polymorphic tables. Public C11 faces contain only read-only ops and private context. Each instance owns independent state; callers supply request and block storage. SDKs/provider storage remain private and product projects external. Use these design contracts together with current delivery; historical documents establish no current qualification. Ledgers retain B0\u2013B6 and original exit conditions, adding implementation_paths/evidence_paths, software_status and hardware_status. Implemented means mechanisms exist, not every exit passed. Development cannot replace clean-source SDKs, formal offline dual builds or candidate sealing. Formal NG028/030 executions update freshly generated evidence. Hardware is disconnected and all applicable physical items are not_executed. Glob denotes explicit directory families of one mechanism, not recursive discovery or default builds; X-Gen-Lab/nexus-examples paths belong to a separate repository. CPU software covers M0/M0+/M3/M4/M7/M23/M33/M55/M85, selecting atomic backends, ABI, optional FPU/MVE/cache/MPU/SAU and baremetal/FreeRTOS ports through explicit CPU/IRQ facts. External CPU runtime generates no SoC/Board/factory. ThreadX maintains a separate explicit Native/M4 subset and inherits no nine-core FreeRTOS scope. See nine-core support for configurations and outstanding conditions. This does not expand whole-platform chip/board scope. Launch boards are Qiming STM32F407ZGT6, SkyStar STM32F407VET6 and Liangshan GD32F470ZGT6, with baremetal/FreeRTOS, explicit ThreadX and Native models. New modes and G474/H743/U575 slices use their documented configuration, complete-image and SDK exits; public types do not imply whole-chip integration. G474 adds PA0/PA1 EXTI. H743 supports four HSI 32/64 MHz \u00d7 Cache off/on startup combinations and finite DMA TX, without runtime reclocking or DMA RX. U575 TZEN0 and independent TZEN1 OS-free synchronous dual-world references remain separate; the latter is not generic dual-world factory, NS RTOS or asynchronous borrowing. SOC-33\u201335 remain unimplemented; SOC-36 remains partial; SOC-37\u201355 physical acceptance remains not_executed. Unreviewed physical PCB facts for all three Boards remain unknown; software fixtures are not physical routes. Roles follow the ten-person configuration: TL1, Core/I/O/OS2, BSP2, common applications/communications2, QA/HIL2, build/release1. Codex owns maintenance without named individuals. Remote branch protection needs independent GitHub verification and cannot be inferred from local role files. Behavior changes follow RED \u2192 GREEN \u2192 REFACTOR. Host GoogleTest/GoogleMock tests execute real providers, factories and owners; configuration tools use Python unittest. Commit hooks and CI run the same gate; MCU targets link no test frameworks. Build-time resources, actual register models, ARM ELF budgets and physical HIL establish separate evidence; actual linked images measure method-table overhead. Source compatibility and SDK versions", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Current design and execution ledgers", "type": "explanation"}, "design/arch-contracts.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This contract covers software mechanisms for M0, M0+, M3, M4, M7, M23, M33, M55 and M85, and the Native model. Implementation starts at Arch; configuration examples and whole-platform boundaries are in Cortex-M support. CPU software support, chip integration and physical acceptance establish separate evidence; adding a CPU profile adds no SoC, Board, peripheral or HIL qualification. tools/configure/cpu.py validates explicit CPU/IRQ facts and returns immutable CpuProfileIR. The same parser serves whole-SoC and external CPU-runtime assembly, deriving compiler options, atomic backend, IRQ-mask types and maintained FreeRTOS ports. Compiler macros validate the selected instruction set; they cannot replace chip facts for DSP, optional FPU, MVE, cache, MPU, DWT or SAU. A complete platform generates a static typed factory from exact SoC facts, Board wiring and one external TOML. CPU runtime reads only one runtime.toml and its referenced cpu.json; it generates no vendor driver, linker script, vector table, startup, pin route, device object or factory. Both assemblies forbid CMake cache, runtime register probing or a second default configuration from overriding validated facts. Arch depends on no SoC, I/O, kernel or vendor SDK. Core acquire/release publication uses compiler load/store directly without Arch. OS and log RMW use Arch atomic ports; optional OS uses Arch\u2019s valid-context and mask mechanisms. Products supply tasks, stacks, TCBs, buffers, memory layout, protection policy and recovery responsibility. CPU-runtime clock_hz is a configuration fact; it neither sets clocks nor generates a monotonic source for request deadlines. nx_arch_irq_save/restore saves and restores incoming PRIMASK; tokens are single-use in strict reverse order on the same CPU/thread. BASEPRI/FAULTMASK remain unchanged where present. Only privileged Thread or configurable IRQ may participate in this exclusion domain. NMI/HardFault are not masked by PRIMASK and must not concurrently access its metadata. Tokens are neither kernel critical-section tokens nor cross-core/security-domain locks. Masked sections update bounded metadata only, with no kernel calls, callbacks, waiting, allocation or payload copies. nx_arch_irq_masks returns a query snapshot, not a restore token. Baseline M0/M0+/M23 never read absent BASEPRI/FAULTMASK. IPSR identity, privilege and security state are queried separately: exception numbers are not SoC IRQ IDs and do not authorize FreeRTOS ISR APIs. Handler privilege cannot be inferred from the interrupted thread\u2019s CONTROL.nPRIV. DMB, DSB and ISB preserve their own ordering semantics; they prove neither DMA drain, peripheral idle, cache coherence nor request SETTLED. DWT reads only an explicitly permitted, enabled privileged cycle counter and never enables or resets it. Unavailability returns failure with outputs preserved. Frequency changes, pauses and 32-bit wrap prevent it from replacing monotonic deadline time. Actual IRQ latency, privilege switching and cycle bounds still need target-chip measurement. Architectural external-IRQ limits are 32 for M0/M0+, 240 for M3/M4/M7/M23, and 480 for M33/M55/M85. SoCs must provide their actual valid vector range; architectural maxima do not replace chip facts. M0/M0+/M23 use two priority bits; Mainline declares 3\u20138 from facts. System exceptions do not count as external IRQs, and CMSIS register-array capacity is not a processor capability limit. M33 DSP is optional and requires explicit dsp; disabling it generates +nodsp, without substituting compiler defaults for chip facts. M4/M7/M55/M85 have DSP; M0/M0+/M3/M23 do not. Contradictory facts are rejected. These boundaries were checked against ARM\u2019s M23 TRM, M33 NVIC, M55 NVIC and M85 NVIC. M7/M55/M85 cache lines are 32 bytes; chip implementation of each cache must still be explicitly declared. nexus/arch/atomic.h operates only on live, naturally aligned uint32_t objects. Initialization precedes publication; all concurrent accesses use these helpers or compatible compiler atomics, never mixed plain reads/writes. It is not for MMIO and guarantees no atomicity against DMA or other bus masters. Acquire loads/release stores use compiler atomics on every maintained CPU without masking IRQs. Successful compare/exchange is acq_rel, failure is acquire; failure updates caller-private expected without inventing spurious failure. Fetch add/sub returns the old value with 32-bit modular arithmetic. Owners remain responsible for published-state and payload lifetimes. M0/M0+ RMW uses a fixed saved-PRIMASK read-modify-write span and restores incoming masks. Only same-CPU privileged Thread/configurable IRQ may participate concurrently; NMI, HardFault, unprivileged threads, other cores, DMA and other security domains cannot access that storage concurrently. This CPU-local mechanism claims neither lock-free nor bus-atomic operation and never silently links libatomic. Its masked span has no loops, callbacks, allocation, blocking or payload copy; a bounded instruction sequence is not measured worst-case cycles. Other cores use compiler lock-free word RMW. Exclusive instructions may retry; lock-free does not mean a fixed cycle bound. M23 belongs to this backend and must not be classified as M0 merely because it lacks BASEPRI. All accessors must stop before deactivating an object; release/acquire does not automatically reclaim requests, notifications or queues. cache.h separates data clean, invalidate, clean-invalidate and instruction sync. Capabilities come from exact CPU facts; maintained cache-line size is 32 bytes and zero means absent capability. Every operation requires a nonzero whole-line-aligned range, without address/length overflow beyond the 32-bit CPU address space or outward rounding. Callers exclusively own each complete line, exclude other CPUs, DMA and security domains, and keep cache configuration stable. Only privileged Thread/configurable IRQ is permitted; operations neither automatically mask IRQs nor enable cache. Present but disabled cache returns UNSUPPORTED. Instruction sync checks all relevant caches before any maintenance write, and callers must also prevent execution from the range. Functions neither flush unrelated dirty lines, validate code nor perform global branch-predictor maintenance. Invalidate may discard dirty data; callers explicitly accept that consequence. DMA-receive maintenance before start and after drain is coordinated by the provider/product. Cache operations themselves neither prove DMA idle nor end request/buffer borrows. Work increases with complete-line count and cannot be broadly called O(1). cache_control.h separately provides reviewed M7 cold-cache enable/disable; other CPU profiles explicitly return UNSUPPORTED. Range-maintenance capability does not imply global cache-control support. Callers provide zero-initialized, exclusive, noncopied control objects and require privileged Thread, PRIMASK=1, BASEPRI/FAULTMASK=0, excluding DMA, NMI/HardFault and other executors using relevant memory. Only trusted reset or a previously successful owned clean/disable establishes known-clean cold state; zero CCR bits do not prove absence of foreign dirty lines or authorize claiming unknown bootloader state. Memory attributes, execution and coherence policy belong to callers. Implementation checks M7 identity and actual maintained set/way geometry before taking responsibility for selected I/D caches, saving/restoring CSSELR. Failed readback cannot report success. Partial failure retains owned-mask or selector-restoration responsibility; the same object must remain for disable retries rather than reclaiming foreign state from current CCR. Disable runs only after all providers/DMA drain. The all-set/way D-cache clean through CCR disable uses a stack/call-free leaf with DSB/ISB so final clean cannot create local dirty lines afterward. Work grows with actual set count; fixed latency is not promised. The mechanism preserves incoming masks, discovers no devices, allocates no storage and establishes no complete system DMA-coherence contract. mpu.h keeps separate v7 size/subregion and v8 base/limit/MAIR formats. Pure encoders are available on any profile and reject invalid/reserved encodings, misalignment and incomplete boundaries without truncation/rounding; failures preserve outputs. Inputs and outputs must not overlap. Encoding proves neither SoC reachability, DMA coherence nor product-permission correctness. Optional M0+ MPU is ARMv6 PMSA with compatible RBAR/RASR format; this does not change its ARMv6-M instruction architecture. M0 provides no MPU programming capability. Actual region programming requires privileged Thread within this security domain, incoming PRIMASK=1, an already disabled MPU, and caller-exclusive selector/state ownership. Implementation validates profile, fields, actual TYPE region count and context before writing the selected region. It preserves CTRL and incoming RNR, does not enable or clear every region, and accesses no other-world aliases. Handler or enabled-MPU calls are rejected without automatically disabling protection policy. v8 MAIR updates only the selected byte and preserves neighboring attributes. The caller owns all regions using that attribute and their effective memory policy; implementation performs no cache maintenance. Later MPU enable, reconfiguration and context switching belong to OS or product integration outside Arch. The platform maintains explicitly scoped FreeRTOS MPU user-task integration; the OS contract defines its CPU, task, storage and syscall boundaries. These capabilities cannot be inferred solely from an Arch encoder or single-region programming, or extended to unmaintained combinations. security.h queries this image\u2019s explicit single/Secure/NonSecure state. Queries neither authorize cross-world access nor probe/infer real SoC IDAU policy. The SAU encoder validates exact 32-byte base/inclusive-limit and NSC flags, preserving outputs on failure. Only images explicitly compiled Secure with SAU declared in facts may perform SAU MMIO. Writing/clearing one region requires Secure privileged Thread, incoming PRIMASK=1, disabled SAU and exclusive ownership; actual TYPE region count is checked before writes. CTRL, ALLNS, incoming RNR and other regions are preserved; SAU is not enabled and no cross-world registers are used. NonSecure/single images write no SAU MMIO. IDAU, overlap rules, Secure boot, NSC veneers, dual-world gateways, shared-resource protocols, keys and secure updates belong to products/chip integration. Compiling Secure-only or standalone NonSecure FreeRTOS kernels does not establish paired-world contexts, Secure-task gateways or user MPU-task isolation. These features have no physical acceptance evidence in this iteration. nonsecure.h observes NonSecure unprivileged read or read/write permission over a complete span only from Secure privileged Thread, using actual CMSE instructions and checking security attribution plus NS MPU. It does not dereference pointers and rejects null pointers, zero lengths, address overflow and invalid access; adjacent valid regions may be conservatively rejected. Native, single-state and NonSecure images return UNSUPPORTED. Programmable SAU is not required; fixed IDAU may establish attribution for observation. Success is only a permission observation at that moment: no pin/copy/borrow, gateway-caller authentication, proof of RAM, DMA reachability, object lifetime, bus firewalls or fault-free actual access; interrupts are not masked and the other world is not frozen. Integrators must exclude MMIO, prevent attribution/mapping changes, separately copy input snapshots and validate outputs before irreversible effects. The independent U575 synchronous dual-world reference explicitly supplies these owner prerequisites; it is not a generic transport for arbitrary user address spaces, asynchronous DMA or NS RTOS. See U575 dual-world contract for exact boundaries. GoogleTest/GoogleMock replace hardware at narr", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "CPU, atomics and runtime assembly contracts", "type": "explanation"}, "design/build-system.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Nexus uses one authored TOML assembly, a validated immutable configuration IR, static instances and typed factory, and one CMake software target graph. Ninja executes the declared graph. Products own application sources and composition. The 48-item execution ledger preserves the requested exit conditions. Implementation, executed software verification, environment distribution and physical acceptance have distinct evidence. Historical source qualification does not qualify changed build tools. Configuration publication is a single-writer transaction. It protects every authored and fact input before changing owned output. A rejected or interrupted attempt cannot expose the previous configuration as current. Successful identical generation preserves file content and timestamps so ordinary reconfiguration does not force unrelated compilation. A configuration digest is not a compiler or generator-source identity. CMake owns source lists and target dependencies. The resolver emits selected facts and its configuration-code dependencies, never a parallel software build graph. ABI, optimization, diagnostics and firmware link policy have explicit target boundaries. The firmware target guarantees its declared delivery outputs and resource check. External application warnings are a consumer policy. Developer presets and native CMake/CTest remain the normal build interface. Focused tests are development feedback. Commit gates and formal qualifications execute their complete declared suites with fresh reports. All runners preserve actual command exits, bounded concurrency, deadlines and process cleanup. Preparation may retrieve dependencies. Formal reproducibility uses two fresh network-disabled builds with the same verified source SDK and recorded environment and toolchain bytes. Compiler caches improve development only; they cannot prove fresh-build reproducibility. Durable evidence stores retain original records, member identities and failed attempts without changing their historical status. Behavioral fixes use actual RED then GREEN execution. Python tools use unittest; C/C++ contracts use the pinned GoogleTest/GoogleMock host framework. CMake graph and policy defects require real small consumer builds and artifact observations. Firmware stays C11 and contains no host test runtime. Normal commit hooks and independent review apply to this maintenance work. The following support matrix records observed host tools and declared locks. The final clean-source software gates and eight exact offline candidates are recorded in the execution record. Host observations retain their own scope; source qualification uses the recorded code commit and never follows a later documentation HEAD automatically. Linux descendant cleanup depends on Linux process observation. Generic process termination paths on other hosts do not establish the same nested-process acceptance. Windows and macOS path handling is an implementation boundary, not an integrated test result. Ninja single-configuration builds are maintained. Ninja Multi-Config is explicitly rejected; other generators have no implied acceptance. C11 remains the firmware language. Native test targets enable C++17 without linking that test runtime into firmware. The Linux x86_64 CP312 offline tool closure contains 15 hash-locked wheels, including the Setuptools build backend required by isolated local pre-commit preparation. A fresh CP312 virtual environment completed an actual --no-index --require-hashes installation and real isolated hook initialization. The complete isolated tool domains retain their actual recorded source scopes; the final ordinary commit gate separately executes the current source. CP311 wheel bytes were checked against their 15-wheel lock; CP311 installation was not executed. The minimum Python version and wheel inventory do not substitute for that missing installation result. The recorded OCI artifact uses Python 3.12.15 and Git 2.47.3, distinct from the current host versions. Its manifest is e936b188b9e30cf37ce109a5407019c91fe2dbc62fb10afceb3f2abbfc823ac8 and its image config is a1ba03c16d424aef88dad6e5b2ead2892734a0bf909dedc23d8855fa4861fbe0. The actual ARM toolchain tree contains 7,190 files and 1,106,755,364 bytes with tree SHA-256 44d2fe14e401730074302ad87bdef9762a6cfe12de4bebc6853d181fd51c74fd. Toolchain and OCI transport executions retain their own tool-source identities; their results do not transfer source qualification to later tool changes. Fixed base/frontend digests, Debian snapshot selection, five APT package versions and hash-required Python inputs are declared in the recipe lock. Reconstruction of the observed OCI bytes has not executed successfully: recipe_reproducible remains false. Verified artifact reuse and declared recipe inputs therefore remain separate acceptance states. The execution ledger keeps all 48 requested requirements and exit conditions unchanged. Its updated paths bind the implementation, original RED/GREEN records and the final-source software gates in the execution record and result index. Historical measurements retain their source and exact selections instead of inheriting a later HEAD. Automatic developer provenance binds a unique immutable command-attempt receipt and checks its command and working directory. Complete current contracts and actual candidate plan execution establish this association; a shared latest report name cannot substitute for an exact attempt. BLD-18 retains full SDK byte checks at each consumption boundary after measurement, with no parent marker or persistent verification bypass. BLD-31 retains common static libraries after measuring declared versus actually compiled units and exact selected-image costs. Their decision_verified states record measured choices rather than speculative source splitting. BLD-38 records actual fixed-checker candidate plans and genuine failed-checker execution. The original orchestration exit condition remains unchanged. The maintained 72-stage software plan did not run through one complete plan invocation; individually executed full gates are recorded separately. BLD-39 validates dependency-upgrade evidence contracts, without claiming an actual vendor dependency upgrade. BLD-40 covers actual CP312 offline installation and isolated hook preparation; CP311 installation remains unexecuted. BLD-41 covers actual whole-toolchain and OCI transport and verified reuse of the same bytes. BLD-42 remains declared_recipe_verified_reconstruction_unverified, with recipe_reproducible=false. BLD-44 covers actual new-source archived proof and independent archive-only verification. Local archive retention and a durable hard-link name do not establish off-site storage, publication or a release. The host observations retain their recorded source scopes in /workspace/nexus-build-maintenance-20261012/documentation/host-tool-support-observations.json. Current locked closure and final-source receipts are indexed in the delivery result index. The ledger\u2019s configuration, target, workflow, delivery and transport reports retain their raw executions, failure attempts and source scopes. Physical HIL remains not_executed throughout this build-system maintenance work.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Build system maintenance contract", "type": "explanation"}, "design/classic-can.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Public interface: nexus/io/can.h. Native, STM32F407 and GD32F470 maintain the same classic CAN ownership mechanism. Both SoCs support only CAN 2.0A/B: 11/29-bit IDs, DATA/RTR and 0\u20138-byte DLC. The interface rejects CAN FD, TX DLC above 8 and out-of-range IDs. Public nx_can_port_t contains only read-only ops and private context. Three physical mailboxes correspond to three static slots, without software TX queues, allocators, global mutable registries, workers or mandatory OS. Assembly supplies the exact receive-ring capacity. Unselected instances generate no state. Current production ARM objects have no .data/.bss; actual assembly builds establish final unselected-ELF GC/resource results. One privileged task executor serializes submit, cancel, service, read, recover and stop. Entries reject Handler, unprivileged or already masked PRIMASK/BASEPRI/FAULTMASK contexts. Four IRQs perform bounded mailbox/receive work: at most three mailboxes and three initially present frames from each of two FIFOs. MCU registers use direct volatile MMIO; Native replaces only external register/NVIC leaves in model builds. Production MCUs carry no model-access tables or runtime-model branches. IRQ wake is a hint, never a request-predicate substitute. Binding reads all four actual IRQ priorities/grouping and validates each policy/syscall ceiling without changing global grouping. Sinks support task service/cancel and IRQ notifications. Executor counts retain the borrow after sink capture; stop, detach or recover returns BUSY while notifications have not returned. Each assembly explicitly generates one nx_can_filter_domain_t owning primary/secondary clocks, 28 shared filter banks, split and immutable filter plan. Domain stop can release clocks only when no other controller retains an active bit. STM32 CAN2 depends on CAN1 clocks/filter domain; GD32 CAN1 similarly depends on CAN0. Stopping one instance neither resets the primary controller nor overwrites peer filters. Private bindings check actual register offsets, bit masks, mailbox/FIFO/filter strides, CPU IRQ counts and NVIC priority bits against locked SDKs at compile/startup time. CAN input has no APB-timer doubling rule. Prescaler/BS1/BS2/SJW are physical counts and must exactly satisfy clock_hz = bitrate \u00d7 prescaler \u00d7 (1 + BS1 + BS2); parameters are not searched or substituted. Initial support uses one-shot NART, ABOM off and locked RX FIFOs. Filters provide only 32-bit masks, explicitly match IDE and optionally RTR. Filter ownership and bank/split ranges are validated before writes. Safe assembly order is reviewed Board electrical/pin handoff \u2192 domain initialize \u2192 each controller initialize \u2192 external executor start. Cold initialization proves no unknown TXRQ in acknowledged init and drains retained RX FIFO. Failed IER, four-IRQ-enable or priority readbacks retain closing responsibility. Exit is each controller stop succeeds \u2192 domain stop succeeds \u2192 Board pin/electrical lease release. Failure prohibits reclaiming provider, ring, filter plan or sink. Prepare copies value frames into READY/consumed SETTLED storage. ACCEPTED borrows the full request until acquire-observed SETTLED. A fourth request, invalid frame/context/deadline or overlap with controller/ring/domain/filter plan is rejected without borrows. Wire completion requires RQCP, TME and cleared TXRQ together; only TXOK proves successful transmission. After W1C completion clearing, RQCP clear is read back before slot detachment and final release-publication of SETTLED; the provider never accesses requests afterward. Cancel/timeout/bus-off/stop requests abort and waits for actual acknowledgement; deadlines do not revoke borrows. Drain timeout or failed W1C clearing retains QUARANTINED. TXOK racing cancel retains actual SUCCESS and cannot guarantee the frame never reached the wire. Successful RTR transferred count is zero; DATA count is DLC. Authoritative bus-off/TEC/REC/last-error snapshots are independent of RX-ring space. ABOM is disabled; recover is explicit controller-local init after all TX loans/publishers exit, never replaying original frames or resetting active peers. Stop with incomplete hardware/IRQ/FIFO drain returns BUSY and retains original storage. After successful stop, read_events rejects STATE without touching released rings. Receive stores value frames, FIFO/filter, raw DLC and IRQ acquisition timestamps. Raw DLC 9\u201315 is normalized to 8 with a flag and at most eight copied bytes, never interpreted as CAN FD. Time is not a physical SOF timestamp. RFOM release must be read back before publishing one event; stuck release closes admission and retains responsibility rather than repeatedly publishing the same hardware mailbox. The event ring never overwrites unconsumed facts. Software loss counts are exact; hardware FIFO overrun reports a lower bound only. Frames previously retained in locked FIFO precede overrun loss markers; pending loss follows old buffered events and precedes future frames. Saturation is explicitly marked. Error state is separately latched; event-queue capacity never decides whether completion or bus-off exists. Qiming V3.1 original tree 08c5f513f1677388fcacaa519dca0c9ef00b6d2e CAN1 example blob 8731616cf0d5bf02917c76463c49e722b39eb415 specifies PA11 RX/PA12 TX AF9; CAN2 blob ef8da679c5e7c7163fe2a48a80b6ce8f9d6ecfaa specifies PB12 RX/PB13 TX AF9. The manual, printed18/physical20, lists TJA1050 U9/U11, 5 V supply, grounded RS and 120 \u03a9 R35/R49. P21/P15 are manually multiplexed jumpers; no software standby GPIO may be invented, and software pin claims do not prove physical jumper position. CAN1 conflicts with USART1 RTS/CTS and USB FS PA11/PA12 and must reject combined selection. SkyStar and Liangshan currently have no reviewed external CAN-transceiver routes; Board bindings are not guessed. Iteration evidence records genuine RED/GREEN, fresh 30 Native + 8 STM32 + 8 GD32 production register-model cases, the same three ASan/UBSan suites, and six GCC14.3 strict ARM units. Historical API-link RED and early harness-configuration mistakes remain separately retained. Development harnesses outside the maintained checkout cannot replace repository build registration, configuration/factory/vector integration, complete ARM ELFs or clean candidates. Physical CAN waveforms, oscillator/propagation/sample-point tolerance, termination, jumpers/voltage, transceivers, arbitration/ACK/error injection, bus-off recovery time and maximum IRQ-masked/WCET timing have no executed HIL. Finite poll_limit is a software-work budget, not proof of longest physical init response; register models prove neither bus faults nor store corruption. External CANopen, product retries and bus-management policy belong to product projects.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Classic CAN mechanisms and integration boundaries", "type": "explanation"}, "design/clock-domain-sleep.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The current IO58 delivery provides static caller-owned clock admission, a drain barrier, and one shallow sleep under fixed STM32F407 / GD32F470 RUN configurations. The component creates no global PM registry, task, timer, maximum object pool or default state. Unselected Nexus::ClockDomain remains an EXCLUDE_FROM_ALL static target; sleep sources for both SoCs also require explicit optional-target references. Boards do not automatically select GPIO wake or alter electrical connections. The public API uses opaque nx_clock_domain_t; mutable storage / constructors are private assembly details. The consumer supplies a naturally aligned, zero-initialized nx_clock_lease_t. Successful admission borrows the token\u2019s identity and storage, with provider-managed fields; copying fields cannot release the original object. Each domain\u2019s explicit capacity bounds alias and retained-identity scans; no capacity-independent constant time is promised. The sole privileged Thread lifecycle owner calls close to prevent new leases, then drives each device\u2019s stop and drains DMA/IRQ/completion notifications itself. Consumers release only after successful detach. Close does not cancel devices, and release does not prove a peripheral stopped. Timeout, close or sleep never revoke existing leases. quiescent succeeds only in the current closed epoch with both count and list empty. Reopen can monotonically advance the admission epoch only after drain; the maximum permanently rejects reuse. Old control values cannot reopen across epochs or establish drain proof. Domain storage outlives all control copies; resetting it with memset is prohibited. Metadata saves/restores incoming PRIMASK; Native uses a real recursive thread-exclusion model. Acquire / release may run in same-CPU privileged Thread, PendSV, SysTick or external configurable IRQ; construction, close, quiescent and reopen require serialized privileged Thread. NMI, fault, SMP, signals and other security domains are outside the contract. Outputs and wake words cannot overlap private provider state, retained tokens or each other; rejection cannot corrupt already borrowed controls. There are no callbacks or accesses after token release. SoC sleep runs in privileged Thread with incoming PRIMASK/BASEPRI/FAULTMASK all clear. Under the same saved mask it finally verifies the closed barrier, actual fixed register configuration and wake sequence, then enters production nx_arch_wait_for_interrupt. WFI preserves PRIMASK; an enabled IRQ becoming pending can wake it and runs after mask restoration. The owner must reread the actual business predicate after return. A changed sequence returns success with slept=false. Set SCR.SLEEPDEEP, STOP and dynamic RUN are explicitly rejected; SCR, RCC, Flash, Tick, pending IRQs and deadlines are never rewritten. A 32-bit wake sequence requires the caller to prove no complete wrap within the observation window; one snapshot does not solve DMA/NMI/SMP publication or unbounded ABA. The verified STM32 profile is nominal HSE8 MHz / M8 / N336 / P2 / Q7, SYSCLK/AHB168, APB1 42, APB2 84 MHz, Flash5WS and SSCG disabled; GD is nominal HXTAL25 / M25 / N400 / P2 / Q9, SYSCLK/AHB200, APB1 50, APB2 100 MHz, Flash7WS, SSCG disabled and TIMERSEL=0. Sources are pinned SDK RCC/RCU/FMC register definitions and fixed startup values in current SoC system.c. GD main Q9 cannot claim USB48 MHz; the separate USB clock owner is responsible. Actual crystal frequency, supply, thermal drift and board layout cannot be measured through ready/divisor readback and still require physical qualification. The owner must exclusively control RCC decisions, register all relevant consumers and provide an actually enabled wake IRQ; this module discovers no omitted consumers and never implicitly pauses a kernel. Actual deep STOP / PLL frequency-change extensions must first drain and stop all Tick/time providers, scheduler deadline borrowers and IRQs, then use an exclusive hardware port for bounded clock restore / rollback. New clock/time domains need cold reconstruction; old absolute deadlines cannot be inherited. This slice does not implement these extensions or disguise an admission epoch as time-domain conversion. Google tests use the real production domain, real Native exclusion, both vendors\u2019 actual SDK MMIO layouts and production Arch sleep, replacing only external CPU context / WFI leaves. Original link RED, actual SSCG/TIMERSEL/non-head-token failure regressions, GREEN, ASan/UBSan, strict ARM checks and identities are in tools/testing/evidence/clock-domain-iteration/manifest.json. These are not clean-release, whole-system zero-RAM/two-offline-build, physical HIL/WCET or power qualifications; later platform-integration/station evidence establishes those.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Explicit clock leases and shallow sleep", "type": "explanation"}, "design/documentation-improvements.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This page derives from the implementation ledger. That CSV is the sole authority for tasks, dependencies, implementation and acceptance values; status updates do not change original IDs or exit conditions. Source SHA-256: d1f5adad33468cb83c2849b9ec5176d10d958db044620943e93fb0d02d6a2c77. The same generator checks missing/stale views at commit and CI. implemented means a page, tool, gate or delivery path exists; development_passed records actual development checks without formal release/exit qualification. pending_formal_evidence awaits exact clean-source, deployment, restoration or independent-review records. See documentation acceptance for actual records/scope and original per-item fields for all states/evidence locations. Items needing formal evidence, failure or unexecuted review: none pending; interpretation still follows actual evidence scope. Previews, software models, diagram sources and successful rendering do not replace public deployment, physical HIL or product qualification. Documentation packages and evidence archives bind the same delivery; never invent unacquired digests or locators. Prioritize P1; P2 is also delivery scope. Codex owns responsibility with independent agent review. New/changed prose follows content/diagram standards and page templates; see the 15 required diagram classes. D1\u2013D6 are dependency-ordered work packages, without aggregating other ledgers\u2019 task or acceptance counts.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Nexus complete documentation-system improvement checklist", "type": "status"}, "design/documentation-standards.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "These standards govern new/changed platform documentation so readers understand tasks, conditions, actions and results for use, integration, maintenance or acceptance. Review existing pages in batches using the implementation ledger. These standards do not establish completion of content migration, diagrams, automation, translation or publication. Must/must not express requirements; should/should not express defaults with justified exceptions; may expresses permitted choices. Normative rules must identify actors, conditions, actions and checkable outcomes. Codex owns responsibility; independent agents review content/diagrams without invented human accounts. Set no mechanical word, sentence or diagram quotas. Complex pages need task indexes and layered explanations; simple actions need not have diagrams. Unscoped \u201cultimate\u201d, \u201ccomplete\u201d or \u201call supported\u201d claims do not replace cost/qualification records. Use the five page templates. They define required questions without forcing identical headings. Architecture explanations must address why layers exist, state/resource ownership, calls, build/runtime responsibilities, legal context, failure responsibility, cost and validation boundaries. Introduce bilingual terms where useful, then use one term per concept. C symbols, configuration keys, error codes, fields and machine identity values in CSV/JSON stay unchanged, including states, IDs, digests, paths, versions and configuration values. Translation must not overwrite authoritative CSV/JSON bytes. Human-facing tasks, explanations and exit conditions may have translations or derived displays while preserving original authority and evidence identities. Distinguish: State capacity/measurement units: B/KiB, Hz/MHz, cycles and \u00b5s/ms. Record Flash LOAD, Flash footprint, reserved RAM and measured stack high-water separately. Define alignment, length granularity, inclusive limits, overflow, deadline domains and maximum configurations. Do not mix theoretical bounds, estimates and measurements. Valid rule: after ACCEPTED, callers retain requests/buffers until acquire-observed SETTLED; reuse of adapter/observer storage also requires drained references. Invalid wording: \u201cmind the lifecycle\u201d, which omits actors, final conditions and failure responsibility. Chip features, SDK offerings, maintained providers, Board authorization, software passes and HIL passes cannot collapse into one \u201csupported\u201d label. Current/target/reference scope must be visible in pages and captions. Docs establish no new platform qualification. Retain original source, failed attempts and status; never relabel old results as current HEAD. Complete examples must come from maintained fixtures or pinned external examples and be associated with actual consumer validation. Prefer literalinclude and explain fragments. Illustrations are not complete tutorials. Ordinary builds do not automatically flash devices, execute arbitrary Markdown commands or fetch remote latest. Every command states working directory, tools/connections, inputs, expected results, success checks and cleanup. Firmware C examples retain C11, 80 columns, four spaces, attached braces, type-side pointers and backslash Doxygen. Tool/test examples retain language conventions, including Python and C++17 GoogleTest/GoogleMock. Docs do not redefine code style. Async examples must explain rejection, accepted borrowing, progression, waits, cancel, drain and final resources. Timeout/cancel does not revoke borrows; failed stops retain QUARANTINED storage. Wakeups are hints, and infinite waits cannot conceal progression conditions. Tasks, private partitions and recovery stay external. Every diagram must have at least an ID, name, purpose, type, scope, relation legend, prose contract and source. IDs support indexes/migration; public anchors use explicit targets/labels rather than automatic front-matter references. Register pairs in the diagram index. One diagram answers one primary question. Layering, build dependencies, generation, data, sequences and states have distinct relations; label each edge when mixed. Explain cross-boundary arrows; unlabeled arrows cannot simultaneously mean calls, generation and ownership transfers. Nodes name source, interfaces or explicit concepts. Mark optional elements with text/line styles, not color alone. Returns, asynchronous events and state transitions point correctly. Aesthetic changes must not invent CPU factories, hidden tasks, unified pools or mandatory OS layers. The following diagrams correspond to DOC-43\u2013DOC-57; order/exit conditions are in the ledger. Runtime diagrams must show that after factory lookup, ordinary I/O calls faces/providers directly; Arch is compile-target-selected; applications own scheduling/progression. Static structure does not determine initialization order or represent every runtime call. REJECTED/ACCEPTED are admission outcomes, not invented nx_request_state_t enum states. Publish SETTLED only after required reference drain; cancel/deadlines never point directly to reclaimed storage. Ending QUARANTINED needs actual recovery/drain proof without implying every provider has recovery APIs. Sequence diagrams must include failure branches or link separate failure diagrams, with Task/IRQ/DMA/security domains marked. Diagrams do not replace preconditions/contracts. Conflicts suspend affected delivery claims until sources are corrected; changing diagrams/tutorials alone must not hide contract conflicts. Initially use editable Graphviz DOT and local SVG pairs. Sphinx may later use its graphviz extension; new chains/locks follow DOC-25/33. Existing Mermaid is migration input, not another authority for the same diagram. Specialized sequence tools need independently reviewed closures justified by real demand. Every diagram satisfies: Reviewable source and stable names/IDs; regenerate after source changes. Dark text/clear boundaries; colors are not sole meaning and grayscale remains distinguishable. Short specific labels with detailed prose; split dense diagrams rather than shrinking unreadably. Complete Chinese/English/C symbols without missing glyphs, overlap, clipping or blurry screenshots. Alt text/captions; narrow screens allow zoom/standalone SVG rather than forcing tiny text. SVG defaults exclude scripts, animation, foreignObject, external href/resources and DOCTYPE. Offline packages include diagrams/sources; loading, font notes and checks need no CDN. Graphviz/actual fonts belong to tool closure; missing/different tools never claim validation. Successful SVG parsing/rendering does not prove architecture correctness. Independent review must check every call/dependency, state and failure edge. Actual display checks desktop, narrow/zoom and print; unexecuted checks remain unverified. Regex prose scanning never automatically decides \u201csupported\u201d or \u201csafe\u201d. New gate behavior uses actual negative-path TDD. Prose/diagram-source changes use applicable checks/semantic review without implementation-mirroring tests. Reviews bind pages/diagrams, code/docs sources, findings, resolution and exit conditions. Correct these before closing tasks: invalid configurations, early borrow reclamation, wrong completion order, invented modules, false support/HIL, broken links, unmarked stale translations or unreadable critical diagrams. Ledger state determines standard application and 58-item implementation acceptance. Unified CLI runs offline commit checks, strict CI, source/output pairing and English/Chinese checks for every current page. Source digests, review markers and newly extracted complete strict-build message sets must all agree; missing/stale/fuzzy translations fail. Mixed sources may use source-language catalogs while code, identifiers and original evidence remain unchanged. Archives do not count toward current translation completion. Semantic, visual, publication and restoration records bind actual inputs; rules/workflows alone never establish execution.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Nexus documentation content and diagram standards", "type": "explanation"}, "design/documentation-system.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Organize maintained contracts, public APIs, usage guidance and evidence into task-oriented, versioned, independently obtainable offline documentation. Reuse Markdown/public headers with Sphinx, Doxygen and Breathe, adding MyST. Product code/policy stays external. The site, CLI, locks, prose, translations and diagram set implement this plan. Actual build/publication/recovery follows the 58-item implementation ledger and execution evidence; the migration mapping records destinations/retention. This document changes no platform support, software qualification or physical status. New/changed docs follow content/diagram standards and templates. The complete checklist derives from that ledger; diagrams distinguish current/target views. Help application developers use the platform correctly first, integrators extend it second, and maintainers/reviewers assess changes/evidence third. Readers complete definite tasks without traversing implementation history first. Applications use current static instances, typed factories and C polymorphic interfaces. One authored TOML is the default configuration; maintained JSON supplies SoC/Board facts. Integration guidance retains context, deadlines, borrowing, draining and recovery. Production support, software tests, ARM links, candidates and HIL are separate. Documents, source, configuration and qualified baselines have explicit identities. Maintain authoritative contracts once; other pages link/include/derive them. Links/search/offline packages cover current prose; history has visible boundaries. Docs create no product apps/tasks/private PCBs/Flash partitions/secure-update/recovery policy. Tutorials pin X-Gen-Lab/nexus-examples; platform source keeps only common fixtures/validation code. Publication does not promote models/linking into hardware qualification. Tutorials follow one verified path; how-to guides solve one goal; references give exact rules; explanations describe principles/tradeoffs. Keep home sidebars two levels deep, using domain indexes for complexity. Search excludes historical tutorials/large raw evidence by default and labels version, page category, actual prose language and translation state. Summaries prioritize tasks/technical content, with source/qualification shown separately so repeated disclaimers do not dominate. First operations reference pinned source/recursive dependencies; never assume GitHub branches match the documentation version. Chip capability, implemented modes, authorized Board wiring, software execution and HIL passes are distinct facts. Derived tables consume existing authorities without new capability/qualification rules. CSV implemented does not automatically become accepted. Overlapping ledgers cannot sum into completion percentages. Preserve original IDs, exit conditions and failed attempts. First change the source root to docs/, retaining docs/sphinx/conf.py during migration. MyST makes design/delivery Markdown navigable/searchable pages. Integrate prose/fix entries before moving mutable content by mapping; do not move everything before repairing references. toctree is the sole navigation composition; READMEs are repository entry points. Initially navigate existing paths, then retain stable page IDs with a small redirect table. Resolve links by actual source location and check every reference/explicit anchor on moves. Review reorganization separately from normative changes. Retain original source paths for: Three existing delivery-results JSON indexes and execution records. Six original domain CSV ledgers and this documentation-system ledger. Original archive content, IDs and historical qualification. Tool-input contracts until references/gates migrate together successfully. Status pages reference/derive these sources without rewriting receipts. External archives preserve original bytes/member locations. New paths do not fabricate old-source execution or change archive identity. No switch to MkDocs/Docusaurus or extra frontend runtime for basic navigation. Evaluate extensions against real failing examples when MyST/Sphinx is insufficient. Do not maintain a second contents/capabilities table or universal snippet DSL per document. Exclude archives, generated Doxygen, _build, raw logs, large archives and fixture outputs. Status pages provide readable summaries/download indexes rather than indexing all evidence. Source-adjacent READMEs are not copied contracts; inclusion verifies relocated links. Other source links bind the site Git revision, avoiding moving development branches. New public pages use standard MyST front matter with a few maintained fields: id identifies pages/migration but does not automatically create MyST anchors. Public anchors use explicit targets/Sphinx labels retained and actually built after path changes; redirects handle old URLs without another reference framework. Derive versions, updates, source links and validation from Git/execution inputs rather than handwritten timestamps/copied hashes. Add legacy metadata in batches without defaults fabricating language or qualification. Replace one aggregate doxygenindex with Core/Arch/I/O/OS/Components entries. Device pages link contracts/call order before generated symbols. Do not hand-copy declarations. Explain factories separately: lookups return generated static objects; ordinary I/O calls providers directly through faces. Examples use actual nexus_factory.h. Default IDs, strong IDs and optional capabilities each bind exact configuration/execution. Public headers and assembly-generated interfaces are not one platform-wide invariant API. Target documentation content, API, status, diagrams and delivery chain This is target design, not implementation evidence. Arrows carry inputs/artifacts; software/HIL records are read-only and documentation builds establish no platform qualification. See FIG-12 for sources/details. Each page has one canonical source declaring its original language. Every current page provides complete English and Chinese prose; Chinese originals generate English through the same gettext chain. Mixed contracts may use reviewed source-language catalogs to translate their other-language passages without duplicating canonical contracts. API identifiers, keys, error codes, CSV/JSON and raw logs retain original bytes or meaning. gettext/PO is the sole translation chain. Review binds source versions; after source changes, affected translations become stale/fuzzy rather than appearing complete because locale builds pass. Only reviewed/stale/missing/original states exist, derived per page and visible. Use both Chinese/English originals to test en/zh_CN round-trips, missing fallback and stale/fuzzy states. Actual prose language/review state determines labels; build locales cannot override them. Visible normative prose on every current page requires independent semantic review with no unresolved fuzzy entries. Fast checks validate source digests/review markers; strict builds also require the fresh complete message set. Missing/stale translations fail. Previews truthfully show actual language/gaps. Archives retain original translation/qualification scope. Keep reviewable diagram sources and local outputs without online Mermaid/CDNs/external fonts/scripts. New renderers need locks/actual validation; arrows, lifecycles and support scope are reviewed with prose. Offline checks cover images/search/navigation/bilingual entries, not home pages alone. Content/diagram standards define all fifteen classes and visual/source/failure rules. Prefer literalinclude from real fixtures or pinned external examples instead of unbuildable handwritten copies. Complete tutorials actually consume current source SDKs. Hardware commands state tools/Boards/connections; normal builds never flash or access devices. Fetch/pin external revision/hash/license inputs beforehand; Sphinx reads them without fetching latest or running external scripts. Full TOML uses production resolvers. Board/CPU JSON follows actual integration paths, not syntax-only checks. Explained fragments retain syntax checks without complete-configuration qualification. Keep nexus-toml-fragment conventions. Only explicitly reviewed fixtures execute maintained shell/CMake flows; never extract/run arbitrary Markdown commands. Complete fragments, illustrations and execution reports state separate scopes. Commit checks default offline without rerunning complete firmware qualification. New doc tools/gates use actual Python unittest RED\u2192GREEN; C behavior tests use GoogleTest/GoogleMock and examples use existing real builds. Prose changes run applicable checks, without implementation-mirroring tests. Unified entry below; tool installation/acceptance is in the maintenance guide: dev.py stays a thin route; docs reuse process runners, timeouts, exits and identity. docs.py/.sh/.ps1/.bat forward or state migration rather than maintaining separate generation. check validates maintained sources/configuration examples without platform qualification. build runs Doxygen/Sphinx in fresh outputs with -n -W \u2013keep-going. preview serves successful outputs without implicit installation/dependency replacement. package verifies success/identity and seals outputs, without publishing products/platforms automatically. doctor distinguishes absent tools, wrong versions and unexecuted qualification. Preserve actual exits, diagnostics and logs. Ownership-controlled outputs cannot overwrite inputs. Old XML/HTML never substitutes for fresh success. Isolate repeated outputs/translation caches. Inject this build\u2019s Breathe XML path rather than reading old docs/api results. Python dependencies/wheel hashes form a separate closure, initially the executed Linux x86_64 Python tuple. Doxygen version/bytes are pinned. HAVE_DOT=NO does not mean the docs chain lacks Graphviz; pin rendering Graphviz/actual fonts too. Execute require-hashes installation in clean environments; lock files alone prove no installation. Other hosts qualify separately. Documentation manifests reuse existing identity and evidence-location structures. The table maps actual schema-1 fields only; the single delivery contract and production packager define the complete format: The three source identities never overwrite one another. Docs may cite verified older code without labeling its execution as current HEAD. Display described/qualified sources separately where different. This plan began at docs HEAD 7abde0e4 and qualified code cc6a065c; historical SoC records remain independent. New implemented tools execute gates on their new source. Version URLs follow this structure; actual publication records determine published versions: release-id binds one manifest; doc updates with unchanged platform versions still cannot overwrite immutable directories. stable is an accepted software-SDK docs entry, not all-HIL-passed. Truthful unexecuted HIL permits software docs publication without invented physical support. Initially preview PRs as identified CI artifacts. Trusted deployment consumes verified output without elevated execution of external PR source. Sites/stable become published only after actual deployment; workflows/build success are not public proof. External-link reports bind current inputs and distinguish bad links/network limits. Unverified essential source/standards/download entries prevent delivery passes; optional-reference limitations remain explicit. Handle legacy /en, /zh_CN and /api individually. Old HAL/OSAL/Kconfig pages point to versioned history/migration rather than silently redirecting same-named functions to different contracts. Relative HTML/images/search/links need no CDN. Public locations do not depend on original workspace paths; retain receipt paths and provide archive/member retrieval instructions. Publish large evidence separately rather than copying it into sites; unavailable downloads are explicitly local-only. Manifests bind inputs/payload; checksums cover payload/manifests but not themselves. Outer archive hashes belong in sep", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Nexus documentation-system architecture and delivery plan", "type": "explanation"}, "design/documentation-templates.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Templates list questions every page must answer. Adapt headings to tasks and reuse standards by inclusion/linking. New/changed prose follows content/diagram standards; metadata alone does not establish content quality. Tutorial code comes from verified fixtures or pinned external examples. Goal and expected completed outcome. Applicable code/SDK version, Board/OS/mode, tools, working directory and equipment prerequisites. Inputs, configuration and resources; label complete inputs separately from fragments. One complete path with commands/APIs, expected results and checks for each step. Diagnosing common rejection/failure while retaining resources. Shutdown, drain and cleanup; when results/storage may be reused. Actual validation records and next steps. Async tutorials link submission/cancellation/drain sequences; external-SDK tutorials link consumer flows. Without equipment, describe software validation/HIL preparation only rather than claiming hardware steps succeeded. One task and its applicable scope. Legal Task/IRQ/privilege context, inputs and resource owners. Shortest complete steps with explicit defaults/exceptions. Normal results, rejection, timeout/cancel and failure recovery. Resources returned and responsibilities still retained afterward. Limitations and authoritative contracts. Link explanations instead of repeating fundamentals, but never omit lifecycle/concurrency conditions affecting correctness. Generate API declarations; manuals never hand-copy competing signatures. Reference complete configurations or explain fragments. Complex controller/endpoint, resource and memory relationships link structural/exact-reference diagrams. Problem and constraints. Current/target structure with diagram identity/sources. Layer responsibilities, state and resource owners. Build/runtime stages and distinctions between dependencies, calls and data flow. Main flows/failure responsibilities; concurrent flows link sequences/state diagrams. Performance/space costs, tradeoffs and conditions. Limitations and actual validation scope. Related contracts, source and how-to guides. Architecture explanations must include corresponding diagrams; simple facts may reference existing diagrams. Do not redraw equivalent diagrams only to change colors/names. Never merge implementation and target proposals in one unlabeled diagram. Separate identities of described code, documentation and qualified source. Exact configuration/scope/tools and actual execution. Retrievable evidence, archives/members and verification. Implemented, executed, unexecuted, failed or blocked items. Support/qualification boundaries and next exit conditions. Never aggregate table numbers across scopes; implemented does not replace software/physical passes. Evidence flows acquire software and HIL independently before joining; software never automatically becomes physical qualification. Readers, goals, versions and scope are explicit. Steps/rules are actionable/checkable with command prerequisites and expected results. Objects, units, contexts, borrowing, failures and recovery are explicit. Current/target/reference and implementation/software/HIL identities stay distinct. Every diagram entity/edge is explainable and matches source/contracts. Code/configuration examples have actual validation or explicit fragment rationale. Prose, diagrams and translations create no competing standards. References, offline resources, alt/captions and legacy URLs are correct. Findings/fixes bind actual pages/diagrams and source identities.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Nexus documentation page templates", "type": "explanation"}, "design/engineering-handbook.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Engineering contracts and risk gates. See delivery status for runnable entry points, individual implementations and actual evidence. This handbook defines team responsibilities and acceptance boundaries; illustrative schemas/APIs do not replace source. OCI preparation, formal offline builds, software candidates and physical qualification are accepted separately. See next-generation-platform.md for the platform design and integration-contracts.md for external product, Board and component contracts. This handbook turns those boundaries into rules that a team of about ten can execute, review and maintain. Those design documents define the final interfaces and directories. The engineering system must answer four questions: what was selected, whether implementation meets its contract, whether actual resources and timing meet product requirements, and whether the delivered artifact is the one verified. Tool counts, test counts, page counts and green pipeline counts are not goals. The first version follows these principles: One default configuration input, one resolved result and one software dependency graph. Different tools must not interpret the same selection independently. Standard C, CMake and testing tools each do their own job. Custom tools add only configuration semantics, hardware resource validation and evidence binding. The platform provides shared mechanisms and truthful capabilities. Products own business workers, protocols, partitions, updates, health and manufacturing policy. Compilation, host models, actual kernel execution, ARM linking, physical measurements and release qualification are recorded separately; none substitutes for another. Make failure paths, resource boundaries and concurrency contracts verifiable before optimizing performance. Never trade ownership or settlement conditions for apparent speed. Each stage has executable exit conditions. Plan completion, implementation completion, software acceptance, physical acceptance and release qualification are different states. The user has deferred physical device operations. The engineering model can first deliver software and HIL tooling preparation; the physical stage remains not executed until a real station and actual results are available. Recommended production-code organization follows these responsibilities. This table is a directory convention, not another automatic loading or startup framework. Products and Boards may be external packages. Public examples demonstrate product assembly without becoming mandatory platform business modules. Maintained SoCs and Boards use explicit targets; directory scanning, name matching and implicit default platforms never select implementations. First-version production modules use C11; necessary startup/CPU primitives use controlled assembly. Enabling tests must not add a C++ runtime, exceptions, RTTI or STL dependencies to production targets. Public C headers must be self-contained and provide required C++ linkage declarations so external C++ products can consume the C API. New behavior tests for host C APIs, components and providers use C++17, GoogleTest and GoogleMock. Helpers, fault injection, host fixtures and mock SDKs belong only to test targets; directory-global includes/defines must not leak into production compilation. Python tools use stdlib unittest and do not require a Python runtime in embedded firmware. Historical C models remain regressions; new behavior follows the TDD contract below. Verify at least three consumption boundaries: a pure C public-header consumer, a C++ public-header consumer and a real external product consumer. Production target interfaces and resource contracts stay consistent with tests enabled or disabled; instrumentation-profile artifacts are identified separately. Separate public, provider and private headers physically. Ordinary consumers receive only selected public include roots; SoC, Board and implementation adapter targets receive the provider/private roots they need. Placing every header in one PUBLIC root and saying \u201cdo not include it\u201d does not establish isolation. Checks include positive and negative cases: public consumers compile necessary types but cannot directly include vendor, private-controller or implementation-registration headers. Visible SDK source does not mean every target should inherit its include paths. Manage vendor source and rules separately. Public structures must not contain vendor types or require products to inherit vendor compilation macros manually to use public platform APIs. Components, SoCs, Boards, tests and products express sources, usage requirements and link relationships through ordinary CMake targets. PUBLIC, PRIVATE and INTERFACE distinctions are contractual; do not depend on directory-global includes, links or compile options. List maintained sources and known generated outputs explicitly. The first version does not discover arbitrary components through GLOB_RECURSE. CMake targets are authoritative for software dependencies. Configuration JSON and generators do not maintain a parallel \u201ccomponent A depends on B\u201d graph. Target definitions and necessary semantic checks establish legal component selection. Generators emit selections and configuration facts; CMake propagates dependencies and determines build order. Do not build a custom linker or reimplement CMake target graphs, test discovery or incremental builds. Startup, vectors, static bindings and strong IRQ handlers that must survive linking require explicit retention roots and ELF checks. Global whole-archive or deleting retention roots must not conceal assembly defects. The default architecture does not generate a generic device registry. Propagate CPU/FPU/ABI, optimization and instrumentation options through named configuration targets, rather than appending conflicting flags in different subdirectories. Component sources, public/private includes and target dependencies stay in their CMake definitions. Optional capability metadata describes implementation facts; it must not redefine software dependencies or claim validation solely from metadata. Generator output should be small and inspectable: resolved configuration, configuration headers, selected-target identities, static resource bindings and necessary linker layouts. Humans maintain ordinary typed C initialization and data paths. Do not generate business workers, scheduling algorithms, driver state machines or a general execution DSL to save a few initialization lines. Assembly uses product-owned TOML schema 2 in assembly.toml: reference an explicit Board, select its exact SoC part, then select the OS/backend, components, instance resources and budgets. Standard Python tomllib parses it; strict field models also provide editor schemas. The CLI exposes init/list-bindings/check/explain/generate. assembly.toml contains no arbitrary software dependency declarations, shell commands or code expressions. Strings cannot redefine CPU instruction sets, ABIs, controller functions or arbitrary compile flags. Modes and optimization options come from supported named profiles; toolchain rules produce and record actual flags. The first version introduces no overlay, inherit, recursive merge or additional configuration language. External products express requirements through complete, explicit assemblies. The same field must not have separate override rules in assembly, Board input, CMake cache and environment variables. SoC inputs declare exact variants, physical memory, controllers and implemented capabilities. Board inputs declare oscillators, wiring and reviewed resource routes. Product assemblies declare application choices and required capacities. Schemas fix field ownership; conflicts fail rather than letting the last input win. Configuration constructs frozen ConfigurationIR, ControllerIR, EndpointIR and MemoryBudgetIR values. Constructors consume IR directly; resolved.json retains the same decisions, input identities and resolver version. The same resolution derives: Compile-time constants, separate private instances, read-only faces and typed IDs/getters in nexus_factory.h. Explicit selections for CMake. Board route and resource-conflict check results. Linker/region definitions for product layouts. Configuration digests and resource-budget inputs. Never edit derived files manually. resolved.json is not user input either: missing or mismatched input identities require regeneration, without fallback to an old generated directory. Failed configuration leaves no partial bundle that can be consumed as success. The resolver rejects duplicate TOML tables/keys, duplicate JSON fact keys, unknown fields, wrong types, out-of-range values, contradictory choices, missing inputs, unimplemented capabilities, illegal IRQ/DMA/pin routes and overlapping layouts. Declared capabilities cannot override implementation capabilities. String conversion to C/headers/CMake requires explicit encoding and escaping; user data is never executed as a script. Each assembly + toolchain + profile combination uses a separate build root. A CMake cache must not silently switch a root to different silicon, Board, backend or ABI. Entry points verify identity and require a new root or bounded reconfiguration on mismatch. Caches improve speed without establishing acceptance identity. CI may reuse correctly keyed caches, but formal candidates need at least clean-build evidence. Keys include tool environment, configuration and dependency identity rather than only branch names. The first version has no Kconfig frontend. Resource safety, static assembly and CMake builds do not require Kconfig. If configuration scale later justifies an interactive choice/dependency UI, implement a separate Kconfig frontend targeting the same typed IR contract. Define one authoritative frontend and preserve semantic checks. Kconfig, TOML and CMake cache must not all become sources of truth, and implicit select/default must not bypass unimplemented-capability checks. A component provides an ordinary CMake target, explicit public headers, private implementation and validation scope. Applications link it explicitly. add_subdirectory must not start threads, register product main functions, read devices or change the external environment. Required hardware enters through typed ports and caller injection. Worker, queue, storage and deadline requirements must be contractual and provided or explicitly approved by products, rather than hidden initialization side effects. A Board package contains exact board/silicon identity, declared inputs, a resource manifest and bounded C/CMake wiring. It owns no product tasks, private protocols, product partitions or manufacturing credentials. The existence of onboard devices and routes does not establish electrical validation. Declared inputs cover compiled Board sources and files needed for generation. Before integration, check routes, resource conflicts, variant/density, clocks and necessary safe initial states. Hashes prove input identity without isolating arbitrary CMake execution; formal builds still use trusted source and controlled environments. Product repositories provide assemblies, main/workers, business scheduling, protocols, partitions, updates and health policy. They consume platform and components explicitly. Standard platform initialization handles infrastructure without generating applications. The first version primarily promises a relocatable source SDK. Binary SDKs, stable ABI, support windows and LTS each need separate policy and evidence. Every formal source package must be verified by real C/C++ consumers outside the package in fresh directories; success inside the source tree does not establish relocatability. Pin external packages to exact versions or source commits/digests. Automatically downloading the newest package by name must not enter formal candidate builds. Do not stack multiple formatters, equivalent analyzers or build systems by default. Add a second tool only when its disti", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Next-generation platform engineering handbook", "type": "explanation"}, "design/ethernet.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This optional mechanism keeps network protocols, tasks, MAC-address policy, negotiation policy, PHY power/reset circuitry and product recovery outside Nexus. Lookup returns immutable Ethernet and MDIO faces; it never starts hardware. The default platform does not instantiate descriptors, buffers, a PHY or a worker. The first mode is RMII, normal four-word chained descriptors, contiguous untagged Ethernet II frames, 14\u20131514 bytes excluding FCS. Length-coded IEEE 802.3, 802.1Q/802.1ad tags, jumbo frames, checksum offload, PTP and scatter/gather are rejected or remain separate unsupported modes. TX padding and FCS insertion stay in hardware. RX storage has at least 1518 bytes; automatic CRC/pad/type stripping is disabled, so the reported RX descriptor length includes its four-byte FCS. The public loan subtracts exactly four bytes after complete-frame bounds checks. No software claim establishes cable, waveform or throughput qualification. Caller assembly supplies exact TX/RX descriptor arrays, TX slots, RX metadata and buffers. No SDK default five-buffer pool or global cursor is linked. The provider validates aligned descriptors, complete Ethernet-bus-master read/write spans, array/control/payload separation and independent buffers before DMA reset or OWN publication. TX payloads need four-byte alignment and may not overlap a request, any provider/control/descriptor/RX storage or another borrowed request. Read-only TX payload sharing is allowed while every corresponding borrow lives. Current STM32/GD32 bindings accept their reviewed main SRAM only: respectively 128/192 KiB at 0x20000000. STM CCM and GD TCM are excluded; GD additional SRAM is not yet admitted by this Ethernet binding. CPU reachability or a SPI DMA selector does not establish Ethernet reachability. A TX request is borrowed only after all validation and atomic admission. Buffer, length and chained pointer are written before DMB and OWN. Normal completion requires a CPU-owned last/first single-frame descriptor with its DMA-written TX status. Both manuals state the last descriptor receives MAC transmit status before ownership returns. The buffer/control reference is cleared, DSB executes, the provider slot is detached and only then SETTLED is release-published. Success reports the submitted bytes transmitted by the MAC, not delivery to a peer application. Returned error status reports IO; no request is retried. Each RX descriptor has DMA, READY or HELD metadata and a monotonically increasing 64-bit incarnation. One complete descriptor is published only after OWN returned and an acquire barrier. READY slots form a caller-metadata FIFO: enqueue, acquire and matching release are constant-time and never scan a variable ring under the mask. A held descriptor remains CPU-owned at ring wrap. No copy pool or overwrite can satisfy backpressure. CRC, length, fragmented, tagged/unsupported and other DMA error frames are discarded with latched flags and saturating discard counts. RBU/overflow observations are visible separately; they do not invent an exact lost-frame count. A stale, foreign or double token cannot rearm a buffer. Epochs survive a successful stop/restart; exhaustion closes admission rather than wraps. The ISR processes at most one TX and one RX descriptor. Coalesced completions repend the selected actual vector when another CPU-owned completed descriptor is ready. Hardware and descriptor accesses are direct volatile loads/stores on the MCU, with no per-register operations table. Optional cold callbacks handle NVIC source control, repending and current-priority validation, rather than payload bytes. The wake is a bounded IRQ-only hint; task service does not invoke it. Its actual current priority/group must satisfy the configured kernel ceiling. A captured wake stays borrowed until its callback returns. Attach/stop reports BUSY while a publisher exists; a callback cannot release its own live context. One serialized privileged task owns control methods, with incoming masks clear. Metadata guards save/restore the incoming PRIMASK. No task, RTOS lock or worker is created. Consumers process their loan outside those short guards. Cold storage validation can scale with descriptor count; it runs before hardware acquisition. Cancellation closes the shared TX engine and is explicitly controller-wide. Other pending TX requests are cancelled too; an already returned successful wire status remains success. Changing speed/duplex requires the external owner to apply link-down first, then observe both DMA processes and MAC/FIFO state idle before applying the new up fact. Until then BUSY preserves the old mode; an unchanged up fact is idempotent. Restarting drained TX in the same mode does not require discarding live RX. This follows the independent process and MAC enable semantics in RM0090 \u00a733.6.1/\u00a733.8 and GD32F4xx manual \u00a727.3.3/\u00a727.4.1. Link-down and fatal DMA/underflow errors close admission and report IO without replay. Task service advances absolute request and drain deadlines. Cancel/timeout alone never revoke request or payload ownership. Withdrawal clears DMA ST and MAC TE, reads their actual state, requires TX process state stopped, issues TX FIFO flush and observes its self-clearing acknowledgement and inactive MAC/FIFO state. Only then may software withdraw an unexecuted OWN bit; the descriptor is read back before references detach. A refused OWN, running engine or stuck FIFO flush retains QUARANTINED requests when drain expires. Later actual quiescence may settle them, but cannot invent a successful transfer. Stop additionally closes RX/MAC/DMA admission and sources, requires RX process state stopped and no active MAC receive path, checks MDIO/reset idle and DMA interrupt-disable readback, detaches and clears the selected NVIC source, then withdraws RX OWN with readback. It clears descriptor base pointers and W1C status and checks those writes before releasing wake/storage ownership. Held consumer loans prevent successful stop and remain immutable. Callers join all task/IRQ publishers and consumers before reclaiming assembly storage or Board clocks/pins. Partial startup owns its residual effects and must use this same explicit stop; a reset/IRQ-enable failure is never replaced with successful cleanup fiction. Clause22 MDIO is a narrow, separate face using the same explicitly owned MAC clock. Addresses/registers are 0..31. Operations run only in privileged task context with masks clear, never wait in IRQ/PRIMASK and require a finite absolute monotonic deadline. The divider comes from actual HCLK: the two supported bindings use 168/200 MHz and HCLK/102, below 2.5 MHz. BUSY rejects without another command. Timeout leaves the real hardware state intact and does not publish a late read value; a timed-out write may already have applied and must not be blindly replayed. DP83848 state contains an explicit address and expected model ID. TI 0x20005ca0 and NS 0x20005c90 variants are selected explicitly; only the revision nibble is ignored. No unknown ID falls back to a supported PHY. BMCR soft reset observes the actual self-clearing bit under the same deadline and invalidates configured truth. It restraps the PHY but proves neither GPIO/power reset nor fitted straps. Products select either explicit 10/100 half/full advertisement or fixed speed and duplex. Configuration success starts negotiation; it does not assert link. Observation reads latch-low BMSR twice plus PHYSTS and reports link only after valid link and, when selected, complete negotiation. Remote fault, loopback/reset or inconsistent fixed mode is not converted to successful link. Production layout/mask assertions use the locked STM CMSIS device and GD32 standard peripheral headers. Mechanism review uses ST RM0090 Rev22, Ethernet normal descriptors, TxDMA default flow, DMA process-state/FIFO controls; GD32F4xx User Manual Rev3.3 chapter27, normal descriptors and station-management clock range. Both first four descriptor words and all registers used by the common mechanism match these proofs; enhanced descriptor/PTP mode remains disabled. DP83848 register truth comes from TI SNLS266E (March2015), Tables6-9/10/11/12/13/18 and https://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/dp83848c.pdf. The documented TI model/revision is 0x20005ca2. The explicit TI and NS ID distinction is independently confirmed by Linux v6.12 drivers/net/phy/dp83848.c; that GPL source is review evidence only and is not linked or copied into the production component. The Qiming F407ZG optional RMII source profile is reviewed separately: manual printed31/physical33 and fixed BSP blob a35e981e8a549e1dbaf84bb4522b2f150bc5d51c show DP83848 address1, an external 50 MHz oscillator and P5/WiFi jumpers; pins PA1/2/7, PC1/4/5, PG11/13/14 AF11. PG routes do not authorize the VE100 package. PHY reset GPIO is unproved. Sky and Liangshan have no reviewed PHY route. Current Board bindings must stay disabled until configuration integration admits an explicit reviewed route; model code is not a fabricated PCB binding. GoogleTest/GoogleMock tests execute the actual common algorithm, both SDK-checked bindings and explicit Native ingress/egress/MDIO hardware, including W1C and stuck reset/engine/OWN/FIFO/IRQ sources. Native hardware uses caller PHY register files, synthetic FCS storage and explicit descriptor-completion injection; it never invents PHY identity, link, real CRC or remote delivery. Host software, strict ARM compilation, linked image resources and physical/HIL are separate evidence. The first three establish only their recorded scope. Physical PHY/link/electrical, actual PCB jumpers, loss under traffic, latency, throughput and EMC are not executed in this environment. Independent review regressions protect the known immutable wake-target span against descriptors, RX DMA buffers, request metadata and writable API outputs. Opaque callback-context size remains an external lifetime contract. Wake replacement rechecks in-flight publishers under the same exclusion as replacing the pointer. Stop rechecks publishers after actual NVIC withdrawal and closes IRQ dispatch before unmasked ring/table cleanup; failed cleanup retains the controller. Closing rejects new RX acquire; existing held loans still release.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Raw Ethernet MAC, MDIO and DP83848", "type": "explanation"}, "design/fault-observation.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Core\u2019s nx_fault_observe(domain, reason, file, line) is an overridable, bounded observation entry. The weak default implementation has no state, allocation, queue, OS lock or side effect; it introduces no implicit event object, registry, worker or instance RAM. Four scalar parameters avoid lending a temporary event structure to the product. Products that need recording must supply a strong implementation and their own static storage. The maintained FreeRTOS default assert/stack-overflow hooks call the observation entry before parking. The stack hook does not dereference potentially corrupted task/name pointers. A returning observer does not allow a corrupted kernel to continue or release request, stack, TCB, IRQ or DMA borrows. The FreeRTOS terminal path reads current masks through a read-only Arch leaf. Existing PRIMASK, FAULTMASK, or BASEPRI equal to or stronger than the kernel ceiling remains unchanged; otherwise it uses the actual port\u2019s disable operation. This prevents a Mainline port\u2019s direct BASEPRI store from weakening an existing mask. Baseline/POSIX builds still follow their own ports and do not claim MCU IRQs on the host. This is a single-core terminal contract; the observer must preserve incoming masks. nx_freertos_assert_failed and the stack hook can still be strongly overridden. Overriding them replaces default observation, so the product must explicitly retain the nx_fault_observe call and guarantee non-returning behavior. Public declarations retain the C11/C++ ABI; returning to a corrupted configASSERT caller is outside the permitted behavior. ThreadX observes a central adapter fail-stop or M4 external timer setup failure before invoking the product fail-stop port. If that port returns, the adapter retains responsibility and parks; it does not pretend that kernel startup, task borrows or clock effects have been undone. Support remains defined by the ThreadX document and actual targets and does not inherit every FreeRTOS CPU combination. The observer runs in the original task, ISR or interrupt-masked context; privilege and context are not normalized. It must be bounded and allocation-free, acquire no OS locks, enter no provider, alter no accepted request, release no retained storage, and assume neither kernel, clock, stack, transport nor peripheral still works. The filename is only a static source literal or NULL; unknown dynamic memory must not be dereferenced. Observation on a corrupted stack is best effort. The kernel ceiling does not prove global quiescence of all interrupts and does not revoke NMI, DMA or external electrical effects. This API implements no safe output, reset, Flash persistence, loan reclamation or safety certification. A product may implement separately reviewed non-returning recovery; responsibility remains in the external project. nexus-examples/apps/uart_owner_runtime/fault_policy.* demonstrates a caller-owned first-fault record and an explicit product output port. It release-publishes diagnostics before calling the port; nested or repeated reports cannot repeat output actions or replace the first fault. A missing or failed port remains unverified, with no cleanup/reset/reclaim. The example selects no GPIO or assumed board-safe level by default. A successful software callback does not prove electrical safety; product/Board/HIL acceptance remains required. Google death contracts verify the default hook\u2019s observation\u2192mask\u2192park order, no dereference of bad task/name, loan retention when an observer returns, and strong/equal/weak BASEPRI, PRIMASK and FAULTMASK. They use the actual default production hooks and explicit port/Arch leaf models; recordability after real faults, physical safe outputs and worst-case time have no board qualification.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Fault observation and retained responsibility", "type": "explanation"}, "design/i2c-bus-clear.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "nx_i2c_bus_clear_t is an optional caller-owned GPIO recovery mechanism. It neither scans devices nor replays failed transactions, and does not automatically replace the SoC\u2019s nx_i2c_port_recover. Existing controller recovery is separate: it proves controller stop and removes hardware references; this mechanism needs explicit board authorization before switching both pins and driving the electrical bus. The public interface is io/include/nexus/io/i2c_bus_clear.h, and the algorithm is io/src/i2c_bus_clear.c. Production remains C11, with no heap, default worker, dynamic registry or hidden OS lock. The caller retains the session, read-only operations table, adapter context and clock, and follows the single-executor contract; these objects must remain alive until the lease is successfully returned. The caller explicitly supplies five methods, acquire/drive/sample/wait_until/release, and an nx_clock_t. Successful acquire means the controller is disabled, DMA and IRQ publishers are drained, other bus masters and pin users are quiescent, SCL/SDA are exclusively owned, and both pins are safely released in open-drain mode. Failure must retain no references or leases and leave no unreported hardware effects. For drive(scl_low, sda_low), true permits only driving low and false permits only high-impedance release. Public nx_gpio_port_write does not prove open-drain mode, voltage, pull-ups or pin multiplexing and cannot directly replace this authorization contract. sample must read actual input levels. The adapter must review PCB revision, both pin permissions, pull-ups, voltage and ownership handoff first; chip capabilities do not establish wiring. This delivery does not automatically bind F407/F470 physical pins or operate unreviewed boards. When scl_low=true, the adapter must establish SCL LOW before changing SDA. The pins may belong to different GPIO ports, so atomic dual-pin register writes cannot be assumed; particularly when retrying STOP from retained failure state, SDA\u2019s previous output state may be unknown. release restores saved peripheral/pin ownership. Failure must retain the lease and saved state; the caller cannot re-enable the controller or reuse the context. All methods are bounded; acquire/release/drive/sample do not wait for another owner. Only explicit wait_until waits against absolute time. The wait adapter must prove a finite return bound and report failure even if the clock stops; it cannot wait forever inside a callback on a frozen timer. The helper cannot preempt or repair a callback violating this contract. It rereads the clock after callback return and reports deadline failure if late. The caller must not recursively call this session from a callback. The caller explicitly selects nonzero low_us/high_us/stop_setup_us/stretch_poll_us from actual device and electrical-mode requirements. There are no default \u2018safe\u2019 values. run takes a finite primary deadline and independent cleanup deadline in the same monotonic clock domain; cleanup must not precede primary. NX_DEADLINE_NEVER, invalid timing and missing methods are rejected before hardware access. After acquiring the lease, the mechanism observes actual SCL/SDA. If SCL is held low, it waits for clock stretching with a bounded poll interval. Every successful wait_until is followed by a clock reread to prove progress to the target. A frozen, backward or exhausted clock reports IO and exits. High hold time starts only after actual HIGH is observed following SCL release. If SDA is already released, no recovery pulses are generated; otherwise at most nine complete low/high cycles occur, stopping immediately once SDA releases. At the end of the low phase, sampling also confirms actual SCL LOW; at the end of a released high cycle, it confirms SCL remains HIGH. STOP first verifies both lines LOW, and after setup again verifies SCL HIGH and SDA LOW before releasing SDA. Host models cannot prove unsampled transients between observations; real waveform acceptance remains required. Every execution that acquired a lease attempts STOP within the cleanup budget: drive SCL low, drive SDA low while SCL is low, hold low, release SCL and wait for actual HIGH, hold STOP setup time, release SDA, then hold high and observe both lines HIGH. STOP preparation edges do not count toward the nine recovery pulses, and an incomplete high cycle is not counted as completed. Failed STOP is never reported as bus free. STOP and recovery state are not transaction completion. Even successful bus recovery leaves the original failed data operation failed; starting a new transaction is external product policy. Outputs include primary/cleanup/pulses/stop_observed/lease_held. run returns the primary failure if present, otherwise cleanup; cleanup never overwrites the primary error. Without an acquired lease, release is not called. After acquisition, failed STOP or a failed release method retains the session and full lease and stops new transactions and recovery pulses. finish retries only unproven STOP or ownership return; it neither reacquires nor generates recovery pulses. If STOP is proven but release failed, the next call retries release only; successful steps are not repeated. finish returns the latest cleanup, preserving the original primary and pulse count in outputs. A new run or reinitialization is rejected while a lease remains; reinitialization cannot conceal residual state. If the release method actually succeeds but returns after the deadline, cleanup reports TIMEOUT while lease_held=false truthfully records returned resources. A deadline error cannot invent a lease. Other retained failures prohibit releasing, discarding or moving session/context; the product must isolate the faulty device and follow explicit recovery/reset policy. The mechanism neither resets automatically nor claims safe outputs. GoogleTest/GoogleMock execute the real production algorithm with external pin/clock/lease mocks to verify the nine-cycle bound, SCL stretching, frozen clocks, primary/cleanup separation, retained STOP failures, cleanup-only retries and instance isolation. This proves the software contract, not real open-drain operation or waveforms. Physical qualification requires actual station binding and raw level capture, measuring SCL low/high, SDA STOP setup, stretching and pin/peripheral ownership after failure. Pull-ups, rise time, multi-master exclusion, stuck-low devices and SDA release timing must be tested using real equipment; boards and devices determine numeric budgets. All relevant physical status remains not_executed.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Explicit I2C bus recovery", "type": "explanation"}, "design/index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "These maintained contracts and active plans describe mechanisms, invariants and exit conditions. Historical documents are excluded from the current site and search.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Maintained design contracts", "type": "reference"}, "design/integration-contracts.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Next-generation integration contract. See current delivery for implemented modes and actual acceptance. tools/configure/ and current public headers define exact schemas and APIs. Illustrative commands do not qualify three physical Boards or promise product releases. This document explains integration of CPUs, SoCs, Boards, external devices, protocols and components, and how external projects produce reviewable firmware. See platform design for boundaries and engineering handbook for development, validation and maintenance. Initial scope is STM32F407ZGT6 Qiming V3.1, STM32F407VET6 Sky Star Youth and GD32F470ZGT6 Liangshan Pi, with bare-metal and FreeRTOS backends. Hardware is not connected now. Source review, software implementation, compile/link results, model behavior and physical qualification are distinct. Actual PCB revisions, jumper settings, electrical facts and fitted parts remain unknown where unverified. Integration does not turn every hardware description into a runtime device tree. It submits bounded facts, selects maintained capabilities, checks resources and explicitly assembles static objects. Use C11, CMake, Ninja, TOML assembly inputs, JSON hardware facts and a narrow Python validator/generator. The default core needs no heap, runtime string lookup, discovery or background workers. The default input is the external project\u2019s assembly.toml. SoC, Board and assembly inputs are validated once into frozen typed IR; resolved.json is its authoritative JSON projection. CMake remains authoritative for code targets and dependencies. Generators produce no component dependency graph, business threads, product main or scheduling policy. Instance selection uses TOML schema 2; SoC/Board facts remain JSON. Tools parse only that assembly input; Kconfig, CMake cache and old generated headers do not become parallel authorities. Directories may follow core/ arch/ soc/ io/ os/ components/ tools/ tests/; Boards may be separate packages. Layer names describe responsibility without requiring each layer, port or object to occupy a separate translation unit. Unmaintained capabilities do not become available merely because the SDK has a macro, a route table has an AF value or another family member has an example. Integrate new routes, DMA modes and controllers separately. SoC providers implement public I/O/core contracts and use Arch. Device drivers use narrow bus/GPIO/time ports; protocol cores depend on neither Board nor OS; component adapters connect I/O/OS. External assemblies wire implementations together. Declaring onboard devices does not make the Board the device-driver owner. Vendor SDKs, kernel implementation headers and mutable controller state stay private; public interfaces do not expose their structures. Similar STM32/GD32 names do not prove register compatibility or direct driver reuse. A maintained SoC package typically includes: Family directories may share code, but soc.json defines STM32F407VET6, STM32F407ZGT6 and GD32F470ZGT6 separately. A family string cannot replace part, package, Flash, SRAM domains and IRQ tables. Adding a part does not establish support for every existing controller. Each routes.json record binds at least exact SKU, controller, pin/AF, clock domain, IRQ, allowed transfer modes, DMA routes and sources. Shared records explicitly constrain applicable SKUs/packages. Source review and physical qualification use separate fields. With an existing SoC implementation, a new Board normally needs only: Boards do not copy SDKs, HALs, RTOS ports or linker scripts. Ordinary GPIO initial levels and pin bindings are data. Add board.c only for bounded electrical actions not expressible through maintained data contracts; that code still uses neutral hardware ports. The following fields are illustrative; example-board does not claim a real maintained Board: Replace oscillator values with the Board\u2019s source facts. Empty bindings provide no UART/SPI/I2C/RS485. Before adding real wiring, verify connectors, pins, levels, jumpers and reviewed SoC routes. Boards reference SoC memory facts rather than maintaining a drifting RAM/Flash copy. External nexus-examples or private products supply assembly.toml selecting Board packages, OS backends, clock profiles, resources and device instances. This minimal illustration declares no peripheral instances: selected-profile-id is an illustrative placeholder; unknown profiles must fail. Category tables such as [uart.link0] select only Board-declared, SoC-maintained bindings with explicit polling/IRQ/DMA modes, required ring capacities and IRQ priorities. [spi_device.sensor0] and [i2c_device.sensor0] explicitly bind controllers and CS/address without workers. Factories return immutable controller and child faces separately. Without an external product layout, images use only the maintained full physical Flash default layout. The platform reserves no generic product storage. Selected product layouts become complete declared assembly inputs and enter resolved IR through the same path. Configuration must follow this fixed order: Read the assembly, locate one Board and its exact SoC part, then check schemas, versions and dependency locks. Check package containment, declared inputs and known/unknown source conditions; expand selected bindings and devices. Build resource claims; check mode capabilities, conflicts, clocks, IRQs, memory, ABI and layout. Atomically write one resolved.json recording decisions, rejections and input digests. Derive hardware headers, read-only resource tables and linker inputs from IR; CMake reads necessary selections and builds its declared dependencies. Verify vectors, startup, handlers, region capacities and artifact identity in actual ELF/map/BIN outputs. Results become evidence without posing as hardware qualification. resolved.json retains schema, packages/parts/backends, effective options, clock/memory/ABI, resource claims, devices, limitations and input hashes. It is neither a CMake dependency graph nor guessed business threads, roles or domain policy. All derived outputs come from that IR. No .config, old headers or separate Board default macros become fallback authorities. Failed generation invalidates configuration; never consume the prior bundle. Write to a temporary directory and switch only after complete success. Separate Board/backend/profile combinations use separate roots. Each CMake library declares code dependencies, public headers and private SDK compile surfaces explicitly. Assembly selects hardware instances/modes without replacing target_link_libraries(). Generators neither infer component needs from arbitrary business source nor link all drivers. Target capability requirements contradicting resolved configuration must fail. Tools may generate static descriptors; mutable contexts and actual construction/init still have explicit implementation owners. Product main owns component/device initialization order and scheduler start. A device node does not create a sampling task. Resource claims must identify physical resource keys, owners, modes and origins. Diagnostics say \u201cDMA1 stream X is claimed by A and B\u201d, not merely \u201cinvalid configuration\u201d. Tools validate explicit choices without finding alternate streams, changing pins or lowering clocks. Sharing SPI/I2C buses does not mean declaring controllers twice. One provider manages the bus; children retain bounded references. Duplicate SPI CS or same-level I2C addresses fail. Direct single-executor calls and explicit queue/service adapters are distinct: only the adapter executor advances shared controllers; waiting clients do not take over. Short locks protect admission/control metadata, never hold task mutexes across DMA lifetimes. Device drivers do not implicitly require kernel locks. Real shared IRQs may use SoC-declared static dispatchers with checked handler lists and bounded execution. Two strong symbols cannot compete for one vector; arbitrary runtime registration is excluded. IRQ response/settlement and subsequent task/worker processing have separate owners. FreeRTOS reserves port resources including SysTick/PendSV/SVC. Configure priority grouping consistently; kernel-calling ISRs must meet the port\u2019s syscall-priority rules, while higher-priority ISRs cannot use those OS APIs. Alternate timer timebases enter the same claims table. Boards supply actual HSE/LSE, electrical and jumper facts; SoCs supply legal constraints and maintained profiles; assemblies choose profiles. Controllers do not reconfigure global clocks independently; drivers do not reset NVIC grouping. Without implemented dynamic-clock switching, runtime switch requests return unsupported. Declare each SRAM domain\u2019s address, size, initialization, DMA reachability and usage restrictions separately. STM32 CCM is not default DMA SRAM; all physical SRAM cannot become one unmaintained linker heap. Initially enable only implemented, checked domains; others remain unmaintained. Linker sections, alignment and maps validate static DMA buffers; runtime validation still checks caller-provided pointers for reachability/alignment. Compile checks cannot replace borrowing/settlement lifetimes. Budgets distinguish MSP, task stacks, OS objects, I/O buffers, component workspaces and optional libc heaps. Flash layouts use exact-part erase geometry and reject out-of-range, overlapping or erase-misaligned regions. Image offsets, VTOR and bootloader/A-B require maintained implementations together; changing link addresses alone does not establish support. CPU flags, FPU enabling, exception contexts and kernel ports must agree. Record toolchain and all precompiled-input ABIs in identity. Source SDKs are recommended; installed-binary ABI compatibility across compilers/versions/flags is not promised by default. Pregeneration validates fact consistency and conflicts. C11 _Static_assert and types establish capacity/type/constant relationships. Linker/map/ELF checks establish layouts, symbols, handlers and retention. Waveforms, electrical levels, clock error, IRQ latency, DMA terminal states and sustained loads need physical evidence. USART1 names a SoC controller; j1_uart and bme280_ext0 are stable assembly instance names; console and ambient_sensor are external application roles. Boards supply physical resources without selecting the product\u2019s primary sensor. External consumers own role mappings/constraints. Their build steps may read resolved information, validate required interfaces and generate typed aliases/accessors. The platform generates no product role policy. After rebinding hardware, applications still use roles rather than searching business source for UART/GPIO numbers. Ordinary data paths need no runtime string lookup. Read-only wiring resides in Flash; required contexts, pools, queues and buffers are static. Role aliases can compile to direct symbols; generic drivers retain one function-pointer layer only where narrow ports require it. Layer names do not justify extra objects or virtual calls. Factories preserve the typed polymorphic-interface architecture. Generators create static objects; factories return existing identities without creating, initializing, locking or reconfiguring devices. Typed getters include nx_factory_uart(nx_uart_id_t). IDs are uint16_t with named constants; out-of-range, COUNT and unselected categories return NULL. IDs are assembly-local, not persistent formats or cross-version binary ABIs. Each public face is read-only { const ops*, void* context }: two pointers on 32-bit targets, with actual Flash/RAM audited from linked outputs. Each provider/mode shares const ops; mutable state and exact-capacity arrays are separate. Methods receive instance context, never treating shared ops as an instance. IRQs statically enter providers without per-byte virtual dispatch. Native instances also have separate generated state; singleton fixtures are explicitly test-only. nx_binding_\u003cbus\u003e and nx_factory_spi/i2c return actual controller ports; nx_device_\u003cchild\u003e and nx_factor", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Integration, resource assembly and source-SDK contract", "type": "explanation"}, "design/io-lifecycle-matrix.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "tools/testing/io_lifecycle_matrix.json maintains 203 reviewed finite properties covering STM32F407/GD32F470 UART, SPI, I2C, ADC, CAN, USB, raw Ethernet, capture/advanced timers, selected STM32G474 software-reference providers/factory, Native descriptor sequences and RS485 direction adaptation. Each property identifies instance mode, production TU, register model, actual Google case, assertions and production calls within that case, and the invariant and limits proved. Names and test counts alone cannot establish evidence that a property holds. Run the maintained command: The gate first runs tool tests and builds the actual CMake nexus_google_contracts. Capture then binds the current catalogue, CMake executable plan, compilation database, compiled objects, each target binary, and explicit SHA256 identities for production/test/model/public-contract sources. Each provider and test TU must belong to its specified Google target\u2019s actual compilation context; shared Core/I/O TUs retain real library objects and commands. Missing objects or targets cannot fall back to matching source names. Capture also saves source, object and command identities for every local TU in selected targets from that actual compilation database, including generated factory bindings.c in the build directory. Sources must reside in the current checkout or build, and objects in the corresponding current-build target. Missing objects, symlinks, out-of-bound paths and changes to generated sources/objects during execution are rejected. This observation reuses the CMake graph rather than registering another build graph. The existing runner performs complete discovery and unfiltered execution, retaining commands, return codes and fresh XML. Audit rechecks that inputs are unchanged, that discovery, XML and execution-summary case identities match exactly, and that every indexed property completed. The commit gate requires the entire CMake Google plan to finish and cannot use allow_subset. Only success produces: build/\u003cpreset\u003e/tdd/io-property-inputs.json: pre-execution input identities. build/\u003cpreset\u003e/tdd/execution.json: the sole runner\u2019s actual complete execution results. build/\u003cpreset\u003e/tdd/io-property-report.json: finite properties bound to those actual results. A new round deletes old summaries and property reports; failures cannot leave previous success reports. If execution succeeds but audit fails, raw execution remains for diagnosis but no qualified property report is generated. Assertion anchors provide reviewable source navigation only. The tool excludes comments, strings and same-named assertions in other cases but cannot automatically prove an assertion\u2019s logical meaning. Models must execute actual production providers; hardware dependencies are explicitly modeled, and model results cannot establish board waveform, electrical or physical timing qualification. Current focus includes residue-free admission, epochs/cancellation, loan retention, quarantine/retry after failed stop, final IRQ/DMA withdrawal, late errors, counts/loss, FIFO/LIFO stream loans, finite deadlines, continuous-receive target replacement and resource exhaustion. This is neither all register combinations, all controllers, all event interleavings nor formal proof. Added properties include CAN abort/domain/loss, USB FIFO/clock/SETUP epochs, Ethernet descriptors/held RX tokens, and generated-factory failure cleanup, foreign-cold rejection, permissions and timer-output readback order. Other GPIO/Flash/basic PWM/watchdog suites derive no coverage from this matrix. The catalogue\u2019s unproven field lists the complete unproved scope. Factory properties actually call current generated entries; models replace only external CPU/MMIO ports. Some assertions use a fixture\u2019s narrow Initialize or declared provider macros. Review must follow fixture definitions to confirm the real production entry rather than interpret arbitrary helper names as production proof. Assertion navigation binds whole sources and compiled contexts but does not automatically analyze reachability of every assertion under conditional compilation. Each target establishes finite behavior only for its actually selected branch. Capture covers listed TUs, selected targets\u2019 local TUs, models, contracts, and actual commands/objects. It does not claim automatic analysis of the complete header dependency closure or machine-code semantics. Clean-source SDKs, candidate source closure, offline reproduction and physical boards remain established by their respective delivery gates. Property reports explicitly state formal_proof=false and hardware_status=not_executed. Developers may use the script\u2019s capture/audit subcommands to inspect existing execution. Explicit --allow-subset produces only development-subset for diagnosis; it cannot replace the commit gate or a software candidate. The script neither runs tests nor creates a second target graph, and adds no instrumentation, RAM, worker or vtable slot to MCU instances. After adding modes or fixing providers, review invariants, real assertions, models and limitations, then bind current sources through fresh build and execution. Historical test counts and hashes cannot become qualification for new sources. Original 80-property development matrices and raw logs preserve their historical bytes; the 203 properties require current-source capture/build/execute/audit, never extension of old successful reports to new properties.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Bounded I/O lifecycle property matrix", "type": "explanation"}, "design/io-observation-recovery.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "IO07, IO09 and IO10 use explicit caller-owned diagnostic objects. Default request, stream, slot and owner fields remain unchanged; the platform creates no global sampler, hidden thread or automatic device-recovery policy. Diagnostics observe actual state; deadlines, errors and diagnostic detach never revoke accepted borrows. Core\u2019s request_observer.h depends only on Core. The caller binds a zeroed nx_request_observer_t to a stable slot and nonzero epoch. Sample first verifies the same live incarnation before accessing the request; it caches no request pointer. After slot release or reuse, the old observer returns STATE. Epochs never wrap for reuse; the existing slot contract rejects further admission at the limit. The caller excludes concurrent sample versus slot bind/release, request prepare/reuse and observer mutation, and ensures bound storage remains valid. Core adds no IRQ mask, OS lock or multicore synchronization on the caller\u2019s behalf. Normal device executors may still advance requests; final result/count is read only after acquire-observed SETTLED. While borrowed, outputs are result=BUSY and count=0, preventing unpublished results from appearing valid. Timestamps share the request deadline\u2019s monotonic clock domain. Backward time returns IO, preserving outputs and the last valid time; diagnostic counters saturate. max_gap and transitions describe differences between samples, not hardware completion time, all internal transitions or WCET. Detach removes only observation binding and is permitted during QUARANTINED, but actual device borrows must still be drained afterward. Outputs must be separate from the complete descriptor, payload and control storage. Public Core knows only the request base size and cannot validate the full derived-descriptor range or detect freed C pointers. Owners usually release their control slot before outer SETTLED; observing that slot then returns STATE. To continue sampling after final-result publication, the caller should retain its own independent stable slot and release it after its readers exit. Optional Nexus::OwnerObserver is called by the sole owner executor. Each call executes exactly one real nx_bus_owner_service and returns its result unchanged. BUSY is counted separately from other errors. Backward observation time sets diagnostic_result without overwriting service_result or preventing continued drain. It then copies active ticket, request state and idle under the existing short metadata guard, without hardware calls or slot-array scans. Copies retain no request/payload references. quarantines counts observations, not failed operations. Service SUCCESS does not mean the current operation finally succeeded; final results still come through acquire-observed request results. It does not infer nonexistent errors from void device service. Observer, owner, slots and guard/executor/clock must retain the same binding and lifecycle. Producers keep the original owner guard; diagnostics add no device hot-path vtable layer. After detach, the owner still owns all undrained work. Diagnostic outputs must not overwrite accepted operations or payloads. nx_stream_observe copies maintained aggregates under one short IRQ guard, with work independent of block count and no slot scans or payload reads. free_blocks is the total free count; next_free says whether the normal stream producer cursor\u2019s slot is available. Out-of-order release can leave free blocks while that cursor remains occupied. A preposted producer has its own cursor and must determine its next target through the corresponding reservation API; next_free is not permission for the next continuous-DMA target. nx_stream_budget_calculate is a pure cold-path check. The caller declares rate, block units, bytes per unit, maximum FIFO-frontier release delay, hardware-target count and spare count. Its calculation is: Rate and block use the same units. For example, multichannel ADC may use complete scans, with unit_bytes explicitly covering every channel per scan. Both uint64 multiplications and size_t addition/multiplication are overflow-checked. Invalid input or overflow preserves old output and returns INVALID/EXHAUSTED; it never saturates into an apparently valid budget. configured_blocks \u003e= required_blocks meets declared capacity; target-replacement latency must be strictly below the rounded-down fill time. This does not create backpressure or remove FIFO blocking, CT ABA or real clock/IRQ latency. Physical qualification still needs measured rates and worst-case service gaps for the board. nexus-examples/apps/uart_owner_runtime/quarantine_policy.c retains its old SDK pin and directly composes existing BusOwner APIs. Actual main owner-progress sites use this policy so service errors enter observable fault stages instead of being silently discarded with (void). BUSY is normal waiting. Real errors close admission, publish FAULT_OBSERVED and establish explicit finite cleanup-time/step budgets. Backward time, deadline-add overflow or budget exhaustion publishes ESCALATED_RETAINED. Later calls still perform actual service; there is no worker kill, reset, automatic replay or undrained-buffer return. Actual idle eventually publishes LOAN_DRAINED, proving only owner loans ended. Producer join, IRQ detach, hardware stop, observer join and notification destruction still follow original lifecycle order. The fault sink runs outside the guard and must be bounded and non-reentrant. Applications retain stages and fault state and wake the coordinator; product safe outputs, device reset and field handling remain external product-project responsibilities. This example establishes no safety level. The following sizeof fixture uses the actual Cortex-M4F GNU Arm 14.3 -fshort-enums ABI; array symbols are ARM-compiled and read with nm. It measures explicit object layout only, not whole-image code size or physical cycles. Measured default sizes are request 24, request slot 16, stream 40, stream slot 40, BusOwner 84 and owner slot 48 bytes. Default structures gain no fields; no sidecar is created when observers are unselected. Other compilers, enum ABIs and pointer widths require fresh measurement rather than reuse of these values. New Google suites contain 15 observer/budget and 7 descriptor/provider state-machine cases. Native UART executes 32 sequences, SPI 16 sequences, and ADC 4 consume/stop sequences; these loops are not additional Google cases. I2C public-dispatch sequences mock external asynchronous-provider dependencies to prove public borrow/retry contracts, without claiming Native I2C asynchronous hardware. Six quarantine-policy cases were added within the external example\u2019s 28 actual owner/lifecycle Google cases. First real stub RED and final GREEN logs, fresh XML, commands and return codes are retained in tools/testing/evidence/io-observer-iteration/. ARM32 capacity checks exposed a strict type-limits compilation error; RED/GREEN for the same production ABI and extreme-capacity regressions are retained together. These are development software evidence. Clean-source SDK, external consumers and the complete candidate matrix bind final sources again through the unified release process. Boards are not connected; physical DMA target switching, error injection, UART timing and safe outputs have not been executed.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "I/O observations, capacity and retained recovery", "type": "explanation"}, "design/multiple-controllers.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The public I/O faces keep their existing operation tables and caller-owned request contracts. Selecting another physical controller creates another private provider instance, not another transport abstraction or global registry. An instance\u2019s cold register, clock, DMA and IRQ facts remain immutable until its publisher set has been withdrawn and the instance stopped. STM32F407 I2C1 and I2C2, and GD32F470 I2C0 and I2C1, execute the same maintained V1 register algorithm. Per-controller cold wrappers validate all register pointers, DATA offsets, line inputs, bus clocks and DMA selectors against the actual SDK. Their EV, ER, RX DMA and TX DMA IRQs come from the selected controller identity. Initialization of an already enabled physical controller returns BUSY before changing its control registers or IRQ priorities. GD32 names in this table are the vendor\u2019s literal DMA0/DMA1 names. The platform configuration\u2019s opaque controller IDs use a separate mapping. A DMA request selector never identifies or allocates a memory engine. Static cold assembly must reject other devices that select an already owned physical engine. Setting the private state\u2019s DMA descriptor to NULL selects IRQ-only messages and acquires no DMA vector. Exact immutable macros NX_STM32_I2C2_DMA(regions, count) and NX_GD32_I2C1_DMA(regions, count) select the secondary finite routes. The initialization API is unchanged. Pin, bus clock, DMA clock and shared resource leases remain cold assembly\u2019s responsibility; the provider does not silently allocate these resources. Each request retains its descriptor, messages, buffers and endpoint until that controller\u2019s engine, interrupt sources and wire have drained. Completing or cancelling one instance cannot acknowledge another stream\u2019s W1C flags, disable its vectors, release its request or change its wake sink. A refused DMA disable retains only the affected instance\u2019s borrow in DRAINING or QUARANTINED; elapsed time does not prove hardware quiescence. The shared DMA acknowledge leaf compiles to a direct selected register write. Its test override models the real W1C side effect rather than treating a clear register as ordinary RAM. There is no per-byte MMIO operation table or new mutable field. Actual Cortex-M4F compilation measures the unchanged state at 56 bytes, endpoint at 8 bytes and optional immutable DMA descriptor at 80 bytes. The provider and shared algorithm objects contain zero global DATA/BSS. Selected object text size is evidence of a compiled object, not an entire image\u2019s retained Flash size or a cycle measurement. The reviewed silicon route sources are STM32 RM0090 Rev22 Table 43 and GD32F4xx User Manual Rev3.3 Tables 10-2 and 10-3. These facts do not establish a board\u2019s external wiring. Secondary-board routes require an explicit reviewed board configuration. Host tests model external CPU/MMIO behavior, and real ARM compilation uses actual vendor SDK headers. Bus timing, electrical levels and DMA contention still need hardware qualification; no physical result is claimed here.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Multiple controller ownership", "type": "explanation"}, "design/multiple-dma-controllers.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The existing typed faces, shared immutable operations tables and caller-owned request/state storage remain authoritative. A cold instance selects one exact reviewed register/IRQ/DMA tuple. There is no stream allocator, runtime registry, extra mutable route pointer or hidden copy pool. The mutable DMA state layout is unchanged; the additional routes consume only selected instances. Reviewed silicon AFs are USART2/USART1 PA2/PA3 AF7, SPI2 PB13/PB14/PB15 AF5, and SPI0 PA5/PA6/PA7 AF5. These facts do not supply external Board wiring, connectors, electrical pull-ups or qualification. Typed configuration remains the single assembly authority. The second UART TX conflicts with the first I2C TX engine and must be rejected in resource resolution. GD literal DMA0/1 is distinct from public opaque controller IDs 1/2. STM UART initialization uses retained exact IRQ, DMA, stream and clock facts. STM SPI initialization validates the selected bus, clock and engine pointers; an idle bus whose DMA vectors remain enabled cannot be acquired by another cold object. GD cold _initialize_at methods select only the two maintained readonly controller descriptors. Existing primary initializers remain thin wrappers. Duplicate/live hardware, retained enabled DMA publishers and unsupported tuples reject before effects. A disabled engine still has an owner while its vector lease is enabled; cold acquisition preserves that owner\u2019s flags/priority. Finite DMA completion is independent from wire completion. Hot W1C clears only observed completion/half-completion bits; fresh error bits remain latched. The provider reads errors after actual engine withdrawal and again before settlement. It never reports success after a detected DMA error. Both engines, IRQ sources and SPI wire must drain before CS release and SETTLED; UART also requires true TC. Failed withdrawal retains QUARANTINED requests and buffers. Peer flags, clocks, vectors, CS and loans remain independent. Shared DMA clock ownership belongs to outer assembly and is never released by an individual stop. Explicit SPI fixed-memory flags preserve caller-owned dummy/discard scalar storage, exact one-byte DMA domains and MINC identity. There is no implicit scratch fallback. STM SPI2 polling abort/recovery pulses APB1 SPI2 reset; it does not reset the neighboring APB2 SPI1 controller. Only finite second-controller UART TX/SPI DMA is implemented here. Existing primary receive modes remain covered by their own suites; secondary continuous RX is not admitted through these new facts. Actual GoogleTest/GoogleMock MMIO regressions and strict ARM SDK object/layout reports are development evidence. Factory/selected whole-image validation is recorded separately. Physical DMA, IRQ latency, CS/wire timing and Board HIL remain not executed.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Independent finite UART and SPI DMA controllers", "type": "explanation"}, "design/next-generation-platform.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Status: Current factory + polymorphic platform architecture contract; see current delivery for implementation and acceptance mapping. Code uses TOML static assembly, pure typed factories, shared read-only method tables, separate private instances and explicit ownership boundaries. Pseudocode explains contracts; current public headers/tests define compilable interfaces. Software execution, offline reproducible builds and physical qualification are recorded separately. The historical architecture is migration context only. This iteration implements design, engineering rules, integration contracts and the 33-item checklist in new production code/build chains. New source/artifacts establish independent qualification; historical passes do not carry over. The user requires existing code formatting/comment style. Architecture may change, while layout, naming and Doxygen retain repository configuration and contribution/comment guides. Better contract content improves maintainability. Companion material: engineering handbook, integration contracts, base execution checklist and factory/TOML/TDD P0\u2013P5 checklist. Experimental results may revise decisions; architecture proposals do not replace experiments. An MCU platform connects fixed hardware, concurrent execution and finite resources so products can reuse trusted implementations. Establish facts/constraints first, then decide which abstractions justify their cost. Priority: satisfy correctness and hard product constraints; reduce necessary runtime cost; reduce reserved resources; reduce integration/maintenance cost. Premature DMA-buffer return, missing errors and unchecked bounds cannot buy better benchmark numbers. First-stage scope: Cortex-M4F, STM32F407ZG Qiming Xinxin V3.1, STM32F407VE Sky Star Youth and GD32F470ZG Liangshan Pi; bare-metal/FreeRTOS backends and Native behavior models. Discovery may remain a development reference without becoming a product dependency. Exact parts, routes, modes and evidence are managed separately. Existing controller support, pin sources and fault tests identify requirements only. New implementations may replace poor structures directly. With no connected hardware, first-stage delivery covers software and HIL tooling preparation. The following compares common mechanisms and their conditions. Trimming, compilers, configurations and workloads affect cost; performance rankings require equivalent experiments. Official mechanism references: Zephyr Devicetree, Zephyr device model, FreeRTOS static task API, RT-Thread I/O framework and Embassy executor. These guide architectural tradeoffs; formal dependencies still need pinned revisions and separate acceptance. Two SoCs, three Boards, fixed BOMs, ten maintainers and C product consumers favor build-time hardware descriptions, link-time implementation selection and only actual execution/borrow state at runtime. High-frequency fixed industrial outputs and enterprise runtime discovery are different problems with different cost models. Products select resources, components, modes and capacities at build time. Startup configures hardware; RUN keeps port identities, routes and write authority stable. Drivers retain actual peripheral runtime state and request borrows. The default has no generic registry, runtime probing, per-pin open/close/ref, global inheritance tree or hidden threads. Fixed resources can still fail; drivers manage BUSY, FAULT, cancellation, draining and controlled recovery. Three paths have different costs: Fixed ports use read-only typed faces: shared const ops and private contexts. Generated uint16_t typed IDs return static identity through factories without construction/startup effects. Production SoC and Native providers implement one public contract; multiple instances share tables but own separate state. Hardware interfaces have a consistent typed polymorphic layout; algorithms use narrow transport/time/storage ports. Provider/mode tables are shared rather than copied per instance. IRQs call concrete providers. OS and bounded owners remain optional, without unnecessary forwarding objects. Not every call is promised inline, nor every LTO build devirtualized. Judge ordinary functions, shared tables and generated direct bindings through ELF/disassembly and physical measurements. Prefer simple maintainable expression first. Responsibility boundaries are not a mandatory layer-by-layer call chain. GPIO can enter SoC implementations directly; pure protocols need not traverse OS, Board or system lifecycle. The proposed directory layout follows; the first vertical slice fixes actual names once. Migrate directories with interfaces, without permanent duplicate paths. Startup/linker/clocks belong to selected chip implementations and assemble once in the final image. No platforms forwarding layer is mandatory. Startup/reverse cleanup can be ordinary C functions; directory symmetry does not justify runtime modules. Components use standard CMake targets with explicit sources, PUBLIC/PRIVATE dependencies and includes. Vendor/generated-private headers and test fakes stay outside ordinary consumer paths. Tools maintain no parallel dependency graph. Assemblies use TOML schema 2, while SoC/Board facts remain JSON. Standard Python tomllib parses the TOML and resolution produces frozen typed IR. Strict models generate editor schemas. init/list-bindings/check/explain lower integration effort; Kconfig/cache do not establish another effective configuration. Reject unknown fields, unimplemented modes, contradictions and conflicts. The first version has no overlay/inherit merging, topology solving or SDK-macro-derived complete routes. Users explicitly select reviewed resources; diagnostics name field paths, resources and declaration origins. Frozen typed IR directly generates resolved.json, headers, read-only faces, shared ops wiring, factories, linker inputs and resource reports. Generators perform deterministic validation/static assembly without business main/workers, schedulers, device state machines or driver algorithms. CMake Presets choose assemblies, formal environments, optimization profiles and separate roots. CMake owns sources/dependencies; it reads results and checks component capabilities without rewriting selections. Cache, handwritten CONFIG macros and generated headers cannot jointly decide one fact. External C composition maps console/control/sensor roles to generated typed physical ports. Schemas/resolved data generate no role policy. Resource declarer IDs support conflict diagnostics, not automatic C access authority or proof of business ownership. Failed resolution blocks builds. Generation commits atomically without old-output success fallback. Separate Board/backend/ABI/instrumentation roots are required; releases record every input and derived identity. Plans check pins/AF, electrical constraints, controllers, shared endpoints, DMA streams/requests, IRQ/shared vectors, EXTI lines, timer/channels, timebases, clocks, RTOS-reserved exceptions, memory, layout and ABI. See integration contracts. Four entities use different models: Controllers own exclusive wire execution. Endpoints do not duplicate controller locks/lifecycles. Sensor drivers use endpoints/narrow transport without choosing PCB pins or creating sampling tasks. GPIO/PWM/ADC streams are not forced into byte transactions. SPI CS/full-duplex, I2C repeated START/ACK/arbitration and Flash program/erase geometry retain concrete contracts. Shared request helpers do not become universal transaction executors. Factories are generated pure typed getters: fixed uint16_t IDs, named constants and static switches; invalid IDs return NULL. They perform no dynamic construction, startup or lifecycle management. Diagnostic names stay outside data paths; SPI/I2C controller/endpoint identities are distinct. GPIOs have no default per-pin owner/generation. Build-time planning proves conflicts absent; applications organize one writer/coordinator. C code obeys that contract; configuration cannot prove every user source avoids unauthorized calls. Introduce checked sessions/epochs only for runtime owner changes, reclamation, rebinding, revocable observers or independent loans. Shared read-only fixed inputs do not need that cost. Managed modes use the same controller and exclude unmanaged direct calls; adding a token while old static calls bypass revocation is invalid. The first stage has no platform-wide dynamic device manager. GPIO batch masks must be disjoint authorized subsets. Runtime out-of-range inputs fail; compiler-proven constants may eliminate repeated checks. Reads snapshot one selected port, not multiple ports simultaneously. Toggle requires an exclusive serial writer or explicit atomic implementation, without implied compatibility with ISR writes. Emergency and ordinary control sharing pins need explicit product arbitration/hardware interlocks; calling both contexts one owner does not remove races. Every fixed controller has one explicit executor. One component controls GPIO outputs; one executor serializes shared buses. Multiple clients may submit through bounded explicit adapter queues. Bare-metal owners can be loops; FreeRTOS owners can be application-declared tasks. No automatic bus/log/completion workers. Requests do not duplicate lifecycle metadata in HAL, OS and completion queues. Distinct business queues have product-owned budgets. Single-owner paths avoid kernel locks; explicit multi-producer adapters add necessary synchronization. APIs define context/serialization; public async calls still check actual BUSY/FAULT, lengths, request states and capacity. One request owner serializes prepare/submit/cancel/result-consume/reuse. Other tasks send adapter cancellation commands instead of using old request pointers. Cross-task controls use stable adapter-owned identity/expected epoch and retain storage until owner acknowledgment. Owners resolve still-valid records before accessing requests; they cannot read epochs from potentially freed pointers. Direct submission has the same request owner/controller executor. Shared clients prepare, submit controls, consume results and reclaim storage; the adapter exclusively starts/cancels/services/drains controllers. Clients do not modify ACCEPTED requests or service controllers through waits. Adapters protect cross-task command storage/identity. Short critical sections protect admission, active pointers, cancellation flags and IRQ publication. Large copies, waits, blocking vendor calls and user code stay outside. DMA does not hold task mutexes throughout transfers; multi-producer adapters admit controls while owners continue cancellation/drain. SPSC requires exactly one nonreentrant producer and one consumer. IRQ, error IRQ and task recovery enqueueing concurrently breaks that condition. Indices use explicit atomics/release-acquire or short critical sections, never volatile as synchronization. Increment/compare wrap avoids mandatory power-of-two capacities wasting RAM. IRQs perform prescribed data actions, publish state and issue supported wakeups. No allocation, formatted logs, unbounded lookup, blocking or arbitrary application callbacks. FreeRTOS FromISR obeys syscall ceilings; higher-priority handlers need proven deferred paths. CPU metadata PRIMASK, FreeRTOS BASEPRI, bus ownership and DMA lifetimes are distinct mechanisms. Architecture review determines minimum barriers; benchmarks do not justify deleting hardware ordering. Plan kernel/HAL time independently; lower RTOS tick rates require a genuinely usable HAL time source. Async defaults use caller-owned requests/payload; queues borrow pointers or small descriptors only. The following illustrates contracts; compilable interfaces are in core/include/nexus/core/request.h: The illustration is direct single-executor use. Wait conveniences require adapters with waiting/service capability. Shared adapters expose their own client admission/cancel/wait APIs; shared request helpers are not mandatory unive", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Nexus next-generation platform: design from first principles", "type": "explanation"}, "design/os-backend-integration.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Nexus maintains Native, Baremetal, the reviewed FreeRTOS ports and the explicit Native/M4 ThreadX subset. ThreadX has its own license, static-task lifecycle and masking contract; it does not inherit the nine-core FreeRTOS port scope. An additional RTOS is conditional on an actual integration requirement and a reviewed kernel license, version, port and resource budget. This document and the executable probe prepare that work; they do not claim RTX, Zephyr or another kernel is supported. Applications continue to own task topology, storage and shutdown. nx_wait_port_t retains its direct callback ABI. The maintained Wait cost models compare actual linked alternatives. Backend integration must not create a second deadline helper, runtime device registry or another assembly authority. Record the actual requirement, kernel license/version, dependency identity, CPU capabilities and explicit exclusions. Obtain the required source under the repository\u2019s existing dependency process; do not edit vendored kernels to hide adapter problems. Add contract regressions first and retain actual RED failures. Use GoogleTest/GoogleMock with real production adapter code, mocking external hardware or kernel boundaries only. Add a real-kernel scheduler probe for kernel behavior; mocked calls alone cannot establish it. Implement the static adapter and its context/deadline/lifecycle documentation. Define notification ownership, shutdown/join and ISR priority rejection before integrating I/O waits. Keep firmware C11, and keep test C++ outside firmware targets. Propose the port in the existing CPU/kernel resolver and CMake target graph. Authored TOML remains the sole selection authority. The resolver must reject unsupported combinations. Integration changes require maintainer review; the probe cannot add a backend to production configuration. Create a strict probe declaration using the Native reference. Select host, baremetal or kernel; list exact Google case identities for each requirement. Kernel candidates add mask/ISR and real scheduler groups, a source lock path and actual retained static kernel API symbols. Run scripts/ci/os_backend_probe.py against the configured production build. It rebuilds the nominated registered Google targets, discovers and executes all their cases with fresh XML, checks exact case identities and reports actual GNU ELF measurements. It rejects missing groups, filtered identities, empty execution, skipped/disabled cases, missing tools and source changes. MCU candidates additionally nominate a production firmware build/target, ELF and its generated resolved-cpu.json or full resolved.json CPU resolution. The probe rebuilds that firmware, verifies ARM ELF format, checks short-enum and floating ABI against the existing production CPU resolver, binds actual owned compile flags, rejects undefined symbols/heap/test runtimes and reads actual retained kernel API symbols and RAM/Flash sections. A missing source lock or CPU/backend not registered by the resolver fails closed. Review raw commands, source identities, tests, actual static storage/Flash, retained instructions and failure paths. Establish a clean candidate through the normal independent CI/reproducibility process. Physical timing, watchdog, low-power clock continuity and IRQ stress still require HIL equipment. The declaration records a required contract; it is not evidence that the implementation honors it. The runner actively produces fresh execution rather than accepting a supplied successful XML or status marker. Source snapshots bind owned implementation/tests, compilation databases and compiled source files before and after execution. They establish a stable working tree measurement, not clean Git qualification, dependency provenance or reproducibility. claim_support must be false. A passing report states support_promoted: false, registration: not_performed, maintainer_review_required: true, candidate_status: not_qualified and physical_status: not_executed. Kernel API retention proves linked symbols; the reviewed scheduler cases and dependency process establish their semantics and identity. A source lock digest alone is insufficient dependency provenance. Native test ELF RAM/Flash counters describe the host executable\u2019s sections only. The Native fixtures allow a 256 KiB caller stack for host sanitizer instrumentation; this is not a recommended MCU stack size. POSIX library, process stack, test framework and sanitizer overhead must never be compared as embedded adapter cost. The native reference command and candidate options are documented beside the probe fixture. CI must retain raw logs, fresh XML and report.json; missing proof is a failed admission probe.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "OS backend integration", "type": "explanation"}, "design/os-contracts.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "OS provides bounded waits and explicit task/synchronization storage; device interfaces do not depend on a scheduler. Default firmware is C11, statically assembled and uses no platform heap. Native pthread bookkeeping is not MCU zero-heap evidence. External products own task roles, priorities, stacks, queue capacities, startup, shutdown, safe outputs and recovery policy. Current waits/shutdown: ready-arm-ready-wait-ready; external owners manage executors and joins See FIG-08/source references for execution semantics and FIG-01 for backend/platform boundaries. The simple source diagram below is historical layering only, not the current optional-backend, progression or shutdown contract. Historical OS layering diagram source: Core has no OS/Arch dependencies. Published acquire/release semantics are kernel-independent. Arch owns CPU-local IRQ masks, atomics and shallow sleep, not ticks, tasks or devices. OS selects backends and validates actual capabilities. Public Wait does not include task creation, device service or dispatch. SoC owns clocks, controllers, actual IRQs and available timers; Boards own actual pins, electrical facts and external devices. Products create owners/producers/coordinators, provide timer/trace/fail-stop ports and start schedulers. nx_platform_start() never starts them on the product\u2019s behalf. Public Wait is cross-backend. Tasks, queues and kernel-specific capabilities use explicit backend types rather than implying every RTOS has identical features. Generated factories return existing device faces without creating OS objects. nx_wait_until() uses ready \u2192 arm \u2192 ready \u2192 wait \u2192 ready. Backends must combine sequence observation and wait entry without losing latched wakeups. Wake is a hint; business predicates alone decide results, and notification sequences transfer no payload ownership. One port has one waiter and may allow multiple publishers under its backend contract. Fewer than 2^32 wakes may occur between arm and recheck. Deadlines are absolute monotonic microseconds in the backend\u2019s domain: Native CLOCK_MONOTONIC, FreeRTOS nx_time_now_us(), or the bare-metal initialization clock. Never switch domains while requests are alive. NX_DEADLINE_NEVER is truly infinite, not UINT64_MAX converted to finite timespec. Microsecond representation does not promise microsecond scheduling; blocking error includes tick quantization and scheduling latency. Finite notification waits must still check expiry while inspecting hints; irrelevant repeated wakes do not extend budgets. Helpers retain one final ready check after backend errors, letting observed actual completion win. Bare metal returns BUSY when unready so callers schedule explicitly. Immediately available queue operations use try semantics; deadlines are consumed only when blocking, and failures preserve caller outputs. Waiting never advances UART/SPI service. Shared-controller producers cannot service inside ready; the sole execution owner must keep running during cancel/stop/drain. Timeout/cancel requests are not settlement and release no request, operation, DMA buffer or notification storage. CPU/SoC facts describe actual IRQ counts, priority bits and grouping. Runtime policy distinguishes no kernel, PRIMASK kernels and BASEPRI kernels; ceiling=0 alone does not prove no kernel. Mainline kernel ISR calls validate publisher IRQ, priority width, grouping and syscall ceiling. Baseline validates actual configurable IRQs under PRIMASK-port rules. Typed IRQ sinks and priority policy constrain only notification paths implemented by the selected provider/backend/mode. Existing types or valid ceilings establish no new mode, kernel or physical IRQ qualification; unsupported combinations fail explicitly. NMI, HardFault and kernel-owned exceptions cannot use these kernel wake APIs. Task APIs require current-world privileged Thread mode, restored manual masks and unsuspended scheduling. Bootstrap permits only the selected port\u2019s prescribed mask. Equal numerical masks do not prove ownership. Reject before mutating objects, publishing references or creating kernel objects. CPU-local masking does not protect SMP, DMA metadata writes or publishers in another security world. IRQ sinks/contexts live until providers stop and publisher quiescence is proven. Every task explicitly supplies TCB, stack and context. Creation checks actual stack capacity against the port\u2019s initial frame/alignment. Startup minima do not replace runtime task, nested-exception MSP or FP/MVE budgets. Native stacks satisfy pthread minimums plus application budgets; their costs do not carry over to MCUs. First scheduler startup requires cold privileged Thread/MSP: CONTROL.nPRIV=0, SPSEL=0 and FPCA=0. Do not leave active FP context or switch main to PSP; clearing flags cannot discard live FP state. MPU bootstrap guards bind actual protected startup instructions, basic exception frames and correct EXC_RETURN. One-shot leases are consumed before restore; repeated raw START SVC cannot regain permission. Ports own subsequent FP/MVE task use rather than banning application floating point. Actual context retention still needs hardware acceptance. Returning joinable tasks follow backend-specific mechanisms: FreeRTOS wrappers publish completion then suspend until external deletion; Native entries return from pthread and pthread_join proves thread exit; ThreadX wrappers publish completion hints then return through the kernel completion path, while join still checks completion and deletes externally. One external lifecycle owner joins each task. TCB/stack/context cannot be reused before successful join; timeouts retain all storage. Native forbids adapter bypass through pthread_cancel, detach or another join. Completion hints alone do not prove actual exit/reference detachment. Permanent tasks use explicit separate APIs. Entries never return; control/stack/context live until reset. They have no completion semaphore, join or destroy and serve products with genuinely permanent task lifetimes. Direct task notification is opt-in: callers identify existing receivers and exclusively owned notification indices; only that receiver waits. Stop/join publishers before unbinding. Receiver handles/storage live until all retained ports become unusable. Platforms reserve no application notification slots implicitly. Raw queues retain minimum storage/native blocking. Absolute-deadline APIs use one domain without repeatedly expanding relative budgets. Large data should queue stable slots/handles rather than copy full payloads in kernel critical sections; slots still have explicit borrows/reclamation. Closable queues are separate opt-in types with exact maximum concurrent waiters. Every blocked operation retains independent caller-owned waiter storage. Close stops admission and latches/wakes registered waiters; old items may drain, but sends cannot continue. Destroy requires closed, drained and quiescent users/publishers. Periodic polling is not immediate close wakeup, and raw queues never silently pay this cost. FreeRTOS closable registries are task-only. Scheduler suspend/resume guards protect admission, waiter registration/exit and close broadcasting. Kernel give can yield; an IRQ critical token does not keep lists unchanged across that yield. maximum_waiters budgets scheduler-pause duration; item-copy/latch-give IRQ masking is measured separately. Optional [os] in platform schema-2 and independent runtime schema-1 TOML is FreeRTOS-only. tools/configure/kernel.py is the sole policy parser and emits immutable KernelProfileIR. standard retains 1 kHz/eight priorities/Idle 128 words/16-byte names; minimal shortens names/priorities and disables unused synchronization; diagnostic enables trace; lowpower explicitly enables custom tickless. Generated capabilities are authoritative; profile names do not imply chip features. Overrides: tick_source, tick_hz, max_priorities, syscall_priority, idle_stack_words, max_task_name_len, notification_slots, mutexes, counting_semaphores, task_notifications, trace, runtime_stats and tickless. Security profiles additionally use memory_protection, system_call_stack_words, security_model and secure_idle_stack_bytes. MPU defaults off and single-world. Syscall stacks default to 128 words with an even 64\u201365536-word range. Secure Idle in split mode defaults to 128 bytes, accepts 64\u201365536 with 8-byte alignment; single mode rejects explicit settings. Physical mpu_regions remains unknown unless declared; runtime policy cannot invent MPU hardware. Maintained combinations are listed below, not inferred from valid parameters. SysTick uses actual core clocks with exact divisibility and valid 24-bit reloads. External ticks require strong nx_freertos_external_tick_setup(uint32_t); runtime statistics require counter start/now hooks. Missing hooks fail linking instead of producing fake clocks or zero-only statistics. Cost reports separate Wait faces, notifications, raw/closable queues, each waiter, joinable/permanent TCBs, caller stacks, Idle stacks and global kernel objects. Preserve the four-pointer Wait ABI; linked images/instance counts decide whether shared ops tables are worthwhile. Build reports compare RAM/Flash/instructions only; CPU cycles/latency need measurements, not disassembly byte counts. Coordinator closes admission; owner stop requests cancellation of every accepted operation. Owners continue service/drain; producers/observers only acquire-observe SETTLED. Coordinator joins producers; successful joins end business borrows of requests/bytes. Coordinator stops/joins owners and verifies that owner idle includes completed publication. Stop hardware RX/IRQ publishers and detach wake ports; join any other publishers. Destroy notifications/queues/guards. Native/bare metal may then stop platforms. Running FreeRTOS retains platform clocks/ticks; coordinators publish status then suspend. Only explicit reset or a complete scheduler-stop port can close kernel infrastructure; I/O idle does not establish stopped kernels. Failures retain storage and products choose recovery. QUARANTINED retains references; it is not a releasable terminal state. BUSY from destroy is defensive, not proof that future publishers stopped. The external UARTOwner example implements monotonic shutdown milestones for one coordinator; retries resume failed stages without repeating completed joins. Arch sequence rechecks/shallow sleep save and restore incoming PRIMASK, execute DSB before WFI and reject default deep sleep. Applications own wake timers/continuous clocks. FreeRTOS tickless uses caller-owned timer ports with separate contracts for exact tick phase, pause/resume, absolute alarms, continuous clocks and abnormal overshoot. Unbound ports retain ordinary ticks without guessed low-power devices or wake pins. Callers supply diagnostic-ring capacity; full rings preserve old events and saturate lost counters. Kernel trace defaults to weak no-op without large buffers. Products override/consume sinks. Exception hooks are bounded extension ports; products own safe outputs, watchdog/reset, persistence and release policy. Explicit MPU profiles use actual restricted-task ports, dedicated linker regions and narrow SVC gateways. Callers put TCB/context/syscall stacks in privileged RAM and actual task stacks in user RAM. Protected tasks require at least 64 words, with separate runtime peak budgets. Only RO/RW XN data grants in ordinary RAM are allowed; reject MMIO, privileged RAM, bounds violations, overlaps and illegal contexts. Ordinary code/data/providers remain privileged; only explicit user text/data enters user regions. Public user services are pointer-free delay/tick-read through actual MPU veneers/SVC. No generic user pools, I/O handles, arbitrary-pointer calls or implicit elevation. Maintained software combinations include M3, M4 integer/FP, M23, M33 integer/FP, M55 integer-MVE, M85 float-MVE and explicit 16-region facts. Native, M0/M0+, M7 kernel MPU and MPU+split fail closed. Basic Arch/o", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "OS execution and lifecycle contract", "type": "explanation"}, "design/provider-assemblies.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The maintained gate links explicitly authored software fixtures through their existing CMake projects. TOML and the sole configuration resolver select the exact part, Board package, backend, routes, modes and budgets. The gate does not rewrite a backend, create a provider registry, select source targets or infer physical Board wiring. Repeat --fixture for a reviewed list of authored TOMLs. Each file must be in tests/contracts, have its maintained sibling CMakeLists.txt, and resolve to an MCU configuration. The optional family assertion compares the actual resolver result. Different backends require distinct maintained authored TOMLs; there is no independent OS override in this gate. CMake builds each fixture\u2019s default target graph using NEXUS_SOURCE_ROOT and NEXUS_ASSEMBLY_FILE. The selected source root must be the checkout that owns the executed gate and its imported tools; tools from a different source checkout are refused. The output directory must be absent. Existing evidence, symlink destinations and source directories are refused. Every actual command records its argv, working directory, timestamps, return code and original stdout/stderr bytes. Missing tools, nonzero exits, timeouts and background artifact writers fail the matrix and preserve its failed report. An empty or duplicate matrix cannot count as successful work. After each real build, the gate requires exactly one firmware ELF and one completed generated bundle. It compares the built canonical configuration digest against the original resolved authored input, rechecks every declared input, and recomputes memory allocation using the existing ELF resource tool. The original post-link report must match the recomputation. Flash LOAD bytes, Flash footprint, each physical RAM domain, MSP and heap retain their existing budget semantics. For selected I/O, the existing retention tool independently follows actual face pointers, concrete operations tables and every non-NULL method in ELF LOAD bytes. Its post-link report must match the recomputation. A deliberately empty fixture instead proves zero authored controllers/devices, zero generated face declarations and absence of concrete I/O provider tables and faces from the actual ELF. This is an executed empty-selection GC check, not a skipped retention check or a claim that all unrelated runtime code is absent. The actual compiler database and Ninja dependency log bind compiled sources, generated headers, selected vendor headers and compiler-provided headers to file identities. Maintained graph/configuration sources and selected locked SDK input bytes are snapshotted before execution and verified afterward. The matrix rejects source, Git revision, tool or artifact changes. A dirty checkout may provide explicitly identified development evidence; it cannot qualify a clean release candidate through this gate alone. report.json records selected_provider_assembly_matrix, actual fixture and command counts, source identities, tool identities, per-fixture configuration digests, ELF/BIN/map/bundle/report identities, RAM domains and retained methods. All successful fixtures must pass; there is no skipped-pass result. CI owns its explicit fixture list and uploads original commands and artifacts alongside the report. This gate establishes software graph, compile/link, budget and selected provider retention evidence. It does not execute MCU startup, interrupts, DMA, USB enumeration, Ethernet traffic, timer waveforms or electrical controls. Physical status remains not_executed; cycles and latency remain not_measured. Model tests and eventual HIL qualification are separate scopes. Tool regression tests run with:", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Selected provider assembly gate", "type": "explanation"}, "design/soc-execution-budgets.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The authored assembly and immutable resolved IR select exact controller modes, capacities and clock profiles. This document explains how to review their execution costs; it is not another capability registry or configuration input. The derived capability matrix owns the maintained selection projection. Product scheduling and overload policy stay external. A finite work count is not a measured time bound. Bootstrap loops use explicit poll limits because the monotonic clock may not yet exist. Runtime absolute deadlines belong to the current boot clock domain. IRQ delay, DMA arbitration, bus stalls, notification latency and service intervals require measurements for the exact firmware and hardware under load. Each selected mode must document its maximum IRQ work, task service work, critical-section scope, caller storage and terminal predicate. A drain deadline limits the period before quarantine; it never authorizes releasing borrowed memory. The executor must keep calling service/recovery until hardware, IRQ, queues and observers actually withdraw their references. The exact implementations and configured limits remain authoritative: UART: soc/*/drivers/uart*.c; G474 and U575 use IRQ bytes. H743 RX also uses IRQ bytes; its explicitly selected finite TX DMA has a separate memory/Cache and drain contract. The F407, G474, H743 and U575 wire-drain allowance is 20000000 / baud + 100 microseconds. GD IRQ uses its reviewed reset-abort protocol; it is not assigned the ST wire-drain formula. SPI: soc/*/drivers/spi*.c; the finite DMA drain allowance is 200 microseconds. I2C: soc/common/i2c_v1_async.c and the SoC wrappers; V1 async drain allowance is 1,000 microseconds. Polling STOP has separate finite work. ADC/EXTI/timer: selected SoC driver and private state; scan, queue and channel limits come from resolved inputs or explicit caller storage. CAN/USB/Ethernet: io/src/can_controller.c, usb_device_controller.c and eth_mac.c; poll/drain limits and selected capacities are validated at cold admission. IRQ notification remains a hint, never a replacement for predicates. Keep the exact resolved configuration, compiler identity, flags, linked ELF/map, resource budget and retention outputs together. Compare equivalent CPU/ABI, backend, clock, memory layout and capacities. A source instruction count, object-file size or different-capacity image cannot establish an equivalent whole-image saving. Report Flash, static RAM, reservations and caller storage separately. Disassembly is real tool output, not a cycle measurement. tools/measurement/soc_costs.py observes actual linked functions and ELF resource costs with complete raw command receipts and input/tool hashes. Its comparison rejects changed routes, bindings, claims, backend or capacity profiles rather than silently attributing their cost to an implementation improvement. The empty versus selected and baremetal versus FreeRTOS dimensions remain useful selection-cost reports, but are not same-profile regression comparisons. The SoC execution record identifies the source scope of completed observations and pending final baselines. For changed interrupt/concurrency behavior, GoogleTest/GoogleMock calls the real production source with external register/CPU ports modeled. Record actual RED, then GREEN; retain admission, loss ordering, cancel, quarantine, wake and instance isolation regressions. ARM budget/retention verifies actual linking. Software checks do not certify physical IRQ latency or DMA throughput. No numerical maximum service interval is asserted without the selected producer rate, storage, hardware and interference budget. External products must qualify that interval and the resulting overload policy. A missed service deadline cannot revoke an accepted request or block loan. Current physical measurements remain not executed until an actual station records them.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "SoC execution budgets", "type": "explanation"}, "design/soc-refinement.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Nexus maintains exact silicon mechanisms. SoC facts and routes, private provider implementations, generated static instances, Board connections and observed physical behavior have separate responsibilities. The existing typed factory and shared readonly operations tables remain the runtime interface; no new registry, worker, allocator, lock or product configuration authority is introduced. The 55-item execution ledger separates existing correction and optimization (SOC-01\u201327), conditional target extensions (SOC-28\u201336), and physical acceptance (SOC-37\u201355). Codex owns all maintenance. Implementation status, executed software evidence and physical status are recorded independently. An authored plan, a synthetic parser test or an ARM link cannot complete a physical exit condition. STM32F407VET6/ZGT6 and GD32F470ZGT6 maintain reviewed fixed clock, startup, monotonic timer and selected controller modes. The derived capability matrix enumerates exact facts, routes, selected production sources and software fixtures. STM32G474RET6 maintains GPIO/LPUART plus selected EXTI edge events. The non-M4 references are STM32H743ZIT6 / NUCLEO-H743ZI2 and STM32U575ZIT6Q / NUCLEO-U575ZI-Q. H743 adds fixed clock/cache profiles and finite UART DMA transmission. U575 keeps its single-state platform and an independent synchronous dual-world software reference separate. CPU ABI/runtime support for other Cortex-M profiles does not imply a full SoC, Board or peripheral implementation. These are source-reviewed Board references. Their physical PCB revisions, silicon revisions, solder bridges, electrical connections and option bytes have not been observed. Reference-only HIL admission fails closed; selecting a matching chip name cannot promote a reference into a qualified physical Board. The extension execution record distinguishes normal commit gates, development ARM observations and final qualification. The earlier baseline retains its original source identity and evidence. Reference software status in the ledger records bounded host/ARM evidence; it does not establish a clean candidate, physical acceptance or every original exit condition. The G474 contract describes independent EXTI0/PA0 and EXTI1/PA1 rings, pending-bit acknowledgement, mux/gate ownership and source withdrawal. Its test Board is an explicit software fixture, with no inferred connector, button or electrical bias. The H743 contract defines all four HSI 32/64 MHz and Cache off/on combinations. Fixed cold profile selection does not implement a running frequency transition. H743 USART3 DMA TX owns DMA1 Stream0, DMAMUX1 Channel0 request 46 and its separate vector; RX remains IRQ bytes. Cache-on buffers occupy exclusively owned complete 32-byte lines in selected AXI SRAM, with no outward rounding or hidden bounce buffer. Cache cleaning follows borrow admission, runs outside the long interrupt mask, and cannot settle storage while preparation is active. DMA completion, wire TC and source withdrawal remain distinct. Failed withdrawal quarantines the loan; the provider must drain before cache and timer shutdown. The U575 dual-world contract is an independent no-OS integration fixture. Its cold owner observes provisioned option bytes, checks actual physical permissions, clears future shared RAM, then configures and seals attribution. A real SG gateway authenticates the NS world rather than an untrusted task identity, checks CMSE permissions and copies a bounded request through the existing GPIO broker. The layout and GPIO grant belong to the reference fixture, not to reusable platform policy. No option-byte programming, RTOS isolation, general factory integration or asynchronous transport is claimed. Clock bootstrap uses finite poll work bounds before the final clock exists; polls are not measured elapsed milliseconds. Cleanup must distinguish remaining recovery obligations from conservative settings allowed to remain. The monotonic timer belongs to one successfully started boot domain, with an overflow-service limit of 2^32 microseconds. Old deadlines cannot be carried across stop/restart. NMI/HardFault, paused timers, dynamic PLL changes and deep STOP require their own exact contract rather than assumptions from ordinary task execution. Cold admission rejects observable foreign selected owners before global clock mutation. Generated pin admission observes all required GPIO gates and validates all selected pin modes and locks before capturing or changing any pin. This includes controller routes and hidden endpoint CS pins; an unrelated shared-bank pin does not become owned. An initialization failure includes its partially initialized object in reverse cleanup. Failed readback preserves exactly the remaining cleanup responsibility instead of returning a successful release. The timer start transaction clears stale UIF, observes a fresh UG update, clears that flag and verifies CNT before publishing the epoch. Runtime overflow advances the epoch only after acknowledged UIF. A refused ACK invalidates public time, records the private timer fault and attempts finite source/clocked-counter/NVIC withdrawal. This loss of the deadline domain is always terminal, including successful source isolation: the CPU preserves a terminal interrupt mask, invokes the existing bounded fault observer and parks if that observer returns. No invalid zero timestamp resumes a deadline consumer, and no request or low-power loan is released. Products may provide separately reviewed non-returning recovery. Ordinary stop/restart and cleanup retry belong to normal lifecycle control; they do not resume this terminal path. A snapshot rechecks epoch validity under the same saved interrupt mask used to read both counter words. .noinit means startup neither copies nor clears those bytes. It does not prove retention through a particular reset, power event or sleep mode. CPU reachability, DMA reachability, linker placement and physical retention are separate facts. Factory access does not initialize or reacquire any of these resources. IRQ and DMA routines have finite work contracts. The selected execution owner must continue required service/cancel/drain progress. DMA completion, wire completion and withdrawal of every request reference precede SETTLED. Failed withdrawal retains QUARANTINED storage; cancellation is not permission to reclaim it. Continuous block providers stop on exhausted replacement storage and never write consumer-owned blocks or invent exact missed-event counts. Public typed I/O contracts belong under io/include; cross-chip controller algorithms have private headers and implementation targets. SoC wrappers own silicon layout/bit assertions and physical IRQ/DMA/clock bindings. Shared code placement follows those dependencies. CMake owns source selection, and resolver facts own configuration identity; moving a file does not create a second graph. SOC-01\u201327 retain their original exit conditions. The following groups identify the implementation, without extending a software result to physical behavior. Per-item status and evidence entry points remain in the CSV. F407 CCM is CPU-only and rejected as a DMA buffer. H743 memory facts describe DMA1/DMA2, BDMA and MDMA separately. The implemented USART3 DMA1 TX lane admits selected AXI SRAM only; MDMA reachability to TCM does not authorize a driver, cache protocol or linker placement. U575 GPDMA/LPDMA facts do not implement linked-list engines or security-crossing DMA. Its synchronous CPU gateway does not authorize DMA buffers or asynchronous storage loans. Legacy dma facts and explicit dma_engines are alternative authored forms; contradictory inputs are rejected rather than repaired. SDK identity includes reviewed original CMSIS Device files, their licenses, upstream revisions and the selected family payload. Verification checks bytes, file sets and observed filesystem identity across its read window. A successful check is not an atomic filesystem snapshot; actual consumers must use the same immutable SDK. Independent installed consumers must exercise the public platform interface without inheriting private vendor include paths. Hot-path observations retain actual ELF bytes, resolved selection, compile flags, tool identities, resources and complete command receipts. A changed Board route, CPU/ABI, backend, clock, memory domain or capacity makes a regression comparison incomparable. Empty-to-selected image growth is reported as a selection cost, not as an equivalent-profile performance improvement. Physical cycles, maximum interrupt blackout and worst-case service intervals remain unmeasured. tools/hil/soc_acceptance.py plan prepares all nineteen physical obligations without touching equipment. Each case contains required hardware, stimulation, raw observations, assertions, safety actions, metric units, unreviewed null limits and an exit condition. Selection gaps and unsupported OS cases remain explicit. The result is always prepared_unbound / not_executed. A real station supplies observed PCB revision, part/UID, probe, supply, wiring, exact ELF, resolved configuration, recomputed resources and reviewed workload. Existing admit.py establishes that admission. A station-authored SoC protocol binds one case, selected controller IDs, required instruments/calibration bytes, stimulation and numeric limits. Invariant failure counts must remain exactly zero; actual work counts/durations require a positive reviewed lower bound. Nothing in the repository supplies product acceptance numbers or fabricates pin wiring. capture delegates to the maintained acquisition runner. The existing six operations retain exact tool/input identities, exclusive station ownership, whole-argument argv without a shell, timeout/output bounds, cleanup and quarantine. The serial/instrument adapter must receive and content-pin the SoC protocol and all instrument records. This runner does not implement automatic waveform, CAN/USB/Ethernet peers or power interruption; station-owned adapters provide those operations. A successful capture is only executed_unqualified. validate rebuilds unchanged admission and recipe, verifies actual operation records, protocol, instrument bytes, session, firmware/configuration, acquisition context, raw observations and sample files. It derives every result from finite, contiguous nonempty samples against the reviewed limits. Rehashed opaque protocols, null limits, missing/failed/skipped operations, stale records, changed bytes and wrong scopes fail closed. A result qualifies only the named case, selected modes and recorded conditions; it does not establish WCET, unobserved loads, certification or product release. Flash power-loss cases additionally require a content-pinned external product layout. The test region must use the exact part\u2019s erase boundaries, remain within physical Flash, avoid every protected region and avoid all actual firmware LOAD bytes. The external layout must protect every admitted firmware LOAD. Products own the region, protected data, power-cut safety and recovery policy. The platform reserves no default test partition and performs no destructive action during preparation, validation or commit checks. SOC-28\u201336 keep their original exit conditions. Choosing H743 and U575 resolves the first non-M4 target decision; it does not complete every conditional feature. Implementing rejection of an unsupported configuration is a valid defensive contract; it is not implementation of the rejected feature. Additional features need exact controller/mode ownership and acceptance evidence before these ledger entries can close. Current first-release devices retain their declared fixed profiles. The remaining software work for SOC-33\u201335 requires coordinated ownership rather than another profile label. Running-state handoff needs an exact outgoing and incoming image/resource contract, validated stack/vector/FP/IRQ/cache/RAM state, and a reviewed terminal transfer. External products retain loader authentication, partition a", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "SoC refinement and physical acceptance", "type": "explanation"}, "design/source-compatibility.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Nexus delivers a source SDK. Consumers rebuild Core, providers, generated bindings and callers under the same configuration, compiler and ABI choices; no stable binary ABI is currently delivered. Precompiled libraries, different enum options, FP ABIs or generated-bundle objects cannot be mixed merely because versions match. dependencies/platform-compatibility.json is the sole source-interface-generation policy data. tools/configure/metadata.py is the strict pure-stdlib loader shared by production assembly and maintenance tools. It rejects duplicate keys, unknown fields, missing policy, boolean pseudo-integers, noncanonical versions and symlink inputs. It neither migrates TOML, selects hardware, runs builds nor replaces unknown values. These numbers are not interchangeable. Documentation edits or optional mechanisms usually do not change configuration schemas. Schema changes mean production validators/emitters explicitly maintain a new format, with updated policy assertions and negative tests. Policy cannot grant support to an unimplemented schema. Adding a file covered by the SDK manifest file-map does not change the manifest format. Factory compact IDs mean something only within their generated bundle. The factory returns existing static read-only faces without allocation or hardware initialization. Reordering instances or changing configuration may change IDs; persisted IDs cannot be used with another bundle. factory_abi_epoch retains ABI in its name but currently marks only generated-source interface generations: consumers, factory headers, public faces and implementations require recompilation. Current epoch is 1; zero, future values, nonintegers or missing policy must reject rather than become compatibility fallbacks. Generated configuration includes original policy bytes in the existing inputs/snapshots/hash closure and derives NEXUS_PLATFORM_VERSION and NEXUS_FACTORY_ABI_EPOCH through the sole loader. These compile-time constants add no runtime registry, instance count or RAM. After production-emitter and SDK-packager registration, generated-header checks and real SDK consumption establish complete acceptance; maintenance tools alone cannot replace them. Historical SDKs missing this policy are not automatically recognized by new tools as maintained generations. --generated takes the actual generated-header path, which may differ between build roots. The tool reads schemas from real Python validator/emitter ASTs, reads actual fact files, and executes existing NexusConfigVersion.cmake.in equal-version, different-version and range-rejection cases. It does not copy resolver selection rules; authority-expression changes fail explicitly and require observer maintenance. All participating bytes have SHA/size and are rechecked before return; concurrent changes cannot report consistency. --sdk reuses existing package_source_sdk.verify to check the entire source file map, dependencies, vendor imports and package exports, then policy/schema/version consistency. consistent means this metadata agrees, not that fresh full tests, software candidates, signatures, physical HIL or product releases pass. Version changes first add rejection/compatibility contract tests, then modify the single policy and actual authorities. Source-breaking changes require explicit major/epoch changes and external migration; optional source capabilities use minor and public-contract-preserving fixes use patch. Without real migration, accepting old formats then silently selecting new behavior is prohibited. tools/maintenance/dependency_proposals.py defaults to observing actual Gitlinks, locks, archives and license bytes with zero network reads. It covers exact FreeRTOS, CMSIS, STM32 CMSIS, GD32 import, GoogleTest, ARM-toolchain, development/Python-tool and GitHub Actions pins. Releases needing license review explicitly record required rather than inferring licenses from version names. Explicit upstream mode queries only heads/tags of public GitHub origins associated with locks. It runs shell-free git ls-remote with credential helpers, interaction and global configuration disabled: at most 10 seconds per request, 1 MiB total stdout/stderr and 8192 refs. Existing bounded subprocess capture/kill/reap is reused without HIL admission or device actions. Output directories must be new and symlink-free; only build directories are allowed within source. Original streams, argv, actual exits, timestamps and tool/response SHAs remain. Network/time/output/format failures are not_observed; GD32/toolchain/environment entries without Git publication need separate release-provenance review and cannot invent successful version observations. Observing refs neither selects a latest reliable version nor approves upgrades. Weekly workflow execution requires separate GitHub evidence. Candidate plans use strict JSON: schema_version=1, observed dependency, explicit proposed_revision, public HTTPS source_url without credentials/query, actual nonempty archive identity, declared license expression and notices identities, and optional evidence mapping. Each identity includes absolute path, full sha256 and actual size. Git provenance needs a full 40-character commit, never main/latest. License content needs independent review; declarations are not approval. Independent provenance/license review also checks whether archives match commits and completely contain notices and changes; tools infer none of these facts from arbitrary user-provided notices. The tool produces proposals only. Missing evidence is not_executed; real file identities become only referenced_unreviewed. Even referenced JSON claiming passed cannot promote qualification, run its commands, modify Gitlinks/locks or create automatic upgrade commits. Existing formal validators independently execute exact upgraded clean sources, full scopes and the same artifacts. Normal staged review, hooks and CI then atomically update source Gitlinks/vendor imports, locks, licensing and external SDK pins. Without boards, affected electrical behavior, IRQ/DMA timing, low power and long-running load remain physically pending. An upstream request, one model pass or a previous version\u2019s software candidate cannot replace acceptance for this upgrade.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Source versions, factory generations and dependency maintenance", "type": "explanation"}, "design/stack-observation-endurance.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Nexus supplies bounded, read-only paint observations. It does not paint a live stack, create a sampling worker, suspend a task or claim that paint detects every reserved stack byte. Product stack sizing and physical station sampling remain external responsibilities. nx_stack_observe accepts the original positive byte capacity of a caller-provenance painted region. It reads at most that capacity, returns the low-address untouched prefix and the remaining touched bytes, and retains no pointer. Invalid, wrapped or output-overlapping spans preserve the output. The original request, stream, owner and task structures gain no fields. The region must have been painted while it had no live frames. Sampling needs a quiescent region and excludes the current SP, all live frames and exception reservation. An MSP observation therefore requires boot paint provenance and a reviewed quiescent sampling point. This API does not access the MSP register or repair missing provenance. An all-painted region terminates at its supplied capacity rather than reading beyond the region. A touched-pattern observation is not a peak measurement: an unwritten frame reservation or a write equal to the paint can remain invisible. It is not stack canary validation or overflow detection. Physical task/MSP sizing still requires workload, nested exception and station evidence. nx_freertos_stack_observe is compiled only when [os].trace = true, including the maintained diagnostic profile. The standard/minimal/lowpower selections do not compile its translation unit. The caller supplies the original static task handle, TCB, stack and word capacity. xTaskGetStaticBuffers confirms the exact live TCB/stack identity; it cannot confirm capacity, so capacity remains the original-creation contract. Calls require privileged, unmasked running Thread mode. The caller excludes join, deletion, prestart discard, rebinding, reinitialization and writes to the sampled stack through the scan. A returned-and-suspended joinable task can be sampled before its external join; the binding must stop being used before join reclaims the task. No stale handle is queried after deletion. The locked FreeRTOS tasks.c fills new static task stacks with 0xA5 under the maintained paint-enabling configuration. The adapter uses that exact paint and performs a capacity-bounded Core scan. It deliberately does not call the kernel\u2019s unbounded free-space helper. No extra task/TCB state, masks, queues or runtime registry are introduced. The POSIX FreeRTOS port stores startup bookkeeping in the supplied static array and creates a separate pthread execution stack. Its successful static-buffer query proves API identity and bounded inspection, not an MCU or Linux execution stack peak. Development evidence uses the existing production Runtime CMake graph with Cortex-M4F hard-float, GNU Arm 14.3, -Os, -fshort-enums and section GC. The standard graph compiles no stack snapshot TU; the diagnostic graph compiles one. A probe compiled with that exact adapter command\u2019s ABI measured a 16-byte caller binding and a 12-byte output. Each is caller-owned and optional. Owned object sections measured 242 bytes of adapter .text and 64 bytes of Core scan .text, with zero .data/.bss in both objects. These are object-code figures before final link alignment and any newly retained kernel identity query; they are not a whole-image Flash delta. Unreferenced sections can be discarded. The snapshot costs at most words * sizeof(StackType_t) byte comparisons and copies a fixed-size output. No cycles or interrupt latency are inferred. Two unfiltered cases run 32 and 256 rounds through the actual pinned FreeRTOS POSIX kernel, two real producer tasks, one real owner task, the UART owner and the Native UART provider. Rounds rotate normal completion, cancellation and a failed-drain quarantine followed by explicit successful drain. Each round verifies that quarantined requests cannot be prepared/reclaimed, producer tasks and owner task are joined, the executor/provider have no retained request/data pointer, the IRQ hint is detached before its sink is destroyed, provider stop completes, notifications are destroyed and the kernel task count returns to the pre-round baseline. Fatal cleanup failure aborts the fixture instead of releasing storage while tasks still borrow it. The large hosted fixture is explicitly test-owned outside the coordinator\u2019s pthread stack. A bounded scheduler-suspend guard around Native hardware model steps prevents the signal-driven POSIX scheduler from parking a pthread mutex holder and running another model user. It is fixture composition and does not change a production provider or MCU scheduling rule. The resulting 288 rounds establish this software lifecycle on Linux. Native IRQ hints and modeled TC/drain are not physical interrupts or wire evidence. Physical soak, real UART/DMA wake latency and board current measurements remain not executed until the three-board station is connected.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Stack observations and owner lifecycle endurance", "type": "explanation"}, "design/stm32g474.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This is a source-reviewed STM32G474RET6 baseline for NUCLEO-G474RE. It implements CPU startup, a fixed clock transaction, TIM2 time, GPIOA, LPUART1 IRQ bytes and independent PA0/PA1 EXTI edge events. It does not establish Flash programming, DMA, ADC, SPI, I2C, FDCAN, USB, Ethernet, low-power continuity or other G4 peripheral support. Unsupported modes must fail the configuration resolver. Actual PCB inspection and all HIL remain not executed. Source hashes and original SDK licensing belong in the dependency lock and review evidence. Vendor notices and LICENSE.md remain unchanged. The public Nexus interface stays independent of register definitions. The initial target is STM32G474RET6, Cortex-M4F, ARMv7E-M, FPv4-SP-D16 hard ABI, DSP and DWT CYCCNT. External IRQ IDs cover 0..101, including TIM2=28, LPUART1=91 and FMAC=101. Four priority bits are implemented. Flash is 512 KiB at 0x08000000. Startup checks observed DEV_ID 0x469, Flash density 512 KiB and actual DBANK=1. DEV_ID identifies a silicon category, not the package or order code. DBANK=1 yields 2 KiB pages across two banks; DBANK=0 is rejected by this first geometry profile. Startup never writes option bytes or performs erase or programming. A product must verify the exact order code and option state. RAM consists of 80 KiB SRAM1 at 0x20000000, 16 KiB SRAM2 at 0x20014000, and 32 KiB CCM with a system-bus alias at 0x20018000. The CCM CPU address 0x10000000 aliases the same physical storage and is not counted again. The first linker layout uses one contiguous 128 KiB region at 0x20000000 and reserves 2048 bytes for MSP. This CPU-only layout makes no implemented DMA-provider claim. No caller buffer is silently relocated into another memory region. The alternate ST-LINK MCO 8 MHz clock wiring needs a different reviewed profile and is not accepted here. The VCP route is LPUART1, not USART2. Source review does not prove physical electrical states, UART reception, board power or oscillator frequency. Private PCB data and bridge policy stay outside the platform. Admission requires privileged task context, incoming masks clear, positive phase poll budget, reset-like HSI16 selected, PLL/HSE idle and reserved TIM2/NVIC unused. Unknown active resources are rejected before effects. A poll count bounds work; it is not an elapsed-time deadline while the timebase is unavailable. The reviewed profile is HSE 24 / PLLM 6 / PLLN 85 / PLLR 2 = 170 MHz. PLL input is 4 MHz and VCO 340 MHz, inside the datasheet limits. HCLK/APB1/APB2 and the TIM2 kernel clock are 170 MHz. G4 uses SW_HSI=1 and SW_PLL=3; F4 clock encodings are not reused. Startup acquires PWREN with readback and a barrier before reading PWR state. It selects voltage range 1, waits for VOSF clear, uses boost mode and four Flash wait states, starts and observes HSE/PLL, switches through AHB/2 (85 MHz), waits at least the required transition interval, then selects AHB/1. The actual M4 object contains 85 dependent SUBS/BNE iterations at verified 85 MHz. At least 170 instructions/core cycles gives a conservative 2 microsecond lower bound; IRQs can extend it. Physical timing remains unmeasured. Volatile accesses, readback and ordering barriers cannot be optimized away. Failure records the primary result, cleanup result and still-owned effects. Rollback restores observed HSI before disabling PLL/HSE or reducing Flash/voltage settings. TIM2 source withdrawal and NVIC proof precede removing its ownership. Failed rollback retains the resource bits for explicit retry; it never reports successful cleanup or publishes usable I/O. Stop requires every external peripheral/task consumer already joined; no hidden coordinator guesses this. TIM2 is reserved: 32-bit count, prescaler 169, ARR 0xffffffff, 1 MHz, overflow IRQ priority0 with no OS API. Snapshot saves/restores interrupt state and corrects one pending overflow; IRQ must run at least once per 2^32 microseconds. NMI, HardFault, dynamic reclocking and deep sleep are outside this time contract. GPIO initialization preloads BSRR and observes ODR before exposing a mode. It observes push/pull and pull configuration, then the selected MODER bits. Partial failure retains the instance for an explicit inactive-level rollback. Hot writes use BSRR directly. Toggle requires the single-owner serialization contract. LPUART1 supports caller-owned byte rings and one borrowed TX request. Its actual 20-bit BRR is round(256PCLK/(16baud)), range0x300..0xfffff; 115200 at 170 MHz uses PRESC=16 (register7), BRR 23611. Initialization observes TEACK/REACK before publishing the face. IRQ is bounded to one TX byte and one RX byte, reports aggregate corruption/overflow loss and preserves older ring contents. It does not reuse F4\u2019s USART divisor, register offsets or error-clear sequences. TXE proves a register can accept a byte; TC proves the final wire byte completed. Request metadata/payload/ring/private registers/wake target cannot overlap. Cancel and timeout disable new sends, then wait for actual wire completion. Failed drain retains QUARANTINED storage; later hardware proof can settle it. The provider detaches its active pointer and interrupt sources before SETTLED, which is its final request access. Cold stop closes admission, joins captured callbacks, observes UE and TEACK/REACK off, disables/clears/readbacks NVIC, then withdraws ring/wake ownership. FreeRTOS-capable sinks require actual priority and priority-group validation; waking does not start a scheduler. EXTI0/PA0 and EXTI1/PA1 use independent IRQ6/7 with explicit priority and caller-selected 1..4096 event slots. RM0440 Rev9 section10.2.3 pp367\u2013368 gives the GPIOA zero encoding of each SYSCFG_EXTICR1 field; sections15.3\u201315.5 pp441\u2013453 describe interrupt selection and W1C EXTI_PR1. The official CMSIS layout and exact-part package inventory supply the register and pin facts. tests/contracts/g474_assembly/exti_board is only a software fixture; it does not assert an actual NUCLEO button, connector, bias resistor or electrical route. The common edge-event face has a single task reader and one IRQ producer per line. Each invocation acknowledges one selected pending bit, samples at most one level and records one timebase timestamp. Hardware edges may coalesce; both-edge direction reflects the sampled level. No polling loop reconstructs lost edges. A full ring preserves older events, exposes a separate loss boundary and only then admits later events. CPU exclusion per copied event is bounded; read work scales with the explicit caller capacity. Platform admission checks line masks, triggers, pending and NVIC before changing global clocks or GPIO. A shared SYSCFG gate has one generated owner and retains a preexisting enable. Each line saves its mux nibble and restores only that field after its own sources and independent vector are observed withdrawn. GPIO cold leases restore the original input/analog state at complete Platform stop. A failed cleanup keeps storage and recovery responsibility. Wake binding checks actual IRQ policy and joins captured callbacks before releasing storage. No public API, dynamic allocation, worker, runtime registry or extra OS layer is introduced. The two authored EXTI fixtures select baremetal/FreeRTOS and exact three/two event capacities. Native GoogleTest/GoogleMock executes the real provider and generated two-line factory; ARM fixtures retain the selected ops/handlers and actual FreeRTOS graph. Empty and existing GPIO/LPUART fixtures remain separate regressions. Physical HIL remains not_executed: a future admitted station must confirm PA0/PA1 accessibility and bias, inject observed edges, compare instrument counts/waveforms with coalesced/loss semantics, measure IRQ latency and prove source withdrawal before storage reuse. Software success is not that evidence. Actual host models execute the production register algorithm with official SDK layout and masks. They verify bounded timeout, failed rollback, initial-latch failure, source withdrawal, request alias rejection, wire drain, overflow, callback borrow and independent state. They do not establish oscillator, voltage, timing, peripheral errata or physical IRQ behavior. An actual ARM C11 build with GCC 14.3 and the pinned official GCC startup links empty, LED, UART and mixed bare-metal ELFs. The selected types occupy System 56, GPIO 12, UART 72 and TX request 32 bytes. The small profile costs are measured from those ELF files and include the 2048-byte MSP reservation; they are not inferred from host sizeof. Unselected GPIO/UART symbols disappear through section GC. Generated typed factories, both OS backend compositions, clean reproducible builds, exact SDK lock/bundle identities and any later Board HIL have separate qualification exits. Software-only delivery must keep physical status unknown.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "STM32G474RET6 / MB1367 initial vertical slice", "type": "explanation"}, "design/threadx.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "ThreadX adds a real additional kernel backend while retaining the static factory, immutable device faces and portable wait contract. It selects the official Eclipse ThreadX GNU kernel port; it does not rename FreeRTOS or substitute a host scheduler model. Backend contracts define caller storage, bootstrap proof, deadlines, close/join and unsupported mechanisms. One authored assembly selects backend = \"threadx\". CPU/IRQ facts remain SoC facts. tools/configure/threadx.py validates the kernel-specific policy into an independent immutable ThreadXKernelProfileIR. It has no invented FreeRTOS idle stack, notification-slot, MPU, TrustZone or tickless capability fields. CMake owns the source graph and exact dependency; the typed IR supplies facts and policy, not another source graph. The first platform bindings accept the actual Board identities: Directory names are not Board identities. Unknown Board/SoC pairings are rejected. CPU-only runtime assemblies may select explicit Native or Cortex-M4/M4F CPU facts; they do not establish a SoC, board route or physical qualification. Cortex-M0/M0+/M3/M7/M23/M33/M55/M85 remain rejected for this ThreadX backend; their separate Arch/FreeRTOS support is unaffected. The board locator selects the reviewed package; its actual identity is then validated. Product tasks, queue payload/depth, stack budgets and startup/stop policy stay external. ThreadX priorities are backend-specific: smaller numbers run before larger ones. Do not copy FreeRTOS\u2019s reversed ordering into a product. Null/omitted OS choices use these explicit defaults; non-object values, unknown fields, booleans in integer fields and invalid tuples fail. profile, idle_stack_words, notification_slots, tickless, memory_protection, security_model, runtime_stats and lowpower_port are rejected for ThreadX. The initial port minimum is 200 caller bytes with 8-byte alignment; it is a fixed admission fact, not an application peak-stack budget or host pthread stack budget. Timer processing in ISR is fixed; there is no implicit timer worker. A PRIMASK policy preserves physical CPU BASEPRI capability but selects PRIMASK kernel behavior and logical ceiling zero. BASEPRI C and ASM consume the same numeric encoded ceiling. Native\u2019s none policy does not authorize simulated hardware IRQ calls into ThreadX. PendSV is claimed for MCU ThreadX; SysTick is claimed only when selected. ThreadX uses no SVC claim, and an external Tick hook must bind a real source without silently retaining SysTick. Existing Core monotonic time remains the deadline domain. The build-derived Linux timer overlay is bound to one exact reviewed upstream source SHA and preserves its original MIT notice. GNU sem_clockwait must pass a real compile/link capability probe. The timer uses CLOCK_MONOTONIC for both clock observation and waits; the exact one-billion-nanosecond carry is normalized. Unknown source or output is rejected instead of falling back to realtime waits. The original vendor checkout is unchanged. Development evidence separates 25 GoogleTest cases inside the real official kernel, production mechanism tests with mocked external CPU/IRQ/libc boundaries, configuration Python tests and actual ARM object size probes. The original development record is not retained at its former repository path. The retained integration follow-up contains its own actual source snapshots and raw execution; it does not qualify final clean source, SDK consumption or HIL and does not replace missing original bytes. Follow-up PRIMASK/configuration records retain their own raw bytes and commands; original RED/GREEN records are not rewritten as the implementation evolves. The follow-up record contains 50 fresh cases: 30 real-kernel service cases, six real-kernel bootstrap cases and 14 production mechanisms with mocked external boundaries. It also records six three-Board policy links, one precise missing-Tick rejection and three CPU-only integer/softfp links. These are development results, not physical execution. FreeRTOS keeps its existing profiles. Its additive lowpower_port is custom by default, retaining the explicit product port/weak-null behavior. Selecting timebase requires tickless SysTick, privileged single-world operation and the reviewed STM32F407/GD32F470 timer implementation. It does not authorize deep sleep. CPU-only runtime/emission rejects this selection because CPU facts do not supply that SoC timer/alarm contract. This field never selects a ThreadX sleep mechanism. Actual timer implementation tests, physical wake latency/power and clock behavior remain separately qualified. python scripts/ci/os_threadx.py --compiler arm-none-eabi-gcc --output \u003cfresh-dir\u003e executes the production platform graph for all three maintained Boards with BASEPRI and PRIMASK. Each ELF retains the real task, queue, close, notification, join, timer and scheduling entry points; the two official PendSV aliases must resolve to the same strong instruction entry. Every official common/M4 source must have a real compiled object and the same numeric C/ASM policy. The gate retains the exact ELF/BIN/map, resource report, authored/resolved configuration, compiler database, command results and pre/post source identities. A seventh build must fail specifically at the ELF link for the absent strong external-Tick provider. Unrelated compiler failures, an existing ELF, weak or missing retained entries, host/dynamic symbols, stale output and source changes fail qualification. No command in this gate flashes or executes an MCU. configured_backends in the compatibility policy lists available selection names only; the actual resolver still rejects unsupported CPU/Board tuples.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "ThreadX configuration and integration", "type": "explanation"}, "design/timebase-lowpower.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "[os].lowpower_port = \"timebase\" selects a maintained private FreeRTOS binding for STM32F407 and GD32F470. The default is \"custom\": existing application-provided ports and the weak NULL fallback keep their behavior. Selection also requires tickless = true, SysTick, a single privileged security domain and no MPU profile. CPU-only Runtime configurations reject this selection because they have no reviewed SoC timer, clock or IRQ facts. This binding uses shallow Sleep. The fixed clock and 1 MHz monotonic timer keep running. It does not enter STOP, suspend clocks, select a different tick source, configure a wake pin or make board electrical claims. The timer is already exclusively reserved by the platform timebase. No output channel is connected to a pin. The getter only inspects the established profile: the actual PLL tuple, oscillator/source ready bits, mux/bus dividers, Flash latency and disabled spread spectrum; the free-running upward timer mode, prescaler, full counter range, disabled compare preload/output, sleep clock, overflow interrupt and enabled NVIC route. Missing facts return NULL; there is no repair or hidden initialization. Clock consumers must remain quiescent throughout platform shutdown. A pending pause or alarm additionally rejects clock release before any register or NVIC effect. The generic Idle adapter owns scheduler suspension and incoming PRIMASK restoration. The private timer callbacks never block or call the kernel. Alarm IRQ dispatch runs under the SoC\u2019s saved interrupt guard. SysTick calls the renamed real kernel tick handler once per real periodic interrupt. Pause rejects a pending periodic tick and a still-active first short interval. The writable snapshot must be disjoint from private retained state, immutable bindings, MMIO and opaque clock-context storage. Known spans, alignment and address wrap are checked before effects; the private caller proves the opaque clock-context extent. A successful pause retains the periodic-source loan until resume. The paused counter records whole microseconds and the remaining core-cycle fraction. Each capture and restore rejects a clock reversal or an observed sampling window larger than four continuous-clock microseconds. An alarm uses an absolute deadline in that same boot domain. Admission requires strictly future time, at most UINT32_MAX microseconds ahead. The low 32 bits match the free-running counter across one wrap. Late arm, compare readback failure or disabled-source readback failure never become successful admission. Rejected alarms remove only their own interrupt source. Timer status is W0C: ones preserve all unrelated sources, including a compare event arriving between an overflow flag read and acknowledgement. Resume programs one short SysTick interval. It proves that VAL consumed the short reload, then restores the full LOAD before the first underflow. Restoring LOAD only in the first ISR would allow a second short interval. The wrapper also restores the full LOAD before kernel dispatch. A sub-microsecond remainder is rounded up by less than one microsecond, rather than relying on an unprovable near-zero reload race. The precision contract is quantized. A 1 MHz clock cannot represent arbitrary core-cycle timestamps, and register operations have latency. The model executes 15 phase/latency combinations with a continuously advancing timer and proves those executions fall in a conservative minus-one to plus-eleven microsecond phase interval. The independent capture and restore window checks are the runtime bound; the model is not a proof of physical MCU instruction latency or all hardware executions. Repeated sleeps can accumulate bounded phase bias. Applications needing tighter synchronization must qualify a separate timer compare clock profile rather than assuming exact sub-microsecond phase. A pending tick, COUNTFLAG, late first underflow, unconsumed reload or incorrect restored control bits makes resume fail stop. Failed pause rollback also fails stop if the source cannot be restored. Failed alarm disable retains both pause and alarm ownership. Fault state never returns a borrowed source to the caller, continues the kernel with missing ticks or attempts automatic reclamation. Selection adds one static private context. For the maintained 32-bit ABI its layout is 16 bytes; the read-only register binding and port face are 52 and 24 bytes. These are structure layouts, not a linked image cost claim. The existing timebase, default request/stream state and OS objects gain no fields. Default source selection omits the common mechanism, SoC binding and selected IRQ branches. Four actual production CMake ARM graphs verify zero default new mechanism TUs/symbols, versus two selected TUs and a strong getter. In the same empty assembly retaining kernel startup, no-LTO Os/GNUArm14.3.1, selection adds 1532 bytes of Flash / 16 bytes RAM on STM32 and 1516 bytes Flash / 16 bytes RAM on GD32. These are development fixture deltas, not application or hardware costs. The GoogleTest / GoogleMock suites execute production common C, actual SDK register layouts, real SoC timebase IRQ and shutdown code. Tests cover pending arrival, reversed time, late compare, counter wrap, failed disable/restore, first-reload consumption, second-pause rejection and shared IRQ flag races. The two chip suites use external CPU, clock-bootstrap and register semantics models; they are software evidence. Physical HIL has not run. Acceptance still requires the real boards and probes: verify shallow-Sleep clock continuity, NVIC wake, actual capture/restore windows, SysTick phase and catch-up across repeated sleeps, wrap, competing interrupts and load. Neither model timing nor ARM linking establishes power savings, physical WCET, IRQ latency or board readiness.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Reviewed shallow tickless timebase", "type": "explanation"}, "design/timer-advanced.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "These modes are independent of the existing general-purpose nx_pwm_port_t. They keep the same immutable typed face and shared operations model. Selection owns static controller storage; no registry, pool, allocator, worker or OS lock is introduced. An unselected mode has no instance storage. Default-image removal is a separate whole-ELF assembly gate, not a conclusion from an object size. The reviewed STM32F407 and GD32F470 datasheets establish these alternate functions. They do not establish a connector, external load, safe electrical level or PCB revision. External Board facts must authorize the actual route. The main clock is the established fixed SoC profile; dynamic reclocking is unsupported. Constructors verify enabled clock gates, APB timer selection and actual AF fields. They do not measure crystal tolerance. Cold assembly owns the clock, pins and IRQ vector before construction and keeps them until stop and publisher quiescence. Its sole immutable configuration defines exact timer clock, integer prescaler, channel, filter or complementary output policy. exclusive is explicit authority over the whole timer, including clock-gated peer writers; a disabled register is not proof of absent ownership. The TIM1/TIMER0 break vectors are shared with TIM9/TIMER8. Assembly must reserve that shared resource or supply reviewed static dispatch; a provider never changes the NVIC vector on behalf of a peer. A cold configuration failure must be cleaned up by its acquisition owner before releasing leases or publishing a factory face. Capture uses four public controls: start, stop, read and state. Start borrows an exact caller-owned ring. An admitted IRQ reads one CCR value, which clears its input capture event flag in hardware, then observes overcapture status. Normal IRQ handling acknowledges only observed overcapture W0C facts; it preserves a new unread edge arriving after the data read. A draining IRQ may discard its observed event without publishing. It publishes at most one value. Consumer read copies one oldest event under a saved incoming interrupt mask. Queue operations use conditional cursor wrap and have no division, scanning or variable-length copy. An observable overcapture or full ring increments a saturating incident counter; old values are never overwritten. Incidents may coalesce edges, so they are not an exact count of physical loss. The raw capture value is a 16-bit modulo timer count. tick_hz describes its post-prescaler unit. observed_at comes from the common monotonic clock at IRQ publication; it is not the edge aperture or a reconstructed absolute hardware timestamp. Hardware filter codes specify the selected silicon sampling/filter policy, not a measured debounce interval. Stop first disables and reads back channel, counter and IRQ sources. Queued events require task drain; a refused detach keeps the ring borrowed and returns BUSY. Successful stop is the final ring access. Failed admission has no retained ring, while the execution owner still checks and cleans any refused hardware-control write before releasing the outer clock/pin/vector lease. Complementary PWM has five public controls: set duty, start, stop, state and clear fault. Period, clock, deadtime, polarity and idle levels are immutable cold facts. Duty stages CCR shadow against that fixed base. Zero and full-period duty remain explicit count boundaries; electrical behavior still requires the reviewed complementary output circuit and physical validation. Deadtime uses the actual timer tDTS with CKD zero, independently of PSC. Counts 0\u2013127 encode directly, 128\u2013254 by multiples of two, 256\u2013504 by multiples of eight and 512\u20131008 by multiples of sixteen. Unrepresentable counts reject; rounding would silently change a safety-relevant physical interval. Every hardware encoding is exercised by the production-register model. The repeated counter, slave clock/gate selection and alternate capture XOR input are cleared and read back during construction, rather than inherited from a prior owner. BKIN is enabled in hardware. Automatic output reenabling and irreversible LOCK are never selected. An active physical input or observed break latches software fault and disables master output. Start verifies the unchanged deadtime/clock and break configuration, rechecks pending break before enabling MOE and verifies actual MOE readback; it cannot clear a fault. These software observations do not prove behavior for a physical pulse wholly inside a read/write window. Pulse width, hardware reaction and any required external fault latch are Board/system qualification facts. Stop requires actual MOE, both channel sources, counter and break interrupt withdrawal. A refused write retains responsibility. Explicit clear fault requires disabled outputs, inactive actual BKIN and a successful pending-break acknowledgment. It does not start outputs. Products own power-stage recovery policy. The private cold release controls require successful source withdrawal, task event drain and an already-quiescent generated IRQ publisher. They clear only the ended static session under a saved mask. Pins, clocks and vector leases remain with assembly until it explicitly drives and reads back the Board\u2019s declared inactive levels, then releases resources. Public stop makes a timer source-withdrawal promise; it does not certify an electrical safety state. The maintained GoogleTest/GoogleMock targets compile real production C against locked vendor register layouts. Models provide only MMIO/CPU/clock boundaries, including input capture read-clear, W0C, rejected stores and asynchronous edges and hardware break. Strict ARM compilation and type/object reports are separate evidence. Physical edge rate, ISR latency, noise/filter response, duty/deadtime tolerance, shared-vector load, break reaction time and power-stage safety remain not executed until a real station supplies measured results. Nexus makes no safety certification claim.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Optional timer capture and complementary PWM", "type": "explanation"}, "design/u575-trustzone-contract.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This is a software reference for an explicit Secure and NonSecure image pair on STM32U575 with 2 MiB Flash. It is separate from the existing TZEN0 platform profile. It does not turn that profile into a TrustZone provider, integrate a NonSecure RTOS, authenticate firmware, or establish physical isolation evidence. No board has executed this reference yet. soc/stm32u575/security.c implements the cold-owner mechanism. Its private nx_stm32u575_tz_layout_t contains caller-selected spans, not a platform-owned product partition. The owner lives in Secure SRAM1 and remains allocated until reset. The boot owner supplies mutually consistent linker scripts, vectors, Secure fault handlers, partition parameters and image-loading policy. tests/contracts/u575_trustzone is the explicit reference assembly. It owns the example partition, a single fixed NonSecure-world principal and a PC7 GPIO grant. Its two Runtime builds produce separate ELFs, a real SG veneer and Secure import library, and an actual compiler-generated NonSecure call. The reference does not use the TZEN0 SoC startup or claim generated factory integration. Cold preflight performs observations without owner updates or configuration writes. This does not promise that all hardware reads are side-effect free: reading SysTick CTRL observes and clears COUNTFLAG. The trusted cold contract starts with no running timer. Preflight rejects incompatible geometry, context, option bytes, Flash permissions, active interrupts, autonomous bus masters, caches and floating-point ownership. It requires Secure privileged Thread mode, MSP within the supplied Secure SRAM1 stack, PRIMASK set, and BASEPRI/FAULTMASK clear. The Secure vector table must already be at the Secure bank1 address. A previously running world or a bootloader-owned peripheral configuration is not adopted. After admission the owner records FAILED and effects=true before enabling the GTZC/SYSCFG clocks. Any later failure keeps that state and requires reset; there is no rollback, retry or release promise. Only complete configuration and readback publish READY. Successful entry publishes RUNNING before the actual CMSE transition. The production transition never returns; an unexpected NS return enters terminal fault handling. The mechanism accepts page-aligned NS Flash within bank2, superblock-aligned NS SRAM within SRAM3, NSC within Secure bank1 outside its first vector page, and a Secure stack within SRAM1\u2019s Secure alias. At least one SRAM3 superblock remains Secure so its clock/reset attribution remains Secure with INVSECSTATE clear. The current hardware admission is intentionally narrow: TZEN=1, SWAP_BANK=0, 2 MiB Flash, SRAM3 ECC disabled, bank1 fully Secure, Secure boot address 0x0c000000, no hide protection, no security inversion, Flash programming locked and idle, and the selected bank2 pages neither Secure nor privileged. In particular, SRAM3_ECC=1 means ECC disabled. The mechanism never writes option bytes, erases Flash, or programs images. Provisioning must establish this boot contract before execution; a late register check cannot retroactively protect instructions already executed. After verifying actual SRAM1/SRAM3 security and privilege configuration, the mechanism zeroes the future NS RAM through its still-Secure alias and reads it back before declassification. It then makes only the selected SRAM3 superblocks NonSecure and unprivileged. SRAM1 remains Secure and privileged. It locks the implemented SRAM1 and SRAM3 superblocks and the corresponding global MPCBB configuration until reset. CMSE checks alone do not establish these bus-firewall permissions: GTZC unprivileged denials may read as zero or ignore writes. The SAU enables exactly three spans: NS Flash, NS RAM and NSC at its Secure Flash alias. All remaining addresses retain Secure attribution. The NS MPU provides read-only executable Flash and read/write execute-never RAM, with no privileged default map or peripheral region. MAIR uses normal non-cacheable memory. Unused regions are disabled. Actual MPU TYPE is read: this mechanism accepts 8 or 16 regions and clears all observed regions, rejecting other counts. This is not a claim that every U575 revision implements 16 regions: RM0456 states eight, whereas DS13737 describes up to sixteen. The existing TZEN0 provider separately maintains an exact eight-region CPU tuple. Protected FreeRTOS preserves its exact comparison with hardware TYPE and rejects an actual sixteen-region configuration rather than silently interpreting the field as a lower bound. The no-OS dual-world mapper\u2019s observed region count does not change that contract. NSC instruction fetch does not require an additional NS MPU region. Arm DDI0553B.z ValidateAddress() selects the MPU for instruction fetch from the resolved security attribution: secureMpu = !sAttrib.ns. An NSC address has Secure attribution, so its SG fetch is checked using the Secure MPU and Secure privilege state. This reference requires the Secure MPU disabled and Secure Thread privileged; it does not grant NS ordinary data access to NSC. After readback, SYSCFG locks NS VTOR, the documented NS MPU controls, SAU and the Secure VTOR/AIRCR security routing. RM0456 explicitly lists MPU_CTRL_NS, MPU_RNR_NS and MPU_RBAR_NS for LOCKNSMPU; it does not explicitly list every MPU alias, RLAR or MAIR register. The contract therefore does not treat that bit as proof that all MPU words are immutable. Final admission rechecks MAIR, the two active regions through the base/alias registers, controls, locks, physical SRAM permissions, Flash permissions and quiescent interrupt/master state without rewriting a locked region selector. The remaining privilege boundary is mandatory: NS enters unprivileged Thread mode with no NS kernel or privileged exception handler. The immutable NS vector table contains only MSP and Reset; every other implemented exception vector is zero. All interrupt target bits and fault routing remain Secure. NS cannot use an SVC/IRQ handler to alter attribution. Secure NMI/fault handlers fail stop, and Secure software never changes mappings or enables DMA after the cold seal. The reference SG transport checks an actual NonSecure caller, Thread context, Secure privilege and RUNNING ownership. Identity is the fixed NS world, not a claimed task ID from untrusted input. It preserves and masks NS interrupts through the synchronous call; the Secure broker retains its own documented masking and serialization rules. The memory port first restricts a request to the explicit NS RAM span, then uses nx_arch_nonsecure_check with real CMSE NonSecure and unprivileged MPU checks. The broker copies a bounded request into Secure storage before acting, checks the fixed GPIO grant, and holds the output mapping until its final copy. The permission check itself is only an observation: it neither copies nor pins memory. The reference\u2019s synchronous pin relies on the already established static mapping, lack of NS concurrent execution/handlers, lack of autonomous masters, and exclusive Secure owner. It does not authorize asynchronous loans, DMA buffers, arbitrary MMIO, callbacks or mutable user address spaces. Retained cleanup or an impossible mapping-release failure is terminal. No security property is inferred from a host pointer model or the presence of SG/BLXNS opcodes alone. Host Google tests establish software admission and failure behavior; real ARM ELFs establish ABI/link/layout properties. Hardware must still verify attribution, fault delivery, CMSE observations, lock behavior, GPIO effect, provisioning and the exact board revision. The implementation uses locked STM32U5 CMSIS v1.4.3, commit 624374fa1e21ca195d6f2102ac0caaa50d0ea4c8, and the repository\u2019s locked Arm CMSIS. Authoritative register review used ST RM0456 revision 7: Sections 3.5.2 and 3.7.1: banked MPU and documented region count. Sections 5.4.2, 5.4.4, 5.4.6 and 5.8.1\u20135.8.5: SRAM granularity, privilege, security aliases, clock attribution, MPCBB inversion and reset-only locks. Sections 7.5 and 7.9.13: Flash attribution and SRAM3 ECC option polarity. Sections 7.9.16\u20137.9.17 and 7.9.29\u20137.9.34: Secure boot address, watermarks, Secure/privileged Flash block configuration and the exact U575 bank geometry. Sections 11.5 and 13.3.18: RCC/GPIO security ownership. Sections 15.3.4\u201315.3.5: CPU NonSecure/Secure configuration lock scope. The reviewed official PDF is https://www.st.com/resource/en/reference_manual/rm0456-stm32u5-series-armbased-32bit-mcus-stmicroelectronics.pdf, SHA-256 6edaaaae0c5d57c58ffc3a576d44e7b62e8d9cd0e495e8f61bab688226fc327c. The instruction-fetch rule was independently reviewed against Arm DDI0553B.z (2026-02-24), E2.1.419 ValidateAddress() pages 2087\u20132089 and E2.1.296 MemI[] page 2044. Official PDF: https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/699eb70d77ad5021756604d8, SHA-256 468594b22cf5a4bae1261f2cd39d5aa51040fbb566be18cc358c169918fd7a72. The actual compiler CMSE helper may contain architecture-defined FP context instructions even in this soft-float image pair. Admission rejects active FP ownership (both CONTROL.FPCA and CONTROL.SFPA), CPACR enablement and lazy preservation; this is not a claim that the ELF contains no FP-related opcodes. DDI0553B.z C2.4.368 (VLSTM, pages 1177\u20131178) specifies that SFPA=0 makes that instruction a no-op; SFPA=1 would require coprocessor access. Therefore SFPA-only state is explicitly rejected before acquiring hardware. The Google suite compiles the production mapper and substitutes only hardware MMIO, physical RAM and CPU instruction leaves. It covers rejected admission, actual permission layout, every changed configuration-write failure, zeroing failure, irreversible ownership, vector/stack boundaries, forbidden privileged handlers, metadata changes and final transition ownership. The paired ARM gate checks the actual vector words and executable addresses against this contract.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "U575 synchronous dual-world reference contract", "type": "explanation"}, "design/uart-dma-rs485.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "UART retains public nx_uart_port_t and shared read-only nx_uart_ops_t. Configuration generates instances; callers retain only interface and context. New mechanisms are selected by mode; existing IRQ, dma-tx and irq-blocks state layouts and default operations-slot counts do not grow. Production remains C11 without heap, device scanning, hidden workers or default OS locks. Initial support is STM32F407 USART1 and GD32F470 USART0 only. STM32 RX is DMA2 stream2 channel4 and TX DMA2 stream7 channel4; GD32 RX is physical DMA1 channel2 selector4 and TX physical DMA1 channel7 selector4. Controller numbers come from chip manuals, not public opaque resource-ID encodings. Configuration must exclusively own UART, DMA targets, vectors, clocks and pins; conflicts are rejected during generation. Receive-block capacity is 1\u201365535 bytes. Continuous mode needs at least three equal-capacity blocks; finite mode permits different capacities. DMA targets must lie within authorized, writable, DMA-reachable regions; CCM on both chips is not receive-DMA-reachable SRAM. Neither MCU has CPU D-cache, and providers perform no invented cache clean/invalidate. Cache-equipped MCUs need new domain/maintenance contracts rather than reuse of these cache-free assumptions. RX-only and duplex use separate read-only operations tables and direct TX calls. The TX hot path has no runtime dma_transmit branch; RX-only links retain no TX-DMA operations. Current providers share the explicitly selected receive-state layout, so RX-only cold storage still includes TX-DMA base fields; this is not a zero-field-overhead claim. Callers initialize nx_stream_t and slots, then lend them through nx_uart_port_rx_start. Devices own producer responsibilities and callers consumer responsibilities. Ordinary producer and nx_stream_post_* preposted-producer APIs must not be mixed. Stream, slots, blocks and provider state do not overlap and remain valid until successful rx_stop and external-observer drain. Active TX payloads must not overlap the entire RX block pool. Finite mode points DMA directly at caller payloads without CPU payload copies. On TC, IDLE or error, it first withdraws UART DMA requests and receive interrupts, then writes DMA disable. Actual EN, request and interrupt readbacks establish quiescence before stable remaining counts are read. Finally it rereads DMA/UART errors and performs the sole status/data clear order. Only reliable positive-length prefixes become READY; unreliable prefixes return FREE and record loss. Stop never falsely publishes an empty, unreceived block as successful data. Continuous mode starts double-buffer DMA with two FILLING loans. After TC it reserves a third FREE block before replacing the completed inactive target. Before publishing the old block, it proves replacement address, other-target address, CT, EN, absence of new TC/errors and removal of the old address from both hardware targets. Failure never hands potentially writable memory to consumers: it withdraws sources, drains and rolls back future loans in LIFO order. Successful publication follows FIFO. IRQ copies no payload and performs bounded control with at most three loans. Without a replacement FREE block, receive-write sources stop immediately and await actual drain while retaining consumer BORROWED blocks. Stable completed and partial blocks can still publish with one recorded stop gap, never a guessed missing-byte count. Failed disable retains every producer loan; later owner service retries boundedly. No block or state may be reused meanwhile. TC is a flag, not a wrap counter. Continuous qualification requires worst-case IRQ latency plus target-replacement time below one block-fill interval, measured from actual baud, block size, interrupt priority, masked windows and concurrent DMA load. Software detects observable errors, target changes and CT anomalies but cannot recover arbitrarily many coalesced wraps. Violating latency budgets prevents claims of reliable sequencing even if all targets remain FILLING and consumer memory is untouched. IDLE, finite-block restart and capacity-pressure recovery create receive gaps. Owner service periods are explicit resource budgets too. Observed UART overrun/error before restart records loss before status/data clear; software cannot infer exact omissions in gaps without error bits. Loss denotes observed boundaries, not exact wire-byte statistics. Upper protocols decide acceptable gaps and recovery. TX DMA TC proves only payload delivery to the peripheral, not the last bit leaving the pin. TX settlement still waits for actual UART TC; cancellation withdraws sources before proving DMA/peripheral drain. RX DMA and TX DMA use separate targets and vectors; their completions never substitute for each other. rx_start/rx_stop/service/stop run on the sole unmasked task executor. ISRs use only exact generated IRQ entries; protected spans save/restore incoming interrupt state. rx_stop closes stream admission and may return BUSY: published or borrowed blocks must be normally returned by consumers. Success requires withdrawn sources, quiescent engines and zero outstanding loans. Closed service never restarts RX. Complete stop first closes TX admission too, and may stop underlying clocks only after RX drain. Failure prohibits owner destruction or replacement. GD32 IRQ TX cancellation uses shared USART reset and first drains active RX. If RX EN refuses stop, TX/RX loans remain and reset is deferred. Selected hardware-flow-control bits are restored after reset. nx_*_uart_flow_configure is explicitly optional cold configuration. STM32 sets CR3 RTSE/CTSE and GD32 CTL2 RTSEN/CTSEN, rolling back failed readback. It runs after initialization but before active TX/RX and cannot modify live borrows. Chip signals are PA11 CTS, PA12 RTS, AF7; chip facts do not prove wiring on the three boards or peer handshake support. Board binding needs PCB, GPIO permission, voltage, multiplexing and peer review. Hardware RTS reflects UART data-register receive state, not remaining software-block capacity; it neither replaces protocol backpressure nor guarantees lossless operation without FREE blocks. RS485 selects nx_uart_rs485_state_t, immutable nx_uart_rs485_config_t and nx_uart_rs485_ops; default UART state gains no DE, timer or task. Callers bind real GPIO, a uniquely authorized bit, DE polarity, setup/hold time and the exact provider\u2019s pure TX validator. Validator and submit/start share rejection rules; validation writes no GPIO/MMIO, admits nothing and retains no references. Config, both faces, ops/context bindings and electrical facts remain immutable; all cold facts are separate from mutable request/payload/control storage. The same executor exclusively owns underlying TX; bypass submissions or transport changes are prohibited. Rejection precedes DE assertion. After acceptance, setup starts only after successful DE assertion; the absolute transfer deadline includes setup and wire completion. Setup timeout never starts UART. Hold starts only after actual underlying SETTLED; successful DE withdrawal then removes internal payload/active references before outer SETTLED publication. Failed drain or DE withdrawal retains QUARANTINED outer loans and DE ownership for owner-service retries. Required drain/hold may extend borrows beyond transfer deadlines; expired deadlines never justify early memory or DE release. Setup/hold uses the nx_time_now_us monotonic domain; worst-case service gaps determine boundary error. Hold guarantees at least declared duration, not exact-time DE withdrawal. Exact waveforms require explicit hardware-timer/electrical budgets; this implementation invents neither transceiver facts nor timing qualification. GoogleTest/GoogleMock directly execute production providers, covering rejected bank replacement, CT flips, wrong address writes, new errors in W1C windows, refused disable, terminal DMA/UART errors, status/data-clear races, epoch exhaustion, duplex cancellation, flow configuration, RS485 setup/hold and DE failures. Native adapters additionally verify real request lifecycles. Results establish software contracts, not electrical waveforms, bus arbitration or physical latency. Actual ARM ELF comparison before/after table splitting in the same no-LTO Release RX-only development fixture reduced STM32 text by 2310 bytes, GD32 by 2262 bytes, and both bss by 8 bytes. This describes section-GC costs only for that intermediate fixture, not final sources, arbitrary applications or all modes. Final candidates must freshly bind configuration, sources, ELF/map/nm and tool identity. After terminal-error fixes, fresh production-graph ARM linking of the same fixture produced STM32 text 7866 bytes and GD32 text 9932 bytes, down 2218 and 2174 bytes from the old baseline; both bss reductions remain 8 bytes. All fourteen final mode fixtures linked; RX-only nm contains no nx_*_uart_dma_* symbols, while duplex retains real TX-DMA methods. This remains development-build software evidence; candidate sealing and board validation execute separately. No physical boards are connected, and all physical acceptance remains unexecuted. Board exit conditions include both engines\u2019 concurrent maximum baud/load, worst IRQ/service latency, target-replacement/wrap pressure, overflow/recovery after long consumer holds, IDLE/last-DMA-beat races, actual TC wire tails, RTS/CTS stall/resume, DE setup/hold polarity/edges, and drained clocks/vectors/SRAM loans after repeated stop/start. Record PCB revision, probes, UARTs, external wiring, raw logic-analyzer waveforms and the same ELF per board. Unexecuted items cannot pass.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "UART receive DMA, duplex DMA and electrical adaptation", "type": "explanation"}, "design/usb-device.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Public interface: nexus/io/usb_device.h. This slice provides internal-PHY full-speed device PIO and actual SDK bindings for Native, STM32F407 and GD32F470. Maintained scope is EP0 control, explicitly selected EP1\u2013EP3 bulk IN/OUT, SETUP/reset/suspend/resume facts, STALL, SET_ADDRESS, cancellation and exit. No Host, HS, DMA, ISO, remote wake, USB class or product stack is included. A request is one packet no larger than MPS; external applications own multipacket control/class transactions, terminating ZLP, descriptors and workers. Controller and endpoint retain separate identities; public faces contain only read-only ops and context. Assembly owns one controller, exact endpoint array, exact event ring and an explicit 48 MHz clock owner. Instances share read-only tables without global mutable registries, maximum endpoint pools, hidden workers or mandatory OS. Unselected instances create no state, and MCU hot paths use direct volatile MMIO. Model builds replace only external MMIO/NVIC leaves; MCUs carry no model-function tables or runtime-simulation branches. Both SoCs use USBFS base 0x50000000, four IN / four OUT directions, 320-word FIFO and IRQ 67. Bindings validate actual offsets, strides, capacity and W1C/read-only bits against locked SDKs. EP0 MPS is fixed at 64 bytes; bulk MPS may only be 8/16/32/64. RX FIFO requires at least 64 words and each selected IN FIFO at least 16 words. Partition validation rejects overlap or totals exceeding 320 words. GD32\u2019s original 200 MHz main-PLL Q9 output is not 48 MHz and cannot directly clock USB. Its clock owner rejects newly active USBHS/TRNG/SDIO/TLI consumers, preserves neighboring fields and never changes SYSCLK. IDLE/CLEANUP/READY and exact consumer counts track responsibility; failed rollback without readback proof retains CLEANUP. DCD validates/retains first, releases consumers after completed exit, and external owners stop clocks last. External resource owners must authorize and quiesce pins/electrical/VBUS and hidden clock-gated consumers; zero gates alone do not prove system-wide exclusivity. Actual ARM GCC 14.3 sizeof probes: controller 144 B, each endpoint 40 B, each event 40 B, request 56 B, STM/GD clock state 8/16 B. Production ARM objects have no global .data/.bss; actual assembly builds separately prove unselected GC/resource changes in complete ELFs. Prepare never accesses payloads; rejected admission retains no new references. ACCEPTED borrows requests and payloads until acquire-observed SETTLED. Requests, payloads, outputs, counts, immutable clock ops and wake targets are checked against controller/endpoint/ring/clock state and existing loans for overlap. Rejected state/read_events output aliases preserve outputs. Writing IN FIFO is not completion. SUCCESS requires XFRC, cleared EP enable, restored FIFO free capacity and successful observed-W1C-completion clear readback. OUT requires both packet data and XFRC; short packets complete normally. Oversized packets consume every FIFO word, copy at most caller capacity, then report OVERFLOW and drain, never writing outside caller buffers. IN TXFE is read-only and contributes neither W1C clearing nor zero-value completion proof. ITTXFE means a retryable host IN token arrived while FIFO was empty; OUT STUP is normal SETUP phase. Neither incorrectly terminates packets. IN control timeout and OUT FIFO overrun use real direction-specific event bits. SETUP/core owns EP0 DATA PID: bits 29:28/16 are reserved on EP0, so software uses DATA0 commands only on bulk endpoints. SETUP strictly decodes eight little-endian bytes and increments epochs monotonically. New SETUP/reset drains old control loans; changing epochs alone never revokes borrows. Reset and queued SETUP in one snapshot have no proven order, so previous queued SETUP is discarded; new admission waits for reset drain. SET_ADDRESS validates current standard SETUP fields and stages the address, writing DCFG only after corresponding status-IN actual XFRC. Failed address readback closes the controller. The event ring retains value facts without overwriting old events when full; exact software-loss markers follow existing queue contents. Latest SETUP/epoch/closing state does not depend on ring space. Timestamps are IRQ acquisition times, not USB bus sampling times; unordered old SETUP in reset batches is not counted as newly received control. Task entries require one serialized privileged executor outside Handler mode with clear incoming IRQ masks. IRQs save/restore masks, consume at most eight RX status items, 128 payload words and eight selected directions per call. Cold/hot paths are separate: cold reset/flush has finite poll budgets; IRQ flush issues one command and readback, never cold polling in IRQ. Timeout uses independent monotonic deadlines; USB suspend/unplug still needs external executor service, not SOF or hidden timers. IN drain requests SNAK, observes actual NAK, then requests disable. EN clear, EPDISD, FIFO flush and W1C clear must all hold. Nonzero OUT first confirms global OUT NAK, then disables and drains RX. Drain timeout retains QUARANTINED and never revokes buffer loans. Control OUT0 EPD is permanently zero and SETUP may bypass NAK. Cancellation cannot be completed by modeling a nonexistent disable acknowledgement. Without actual completion proof, individual OUT0 cancellation remains DRAINING then QUARANTINED; users may select full device stop. Full stop reads back soft disconnect, global OUT NAK and DMAEN=0, consumes RX FIFO, disables global/endpoint/NVIC sources and joins all notification publishers, flushes FIFO, then rechecks disconnect/NAK/FIFO/IRQ state before releasing OUT0 PIO loans and finally publishing SETTLED. OUT0 EP enable may still be one because hardware owns only internal FIFO and withdrawn PIO executors no longer own caller buffers; next cold core reset reestablishes hardware state. Missing proof at any step returns BUSY with caller storage and clock consumer retained. After wake-target capture, publisher counts still retain that borrow; stop/detach returns BUSY until notify returns. Wake must support both service/cancel task context and true IRQ context. Binding reads actual priority/grouping to validate syscall ceilings without changing global grouping. Development evidence: tools/testing/evidence/usb-device-iteration/manifest.json. Complete execution covered Native 43, STM32 7 and GD32 7 Google cases; ASan/UBSan ran the same 57 cases with zero skips. Six production ARM units using both actual SDKs passed strict C11 compilation. The clock owner\u2019s separate 27 cases remain in usb-clock-iteration and are not counted again in DCD\u2019s 57. Historical incorrect TXFE/EP0/PID model assumptions and failure logs remain retained; current-source and old-report identities are not mixed. Sources are FS register facts in locked ST CMSIS headers, GD32F4xx User Manual Rev3.3 \u00a729.3 (especially printed p986\u2013995 for EP0/W1C/TXFE), and \u00a74.3.24/26 for PLLSAI/CK48. GD manual SHA256: c8e1d20d7cde338892d08dcb233345e03f7960256a60a36c755c576d9cd65c13. Complete provenance is bound in the manifest. This is not a clean software candidate, product release or physical qualification. USB role, VBUS sensing/power/backfeed, pull-up/ESD, electrical jumpers, pin leases and shared-pin conflicts on all three PCBs need separately reviewed Board assemblies; this batch configures none of those pins. Enumeration, host retries, reset/unplug, transfer errors, PHY three-cycle flush timing and actual ISR/WCET/HIL remain unexecuted and inherit no historical example results.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "USB FS device packet ownership and integration boundaries", "type": "explanation"}, "design/user-io-broker.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The optional broker separates untrusted scalar input from trusted kernel policy and retained user-memory ownership. The implemented operation is a bounded typed GPIO read or set/reset write. There is no user-provided port, MMIO address, callback, principal, private slot pointer or asynchronous DMA operation. The 32-byte input and 8-byte output contain explicit uint32_t fields. The trusted gateway calls the public API from privileged Thread mode with incoming interrupt masks clear. A kernel identity callback authenticates the current principal; the public message cannot choose it. Construction retains immutable nonoverlapping nominal user RAM windows, exact device/mask grants, a positive loan capacity and the kernel memory port. All known broker, policy, port-table and private context spans stay outside those user windows. Admission validates full-span arithmetic, alignment, access and one-window containment before pinning. pin must establish the specified CPU access and retain address-space/process/mapping lifetime until successful unpin. A failed pin must leave no mapping references or residual effects. The whitelist alone does not establish actual mapped RAM, DMA reachability, MMIO exclusion or MPU isolation; the trusted kernel and SoC memory layout establish those facts. The input is pinned and copied once into kernel storage. Policy checks use that snapshot. The reply span is pinned before any GPIO operation. A final saved-mask guard serializes revocation with the bounded scalar provider operation. Close before this guard prohibits the effect; close after dispatch cannot undo it. Every dispatched result copies a fully initialized reply, including provider errors. A cancelled admission leaves the reply untouched. Pins remain held through all copies. result reports admission/device execution and cleanup_result reports the most recent release attempt. accepted becomes true at the first successful pin. device_effect_possible is set before calling the WRITE provider, even if that provider returns an error. retained requires the exact kernel call slot, user memory and address-space identity to stay alive. Cleanup tries output then input release, records errors and preserves every failed pin independently. gpio_finish retries only retained cleanup; it never rereads users, writes a reply or repeats a device operation. Neither an operation error nor successful cleanup authorizes replay of an earlier write. The private generic loan mechanism retains one exact slot pointer with a monotonic 64-bit epoch. Copied tokens and stale epochs cannot release it; epoch exhaustion rejects reuse. Read-only spans may share bytes; any writable overlap with a peer loan rejects. Known policy, provider, other loan and drain-control aliases reject before mutation. Only the two exact internal GPIO child slots may share their own call proof. A trusted drain callback must prove actual peripheral/DMA/IRQ/deferred/copy final access and observer join. User input cannot claim this proof. A failed proof or unpin keeps the exact pin and token retained. One privileged serialized kernel owner controls a broker and its slots. All memory/proof callbacks are bounded and run outside Arch guards; they may mark close, but cannot recursively admit, release, execute or retain the cookie output. Close inhibits admission and preserves every live loan. Quiescence is valid only after close, with zero loans/list entries and no executor. It does not implicitly stop providers or establish an application shutdown barrier. There is no reopen or hidden identity registry. NMI/fault/SMP publishers and unserialized privileged memory writers are outside this contract. This is a component callable by an integrating privileged kernel, not an implemented userspace transport. FreeRTOS SVC/MPU wrappers, task principal authentication, real memory pins, fault-safe copy mechanisms and TrustZone veneers are not connected in this slice. Generic loans provide ownership mechanics; asynchronous UART/SPI/CAN user messages are not implemented. Those future transports must supply their actual provider drain and memory-port contracts rather than accepting a user-supplied \u201cdrained\u201d flag. Google tests run the actual broker, typed GPIO wrapper, Native GPIO provider, Core request state and Native saved-mask implementation. They model only the external kernel identity/pin/unpin/proof boundary and privileged context. Behavioral RED/GREEN logs, sanitizer runs, public-header probes and strict ARM compilation are recorded in tools/testing/evidence/user-io-broker-iteration/manifest.json. These establish software behavior and compilation, not physical isolation, HIL timing or a qualified product release. The optional archive defines no mutable globals; default factory/source/ELF omission is verified separately by the integrating build graph.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Kernel-invoked user I/O admission", "type": "explanation"}, "development/documentation.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Use the content and diagram standard and five page templates for every new or changed page. Keep one authoritative contract and one canonical prose source. Current implementation, target design and exact reference configurations remain distinct. State the result, prerequisites and limit at the start. Explain who acts, on what input, in which context, and what observable result or failure follows. Use stable terms and explicit units. Keep raw hashes/logs in evidence links rather than the operation narrative. Do not replace scope with claims such as complete support. Tutorials provide a complete maintained fixture or pinned external consumer, actual commands, expected results and cleanup. Use literalinclude for source-owned inputs. Fragments state why they are incomplete. Ordinary document builds do not execute arbitrary Markdown commands, fetch latest examples or operate hardware. A diagram has an ID, scope, caption, alt text, editable source and local SVG. Label arrow meanings, optional paths, owners and failure responsibility. Review against actual code/contract, then render and inspect text, line crossings, zoom, print and offline resources. Successful rendering alone does not prove semantics. English canonical pages use gettext PO for Chinese; canonical Chinese contracts keep their source language. A translation tracks the actual canonical source digest, actual extracted messages and independent review. Changed source, fuzzy or missing messages display the true status instead of pretending a complete translation. Do not translate C symbols, configuration keys, result codes or original receipts. Use the reviewed bilingual terminology for these shared terms; its authority links preserve the single contract. Follow the unified document commands documented by the current CLI. A source check, strict bilingual build, real configuration/consumer execution, diagram audit, offline package and actual published-site check are separate results. Record command exits and exact inputs; never claim deployment from local HTML or hardware from a plan. The documentation ledger owns implementation and validation exits. The migration map preserves historical paths; old API redirects point to a version-aware migration or history page, not silently to a different new function contract. Retain qualification records at their original paths and identities.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Maintain clear and verifiable documentation", "type": "how-to"}, "development/implementation-map.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Use this map to locate an existing mechanism before changing it. It is a source navigation, not another build graph or support database. Source links bind the site\u2019s exact document revision; offline consumers can inspect the same paths in the matching checkout or Source SDK. Read the relevant contract and adjacent README before changing behavior. Select only maintained modes, keep private provider/vendor types out of consumers, and update declarations, diagrams, examples and translations together when their meaning changes. Preserve original evidence bytes and scope instead of renaming historical results to current HEAD.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Find the implementation authority", "type": "reference"}, "development/index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Preserve current formatting and comment conventions. New behavior uses real RED\u2013GREEN execution; documentation checks and firmware qualification have separate scopes.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Maintaining Nexus", "type": "reference"}, "development/workflow.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This workflow preserves current C11 architecture, formatting and explicit evidence. Codex owns platform maintenance; independent agents review high-risk changes. No fictitious personnel or local file can establish GitHub branch protection. First complete the fixed source and recursive dependency guide. From that repository root, install the maintenance tools: Windows uses .venv/Scripts/python.exe. Installation establishes pre-commit and commit-msg hooks. Hooks inspect staged inputs without staging or silently fixing files. Preserve unstaged work and actual failure diagnostics; never bypass hooks. For new C behavior or defect fixes, first add a meaningful GoogleTest/GoogleMock regression. Execute it and retain the expected failure reason and original command. Implement the smallest correct production change, execute GREEN, then refactor. Mock external ports/MMIO, not the implementation being tested. Cover relevant rejection, cancellation, drain, overflow, quarantine and instance isolation. Python production tools use stdlib unittest with the same actual RED/GREEN rule. The full gate builds the pinned offline framework and executes discovered cases with fresh reports and no filters/shards. Empty, skipped, partial, stale or changed inputs fail. The hook checks the staged tree remains unchanged; CI independently executes the gate. The framework is host-only C++17; firmware remains C11. Automation proves execution, while retained development logs document historical RED/GREEN order. Pure prose or diagram edits need corresponding document checks and semantic review rather than mirrored implementation tests. Use the unchanged .clang-format and .editorconfig: 80 columns, four spaces, attached braces, type-attached pointer stars and backslash Doxygen comments. Public headers document context, deadline, ownership and failures. Source comments explain invariants and hardware ordering instead of repeating declaration parameters. New/changed files pass whole-file checks; existing frozen debt may only shrink. Commit one reviewable change with Conventional Commits. Review interface/configuration changes together with diagrams, examples, translation and execution scope. Keep products, workers, private PCB and recovery policy outside Nexus. Record actual source/configuration/tool/ELF identities and physical pending items; a successful commit or CI does not promote a product release.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Develop with TDD and normal commit gates", "type": "how-to"}, "explanation/factory.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The current platform keeps typed factory lookup and C polymorphism. Configuration generates the exact instances; factory lookup returns them without constructing, initializing, locking or discovering anything. Hardware startup and request execution are separate explicit actions. Current factory IDs, shared readonly ops and independent instance contexts FIG-04 shows the current identity structure. A controller and a child endpoint have different interfaces and IDs even when their implementation shares a physical bus. One schema-2 TOML selects instance names, bindings, modes and exact capacities. The strict resolver produces typed IR; emitters publish readonly faces, shared ops references, private independent contexts, fixed IRQ wiring and compact getters. Native assemblies receive independent model state too; a test singleton is not the production instance-generation mechanism. nexus_factory.h is generated for each assembly, so its IDs are not a universal header or persistent numbering scheme. Default IDs are compact uint16_t aliases with named constants. COUNT, invalid and unselected entries return NULL. Optional nominal strong IDs and capability records are explicit configuration choices; a chip capability record does not grant Board routing or physical qualification. Products may retain a face for the provider-storage lifetime. Stopping hardware does not revoke a pointer or synchronize callers. The application must quiesce callers and publishers before reclaiming external adapter storage. No getter changes mode or silently acquires execution ownership. This complete maintained assembly selects independent Native instances: The async tutorial runs the complete generated factory binary. It verifies cold lookup, distinct contexts with shared ops, bus/endpoint identity, invalid IDs, stop/restart and retained quarantine. The authority for these interfaces is the integration contract and generated/public headers; this page explains the design rather than duplicating function declarations.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Typed factory, static instances and polymorphic faces", "type": "explanation"}, "explanation/index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Understand boundaries, static assembly and retained storage before changing a provider. The linked design contracts remain authoritative.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Architecture explanations", "type": "reference"}, "explanation/ownership.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The caller owns request and buffer storage. Accepted asynchronous I/O borrows it; rejection does not. Cancellation, a deadline or a failed stop cannot revoke that borrow. This separation prevents IRQ/DMA or an executor from accessing recycled memory. The lifecycle contract and public operation declarations define exact context and result behavior. Current borrowed request state and settlement proof obligations FIG-06 does not treat REJECTED/ACCEPTED as request-state enum members. Core helpers cannot observe a peripheral\u2019s physical drain; the provider must establish that proof before publishing settlement. Current UART and SPI memory, wire and final-publication order FIG-07 separates memory completion, wire completion and final publication. UART TC is not NDTR zero; SPI idle and CS interval are not a DMA interrupt. Disable and detach the actual IRQ/DMA/queue references before release-publication of SETTLED. The caller\u2019s acquire observation then exposes the final result/count. A timeout result can be final only after this lifecycle, not at deadline expiry. Use stable slots plus monotonically increasing epochs for remote control. Do not send a raw pointer to storage another task can recycle. Detach control/observer references and join observers before releasing the slot and preparing the next request. A generation counter detects stale identity but does not itself join a task. Continuous receive and ADC use explicit caller-owned block loans. A consumer keeps a block immutable until matching release. The producer needs a replacement target before publishing an old hardware bank. No replacement means visible stop/backpressure; it must never overwrite a consumer. Stop waits for filling, ready and borrowed blocks, not just active DMA. The stream contract records what loss counters and software timestamps do and do not prove. Current continuous block producer, replacement and loan drain FIG-15 includes observable loss and stop/drain responsibilities. Capacity and service-latency budgets are explicit product inputs; arbitrary IRQ delay or consumer speed cannot be promised lossless. The tutorial executes rejection and quarantine; OS shutdown explains wait and join responsibilities.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Borrow, completion and observer lifetime", "type": "explanation"}, "explanation/platform-architecture.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Nexus is a statically assembled C11 MCU platform. It separates reusable mechanisms from external product policy. One authored TOML selects exact hardware and optional adapters; immutable I/O faces expose the chosen private providers. The application owns execution and recovery. These are current design boundaries, not whole-platform physical qualification. Current platform layers and external product boundary FIG-01 expresses current layer responsibilities. Arrows describe labeled dependencies or access; it is not an initialization sequence. The resolver combines authored TOML with reviewed SoC/Board facts into immutable typed IR and an atomic bundle. It validates modes, resources, clocks, IRQs, ABI, DMA reachability and budgets. CMake owns the source dependency graph; the resolver does not discover software from device names or generate product workers. Actual compile-target dependency relationships FIG-02 is the checked compile graph. Vendor and provider includes remain private; public consumers receive Nexus types and selected compile/link policy. Authored inputs, atomic generation and firmware artifacts FIG-03 shows current generation. Unknown and conflicting selections fail; no Kconfig/cache/old-header fallback repairs them. Runtime factory lookup returns an existing face. After explicit successful platform start, ordinary operations call its shared ops table with its instance context. IRQ dispatch is fixed to provider instances and does not repeat factory lookup for every byte. Current initialization, failure withdrawal and retained cleanup responsibility FIG-05 shows explicit startup and reverse cleanup. A static face can exist before start and after stop; its pointer is not a capability to execute cold hardware. Partial failure can retain irreversible watchdog effects or busy borrows. Products must inspect primary and cleanup outcomes, continue the real executor, and retain quarantined resources. Platform start never starts a scheduler. A 32-bit face contains two pointers: eight bytes of readonly storage. Provider/mode ops are shared; exact mutable state and selected buffers remain separate. Indirection lets one public interface serve independent concrete providers and GoogleMock ports. Its cost is one public dispatch and code/readonly table retention; actual Flash/RAM and timing are measured on the selected linked image. Static assembly rejects conflicts early and prunes unselected drivers. It deliberately forgoes hotplug discovery, runtime string registries, arbitrary dynamic clock changes and automatic recovery policy. Optional shared owners add explicitly budgeted slots and synchronization; direct single-executor paths need no default OS lock or worker. Read factory, ownership, the integration contract and exact support. Architecture diagrams explain mechanisms; tests, image checks and HIL establish their separate execution scopes.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "How the platform is assembled and executed", "type": "explanation"}, "explanation/source-map.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The layer README files remain canonical beside their implementation. The following inclusions read those exact files rather than copying a second contract. Relative source links are bound to the website source revision. C11 caller-owned request admission, stable control identities, completion state and monotonic absolute deadlines. No devices, OS scheduler, allocation or global registry. Public interfaces are in include/nexus/core. REJECTED retains no references. An accepted chain borrows request and buffers until acquire-observed SETTLED. SETTLED is its last request access. Cancel/timeouts never revoke a borrow; failed drain keeps QUARANTINED storage alive. Producers wait on latched state; a single executor services the operation. Queue adapters own their outer request and use explicit inner provider metadata where needed. tests/contracts/core_contract_test.c executes publication, admission, wrapping time arithmetic and terminal-state invariants. Host results do not prove MCU interrupt ordering or worst-case execution time. Nexus::Arch exposes saved interrupt masks, exact exception identity, current privilege, ordering barriers and an optional already-enabled DWT cycle snapshot. It does not call I/O, OS or vendor SDK code. One compile-time Cortex-M primitive implementation covers M0/M0+/M3/M4/M7/M23/M33/M55/M85, including ARMv8.1-M Mainline compiler profiles. STM32F407 and GD32F470 maintain the initial platform scope; G474, H743 and U575 provide explicit reference slices described in the support matrix. CPU support does not extend those SoC or Board slices. The separate CPU runtime assembly selects Core/Arch and optional OS software for all nine cores without creating a SoC, Board, startup or factory. See CPU contracts and the runtime assembly example. Save/restore tokens on the same CPU/thread in strict reverse order. Cortex-M preserves incoming PRIMASK and does not overwrite BASEPRI/FAULTMASK where those registers exist. Only privileged Task/configurable-IRQ callers may change masks. NMI and HardFault remain unmasked and must not access this protected metadata. Keep metadata sections short: no blocking, allocation, large copies, kernel calls or user callbacks. A saved Arch token is not a kernel critical-section token or an SMP lock. Mask/context queries let blocking I/O reject contexts that prevent its progress. The read-only mask snapshot distinguishes PRIMASK, BASEPRI and FAULTMASK; it is not a restore token and does not freeze interrupt or security-world execution. Baseline M0/M0+/M23 queries never read absent BASEPRI/FAULTMASK. Handler mode is privileged even when CONTROL.nPRIV describes an interrupted unprivileged Thread. An exception number identifies the current CPU/security state; it does not grant permission to call a kernel ISR API or coordinate another security world. DMB/DSB/ISB encode different ordering guarantees; they do not prove peripheral or DMA quiescence. DWT snapshots require an explicit reviewed CPU capability and privileged access. A disabled capability emits no DWT reads. Snapshots do not enable or reset the counter and are not monotonic deadlines. Their wrap/frequency limits are documented in the header. Reviewed capabilities are explicit CPU facts, never inferred by probing arbitrary registers or enabling optional units. sleep.h exposes two explicit shallow-sleep operations. The masked nx_arch_wait_for_interrupt() requires privileged Thread mode, PRIMASK=1, BASEPRI/FAULTMASK clear and SCR.SLEEPDEEP clear. It executes DSB/WFI/ISB without changing masks, clocks or pending interrupts. The caller owns the final readiness check and an enabled IRQ wake source; a pending enabled IRQ wakes WFI despite PRIMASK and runs after the caller restores its saved mask. nx_arch_idle_if_unchanged() instead requires all incoming masks clear, saves PRIMASK, rechecks an aligned published sequence with acquire semantics and sleeps only when it still matches the caller\u2019s snapshot. It restores the exact incoming mask; the caller retries the authoritative predicate and supplies an IRQ timer for finite deadlines. This is one CPU and one security state\u2019s publication domain. NMI/HardFault, DMA, SMP and other security-world publishers are excluded. Native reports unsupported. Compiler/model evidence does not qualify a concrete timer, clock continuity, physical power behavior or wake latency. cache.h exposes distinct data clean, invalidate, combined maintenance and instruction synchronization. Every range must own complete reviewed 32-byte lines, fit the CPU address space and be quiescent across CPU/DMA/security users. The functions do not round ranges, enable caches or flush unrelated dirty lines. cache_control.h separately owns cold M7 transitions. The caller supplies one zero-initialized control object, masks configurable interrupts and excludes DMA and all other memory users. Trusted reset or a prior successful owned clean/disable establishes cold state; clear CCR bits alone cannot prove that foreign dirty lines are absent. Enable rejects pre-existing caches; disable cleans every reviewed data set/way and changes CCR in a leaf that cannot dirty its own stack. Failed partial changes retain cache/CSSELR recovery responsibility. Actual instruction audits verify addresses, loops and barriers at O0/Os/O2. Other CPU profiles remain unsupported by this cold-control API; their range maintenance capability does not imply a reviewed global cache transition. mpu.h retains distinct v7-format and v8 base/limit/MAIR encoders. Single-region programming requires an already masked privileged Thread and disabled MPU; actual TYPE bounds and fields are checked before writes. CTRL and incoming RNR are preserved. Memory policy and any later enable operation belong to the caller. The optional M0+ MPU uses the v7-format registers confirmed by CMSIS. security.h identifies the reviewed image state and encodes explicit SAU regions. Only a Secure compiler/profile with reviewed SAU regions can program one owned region, while SAU is disabled and the privileged Thread has PRIMASK set. No API changes SAU CTRL/ALLNS, another region, Secure gateways or another world\u2019s alias. NonSecure and single-domain images return unsupported without SAU MMIO. IDAU, overlap rules, memory permissions and gateway policy stay outside these mechanisms. nonsecure.h supplies an instantaneous Secure Thread observation of a complete span using the actual CMSE NonSecure unprivileged read or read/write check. It needs no programmable SAU when an IDAU establishes attribution. Native, single-state and NonSecure images return unsupported without crossing domains. Success does not pin, copy, authenticate, exclude MMIO or freeze mappings, DMA and bus firewall policy. SoC and gateway owners must separately establish these conditions before accessing memory or performing an operation. features.h reports immutable reviewed cache/MPU/DWT/security/SAU capabilities, not enabled state or hardware qualification. Native exposes the same APIs with absent-hardware results; pure encoders remain usable. Extending CPU mechanisms does not provide a SoC startup, vector table, clock tree, memory/DMA routes, Board wiring or an RTOS FPU/MVE context port. Each new platform must supply and verify those boundaries independently. Publication and RMW atomics have separate contracts in atomic.h; CPU-specific fallback never silently links libatomic. Aligned acquire/release word loads and stores do not mask IRQs. M0/M0+ RMW uses bounded saved-PRIMASK exclusion and restores the incoming mask; all concurrent users must be privileged local Thread/configurable-IRQ callers. NMI, HardFault, another core, DMA and another security world are outside this exclusion domain. Other maintained cores use compiler lock-free RMW; exclusive retries do not establish a fixed cycle bound. Initialize words before publication and retain their lifetime until every reader/writer has stopped. Native models recursive exclusion and C11 fences; it does not model real NVIC, DMA, physical timing or signal-safe interrupts. Invalid restore nesting aborts in both Debug and Release. GoogleTest/GoogleMock compiles the real Cortex-M algorithm against a narrow register/instruction hardware boundary for nine CPU profiles; the normal ARM path inlines that boundary and never links the mocks. Native tests exercise real recursive exclusion, strict token nesting and context queries. Cache, MPU and security models mock only external CPU/MMIO operations and run the production C algorithms, including rejection before state changes. Pinned CMSIS core_cm0plus/7/23/33/55/85.h, mpu_armv7/8.h and cachel1_armv7.h establish the maintained register layouts. Actual IRQ latency, privilege transitions, DMA/cache coherency and CPU timing require HIL. GPIO, UART, SPI, I2C, Flash, watchdog, EXTI, PWM and ADC expose C11 interfaces. Each selected instance has an immutable face with two pointers: shared read-only methods and a pointer to private provider state. Generated faces live in read-only storage; their state and selected buffers are private static objects in the generated translation unit. Manually assembled providers use storage supplied by their explicit constructor owner. A 32-bit target spends eight bytes of Flash per face; mutable state and exact-capacity buffers are separate. Method pointers are shared per provider and mode rather than repeated in every mutable instance. Public operations check the face and dispatch its exact context. Different provider tables can coexist in one image without redefining public symbols. A missing optional method reports NX_ERROR_UNSUPPORTED; invalid faces reject before provider access. A method must preserve the public operation\u2019s execution, deadline, buffer ownership and failure contract. Provider headers, register layouts and SDK types stay private. Generated typed factory IDs select instances and distinguish SPI/I2C controllers from their child endpoints. Lookup returns a static identity. Platform start, request admission, service, recovery and stop remain explicit; lookup performs no hardware initialization, allocation, name search or locking. Faces remain valid while provider storage exists, so stopping requires caller and IRQ quiescence rather than promising that a pointer has been revoked. Stream reserve, publish, acquire, release, abort and stop access constant metadata independent of block count. Stop checks an outstanding-block count; filling, ready and borrowed blocks remain counted until proved abort or matching release. It never scans the block array or revokes a loan under the interrupt mask. Storage overlap validation remains a cold assembly operation whose work grows with block count. This complexity contract is not physical IRQ latency or cycle qualification. Default paths need no OS locks or hidden tasks. Requests and transfer buffers remain caller-owned; generated provider buffers are assembly-owned and separate. UART tracks physical TC separately from loaded bytes and request release; error-only RX events never invent data. SPI and I2C preserve their bounded polling contracts, and controller recovery never replays a failed transaction. Flash exposes physical geometry; products provide explicit restricted regions. The Native model owns independent state per instance. Its explicit default fixtures support focused tests; generated assemblies use separate state and buffers. Native behavior, production-register fault models and ARM link fixtures establish software evidence. Board facts authorize reviewed physical routes; software-only fixtures do not establish PCB connectivity or physical timing. Thin wait/wake and per-object storage contracts. Baremetal has no scheduler; Native uses POSIX threads; FreeRTOS uses explicit static TCB, stack and queue storage selected by the caller. No universal maximum pool, hidden worker, default timer daemon or device dependency on OS. Notifications are hints: arm/recheck the authoritative request predicate. Kernel ISR operatio", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Source-adjacent contracts", "type": "explanation"}, "guides/board-porting.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Use this guide when the exact SoC already has a maintained provider. A Board adds PCB facts and reviewed bindings; it does not copy a vendor SDK or authorize every silicon route. Actual unobserved PCB revision, bridges and voltage stay unknown. Prepare the fixed source and recursive dependencies before using the resolver or build commands below. Record the full part, package, source document revision and retained provenance. Record oscillator values, supply conditions, SWD reservations, pins, active levels, jumper conditions and safe initial outputs. Distinguish document defaults from an observation of your particular board. Start with one LED and one console route. The following is a complete maintained Board file, including soc_family: The integration contract owns field and resource requirements. Keep your Board package in its external product repository when it describes private PCB data. Public reusable packages live under boards/; ordinary packages require only board.json and a README. Add bounded neutral Board hooks only for electrical actions not expressible by maintained data. From the Nexus repository root, inspect a known-good reference before authoring: For your package, select board_package in an external complete assembly and run those same commands with its absolute path. Relative package paths resolve from the authored assembly. A JSON syntax check alone cannot prove SoC identity, clock, route or resource compatibility. Unknown fields and unreviewed routes must reject. Configure your external consumer using its assembly and the locked ARM toolchain. Keep baremetal and FreeRTOS in separate output roots. Check resolved claims, vector/startup, strong handlers, ELF/BIN/map and every physical RAM/Flash budget. Factory lookup must be pure before start and instance state must remain isolated. Use a software Board fixture for model/link testing; label its wiring accordingly. Prepare HIL plans without equipment. Physical qualification requires a separately reviewed station, observed Board/part/UID/probe/wiring, exact image and real raw measurements. Source-reviewed pads never establish connector continuity, immunity or safe electrical behavior.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Add a reviewed Board package", "type": "how-to"}, "guides/index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Each guide defines inputs, ownership, rejection and an observable result. Hardware commands require an actual connected station; the documentation build does not access hardware.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Task guides", "type": "reference"}, "guides/migration.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Use this guide to migrate an older HAL/OSAL/Kconfig consumer to the current Source SDK generation. Keep a fixed source revision while migrating. The platform retains typed factory plus C polymorphic interfaces; it changes instance generation and ownership rather than replacing interfaces with a runtime service registry. The compatibility contract owns version and interface epoch behavior. Obtain the fixed source and recursive dependencies first; keep the old consumer in its own checkout while validating the new one. Inspect this maintained Native assembly; it is complete, not a fragment: From the repository root, execute the actual parser and generation path: Use a new output path you own. Check resolved.json and generated headers; never author the resolved output or reuse stale generated files after a failure. Legacy schema-1 JSON exists only for explicit migration fixtures. A failed TOML does not fall back to JSON, Kconfig, old headers or cache values. Use compact typed IDs from the current assembly and check getter NULL results. Getter names and ID constants are generated from authored instance names; IDs are not persistent IDs or a binary ABI. Optional nominal strong IDs and capability records require their explicit configuration. Start hardware through nx_platform_start() and inspect both primary and cleanup results. After successful startup, ordinary I/O uses the returned face directly. Convert synchronous and asynchronous calls according to their actual mode. For accepted requests, migrate storage lifetime, service/wait progress, cancellation, settlement and shutdown together. The async tutorial executes these failure paths; replacing a function spelling alone is insufficient. Build an independent relocated SDK consumer, then link the exact MCU configuration with the locked compiler. Retain evidence for the migrated source and configuration. Old HAL/OSAL documentation remains historical; a redirect does not make an old API contract equivalent to a new one.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Migrate legacy configuration and interface access", "type": "how-to"}, "guides/os-shutdown.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Use this guide for applications selecting an optional Native, FreeRTOS or ThreadX adapter. The application owns tasks, stacks, notifications, queues and execution progress. Baremetal has no scheduler. Platform start never starts a kernel. The OS contract remains authoritative. Use the request\u2019s latched predicate as truth. Check the completion predicate, arm the notification sequence, recheck, wait with the same absolute monotonic deadline, then recheck again. Wakes are hints and may be coalesced or unrelated. On timeout, perform the contract\u2019s final predicate observation. Do not restart a relative budget after each hint and do not block the only executor that must make progress. Predicate, wake and explicit shutdown responsibilities FIG-08 shows the current wait/wake responsibilities. A provider IRQ sink must respect the selected kernel syscall ceiling; an unqualified priority cannot call an ISR kernel API. ThreadX has its own reviewed M4/Native subset and masking policy, not the FreeRTOS port or tickless/isolation capability set. Close client admission and reject new work without borrowing it. Keep the resource owner/executor running. Request cancellation using stable slots and matching epochs; cancellation does not release accepted storage. Drain transfers and block loans. Acquire-observe SETTLED and read results. A failed drain retains QUARANTINED storage and explicit recovery responsibility. Join producers and detach/join control observers before slot or request reuse. Stop and join the owner. Quiesce hardware IRQ publishers before destroying their notification, queue, guard, stack or TCB storage. Stop platform providers only when their selected lifetime and kernel clock/Tick ownership permit it. A running scheduler is not stopped by ordinary I/O teardown. Task return has a backend-specific implementation: Returning from an entry function or observing a completion hint never authorizes stack/TCB reclamation. Keep wrapper, stack and kernel-control storage until the one external lifecycle owner successfully joins it. Raw queues and closable queues have different lifecycle contracts; close is not destruction. Keep all storage on the failure path and retry cleanup only through its real owner. First prepare the fixed source and recursive dependencies. From that repository root, with host build tools available: Both binaries must execute nonempty passing cases. This targeted development scope does not replace the complete TDD gate. The external UART owner application in nexus-examples supplies product workers; its pin/revision must be recorded before claiming consumer execution. Physical scheduler context, IRQ ceiling, FP/MVE retention, stack peaks and wake latency require real station evidence. Do not inherit POSIX behavior as MCU timing.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Wait correctly and shut down an explicit owner", "type": "how-to"}, "guides/performance-hil.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Use this guide to separate linked software costs from physical MCU behavior. The platform supplies measurement and HIL tooling; products choose workloads, electrical limits, safe outputs and release policy. No Board is physically qualified until real equipment executes its reviewed scope. Prepare the fixed source and recursive dependencies, then the locked toolchain for the exact selected assembly. Record clean source, assembly/resolved digest, dependencies, compiler and flags. Build the same workload before and after a change. Check actual ELF/BIN/map and resource reports, not estimates derived from object counts. Separate Flash LOAD, Flash footprint, each physical RAM reservation, MSP, task stack/TCB, queue and I/O buffers. Stack capacity is not a measured stack high-water value. The build contract owns resource checks. Use maintained tools/measurement/ gates; execution budgets state scope. A speed claim needs units, clock, input size, load, sampling method and uncertainty. DWT 32-bit snapshots require a bounded wrap interval; host arrival timestamps do not establish MCU cycles or IRQ latency. H743 exact memory and Cache ownership reference FIG-09 is an exact H743 reference. Cache-enabled TX requires complete 32-byte line ownership and AXI SRAM reachability; DMA completion, wire completion and borrow settlement are separate. It does not authorize RX DMA or TCM placement. First configure and build the selected maintained assembly. For the Liangshan baremetal reference, these repository-root commands consume its actual output: The locked ARM compiler must be on PATH. Check the part, pins, selected modes and not_executed cases in reports. Preparation does not open serial, flash or power equipment. Numeric limits and instruments remain unbound until reviewed. G474/H743/U575 software-reference fixtures deliberately reject physical station admission; a future physical fixture needs separately reviewed tooling integration. Bind observed PCB/part/UID, probe, stable serial path, wiring and supply to the exact ELF, resolved configuration and resource report. Pin acquisition tools, recipe, instruments and numeric lower/upper limits. Follow the maintained HIL acquisition contract: identify, flash, verify, reset, serial, cleanup. Failure preserves raw logs; cleanup failure retains station quarantine and requires physical inspection. Capture completion means executed but unqualified. Validate the actual smoke, measurement and OS records separately. Do not synthesize physical traces or convert a software model into HIL. Qualification covers recorded observations; it does not establish unobserved WCET, electrical safety or a product release.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Measure resources and prepare hardware acceptance", "type": "how-to"}, "guides/provider-porting.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Use this guide to add a maintained implementation with explicit resources and measurable costs. A new family or mode requires behavior, configuration and build integration together. Similar STM32/GD32 peripheral names do not prove register compatibility. Read the architecture, integration contract and provider assembly contract. Prepare the fixed source and recursive dependencies. Define the new behavior, supported modes, public ownership obligations and rejection boundaries before changing its production implementation. Add and execute a meaningful RED GoogleTest/GoogleMock contract against the current production path. Retain the actual command, nonzero execution and expected failing assertion. Test MMIO/CPU/dependency ports through mocks; Python resolver behavior uses stdlib unittest. A missing tool or compile-only error is not the expected behavioral RED result. Add exact soc.json/routes.json facts and documented provenance. Feed the shared CPU resolver; do not duplicate CPU flags or infer optional features. Add the family to the explicit maintained provider map in tools/configure/. Define supported modes, numerical limits, constructors, rollback and IRQ wiring. Unsupported modes reject; there is no runtime registration or plugin discovery. Add explicit CMake targets and private SDK dependencies. Update declared resolver reconfiguration inputs and the Source SDK required-file closure. Implement the smallest correct production change. Define an immutable public face with shared readonly ops and independent context. Reuse public Nexus types; keep vendor structures and concrete storage private. Execute GREEN against the real provider. Verify rejection without residual effects, accepted borrow, cancel/deadline, failed drain, quarantine, stop/restart and instance isolation where the mode needs them. Refactor while keeping contracts passing. Link exact ARM fixtures and verify actual resources, handler retention, vector bytes and unselected-driver pruning. Validate an independent relocated SDK consumer and record physical work as not executed. For a component, pass explicit bus, GPIO, time and optional OS ports. Do not create workers, probe devices, invent application roles or supply a maximum object pool. Flash drivers expose physical geometry; persistence and update policy remain external. Measure the selected empty image and the same fixture after enabling the new mode. Report Flash LOAD, Flash footprint, each RAM domain and reserved task/IO storage separately. Hot IRQ work should directly access its fixed provider; an interface lookup is a cold action. Keep bounded admission and stream metadata operations independent of queue length where their contracts require it. A passing host register model proves its modeled software scope. New CPU, Cache, FPU/MVE, security or low-power behavior needs its own actual execution boundary; platform support does not infer it from a name.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Add a SoC, provider or reusable component", "type": "how-to"}, "guides/source-checkout.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Use this guide before running repository commands in a tutorial, configuration reference or maintenance workflow. You will obtain a detached, exact Nexus source revision and its recursive dependencies. This requires Git, Python 3.11+ and network access for the initial clone and dependency retrieval. It does not build, flash or establish software or hardware qualification. Open the immutable documentation version identified by the page\u2019s version banner. Download documentation-manifest.json from that version\u2019s root, or use the file already present at the root of its verified offline package. Keep it outside your future checkout, for example as nexus-documentation-manifest.json in your working parent directory. If you entered through stable/, record the resolved immutable version before downloading; do not mix two versions. The default GitHub branch may lag a reviewed documentation branch. A branch name, successful clone or documentation release is not a substitute for the exact commit. The previously reviewed documentation snapshot is 5a7a75c321ea173df8692714f95a8df13715293d; choosing it recreates that snapshot, not later documentation. Its immutable package manifest is one concrete example. Use your version\u2019s manifest for the current tutorial. Run these POSIX-shell commands from the parent directory containing the downloaded manifest. Choose a new checkout directory; the commands do not replace one that already exists. The Python command validates the identity before passing it to Git: Check every command\u2019s exit status. git rev-parse HEAD must equal the manifest\u2019s full commit. Recursive submodule lines must have neither a leading - (missing), + (different revision) nor U (conflict), and git status --short must be empty before starting a clean-source workflow. Stop on an unavailable commit or failed dependency retrieval; never substitute main, HEAD or a latest dependency. For a preview without a package manifest, record the full commit from its source banner or documentation-build.json and use that validated commit explicitly. On Windows, run the POSIX snippet in Git Bash, or read the same manifest field and perform the corresponding Git commands in your shell. Do not treat the shell variable syntax as a portable PowerShell command. Each tutorial starts from the repository root established above. Keep independent consumer and package outputs outside the source checkout when instructed. Retain manifests, source identities and required reports before deleting only directories you created. A clean checkout supplies input identity; actual execution and qualification evidence remain separate.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Obtain the exact source and pinned dependencies", "type": "how-to"}, "index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Complete one task using the current reusable C11 platform. Product applications, scheduling policy, private PCB data and recovery policy belong in external consumer repositories. Source documentation, executed software evidence and physical board qualification remain separate. The current implementation uses generated typed factories and immutable C interface faces. Factory lookup returns an existing static object; ordinary I/O goes directly through its operations table. Startup does not start a scheduler.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Nexus platform documentation", "type": "reference"}, "reference/api/arch.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "These declarations come from public header files. Selection, lifecycle, supported execution modes and physical authorization remain bounded by the corresponding design and support records. CPU-local mask, context, ordering and cycle snapshot contract. Author Nexus Team Save incoming local mask and mask configurable exceptions. Privileged Task/configurable-IRQ short metadata sections only; do not block, allocate, call OS functions, copy large buffers or invoke callbacks. NMI/HardFault remain unmasked. Native uses a recursive host exclusion model, not physical IRQ timing or signal safety. NMI/HardFault must not access shared metadata protected by this mask. ARM unprivileged callers need an explicit privileged gateway owned by their OS. Restore the incoming mask, preserving nested exclusions. Privileged contexts only. Restores PRIMASK only; BASEPRI/FAULTMASK, where present, are not modified. Snapshot the implemented local interrupt-mask registers. Bounded read-only privileged task/IRQ query. Does not mask interrupts or grant a stable critical section. Values refer to this CPU/security state, not another security world or an SMP peer. Unprivileged reads do not establish mask state. Test masks that could prevent required completion progress. Bounded privileged task/IRQ query, no state change. ARM unprivileged reads do not establish the actual mask state. Query the exact current CPU exception identity. Bounded read-only task/IRQ query. The identity belongs to the current CPU/security state; it is not a portable SoC IRQ number or evidence that a kernel ISR call is allowed. Query privilege in the current CPU execution context. Bounded read-only task/IRQ query. Handler privilege does not depend on the interrupted Thread\u2019s CONTROL.nPRIV. This query does not establish Secure-world ownership. Query current CPU exception context. Order explicit data memory accesses in the CPU domain. Cortex-M DMB; Native sequentially consistent thread fence. Does not prove DMA idle or clean noncoherent cache lines. Complete preceding explicit memory accesses. Cortex-M DSB; Native thread fence is a behavioral model, not a physical bus/DMA completion guarantee. Synchronize subsequent CPU instruction execution. Cortex-M ISB; Native thread fence has no pipeline semantics. Snapshot an already enabled free-running cycle counter. Counter frequency follows CPU clock. Use unsigned snapshot subtraction only for intervals shorter than 2^32 cycles; this counter is neither shared monotonic time nor a deadline. Saved incoming PRIMASK or calling-thread Native nesting. Restore on the same CPU/thread in strict reverse order. This is neither a FreeRTOS BASEPRI token nor an SMP lock. Read-only snapshot of implemented local interrupt masks. Absent BASEPRI/FAULTMASK registers are zero. Native reports PRIMASK as zero or one according to current thread nesting. This snapshot is neither a restore token nor a kernel lock. Inline naturally aligned 32-bit publication and RMW ports. Storage is a live, naturally aligned uint32_t; all concurrent accesses use these helpers or compatible compiler atomics. Initialize before publishing storage. Load/store use compiler acquire/release operations on all maintained CPUs and never mask interrupts. Other than ARMv6-M, RMW uses compiler lock-free operations; exclusive instruction retries do not establish a fixed cycle bound. ARMv6-M RMW is CPU-local, bounded saved-PRIMASK exclusion. Only privileged Thread/configurable-IRQ contexts on one CPU may access this domain. NMI/HardFault, unprivileged callers, another core, DMA and another security domain must not access its storage concurrently. It is not an SMP or bus atomic operation. No callback, allocation, blocking call or payload copy occurs while masked. Read one word without publishing or acquiring other data. Read one word and acquire data from the observed publication. Store one word without publishing other data. Publish preceding data through one aligned release store. Strong acq_rel compare/exchange, acquire on failure. Add modulo 2^32 with acq_rel ordering, returning the original word. Add modulo 2^32 with release ordering; return original word. ARMv6-M conservatively acquires the observed word too. Subtract modulo 2^32 with acq_rel ordering; return original. Subtract modulo 2^32 with release ordering; return original. Explicit whole-line CPU cache maintenance mechanisms. Publish dirty data cache lines to the point of coherency. Privileged task/configurable-IRQ context in the current security domain only. The caller excludes other CPU, security-world and DMA users of every complete line until return, and keeps cache configuration stable. No hidden IRQ mask, allocation or cache enable occurs. Supported geometry is an explicitly reviewed 32-byte line; range rounding is forbidden. Work is bounded by bytes/32 and is not a DMA drain, device completion or transfer of ownership. Discard complete data cache lines without writing them. The caller permits all dirty data in these lines to be discarded and satisfies clean\u2019s exclusion/context contract. No adjacent line is touched or rounded into the range. For DMA receive, providers coordinate both pre-DMA and post-drain maintenance; this call does not establish idle. Publish dirty data and discard the complete cache lines. Satisfies clean\u2019s exclusion/context contract. Distinct from invalidate: dirty contents are written before discard. Make a complete owned range coherent for CPU instructions. Satisfies clean\u2019s exclusion/context contract. All present caches are checked before any maintenance. With only one reviewed cache, only that cache is maintained. The caller prevents execution from the changing range, including other security-world users. This is not code validation, an MPU policy change or a global cache/branch-predictor flush. Explicit owned Cortex-M7 cold cache transitions. Data-cache ownership bit, independent of the hardware CCR layout. Instruction-cache ownership bit. Cold-transition admission or execution result. Values: Admit cold caches and enable the explicitly selected ones. Reviewed M7 only. Requires privileged Thread, PRIMASK=1, BASEPRI=FAULTMASK=0 and known-clean caches from a trusted reset or prior successful owned clean/disable. CCR bits being zero alone do not prove clean foreign state; never adopt an unknown bootloader state. Caller excludes all other cache users, DMA, NMI and HardFault access to affected memory throughout the transition. It owns memory attributes, execution and coherency policy; enabling a cache does not establish DMA coherence. No IRQ mask changes, allocation or cache adoption occur. Rejected initial admission has no retained responsibility. An IO failure can retain owned_mask/selector_pending; call cold_disable and retain storage until it succeeds. Clean and disable caches owned by this control object. Satisfies enable\u2019s exclusion and context contract after every DMA/provider has drained. Rejects enabled caches outside owned_mask. The data clean/disable transition uses a stack-free leaf so no local dirty line can appear between the final clean and CCR update. Its bounded work is four ways times the verified set count, not constant latency. Failed operations remain retryable without adopting a new cache or forgetting the original selector. A zero object is an idempotent no-op on a supported CPU profile. Caller-owned cache responsibility and selector recovery. Zero initialize before first use; do not modify fields or copy a live object. Retain the object until owned_mask and selector_pending are both zero. owned_mask conservatively records cleanup responsibility, not enabled hardware bits. Retained data/instruction cache responsibility. Original CSSELR for an unfinished operation. Original selector restoration is outstanding. Reviewed CPU mechanisms and allocation-free result types. Result of a validated local CPU mechanism operation. Security state compiled for this firmware image. Query immutable, compiled CPU mechanism capabilities. Read-only Task/IRQ query, no hardware access or discovery. Reviewed mechanisms in the exact compiled CPU profile. Cache sizes are zero when absent, MPU version is zero, seven or eight. These facts do not prove enabled hardware, memory reachability, cross-domain ownership or HIL status. Distinct Armv7-M and Armv8-M MPU encoding mechanisms. Encode an exact Armv7-M region without accessing hardware. Pure bounded task/IRQ function. Inputs and output must not overlap. This encodes fields, not SoC memory reachability, cache ownership, DMA coherency or product protection policy. Encode an exact Armv8-M region without accessing hardware. Pure bounded task/IRQ function. Inputs and output must not overlap. No address rounding or v7/v8 policy conversion. Program one disabled-MPU Armv7-M region in this world. Privileged Thread only with PRIMASK set and exclusive caller ownership of this MPU. NMI/HardFault/another security world must not modify it. Disables only the selected region, writes its fields, restores incoming RNR and leaves CTRL unchanged. No allocation, OS calls, implicit MPU enable, bulk clear or Secure access to the Non-secure alias. Program one disabled-MPU Armv8-M region in this world. Privileged masked Thread, disabled MPU and exclusive local ownership required. No MAIR updates or cross-world alias access. Incoming RNR and CTRL are preserved. Update one Armv8-M MAIR byte without changing its neighbors. Caller owns valid memory attributes and all regions using this slot. Privileged masked Thread, disabled MPU and exclusive local ownership required. No CTRL or RNR writes, no cache maintenance, and no cross-world register access. Armv7-M region fields, without an implicit memory policy. Armv7-M words targeting the caller-selected RNR, not RBAR.VALID. Armv8-M fields; limit is the exact inclusive final byte. Armv8-M words retaining its separate MAIR indirection. Explicit NonSecure unprivileged address permission checks. Requested NonSecure unprivileged CPU access, never execute access. Check current NonSecure unprivileged access to a full span. Allocation-free permission observation only. Hardware CMSE checks both security attribution and NonSecure MPU rights as an unprivileged caller. Adjacent permitted regions may be conservatively rejected. No memory is copied, pinned or borrowed. Success neither authenticates a gateway caller nor proves RAM, DMA reachability, lifetime or fault safety. The integrating owner must exclude MMIO through its SoC RAM policy and prevent attribution/mapping changes through the subsequent access. Interrupts, another world, DMA and faults are not excluded here; no masks or hardware policy are changed. A gateway must separately snapshot its input and validate output before any irreversible operation. Local security-state and explicit SAU region mechanisms. Query this image\u2019s compiled security state. Bounded read-only Task/IRQ query, no hardware access. This does not grant access to another world\u2019s memory or devices. Encode an explicit SAU region without hardware access. Pure Task/IRQ operation. Encoding marks the requested region enabled but does not enable SAU hardware. External policy owns overlap, IDAU restrictions and NSC veneers. Program one owned SAU region while the SAU is disabled. Secure privileged Thread with incoming PRIMASK set only. Caller excludes all other region/selector users and keeps NMI/HardFault outside this metadata. Requires reviewed SAU region support. Does not modify CTRL, ALLNS, another region, another security world\u2019s registers or enable the SAU. Current RNR is restored; DSB/ISB complete region writes. Policy, secure gateways and IDAU configuration stay external. Disable one owned SAU region while the SAU is disabled. Same context and ownership contract as nx_arch_sau_write. Preserves CTRL, ALLNS, other regions and incoming RNR. Exact 32-byte-aligned SAU region owned by the Secure caller. Validated SAU words; encoding never changes hardware. Reviewed single-CPU shallow sle", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Arch: CPU mechanisms and feature contracts", "type": "reference"}, "reference/api/components.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "These declarations come from public header files. Selection, lifecycle, supported execution modes and physical authorization remain bounded by the corresponding design and support records. BMP280 calibration and explicit forced-conversion driver. Author Nexus Team Parse the exact 24-byte little-endian calibration block. Compensate one raw sample using checked integer arithmetic. Validate chip ID and load calibration, without scanning. Task context; failure leaves initialized false and no borrow. Does not soft-reset the part or create a wait loop/task. Retry BUSY explicitly. Start one forced temperature/pressure conversion. Task-only serialized driver owner; oversampling x1/x1. Poll conversion without an implicit worker or blocking delay. Task-only. Every port call releases temporary buffers before return. BUSY retains device conversion state, never stack or caller buffers. After terminal failure, begin may retry; the external owner chooses policy. Stop software use after all synchronous calls have exited. Does not power off or reset a physical part. Sensor-internal conversion can finish without accessing MCU storage. No MCU borrow remains. Synchronous settled SPI transaction port. exchange always releases every TX/RX buffer before returning, including TIMEOUT/error. One CS interval covers all bytes; mode 0 or 3, 8-bit words, \u003c=10 MHz. Caller serializes this device and its bus owner. Driver stack transactions never cross asynchronous borrowing boundaries. Register calibration, owned by one device instance. Output is valid only on successful conversion/compensation. Explicit device context, no Board pins or automatic sampling task. Optional BMP280 adapter over a fixed SPI endpoint. Bind a real polling SPI endpoint as settled transport. Explicit bounded multi-producer, single-executor adapter. Initialize unused owner/slot storage. Startup context; no task, worker, timer or heap is created. Establish the chain\u2019s unique admission. Task multi-producer context. Free-slot admission and FIFO insertion use constant metadata work under the guard. An exhausted epoch permanently retires its slot. Deadline includes queue residence. Caller must not modify request, operation or buffers after admission. A notification error after admission cannot change SUCCESS to rejection; executor must recheck/pump periodically. Submit cancellation through stable adapter storage. Task multi-producer context. No request pointer is accepted. Queued cancellation settles without starting. Active cancellation drains cooperatively; an irreversible operation may already have transferred data. Advance at most one bounded execution step. Task/explicit loop only. Consumers never service through wait. Keep calling during stop until idle; no callback creates a worker. Close admission and request every accepted operation to stop. Task context. Executor must keep servicing/draining while producers wait/join. Stop is not settlement or storage reclamation. Test whether execution and completion publication have exited. Join producers and any wake publishers before reclaiming owner, slots, guard or notifications. No future submit/cancel is permitted. Explicit bounded metadata exclusion port. Task producer calls only. enter/leave must serialize all producers and the executor. Never hold this guard over start/service/cancel, waiting, payload copies or notification callbacks. State restores incoming interrupt mask when an architecture guard is used. Port storage stays live. One real execution owner, injected without OS dependency. start SUCCESS borrows operation until service SUCCESS. BUSY or any start error retains zero operation references/hardware effects; an error after partial start must instead accept and report through service. service SUCCESS proves all hardware/IRQ/deferred accesses stopped, writes final result/count, and releases the operation. BUSY still borrows. Other service errors retain ownership and require continued recovery/service. cancel requests drain; it never by itself releases operation. Callbacks run only in the externally scheduled executor, outside adapter metadata guard. terminal_authority selects providers that observe deadlines and latch their first terminal hardware fact themselves. Their final result survives late cancellation/publication. Other executors use adapter deadline/cancellation results. Queued deadlines always belong to the adapter. Stable control identity; never dereference a stale request pointer. Exact caller-owned slot, linked in either the free or FIFO chain; initialize only with owner init. Explicit adapter state; external application supplies all storage. Explicit clock consumers and epoch-bound drain control. Author Nexus Team Version 1.0.0 Date 2026-10-11 Copyright Copyright (c) 2026 Nexus Team Opaque provider, statically supplied by its privileged owner. One stable caller-owned consumer token. Zero-initialize before first acquisition. On SUCCESS the provider borrows this exact object; do not copy, mutate or reclaim it until release succeeds. Internal fields are not application controls. A release is the provider\u2019s final access, not proof that a peripheral has stopped. Release only after that consumer\u2019s IRQ, DMA and observer drain. Only shallow sleep preserves the established clock/time domain. Values: Acquire one admitted clock consumer without waiting. Privileged Thread or configurable IRQ only; same CPU, saved-mask metadata guard. NMI/HardFault/SMP excluded. Capacity bounds token/alias scans. Token remains borrowed until successful release, even while admission is closed. Remove the exact retained token after consumer drain. Same contexts as acquire. Copied tokens cannot release the original. There is no callback or last access after SUCCESS. Close admission and return the current epoch control. Serialized privileged Thread only. No waiting, cancellation, clock writes or implicit consumer shutdown. Idempotent in the same closed epoch; output must not alias live storage. Validate closed admission and zero retained consumers. Same CPU privileged Thread. This certifies only explicitly enrolled consumers. It does not authorize PLL reprogramming, suspend kernel Tick or change the Core deadline domain. Reopen a drained domain under a new admission epoch. Serialized privileged Thread. Does not restart the clock or reinterpret existing deadlines. Actual cold clock changes additionally require all Tick/time providers stopped and a new explicit deadline domain; this API performs neither. Epoch-bound close control and quiescence evidence. A value copy is allowed. This is not an authorization token or proof about unregistered consumers. Domain storage lives longer than every copy. A successful reopen invalidates all prior controls without resetting the epoch. Explicit DP83848 identity, reset and observed link mechanism. Read exact supported identity; mismatch never becomes a default PHY. Bounded BMCR soft reset and observed self-clearing acknowledgement. Task-only finite absolute deadline; reset restraps the device and clears configured truth. This is not a GPIO, power or strap proof. Start explicit negotiation or fixed mode, never wait for fake link. Task-only; identity must be proved. SUCCESS means registers configured, not autonegotiation complete. Failures retain configured=false. Observe latch-low BMSR twice and DP83848 PHYSTS speed/duplex truth. Task-only; output changes only on SUCCESS. up requires actual link and complete autonegotiation when selected. Protocol or remote fault does not invent speed/link. MAC application remains an external action. External policy explicitly selects autonegotiation or fixed mode. Caller-owned PHY state; no board address, reset GPIO or worker default. expected_id selects TI or NS model, ignoring revision nibble only. One task owner serializes this component and the shared MDIO port. Native persistent file Flash model with fault injection. Author Nexus Team Version 1.0.0 Date 2026-10-09 Copyright Copyright (c) 2026 Nexus Team Open an exclusively locked persistent host Flash model. Task-only POSIX model. Does not qualify MCU Flash or actual power loss; existing file size mismatch is rejected without truncation. Close the host file after all store users have quiesced. No future port calls are permitted until explicit reopen. Set an explicit model interruption boundary. Zero simulates power-off until explicitly cleared. Explicit physical Flash-region adapter without global registry. Bind an explicit writable uniform-erase physical region. Task-only, serialized caller. No region is reserved by the platform. Mixed sector geometries require another explicit storage adapter; this one rejects them. IO pulse settlement guarantees sync durability; no caller buffer survives a port call. Physical power-loss remains unqualified. Set the next transaction\u2019s one absolute mutation deadline. Caller serializes and sets this before open/save/recovery. Per-pulse timeouts cannot preempt hardware pulses; IO settles before return. Caller-owned adapter, no dynamic binding or global token lookup. Instance-local synchronous bounded log sink. Structured severity, with filtering before touching a sink. Initialize an unused logger before exposing it to tasks. Write caller bytes, with no implicit allocation/formatting. Task multi-producer entry with nonblocking serialization. M0/M0+ use CPU-local saved-PRIMASK metadata exclusion and require privileged callers on one CPU. Sink calls run after restoring the incoming mask. This does not make NMI/HardFault or cross-security-domain writers valid. IRQ use is prohibited. Drop/retry policy remains with the external caller. Flush an explicit sink without creating a background worker. Same task/privilege/CPU domain as write. Reject new writes and test whether the sink has exited. Task context. A stop caller may retry; no active sink call is forcibly cancelled. Join publishers before reclaiming logger/sink storage. Synchronous caller-injected sink. write/flush release every byte buffer before returning even on timeout/error; asynchronous/DMA sinks require their own settled adapter. Calls are bounded by absolute monotonic deadlines, in task context. No sink discovery, allocation, formatting, worker or global error handler exists. Logger storage belongs to its caller; shared immutable port is copied. Bounded Modbus RTU core and explicit RS485 ports. Compute standard Modbus CRC16 over caller bytes. Initialize an unused or fully quiesced protocol instance. Task-only; configuration is copied, contexts stay live. First direction(false) failure is reported; no worker or UART is created. Reinitializing a faulted TX borrow is prohibited; stop/recover it first. Feed an externally timestamped UART byte. Task-only. IRQ queues are external, bounded and explicit; queue overflow must call rx_error. No live UART discovery or ISR callback. Service RTU silence and bounded synchronous RS485 response. Task-only, explicit external scheduling. RS485 drain must observe the final stop bit. Failed abort retains TX bytes and asserted DE in LINK_FAULTED; keep instance/port alive until successful recover or stop. Discard an errored or overrun receive frame. Task-only. Does not discard a retained faulted TX borrow. Settle retained TX then restore receive silence acquisition. Explicit task maintenance action, no fabricated recovery. Settle the port before reclaiming protocol storage. Stop future feed/poll users first. No future use except explicit initialization is allowed after successful stop. Explicit owner progress diagnostics without default state. Bind an initialized exact owner without creating work. Execute exactly one real service step and copy diagnostics. Clock reversal never blocks drain or rewrites its result. Snapshot uses the existing guard outside hardware service. No callback, slot scan or worker occurs under that guard. Quarantine and elapsed deadlines never release a loan. Output also excludes accepted descriptors and payloads. Only the owner", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Optional common components", "type": "reference"}, "reference/api/core.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "These declarations come from public header files. Selection, lifecycle, supported execution modes and physical authorization remain bounded by the corresponding design and support records. Bounded observation before retaining platform fail-stop. Author Nexus Team Fault origin, without any product safety or recovery decision. Values: Observe a terminal fault before the caller retains effects. The weak default returns and has no state or side effects. A product may supply one strong implementation, bounded and allocation-free in every calling task/ISR/masked context. No functioning kernel, clock, stack, transport or peripheral may be assumed after corruption. This is best-effort observation, not stack repair, safe outputs or reset proof. The hook must not acquire OS locks, reenter the provider, change incoming masks, mutate accepted requests or release retained storage. If it returns, the terminal caller still parks. A product may perform a separately reviewed non-returning recovery action. No retained event, registry or worker is implicit. Variable-only placement in explicitly selected data regions. Place static storage in an authored optional memory region. Use these attributes on variable definitions, before the type, one variable per declaration. They are not function or instruction attributes. Each use has an independent input section for garbage collection of unused storage. DATA copies its initializer from Flash at reset. BSS is zeroed at reset. NOINIT is neither copied nor cleared; initialize it before reading. Power-loss retention is not promised. BSS/NOINIT reject nonzero initializers. Storage is word aligned and subject to explicit ELF budgets. Unselected use is a compile error. TCM is CPU-only data: never DMA or executable storage. CCM is also CPU-only data; placement never grants bus-master access. ADDSRAM DMA requires provider address, alignment and ownership validation. Ordinary storage, the main MSP and unselected images keep their existing layout. Caller-owned admission, borrow, settlement and stable cancellation. Request state; only READY and SETTLED have no provider borrow. Initialize fresh unborrowed request storage. Task-only. Never use initialization to revoke a live borrow. Prepare a READY or consumed SETTLED request. Single owner, task-only; failure creates no borrow. Read request state with acquire ordering. Task/IRQ bounded; SETTLED guarantees provider\u2019s final access has occurred, not that separate adapter observers exited. Establish the chain\u2019s unique admission and provider borrow. One serialized execution owner; validate hardware/buffers first. Multi-producer adapters hold their metadata guard across admission; concurrent admissions are unsupported. Advance an already admitted request without readmission. Execution owner; short task/IRQ metadata operation. Release-publish SETTLED as the final provider access. Detach active/queued references and capture any static wake target first. No hardware/IRQ/deferred observer may access request or payload afterwards. Cancel/timeout alone do not satisfy this requirement. A failed drain stays QUARANTINED. Copy the unique settled result after acquire publication. Bind a stable slot to a new request incarnation. Resolve cancellation using stable storage before dereference. Drop a slot reference only after settlement and control drain. Caller-owned request metadata, without payload or callbacks. Fields are private to helpers/providers while borrowed. Initialize before first use. One owner prepares/submits and consumes results. GCC/Clang lock-free 32-bit atomic access publishes state; fields are read only after acquire SETTLED. Adapter-owned stable cancellation identity. The slot, not a possibly freed request, is targeted by cross- task commands. Serialize bind/lookup/release in the adapter. Keep slot and command storage until command acknowledgement; epoch exhaustion rejects rebinding rather than reusing IDs. Optional stable-identity request observation without locks. Bind the exact live incarnation without storing its pointer. Copy state and acquire-observed final result for exact epoch. Borrowed state exposes no speculative result/count. NULL slot, stale epoch and overlap reject before request access. This neither services hardware nor interprets deadline as borrow release. Caller guarantees binding storage lifetime. Output is disjoint from the complete descriptor and borrowed buffers; only the common request span is checked here. Remove only this sidecar reference, leaving loans untouched. Caller-serialized; allowed while request is quarantined. It is not request settlement, slot release or destruction. Copied facts; result/count are valid only when settled is true. Caller-owned optional sidecar, fresh zero storage before bind. One serialized observer; no Arch/OS/SoC dependency or lock. Caller excludes slot bind/release, request prepare/reuse and concurrent observer calls during sample. Stable slot stays alive until detach; observing never acquires/releases a loan. Bounded read-only observation of caller-painted stack spans. Read a bounded low-address paint prefix without mutation. Caller ensures this region is quiescent and painting had no live frames. No Arch/OS dependency or internal mask occurs. Work is at most bytes reads. Unwritten reservations or writes equal to paint can hide usage; this is not overflow detection. MSP qualification needs independently established boot paint and a quiescent sampling point. The span excludes current SP, all active frames and exception-stack reservation. It never paints or claims to read the physical MSP register. Byte-prefix paint observations, not an exact physical stack peak. Small vendor-independent result vocabulary. Operation result; success at submission means admission only. Absolute monotonic microsecond deadlines and wrap extension. Microseconds in one boot/clock domain; no wall-clock semantics. Read the selected provider\u2019s monotonic clock. Physical providers require successful platform startup and a privileged task/configurable IRQ; NMI/HardFault callers are unsupported. No OS, allocation or wait. Physical clocks return zero outside a successfully started live epoch; zero alone cannot prove readiness. Startup status is authoritative. All consumers join before clock withdrawal. Stop/restart or a clock change invalidates every old deadline, even if a new epoch has the same numerical value. UINT64_MAX is reserved; clock exhaustion requires controlled restart. An observed hardware overflow acknowledgement failure ends the live epoch and is terminal, even when finite source isolation succeeds. The provider retains ownership, masks interrupts, calls nx_fault_observe and parks if the observer returns. It never resumes deadline consumers with invalid zero time. Observation neither releases loans nor proves safe outputs; products own separately reviewed non-returning recovery. Ordinary shutdown still requires every consumer and optional low-power owner joined first. Test an absolute deadline against a clock snapshot. Add a duration without wrapping the monotonic domain. Initialize counter extension and its clock domain. Single execution owner. Reset creates a new clock domain; do not reuse outstanding deadlines after reset/clock change. Extend a wrapping counter and convert to microseconds. Observe at least once per 2^32 ticks, even when idle. Multiple missed wraps cannot be detected from two snapshots. Counter must continue through waits; frequency cannot change. An explicitly injected clock; its context outlives all borrowers. State for extending one 32-bit free-running hardware counter.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Core: requests, status and time", "type": "reference"}, "reference/api/index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Doxygen reads only current public header trees. The module pages render real declarations through Breathe; vendor SDKs, mutable provider storage, tests and historical APIs are excluded. Generated nexus_factory.h belongs to one resolved assembly and is described in the factory explanation.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Public C API", "type": "reference"}, "reference/api/io.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "These declarations come from public header files. Selection, lifecycle, supported execution modes and physical authorization remain bounded by the corresponding design and support records. Reviewed ADC single-shot and low-rate polling scans. Author Nexus Team Query the fixed reviewed ADC sequence and raw-count format. Read fixed channel order and complete-scan timing facts. Attach optional observer storage before acquisition starts. Stop and join before detach/reclaim. No hidden allocation; polling and finite modes need no observation storage. Query one live consumer loan\u2019s software completion record. Sample the fixed single/scan sequence by bounded polling. CPU-active task-only acquisition. Reference voltage, sampling error and maximum rate need physical qualification. Start selected trigger/block acquisition with caller storage. Blocks contain native uint16_t raw counts in channel order. Capacities and lengths are multiples of a complete scan; DMA domains/alignment and physical rate require separate evidence. Stop trigger and memory sources without revoking consumer loans. Consumer READY/BORROWED blocks must still be drained/released. Explicitly resume discrete block acquisition after consumer drain. No task, retry or hidden rearm is created. Block modes state their trigger gaps; continuous zero-gap capture requires a separately declared hardware capability and qualification. Attach an optional hint sink after checking publisher IRQ priority. Block/error predicates latch before notifications. Stop and join publishers and waiters before reclaiming old sink storage. Raw count facts; nominal Vref is not a calibrated physical measurement. Immutable complete-scan timing facts for the selected mode. sample_times are codes into sample_cycles, in channel order. scan_cycles includes each channel\u2019s sample and conversion. Clock declarations and cycle counts describe scheduling; they do not establish electrical accuracy or sample aperture timestamps. Arrays remain valid through provider lifetime. Shared read-only methods using one provider state. Methods follow each public operation\u2019s ownership contract. Missing optional methods report UNSUPPORTED. Immutable interface pointing to caller-owned state. Face and state outlive callers, IRQs and retained borrows. Factory lookup neither initializes nor acquires hardware. Optional caller-owned ADC publication observations. Initialize fresh unborrowed optional observation storage. Provider-only cold bind validates every metadata/payload span before the first DMA loan. Provider-only record before matching release-publication. Read only a currently acquired block\u2019s exact epoch record. Provider-only unbind after all producer/consumer loans join. One loan\u2019s software observation, never a sample aperture. observed_at uses the platform monotonic clock after target detach readback. IRQ delivery and publication latency are included. sequence counts observed valid block records, not timer triggers, samples or guessed missed scans. Optional caller-owned observer control and exact records. Initialize before attaching; internal fields belong to one producer. Records and control remain live through successful provider stop and all consumer releases. No default stream, slot or finite provider storage is enlarged. Optional fixed complementary PWM with latched break. Stage duty against the fixed declared PSC/ARR and complementary pair. Shadow duty becomes effective at the next update. Hardware deadtime encoding must exactly represent the cold-configured tDTS count; PSC does not scale deadtime. This API does not certify pin timing. Enable outputs only with inactive break and no latched fault. Automatic output reenabling and irreversible hardware LOCK are prohibited. A break never silently starts outputs again. Disable/read back master output and both channel sources. Cold assembly keeps the reviewed inactive-pin and shared-vector leases until success and IRQ quiescence. A latched break survives stop. Query staged duty, actual enable state and latched break facts. Clear a software fault only while disabled and BKIN is inactive. Physical input and pending hardware break are rechecked. Application recovery policy and external power safety remain outside Nexus. Counts use the fixed post-prescaler clock; deadtime uses tDTS. Shared immutable controls; configuration is a cold assembly fact. Immutable identity; controller state and configuration outlive IRQs. Classic CAN value frames and caller-owned mailbox requests. Values: Validate and copy a classic frame into unborrowed request storage. Task owner only; failure retains no reference and changes no frame. Admit one frame into one of three hardware mailboxes. Serialized privileged task owner, incoming IRQ masks clear. REJECTED retains nothing. TXOK plus completed/empty hardware proves success. Request abort without revoking a borrowed request. Abort may race real transmission; observed TXOK remains SUCCESS. Failed abort remains QUARANTINED and must keep storage alive. Process latched completions and absolute timeout/drain deadlines. Bounded three-mailbox/six-frame work, no worker, blocking or replay. Consume value events without overwriting earlier unconsumed facts. Each bounded record copy has a short metadata exclusion; pending loss is ordered after older records and before future frames. Copy authoritative error/admission state, even when RX is full. Task-only, bounded; bus-off is latched until explicit recovery. Close admission, abort TX and detach IRQ/RX publishers. Never resets the shared primary controller to release a peer\u2019s loan. Retry after progress. Success ends provider access to RX/wake storage. Explicitly reenter initialization after bus-off and complete drain. No automatic recovery or retransmission; domain/filter identity stays. Borrow an optional wake target after checking all four actual IRQs. No implicit OS policy; NULL detaches. Wake is only a hint. The sink must support both task service/cancel and configurable IRQ contexts. Classic 2.0A/B value frame; DLC is always 0..8, never CAN FD. Caller-owned frame copied at prepare; base must first be initialized. Value receive fact or ordered loss boundary. Timestamp is IRQ acquisition/report time, not hardware SOF. Hardware overrun proves only a lower bound. Raw DLC 9..15 is normalized to 8. Latched controller state, independent of receive-ring fullness. Shared immutable operations; execution owner serializes task methods. Immutable face; lookup does not allocate, start or acquire hardware. Explicit DMA memory domains, physical streams and drain facts. Transfer direction determines device access to caller memory. Validate an entire buffer within one explicit memory domain. Bounded task/IRQ check, no MMIO, allocation or side effects. Reject duplicate physical streams in an assembled resource set. Cold conflict check; the selected SoC must validate all identities against its actual topology before using them. This helper does not infer silicon support or allocate. Require engine, IRQ and peripheral drain independently. The provider must observe actual registers and ordering before setting these facts. This helper does not stop hardware. Reviewed DMA-visible memory interval, never CPU accessibility. begin plus size must not wrap. Cache maintenance remains a provider responsibility on cached architectures. Opaque physical engine and request selector identities. Controller zero is reserved as invalid. Other values have provider-defined meanings; the SoC validates its topology. Two selectors on the same controller/stream conflict. Independent facts required before memory borrow release. remaining describes DMA memory transfers, not wire bytes. A zero counter never establishes peripheral_idle. Caller-owned untagged Ethernet II frames and receive loans. Prepare an initialized, unborrowed request and its immutable frame. Task owner. Ethernet II only, 14..1514 bytes excluding FCS; no VLAN or length-coded 802.3. Failure changes no request or payload. Admit one frame into caller-owned hardware descriptors. Privileged serialized task, masks clear. SUCCESS is ACCEPTED. Borrow includes request and frame until acquire-observed SETTLED. Completion reports MAC wire status, never delivery to another application. Close TX admission and drain the shared TX engine without replay. Task owner. Cancel is controller-wide: other pending TX requests also become CANCELLED. It never revokes a borrow. Stuck sources quarantine. Apply a confirmed link again after successful drain to reopen TX. Task-owner bounded progress; no worker, lock, pool or wait. Task-consumer complete-frame loan; EMPTY creates no borrow. Final consumer access; stale, foreign and double releases reject. A held descriptor is never rearmed, even at ring wrap or stop. Copy latched instance/link/error facts; output is not retained. Apply external PHY observations, not auto-negotiate or invent link. Task owner; changing speed/duplex first requires explicit link-down and observed idle TX/RX engines and FIFOs. BUSY keeps the old mode. Reapplying an unchanged up fact is idempotent. Link-down closes admission and drains pending TX with IO; later up requires real quiescence. This is not a PHY power/reset/electrical proof. Close admission and withdraw MAC/DMA/IRQ sources with readback. Task owner. Closing rejects new RX loans; existing loans still release. BUSY retains hardware/storage/wake ownership; timeout quarantines pending TX. Held RX loans remain valid and must be released before SUCCESS. Caller joins IRQ/consumers before reclaiming storage. Bind an optional borrowed hint after actual IRQ-policy validation. Task owner with no pending TX; notify is bounded and legal in IRQ. Service does not invoke the IRQ hint from a task. Detach after stop. Caller-owned immutable contiguous frame, excluding its hardware FCS. Observed PHY fact, supplied explicitly by the external link owner. Complete immutable frame; release requires this exact incarnation. data excludes FCS; storage stays live until matching release. Copying a token does not create a second loan. Timestamp is acquisition, not SOF. Latched facts survive receive backpressure and failed wake hints. Static EXTI line bindings, bounded event storage and drain. Read queued EXTI events from an exact static line binding. Disable the line source, clear pending and drain its handler. Failed withdrawal may already have disabled part of the source. Retry and observe success before reclaiming event or wake storage. Face and state remain live while a shared dispatch domain still refers to them. Attach or detach an explicit event/loss IRQ wake target. Actual shared-vector priority must satisfy the target. Stop and join publishers before freeing old target storage. Wake callbacks observe already-latched event or loss facts. Edge facts; timestamp precision and coalescing are provider-specific. LOSS is an independent boundary after older buffered facts and before later facts. Its edge is invalid; its timestamp is detection/report time, never a reconstructed missing edge. For COALESCED facts, edge may derive from sampled pin level after an IRQ and does not represent complete edge history. Full physical Flash geometry and explicit bounded regions. Query exact physical Flash geometry without side effects. Read bounded physical Flash without retaining destination. Program aligned physical Flash and settle all hardware pulses. Task-only CPU-active operation. Product owns execution-from- Flash stall, watchdog and power-loss policy. No RAM fake on MCU. Erase a whole physical sector range with pulse settlement. Validate region bounds against physical geometry. Read within an explicit physical region. Program within an explicitly writable physical region. Erase whole physical sectors within a writable region. Erase sector geometry, with offset relative to full physical Flash. Read-only physical geometry; platform reserves no product storage. Explicit consumer-selected bounds; no implicit pr", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Typed I/O: devices and execution contracts", "type": "reference"}, "reference/api/os.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "These declarations come from public header files. Selection, lifecycle, supported execution modes and physical authorization remain bounded by the corresponding design and support records. Bare-metal poll notification with caller-owned storage. Author Nexus Team Initialize storage before exposing it to any ISR. Startup context; not a task/queue emulation. Word publication uses the reviewed inline compiler/CPU port. Bind the explicit polling notification. wake uses compiler RMW or, on M0/M0+, a bounded saved PRIMASK guard. That guard requires one CPU and privileged Thread/configurable-IRQ callers; NMI/HardFault, unprivileged, DMA and other security-domain access to its metadata are unsupported. The owner loop continues to service controller/drain after BUSY. No WFI/WFE is inserted without a reviewed architecture-specific atomic sleep contract. Explicit nonblocking bare-metal notification storage. Bounded diagnostics with explicit caller-owned ring storage. Author Nexus Team Version 1.0.0 Date 2026-10-11 Copyright Copyright (c) 2026 Nexus Team Initialize unpublished ring and caller-owned entries. Startup or exclusive lifecycle owner only. Both allocations remain alive until all publishers and readers are stopped. Entries cannot alias ring state or a submitted record. Copy one event under a bounded saved interrupt mask. Privileged Thread/configurable IRQ on one CPU only. NMI/HardFault, unprivileged, SMP, DMA and other security worlds are excluded. Full rings retain older records and saturate the lost-record counter. No clock callback, OS operation, heap, string formatting or transport is used. Consume one event from caller-owned ring. Same privileged single-CPU domain as write. Failed reads leave destination unchanged. This is diagnostic storage, never a completion queue or application work queue. Snapshot the saturating overflow count. Uses the same bounded saved-mask domain as record access. One opaque diagnostic record; interpretation belongs to its owner. Explicit ring state, with no default pool, worker or transport. Per-object static FreeRTOS storage and optional thin services. Running task operations require privileged unmasked Thread mode; IRQ wake has its own contract. Before scheduler start, the selected Baseline port may retain PRIMASK=1 or the Mainline port its exact syscall BASEPRI; all other masks must be zero. Matching a value cannot prove mask provenance. Unsafe contexts return CONTEXT before mutating kernel APIs or object storage; a scheduler-state read may be needed. Startup permits init, task creation, wake and zero-wait queues. Wait, join and nonzero waits require a running scheduler. Port binding and sequence snapshots remain reads. MPU user-task gateways and cross-world secure task calls are outside these privileged, current-security-domain APIs. Caller-owned single-operation latch for a closable queue. Test whether the current context may call kernel ISR APIs. Native POSIX has no physical IRQ and returns false. NMI, faults and kernel-owned exceptions are rejected before entering FreeRTOS. Mainline priority/grouping must respect the syscall ceiling. Baseline PRIMASK ports accept every configurable IRQ and do not read Mainline priority grouping. Nonsecure permission does not exclude Secure-side accesses. Initialize unused notification storage. Task/startup context; no heap or timer daemon is needed. Bind a latched single-waiter notification. Task wake uses xSemaphoreGive. IRQ wake validates priority then uses xSemaphoreGiveFromISR. Saturated latch is harmless; sequence and request predicates remain authoritative. No deferred daemon publishes wakes. Destroy after publishers and waiter have stopped and joined. Task context. Quiescence is required beyond this busy check. Create one task from its exact caller-owned stack and TCB. Task/startup context. No hidden worker or maximum pool. Entry must return in privileged Thread mode, with manual interrupt masks restored and the scheduler resumed after any temporary suspension. Discard one created task before its wrapper has executed. Single privileged startup lifecycle owner; scheduler state must be NOT_STARTED. The kernel candidate is first suspended to remove its retained TCB reference before deletion. No unmatched vTaskSuspendAll may remain during bootstrap: NOT_STARTED cannot reveal the kernel\u2019s private suspension nesting. This caller precondition applies before mutation. Success permits TCB, stack and context reuse after all external users are quiescent. This does not cancel or join an executed task. Join a cooperatively returned task and reclaim kernel state. Single joiner, task context. The entry return parks its wrapper; no self-delete/idle cleanup race releases caller storage early. Stop producers, keep bus owner draining, join producers, then join owner. Create an exact-sized static byte-copy queue. Copy an item with a bounded relative kernel wait. Task context. Admission/stop belongs to the application\u2019s explicit owner adapter, not this generic copy queue. Receive one byte-copy item. Destroy after admission is stopped and all users joined. Send using one absolute monotonic deadline. Immediate readiness wins over expiration. Stop admission and user quiescence remain external raw-queue obligations. Receive using one absolute monotonic deadline. Bind an exclusive slot of one live receiver task. Caller ensures slot exclusivity. No global slot registry, allocation, slot discovery or cross-world synchronization. Get a direct notification wait port. Only receiver may wait. Task/validated IRQ publishers use the exclusive slot; notification remains a predicate hint. Unbind after all publishers stop and join. Does not delete receiver or clear its kernel slot. Receiver and storage must outlive every retained port and publisher. Start a task whose entry must never return. No join latch, destroy or forced deletion. Choose joinable tasks for cooperative shutdown and reclaimable storage. Initialize one exact caller-owned queue waiter. Destroy only after all operations stop and join. Initialize an optional reliably closable byte-copy queue. No hidden worker or waiter pool. Each concurrent blocking operation supplies its own initialized waiter. Broadcasting pauses scheduling while visiting at most maximum_waiters latches. Kernel payload copies and each latch signal have their own short IRQ guards. Budget scheduling delay and IRQ latency separately. Large payloads should queue stable handles. The registry is single-core and task-only. Send with close-aware admission and absolute deadline. Readiness/admission and close serialize in a kernel task scheduling guard. A latched wake closes wake-before-sleep. Receive existing items after close, then CANCELLED. Stop sends and wake every currently registered waiter. Task-only, no forced cancellation or task deletion. Close does not join users or authorize storage reclamation. Destroy a closed, drained and quiesced queue. Stop/join producers, drain with owner, join consumers and then destroy queue and every waiter independently. Static binary latch; one waiter, independently quiesced publishers. Task has its own TCB; stack is supplied with its actual word count. Control fields are adapter-owned. The caller zero-initializes storage before first use and does not modify live fields. Entry is consumed as the wrapper begins execution; context remains borrowed until successful join or prestart discard. Queue control only; actual depth/item byte storage is external. Fixed receiver and exclusive kernel notification slot; no latch. Permanent task control without a join completion semaphore. Opt-in close broadcasts; raw queue storage remains unchanged. Bounded explicit FreeRTOS trace sink without global storage. Maintained kernel trace identities; product interpretation is external. Values: Consume one bounded kernel trace event. Application may override the weak no-op and write an explicit nx_diagnostic_ring_t. May run under kernel masks or configurable IRQ, so never block, allocate, format, transmit, acquire OS locks or call kernel services. The sink owns timestamp and lifetime; no buffer is provided automatically. Trace profile enables real kernel hooks. Privileged static lifecycle for isolated MPU user tasks. User data is nonexecuting normal RAM with only these permissions. Create an unprivileged task from exact caller-owned storage. Privileged Thread only, scheduler not suspended, no manual masks. Rejection retains no references and calls no kernel creation API. Actual linker bounds and MPU_TYPE are checked. Minimum creation capacity is 64 words; applications budget actual execution depth separately. No heap or object pool. Delete another isolated task after external users quiesce. One privileged lifecycle owner stops external access first. Single-core kernel deletion removes scheduler references synchronously. Self deletion is rejected; no hidden reaper. A caller-selected exact region, copied during task creation. Complete caller-owned protected task metadata. Zero-initialize in privileged RAM. The TCB includes the real port\u2019s context and syscall stack, inaccessible to users. One privileged lifecycle owner serializes start/delete. Immutable creation inputs, borrowed for this call only. The entry must be explicit user code and must not return. Stack and context data remain borrowed until owner deletion; regions never include kernel RAM, code or MMIO. Context must be contained in the user stack, user flash or mapped data. Names terminate within configMAX_TASK_NAME_LEN in a readable reserved linker region; names are copied cold. Optional bounded task stack paint snapshot. Copy bounded stack paint facts using actual kernel identity. Privileged unmasked running Thread; task stack quiescent throughout the scan. Available only with NEXUS_OS_TRACE. xTaskGetStaticBuffers verifies TCB/stack identity, not size. The original capacity is a caller contract. No task suspend, interrupt mask, allocation or live painting is performed. Worst-case work is words*sizeof(StackType_t) byte reads. Paint is the locked kernel\u2019s 0xA5. Untouched/touched bytes do not prove physical stack peaks. The POSIX kernel port uses a separate pthread execution stack; its query is not an MCU or Linux execution-stack peak measurement. Exact static task facts supplied at original creation. The caller retains all storage, excludes delete/join/discard and never replaces the live binding. This is not a registry. Narrow pointer-free services for MPU-isolated user tasks. Explicitly reviewed unprivileged executable code section. Explicit user RAM, separate from kernel and provider state. Trusted static task metadata placed in protected kernel RAM. Delay the calling user task through the real MPU SVC. Thread only with a running scheduler. No handle or pointer crosses the boundary. This is not a microsecond deadline. Read the kernel\u2019s wrapping 32-bit tick counter through SVC. Thread-only MPU profile service, distinct from Core time. No object, memory, I/O or privilege-changing API exists. Explicit continuous-clock tickless timer port. Bounded late alarm catch-up; larger delays violate the timer port. Return the explicitly selected continuous timer port. A maintained SoC binding or application implementation overrides the weak NULL default. The port and context remain live throughout the scheduler; no runtime rebinding, hidden pool or worker is provided. Absence of a port never stops ticks or sleeps. Kernel tickless hook with measured phase preservation. Called only by Idle with scheduler suspended. Uses reviewed masked shallow WFI, exact saved PRIMASK restoration and bounded pended-tick catch-up. The configured tick rate must divide 1000000 exactly. The final kernel readiness check precedes tick pause; a rejected pause makes no changes. Default profiles do not call this hook. HIL must verify the concrete timer, clock continuity and actual wake latency. Stopped-tick epoch with the concrete port\u2019s declared precision. Caller-owned, imm", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "OS adapters: tasks, waits and explicit storage", "type": "reference"}, "reference/configuration/assembly.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The authored configuration authority is one schema-2 TOML assembly. SoC and Board facts remain maintained JSON; resolved.json is output, not an authored alternative. CMake owns software dependencies. Failed TOML resolution never falls back to Kconfig, cache variables, schema-1 JSON or old generated headers. Before running the resolver, prepare the fixed source and recursive dependencies. Resource checks cover actual pin/AF, controller, IRQ ceiling, shared timer/timebase, physical DMA controller/stream, clock, exact memory domain, mode and endpoint identity. A syntactically valid selector can still reject because that provider does not implement the route. The resolver never silently changes pins, modes or clock. Run from the repository root, using a new output you own: check and explain do not generate output. generate publishes one atomic owned bundle; failure invalidates its owned output without replacing unrelated data. Inspect resolved.json, input identities, generated nexus_config.h, bindings, factory, linker memory and budgets. Changing declared inputs requires a fresh resolution; separate Board/backend combinations use separate build roots. CPU-only Runtime has a separate authored runtime TOML using the shared CPU resolver. It emits Core/Arch/optional OS policy, no SoC/Board/startup/factory. Do not use it to claim a full device platform. The build contract, integration contract and production field model/schema own detailed accepted fields; editor completion does not establish hardware capability.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Assembly input and rejected selections", "type": "reference"}, "reference/configuration/index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Use one authored TOML assembly and maintained facts. Unknown keys, unsupported modes and stale inputs are rejected rather than repaired silently.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Configuration and tools", "type": "reference"}, "reference/configuration/tools.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Tools preserve actual process exits and input identities. A prepared plan is not execution evidence; a model pass is not physical qualification. Run maintained commands from the repository root with the selected tools on PATH. Use the fixed source checkout guide before executing these tools; a default-branch clone may not contain the documented entry. Use the SDK tutorial, assembly reference, TDD workflow and performance/HIL guide for complete tasks. Detailed flags come from each maintained CLI --help; do not maintain a second parser specification here. Fresh reports require nonzero actual executions and original logs. A missing tool, empty XML, skipped-only run, stale output or changed input cannot satisfy a gate. Do not filter or shard complete acceptance runs. Development subsets retain their limited scope and cannot close a full candidate exit.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Configuration, development and evidence tools", "type": "reference"}, "reference/documentation-delivery.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This page defines the delivery contract of tools/documentation/delivery.py. The same documentation must be readable online and offline and independently restorable from its archive, while preserving exact source and software-verification identities. Documentation delivery grants no new firmware qualification and establishes neither physical HIL, product release, safety certification nor long-term support. Delivery consumes the strictly built HTML root and the current Git source. The build root must contain: Local diagram sources, SVG, CSS, JavaScript, fonts, download instructions and other licenses are inventoried with the actual build output. Web resources use relative paths and cannot depend on an online CDN. Normal HTTP(S) reference links may remain; pages, scripts, styles and images needed for offline reading must already be in the package. The build record uses schema 1 and kind nexus_documentation_build. Its source contains actual observations from tools.evidence.identity.observe_git_source(), tools records observed tool versions, and languages is fixed to en and zh_CN. Delivery rejects dirty source, missing or mismatched pinned submodules, and stale builds that do not match the current source. The version comes from project(nexus VERSION ...) in the root CMakeLists.txt and is checked against dependencies/platform-compatibility.json by the existing strict policy loader. The manifest also records the policy digest, interface epoch and schema epochs; mismatched versions, duplicate JSON keys and invalid constants reject. A separate explicit release_id, such as 1.0.0-docs-g\u003ccommit\u003e, identifies the delivery. It permits only letters, digits, dots, underscores and hyphens, never a directory or URL. The manifest distinguishes three identities: documentation_source: the full build-time commit/tree and the scoped tree digest of documentation directories. code_source: the full commit/tree of the same checkout and the scoped tree digest of platform code directories. qualified_code: the commit/tree of previously verified code in the delivery index, together with the index-file digest. The first two may share a Git commit but cover different paths. The third references existing software evidence; a documentation commit cannot turn it into new firmware verification of the current HEAD. physical_status is fixed to not_executed. files in documentation-manifest.json lists only actual payload. Each entry contains a normalized relative path, byte count and SHA-256. The manifest, SHA256SUMS, archive itself and execution receipts are excluded to avoid digest self-reference. SHA256SUMS covers payload and manifest, but not itself. Delivery commands return manifest and archive digests, retained outside the package in execution evidence and remote publication records. Independent restoration receives the saved manifest digest to detect changes to the payload or manifest. Digest verification is not a signature: it cannot authenticate authors or defeat an attacker controlling every trust source. Build metadata must have status passed. failed, running, missing status and stale successful records cannot be packaged. Independent verification still rejects a failed build record even if someone manually recalculates the manifest and digests. Verification also checks: Completeness of manifest schema, source identity, languages, tool records and software qualification references. An exact file closure: missing or extra files, duplicate entries, digest mismatches and byte-count mismatches reject. Required entries and licenses, with pages, resources and anchors resolvable within the package. Normalized paths: absolute paths, directory escape, symlinks, tar hardlinks and duplicate members reject. Only regular archive files, with member count and total bytes within tool limits. package() verifies, copies and archives in an independent temporary directory before installing into a fresh output path. Output cannot overlap source or input payload; publication webroot cannot contain the input package or sit inside it. Reject before writing so bound input bytes remain untouched. Source and build payload are rechecked before and after the operation to reject concurrent changes. Identical verified inputs produce identical tar.gz bytes; archive order, timestamps, owners and modes are fixed. restore() verifies every archive member and the exact file closure before writing into a fresh independent directory. It does not use extractall(), overwrite an existing directory or leave a partially restored destination on failure. Restored HTML can be opened in a browser without the original Git checkout, Python virtual environment or network; executing verification requires the tool. The Python interface is below; the unified docs command provides the CLI entry: This is an interface fragment with paths explicitly supplied by the caller. Executable commands and actual acceptance results come from the unified docs CLI and this delivery\u2019s receipts. docs/reference/redirects.csv is the sole authored input for legacy URL migration. Each row names the old address, target page, source document and fixed revision, and chooses either a historical contract boundary or a location change of the same current contract. Never treat legacy HAL/OSAL/Kconfig APIs as current interfaces, or describe a location-only move as an API change. Keep old addresses in the ledger; subsequent changes check source, destination and actual bookmark behavior together. The formal bilingual build validates this CSV through install_alias_catalog() and generates packaged explanation pages plus documentation-aliases.json. The catalog is derived, records the original CSV digest and enters the manifest payload; do not author a separate alias configuration. Single-language development previews lack a complete catalog and cannot be formal bilingual packages. Historical test fixtures without a catalog remain verifiable but do not establish legacy URL acceptance. When an old URL collides with a current prose path, the generator preserves current prose in the version directory and derives canonical_collisions with the corresponding current source path/digest and HTML digest. Real current source and HTML must exist; handwritten markers cannot bypass consistency checks. The site root still explains the original CSV semantics. Both languages currently have such collisions for development/index.html and tutorials/index.html. Publication preserves immutable versions/\u003crelease_id\u003e/ and generates legacy bookmark explanation pages at the site root. en/index.html and zh_CN/index.html explicitly point to the current version; root api/index.html explains the legacy API contract and directs readers to its historical boundary, without automatic redirection or silently presenting current APIs. The current ledger contains 287 legacy addresses plus three language/API entries, yielding 290 verified root gateways. All links are relative, without scripts, meta refresh or external redirect services. Unknown fields or semantics, duplicate/noncanonical URLs, reserved namespaces, missing targets, alias chains, and inconsistent language or source semantics reject before writing. Unrelated existing pages, symlinks and parent-directory conflicts cannot be overwritten. Root documentation-gateways.json inventories only tool-generated explanation pages and entry digests; registered bytes are checked before republication, and external changes require explicit maintainer handling. This record lives outside the package and its own digest closure, is unsigned, cannot authenticate a publisher, and grants no board or HIL qualification. For the current published version, post_deploy() actually reads every root alias and all three language/API entries and checks exact bytes derived from the source catalog. Successful version-directory explanation pages do not replace root-bookmark acceptance. When checking an immutable historical version with stable verification explicitly disabled, the receipt truthfully records that root gateways were not checked. publish() installs the verified directory at \u003cwebroot\u003e/versions/\u003crelease_id\u003e/, then updates \u003cwebroot\u003e/stable/index.html. The stable English, Chinese and API entries use relative ../versions/.../ links without altering the selected version. When archive is supplied explicitly, the archive is stored outside the package at \u003cwebroot\u003e/downloads/\u003crelease_id\u003e.tar.gz. Stable displays the download link and archive digest; root entries and download indexes use relative links. An archive of the same name cannot be replaced with different bytes. If a release_id already exists, the tool verifies its manifest and entire payload. Repetition is allowed only for identical original bytes; the same identity with different bytes rejects. New content receives a new identity, never an overwritten historical version. Preview builds and publication permissions remain separate; previews do not automatically become stable, and documentation versions are not software-candidate state machines. Post-publication acceptance must access the actual site URL. post_deploy() reads the manifest, checksum file and every inventoried payload over HTTP(S), compares against externally saved digests, and verifies actual downloaded bytes and packaged links. By default it checks that stable selects this version; when an archive digest is supplied it also checks the download entry and the complete downloaded archive closure. Success writes an independent receipt recording each requested URL, final URL, status, bytes and digest. Local HTTP verification does not replace public-site acceptance. Before a new acceptance run, the tool marks its owned prior receipt as running. Failure writes failed; a prior verified cannot remain as a false current success. Unrelated files and symlinks cannot act as receipts. An immutable historical version need not be current stable. For historical verification only, set verify_stable=False explicitly; the receipt also records that stable was not checked and cannot establish acceptance of the latest publication entry. Remote publication retains the immutable documentation archive, archive and manifest digests, exact source identity and restoration receipt. The deliverer must actually upload, download again, verify and restore: configured upload is not completed long-term storage. After restoration into a second independent root, check language entries, API, diagrams and licenses, and record results against actual files. Documentation release notes may point to Release assets or an immutable Git commit on an independent assets branch. Availability depends on the channel that actually succeeded: a failed asset upload cannot be reported as a published asset. Git downloads must include the full commit and exact path/bytes/SHA-256; mutable branch names are discovery entries only. Tool or evidence archives exceeding the per-file limit are stored in ordered parts no larger than 32 MiB, with a full-archive digest. Restoration is established only after all parts are downloaded, actually reassembled, checked as a whole and verified member by member. Preserve original release commits and existing version directories; later evidence adds independent paths or commits instead of replacing same-identity archives. Retention cannot guarantee that a provider never deletes objects; maintainers still keep independent copies according to the download instructions. Software-tool regressions use stdlib unittest and cover dirty/stale source, missing entries, content tampering, directory escape, link members, same-identity conflicts, independent restoration, and actual HTTP success and tamper rejection. New regression executions do not inherit historical test counts. Without physical execution, physical status always remains not_executed.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Documentation packages, publication and independent restoration", "type": "reference"}, "reference/documentation-tools.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Use this reference to prepare one maintained documentation environment. The qualified build tuple is Linux x86_64 with CPython 3.12. The local preparation uses CPython 3.12.14; CI selects the same patch version. Documentation tool qualification does not grant firmware, hardware or product qualification. First prepare the fixed source and recursive dependencies. The manifest and tool lock must come from the same selected source revision. dependencies/docs-tools.lock.json owns archive identities, target constraints, direct requirements and license provenance. The complete Python dependency set is also expressed in dependencies/docs-python-linux-cp312.txt, using exact versions and SHA-256 hashes. docs/sphinx/requirements.txt references that file; it does not define another resolver input. The native closure includes its libc loader, C/C++ runtime libraries, Graphviz core/dot/Pango plugins, fontconfig and each library actually linked by those executables and renderers. Preparation executes the locked loader\u2019s --list operation and rejects paths outside the prefix. It removes unsupported renderers instead of discovering host GTK, GD or Ghostscript installations. This tool set supports the maintained DOT/SVG/Cairo diagram path, not every Graphviz renderer. Linux kernel, the CPython/venv bootstrap, dpkg-deb and a POSIX shell are explicit host prerequisites. Their observed values belong to the preparation receipt. This lock does not claim a reproducible host image or a bitwise identical kernel. Run from the repository root, with CPython 3.12 available: Preparation may download archives over HTTPS. It verifies the entire archive closure before creating the prefix. Wheel installation then uses --no-index, --find-links and --require-hashes; no transitive package is left for a fresh online resolution. Native packages are extracted locally without sudo or system package installation. For an independent installation from a previously prepared cache: The expected result is exit code zero and installation.json in the fresh prefix. It records the lock digest, observed tool versions, kernel, resolved ELF closure, archive digests, installed file digests and copied license digests. The installed bin/fc-match must resolve Noto Sans CJK SC from the locked font package. A missing archive, changed byte, failed command or mismatched font fails preparation and leaves no success prefix. An existing prefix is rejected. Choose a fresh path for an upgrade; do not silently mutate an installation already referenced by build evidence. Archive cache corruption also fails rather than being silently replaced by a download. Run the maintained CLI with the prepared Python and explicit tool prefix: install.environment(prefix) returns a verified subprocess environment with the locked tool executables first on PATH. install.diagnose(prefix) rejects a changed lock, changed installed file or changed license before reporting tool identity. Neither operation installs or downloads tools. Builds must generate fresh XML and HTML; a former successful output cannot rescue a failed command. The scripts/tools/docs.py, .sh, .ps1 and .bat entries only forward to this CLI. Legacy all, doxygen and sphinx targets all select the same complete strict build. Removed generation modifiers are rejected; wrappers cannot create a fallback Doxyfile or Sphinx configuration. Windows and macOS wrappers preserve CLI forwarding, while a formal build still requires the maintained Linux tuple. This optional diagnostic needs an existing Chromium and Playwright in addition to the documented Sphinx tuple. It never installs tools. Use a separate prepared browser test environment; the formal documentation build and default stdlib test suite do not import Playwright. Run from the repository root, supplying the existing browser executable: The helper builds a temporary English/Chinese C API fixture from the production theme. At 390 CSS pixels it checks long signatures, unchanged symbols and anchors, complete code and table content with internal scrolling, and an unclipped page. Missing tools or failed checks return nonzero. A successful receipt qualifies only that fixture and observed browser; full-site responsive acceptance and archive verification remain separate. The English and Chinese search pages use their own local Sphinx index and current-language metadata. The Chinese tokenizer has no external dictionary dependency and accepts Han character sequences and Latin identifiers. Separate keywords with whitespace for combined queries, such as \u501f\u7528 \u6392\u7a7a or \u501f\u7528 HAL. Results show the version, content type, source and actual language, and body excerpts. Search assets load only on the search page. Archive links and original qualification records retain their frozen source and language scope. docs-build.yml has only contents: read. It prepares tools explicitly, runs source checks, builds both languages, packages offline output and records observed job identity. A failed build has no successful documentation upload. The package producer writes outside the source checkout. CI then copies the verified result to fresh ignored upload staging, compares the archive digest and verifies the copied manifest and payload before the existing job-identity gate. The staging copy does not rebuild or replace the original package. docs-publish.yml runs only for the reviewed docs-delivery branch. Its read-only job verifies the prepared public bytes and publication receipt before uploading a Pages artifact. The separate deployment job alone has Pages and OIDC write permissions and uses the nexus-documentation environment. It never checks out or executes external pull-request source code. Deployment branch rules and actual remote execution remain independently observable repository state. Each native archive carries its Debian copyright files. The locked base-files archive supplies every referenced complete text in licenses/common-licenses/; preparation rejects a missing shared license reference. Wheel license files are copied from the verified wheel; the five upstream wheels which omit license text use checked-in texts extracted from their exact verified source distributions. The lock records source archive digest, member and extracted text digest. The Doxygen archive carries its complete license text. Preparation copies these texts into prefix/licenses/; packaging retains the applicable license payload. To upgrade, review upstream changes and licenses, resolve one new exact closure, update both lock and hash requirements, execute a fresh online preparation and an independent offline preparation, then run strict bilingual build, diagram freshness, package verification and restoration. Retain raw failures and results; an updated version string alone is insufficient acceptance evidence.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Documentation tools and preparation", "type": "reference"}, "reference/history.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Legacy HAL/OSAL/Kconfig pages describe old directories, interfaces, instance generation and ownership. They cannot govern current typed factories, static instances or borrowing lifetimes. This page gives old URLs an explicit version boundary; it never silently interprets old function names as current public APIs. Migration changes configuration, interface access, storage ownership and shutdown together, then revalidates the migrated source and assembly. Old parameters, object IDs and neighboring configuration results do not transfer to a new bundle. docs/archive/sphinx-before-next-generation/ and docs/archive/implementation/ preserve historical source and original numbering. They stay outside current default site search and establish no current capability or qualification. Select a specific Git commit to browse or download old source: Legacy Sphinx source: frozen documentation planning baseline 0111c8d3 Historical RF implementation source: the same frozen baseline This fixed revision identifies historical bytes only; it does not grant historical firmware qualification to that revision. Old locale PO files are historical translations, not reviewed current translations selected automatically by the language selector. For offline historical reading, obtain the fixed Git snapshot above. The current documentation package provides migration explanations and legacy URL aliases without adding every historical tutorial to current search or reinterpreting its contract. The URL migration table lists legacy RST paths, languages and target pages. Old en/ and zh_CN/ addresses remain separate. Public site root api/index.html is a historical API boundary entry directing readers to confirm the version before choosing migration guidance; it cannot silently mean the current API. Current public APIs live within an immutable version at the site-relative path versions/\u003crelease_id\u003e/api/index.html, or at api/index.html under an extracted offline package root. Always identify the enclosing version root when interpreting an address. Legacy HAL/OSAL/Kconfig paths with different semantics enter this page to establish their version boundary before migration. Equivalent entries from the preceding simplified Sphinx site can enter corresponding current pages. Aliases are local HTML with relative links, without JavaScript, CDN or external redirection services. The page migration and authority map records source pages, authority, reader tasks, actual site destinations and retention rules. Preserve original contract and result paths, linking originals or derived views rather than creating a second status or specification authority. Subsequent path changes update mappings, navigation, aliases and offline checks together.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Historical documentation and version migration", "type": "reference"}, "reference/index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Public headers define API contracts. Configuration and platform references connect the actual resolver and maintained records; a symbol in the API index does not establish Board authorization or physical qualification.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Reference", "type": "reference"}, "reference/platforms/index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Read the support scope and exact evidence identity separately. A documented capability is not a current-source software candidate or a physical result.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Platforms and qualification", "type": "reference"}, "reference/platforms/qualification.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "A document describes code; it does not qualify that code merely by being published. A result applies to the exact source, configuration, environment and artifacts that were actually executed. Current document source, described code source and qualified historical code source are separate identities. Separate Native, ARM, SDK, reproduction and physical evidence FIG-11 shows evidence inputs and their independent scope. No arrow turns a model or a workflow into physical measurements. The delivery index retains original results and limitations. The build-system result record binds qualified code cc6a065c; its document updates do not inherit those tests to a new HEAD. Earlier SoC extension evidence binds 98270512 independently. Read each linked JSON result and raw receipt for exact full source identity, selected scope, artifact hashes and failed attempts. Current physical MCU records remain not_executed: no connected Board station has established clock accuracy, IRQ/DMA timing, Cache behavior, context retention, electrical outputs, Flash power-loss or long-duration performance. Software-reference HIL fixtures cannot be edited into physical qualification. A new implementation must run its applicable gates with fresh nonempty reports. A new documentation-only build may reference old qualified code while explicitly showing that identity; it must not rewrite the old record or reuse test counts as current execution. See the performance/HIL guide and the build contract.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Source, software evidence and physical qualification", "type": "reference"}, "reference/platforms/support.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Support has five separate dimensions: chip fact, maintained provider/mode, reviewed Board route, executed software scope and physical station scope. The derived capability matrix owns mode/route/ fixture/source associations. The support record owns current detailed limits; this page explains how to select them. ThreadX currently maintains Native Linux GNU and M4 GNU profiles; it does not inherit the FreeRTOS Cortex-M, MPU, split-security or tickless matrix. Platform startup does not enter either kernel. The application owns scheduler startup. CPU primitive, runtime, SoC and physical qualification boundaries FIG-13 separates CPU software coverage from exact SoC integration and physical acceptance. FPU, DSP, Cache, MVE, SAU and IRQ width are explicit required facts; a CPU name never enables an optional capability automatically. Find the exact full part and Board package, including unknown physical facts. Inspect the selected controller route and mode in the capability matrix. Resolve the actual complete assembly; unsupported modes and conflicts reject. Check software evidence for its exact source/configuration, not a nearby tuple. Check an actual station record before claiming physical behavior. All maintained MCU physical HIL remains not executed in the current records. Default Board bindings do not cover every connector, transceiver or external part. Finite block modes include rearm gaps. Continuous modes need explicit replacement storage and service budgets; they do not promise losslessness under arbitrary load. Flash geometry is physical, while product partitions and update policy are external. Selected U575 synchronous Secure/NonSecure reference FIG-14 applies only to its declared TZEN=1 synchronous reference. It does not change the default TZEN=0 Platform configuration or establish physical isolation. Read qualification before treating any software result as a release.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Read exact platform support", "type": "reference"}, "sphinx/development/coding_standards.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Nexus follows strict coding standards for quality and safety. C11 for source code C11 for production code and maintained C contract fixtures C++17 for host GoogleTest/GoogleMock and external ABI consumer checks; neither the test framework nor its C++ runtime enters firmware The project uses clang-format for automatic code formatting. Run clang-format before committing code. See also: .kiro/steering/comment-standards.md for detailed comment guidelines. Use 4 spaces for indentation (no tabs) Indent case labels inside switch statements Do not indent preprocessor directives Maximum line length: 80 characters Break long lines at appropriate points Use attached braces (K\u0026R style): Space after control keywords: if (, for (, while ( No space after function names: func() No space after casts: (int)value Two spaces before trailing comments Space around binary operators: a + b, x = y Pointers align to the left (with the type): Maximum one consecutive empty line Use empty lines to separate logical sections Functions: module_action_object() (e.g., nx_gpio_port_write()) Types: module_type_t (e.g., nx_result_t) Macros: MODULE_MACRO_NAME (e.g., NX_SUCCESS) Constants: MODULE_CONSTANT (e.g., NEXUS_UART0_SELECTED) Static variables: s_variable_name prefix Global variables: g_variable_name prefix (avoid when possible) All public APIs must have Doxygen comments. This project uses \\\\ style Doxygen tags (not @ style). Align the description after each Doxygen tag as shown below. Excluding any surrounding code indentation, the description starts at column 21: the tag and its padding occupy 17 characters after the opening comment star: Every source file must have a file header comment. Header files (.h) - Minimal format: Source files (.c) - Full format with version and copyright: Header files contain full API documentation including parameters and return values: Source definitions explain hardware ordering and invariants without duplicating \\param or \\return from the header. Use \\brief, \\details and \\note as needed: Use section comments to organize code into logical blocks. The separator line must be exactly 79 characters (/* + 75 characters + */), preserving the existing template: Important: Do NOT use /*===...===*/ style separators. Always use /*---...---*/ for consistency. Use /* comment */ style for inline comments, not //: Use Doxygen block comments or inline comments for macros: Static functions use simplified comments with only \\brief: The following practices are NOT allowed: Using @ style Doxygen tags (use \\\\ instead) Using // single-line comments (use /* */ instead) Using /*===...===*/ section separators (use /*---...---*/ instead) Duplicating \\param and \\return in source files (only in headers) Public declarations specify context, deadlines, retained storage and failure state. Describe why a volatile read, ordering barrier or drain is necessary. SETTLED is the provider\u2019s final request access. Cancellation never permits reclaiming a borrowed buffer before settlement or proved controlled recovery. Changed files pass whole-file formatting and mechanical comment checks. Install local Git hooks with python scripts/setup/install_dev_tools.py. The commit and CI gates share scripts/ci/style_gate.py; no hook bypass is a validation result. Actual warning/analyzer and behavioral evidence is separate from style. Selected analyzer checks do not establish MISRA certification or industrial functional-safety qualification.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Coding Standards", "type": "reference"}, "status/documentation-acceptance.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "All 58 tasks in this documentation-system iteration completed independent acceptance against their original exit conditions. Actual records cover implementation, tutorials, diagrams, automated gates, locked tools, formal builds, public deployment and remote recovery. The 58-task source ledger is the sole authority for task values; the readable checklist and original fields by task are generated from it. The delivery results index records exact commits, archive digests, download locations, evidence members and per-task acceptance grounds. Here, passed means the corresponding documentation exit condition was accepted. It does not change the source, configuration, tools or physical status of original test records, or mean that this documentation commit reran all historical firmware tests. The primary documentation qualification is bound to clean source 3835e13cd7da34bd75307746859d4514745ae2ff, version docs-20261014-3835e13cd7da. Later ledger and delivery-index commits record those actual results and have their own build manifests; their HEAD must not be inserted into earlier execution records. The source used for reader-task execution remains 7aadd347. Actual Git comparison proved that only print CSS changed between it and the primary documentation-qualification source; relevant code, configuration and tool subtrees are identical. Printing was separately accepted at 3835e13c. Normal commit-hook records retain each staged scope and original execution identity; they are not relabeled as new firmware qualification for a later clean HEAD. Newly generated PDFs after recovery passed mechanical and complete-code checks. The original 18-page visual results were reused through per-SHA equality of all 1,600 rendering inputs, without claiming those pages were inspected again. The online entry selects the current published version. Immutable versions/\u003crelease_id\u003e/ retains original bytes; old bookmarks at the site root show accurate historical or current-contract explanations. Legacy HAL/OSAL APIs are not silently mapped to current interfaces. The documentation release notes provide fixed-commit access to offline HTML, the complete documentation-tool cache and execution evidence. Actual long-term storage uses independent Git asset commits. The Release-attachment API returned 401; Git files were not described as uploaded attachments. The evidence index records actual upload, redownload and member verification results, while retaining original failed attempts. Check bytes and SHA-256 before using an archive. Fetch multipart archives in the recorded order; each part is at most 32 MiB. Verify each part before reconstruction, then verify the whole archive and every inventory member. Receipts retain original commands, exit codes and original host paths; portable locators map those paths to relative members. Separately retained tool archives and documentation packages are not duplicated in the evidence package. An explicit exclusion table records each file\u2019s digest, immutable download location and archive member, preserving original execution records completely. Retained tags and later commits keep original versions and asset commits reachable. New versions add identities without replacing same-name archives. Maintainers keep independent copies according to the release notes. Digests detect byte differences; they are not digital signatures. See the documentation delivery contract for exact rejection rules. The documentation-tool closure was actually installed and verified on Linux x86_64/Python 3.12, using Chromium 151 on Linux. Local HTTP, browser offline reading after server shutdown, search, language switching and local diagrams were actually exercised. Existing enterprise policy blocked file://; the policy was unchanged and that path is not claimed as accepted. Noncritical translations display original/missing/stale/reviewed per page; bilingual entry points do not mean every historical page has been translated. Platform firmware qualification still references cc6a065c and the original exact SoC indexes; see the software-verification entry. MCU HIL remains not_executed. Documentation builds, publication, recovery and software-model checks add no chip, board, product-safety or physical qualification.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Documentation-system implementation and formal acceptance", "type": "status"}, "status/hardware.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "No executable MCU boards, debug probes or serial ports are currently connected. Physical status for F407ZG Qiming, F407VE Sky Star, F470ZG Liangshan, and the G474/H743/U575 source-reference slices is not_executed. Prepared tooling, a successfully generated plan or a passing host validator is not an experiment actually performed on an MCU. OS plans remain prepared_unbound; physical status remains not_executed. Real execution first requires station admission and an accurate workload, instrument, raw trace, time and target identity. Do not enter passed on an unbound plan as a substitute for acquisition. Check the exact PCB, option bytes, electrical conditions, safe initial levels and clock; do not guess wiring. Bind actual probes, serial ports and required waveform/power-cut instruments to the station. Fix the source, resolved configuration, tools and candidate ELF; use actual budgets. Acquire raw observations and exercise failure paths for the selected mode, retaining every failure and retry. Assess qualification for that exact tuple. Controllers, modes or boards not executed remain unexecuted. Tool source entry points are in the implementation map. Physical scope advances only after execution at a real station, never because of documentation publication, software CI or on-chip capability.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "HIL and physical-board status", "type": "status"}, "status/index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Use these maintained records to locate implementation, software verification and physical work. Their recorded source IDs and configurations remain unchanged; this website build creates documentation evidence only.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Status and evidence", "type": "reference"}, "status/ledgers.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "The pages below derive from seven original CSV files and do not establish a second status database. Each task retains its original ID, order, all fields, exit conditions, implementation status, software/physical status and original evidence locators; the source SHA-256 is checkable. When the original CSV has no separate exit field, this is explicitly stated; do not construct a supposedly passed condition from other ledgers. implemented means implementation exists; it does not guarantee complete software qualification for the current checkout. development_passed retains its development-validation meaning; do not expand it into formal clean-source archiving. Consult the exact results index for qualification_record_authority. implemented_tooling only means tooling is implemented; unexecuted physical work remains not_executed. not_implemented, not_executed, failures and conditional extensions retain their original values. Different workstreams cover overlapping mechanisms; do not add their counts or calculate project-wide completion percentages. The old 48-item OS checklist has been expanded. Current CSV IDs are OS01\u2013OS55; OS49\u2013OS55 cover physical tooling/actual acceptance, with all original statuses retained. The 48 build-system tasks form a separate ledger. Historical RF ledgers remain under docs/archive/implementation/, reachable through the history entry, and cannot establish current support qualification. The first command only regenerates views from the same original CSV. The second checks missing/stale output, returns nonzero on failure and writes nothing. Regenerate after changing original ledgers; commit/CI checks the same derivation relationship. Successful generation/checking only proves documentation matches original fields; it executes no firmware and acquires no hardware observations. Actual test evidence is in the original results indexes.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Original ledgers, exit conditions and derived views", "type": "status"}, "status/ledgers/build-system.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This page is derived from the original CSV; it is not a new status authority, execution record or software qualification. Original IDs, order and all fields come from the original ledger. Source: docs/design/build-system-execution.csv; SHA-256: cc459a0d064abc1ec360f1d3daf4ed5e5b531f299e581d0b2724d04aa7c7074c. Different ledgers overlap in scope; do not add their counts or calculate a project-wide completion rate. Interpret implementation, software and physical status separately. development_passed does not mean execution at the current documentation HEAD. Consult the exact results index for the source and scope of qualification_record_authority. Absolute /workspace and /dev/shm locators retain their original bytes and only identify the original execution host. See the software-evidence entry for retrievable archives and member locations. Original exit fields are only fields actually present in the original CSV. Tasks, qualification prerequisites and contract links also retain their original wording; they cannot be used to invent an execution. This table contains 48 original records; this is only the source row count. BLD-01 BLD-02 BLD-03 BLD-04 BLD-05 BLD-06 BLD-07 BLD-08 BLD-09 BLD-10 BLD-11 BLD-12 BLD-13 BLD-14 BLD-15 BLD-16 BLD-17 BLD-18 BLD-19 BLD-20 BLD-21 BLD-22 BLD-23 BLD-24 BLD-25 BLD-26 BLD-27 BLD-28 BLD-29 BLD-30 BLD-31 BLD-32 BLD-33 BLD-34 BLD-35 BLD-36 BLD-37 BLD-38 BLD-39 BLD-40 BLD-41 BLD-42 BLD-43 BLD-44 BLD-45 BLD-46 BLD-47 BLD-48", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Original build-system workstream ledger", "type": "status"}, "status/ledgers/documentation-system.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This page is derived from the original CSV; it is not a new status authority, execution record or software qualification. Original IDs, order and all fields come from the original ledger. Source: docs/design/documentation-system-execution.csv; SHA-256: d1f5adad33468cb83c2849b9ec5176d10d958db044620943e93fb0d02d6a2c77. Different ledgers overlap in scope; do not add their counts or calculate a project-wide completion rate. Interpret implementation, software and physical status separately. development_passed does not mean execution at the current documentation HEAD. Consult the exact results index for the source and scope of qualification_record_authority. Absolute /workspace and /dev/shm locators retain their original bytes and only identify the original execution host. See the software-evidence entry for retrievable archives and member locations. Original exit fields are only fields actually present in the original CSV. Tasks, qualification prerequisites and contract links also retain their original wording; they cannot be used to invent an execution. This table contains 58 original records; this is only the source row count. DOC-01 DOC-02 DOC-03 DOC-04 DOC-05 DOC-06 DOC-07 DOC-08 DOC-09 DOC-10 DOC-11 DOC-12 DOC-13 DOC-14 DOC-15 DOC-16 DOC-17 DOC-18 DOC-19 DOC-20 DOC-21 DOC-22 DOC-23 DOC-24 DOC-25 DOC-26 DOC-27 DOC-28 DOC-29 DOC-30 DOC-31 DOC-32 DOC-33 DOC-34 DOC-35 DOC-36 DOC-37 DOC-38 DOC-39 DOC-40 DOC-41 DOC-42 DOC-43 DOC-44 DOC-45 DOC-46 DOC-47 DOC-48 DOC-49 DOC-50 DOC-51 DOC-52 DOC-53 DOC-54 DOC-55 DOC-56 DOC-57 DOC-58", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Documentation-system ledger", "type": "status"}, "status/ledgers/factory-platform.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This page is derived from the original CSV; it is not a new status authority, execution record or software qualification. Original IDs, order and all fields come from the original ledger. Source: docs/design/factory-platform-execution.csv; SHA-256: 49215c9d35115fd258b86d516869215950c78ee5579ca440d9427de731780836. Different ledgers overlap in scope; do not add their counts or calculate a project-wide completion rate. Interpret implementation, software and physical status separately. development_passed does not mean execution at the current documentation HEAD. Consult the exact results index for the source and scope of qualification_record_authority. Absolute /workspace and /dev/shm locators retain their original bytes and only identify the original execution host. See the software-evidence entry for retrievable archives and member locations. Original exit fields are only fields actually present in the original CSV. Tasks, qualification prerequisites and contract links also retain their original wording; they cannot be used to invent an execution. This table contains 24 original records; this is only the source row count. FP-001 FP-002 FP-003 FP-004 FP-005 FP-006 FP-007 FP-008 FP-009 FP-010 FP-011 FP-012 FP-013 FP-014 FP-015 FP-016 FP-017 FP-018 FP-019 FP-020 FP-021 FP-022 FP-023 FP-024", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Factory and TDD ledger", "type": "status"}, "status/ledgers/next-generation.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This page is derived from the original CSV; it is not a new status authority, execution record or software qualification. Original IDs, order and all fields come from the original ledger. Source: docs/design/next-generation-execution.csv; SHA-256: 7ac51ba75151159ac854db5e5f5791652fab48827ea8d1373d87432ae5ad93e1. Different ledgers overlap in scope; do not add their counts or calculate a project-wide completion rate. Interpret implementation, software and physical status separately. development_passed does not mean execution at the current documentation HEAD. Consult the exact results index for the source and scope of qualification_record_authority. Absolute /workspace and /dev/shm locators retain their original bytes and only identify the original execution host. See the software-evidence entry for retrievable archives and member locations. Original exit fields are only fields actually present in the original CSV. Tasks, qualification prerequisites and contract links also retain their original wording; they cannot be used to invent an execution. This table contains 33 original records; this is only the source row count. NG-001 NG-002 NG-003 NG-004 NG-005 NG-006 NG-007 NG-008 NG-009 NG-010 NG-011 NG-012 NG-013 NG-014 NG-015 NG-016 NG-017 NG-018 NG-031 NG-032 NG-033 NG-019 NG-020 NG-021 NG-022 NG-023 NG-024 NG-025 NG-026 NG-027 NG-028 NG-029 NG-030", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Original platform-reconstruction ledger", "type": "status"}, "status/ledgers/os.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This page is derived from the original CSV; it is not a new status authority, execution record or software qualification. Original IDs, order and all fields come from the original ledger. Source: docs/design/os-execution.csv; SHA-256: df25e8d6e399df3b1aa6a1c444b9570be04929bf70d30f8484a11dff4e3ed747. Different ledgers overlap in scope; do not add their counts or calculate a project-wide completion rate. Interpret implementation, software and physical status separately. development_passed does not mean execution at the current documentation HEAD. Consult the exact results index for the source and scope of qualification_record_authority. Absolute /workspace and /dev/shm locators retain their original bytes and only identify the original execution host. See the software-evidence entry for retrievable archives and member locations. Original exit fields are only fields actually present in the original CSV. Tasks, qualification prerequisites and contract links also retain their original wording; they cannot be used to invent an execution. The original ledger defines no separate exit field. This page retains original task fields and fields such as qualification_authority. Review specific contracts and actual validation at their original paths; do not write a second set of exit conditions. This table contains 55 original records; this is only the source row count. OS01 OS02 OS03 OS04 OS05 OS06 OS07 OS08 OS09 OS10 OS11 OS12 OS13 OS14 OS15 OS16 OS17 OS18 OS19 OS20 OS21 OS22 OS23 OS24 OS25 OS26 OS27 OS28 OS29 OS30 OS31 OS32 OS33 OS34 OS35 OS36 OS37 OS38 OS39 OS40 OS41 OS42 OS43 OS44 OS45 OS46 OS47 OS48 OS49 OS50 OS51 OS52 OS53 OS54 OS55", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Original OS workstream ledger", "type": "status"}, "status/ledgers/platform-refinement.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This page is derived from the original CSV; it is not a new status authority, execution record or software qualification. Original IDs, order and all fields come from the original ledger. Source: docs/design/platform-refinement-execution.csv; SHA-256: e4496e72ba53be176407804fffa805ef5b12f9da1e0949d2f3e8a20292d27e8b. Different ledgers overlap in scope; do not add their counts or calculate a project-wide completion rate. Interpret implementation, software and physical status separately. development_passed does not mean execution at the current documentation HEAD. Consult the exact results index for the source and scope of qualification_record_authority. Absolute /workspace and /dev/shm locators retain their original bytes and only identify the original execution host. See the software-evidence entry for retrievable archives and member locations. Original exit fields are only fields actually present in the original CSV. Tasks, qualification prerequisites and contract links also retain their original wording; they cannot be used to invent an execution. This table contains 60 original records; this is only the source row count. IO-01 IO-02 IO-03 IO-04 IO-05 IO-06 IO-07 IO-08 IO-09 IO-10 IO-11 IO-12 IO-13 IO-14 IO-15 IO-16 IO-17 IO-18 IO-19 IO-20 IO-21 IO-22 IO-23 IO-24 IO-25 IO-26 IO-27 IO-28 IO-29 IO-30 IO-31 IO-32 IO-33 IO-34 IO-35 IO-36 IO-37 IO-38 IO-39 IO-40 IO-41 IO-42 IO-43 IO-44 IO-45 IO-46 IO-47 IO-48 IO-49 IO-50 IO-51 IO-52 IO-53 IO-54 IO-55 IO-56 IO-57 IO-58 IO-59 IO-60", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "I/O refinement ledger", "type": "status"}, "status/ledgers/soc-refinement.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This page is derived from the original CSV; it is not a new status authority, execution record or software qualification. Original IDs, order and all fields come from the original ledger. Source: docs/design/soc-refinement-execution.csv; SHA-256: 03add7405a9c459686bb0b6af326a56c4148b55a37202fb9bfd221bc9bcd6725. Different ledgers overlap in scope; do not add their counts or calculate a project-wide completion rate. Interpret implementation, software and physical status separately. development_passed does not mean execution at the current documentation HEAD. Consult the exact results index for the source and scope of qualification_record_authority. Absolute /workspace and /dev/shm locators retain their original bytes and only identify the original execution host. See the software-evidence entry for retrievable archives and member locations. Original exit fields are only fields actually present in the original CSV. Tasks, qualification prerequisites and contract links also retain their original wording; they cannot be used to invent an execution. This table contains 55 original records; this is only the source row count. SOC-01 SOC-02 SOC-03 SOC-04 SOC-05 SOC-06 SOC-07 SOC-08 SOC-09 SOC-10 SOC-11 SOC-12 SOC-13 SOC-14 SOC-15 SOC-16 SOC-17 SOC-18 SOC-19 SOC-20 SOC-21 SOC-22 SOC-23 SOC-24 SOC-25 SOC-26 SOC-27 SOC-28 SOC-29 SOC-30 SOC-31 SOC-32 SOC-33 SOC-34 SOC-35 SOC-36 SOC-37 SOC-38 SOC-39 SOC-40 SOC-41 SOC-42 SOC-43 SOC-44 SOC-45 SOC-46 SOC-47 SOC-48 SOC-49 SOC-50 SOC-51 SOC-52 SOC-53 SOC-54 SOC-55", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Original SoC workstream ledger", "type": "status"}, "status/roadmap.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Current iteration follows actual measurements and original exit conditions. The complete strategy remains in later iterations and team execution; the original ledgers maintain per-task status. This page provides task entry points without duplicating status updates. Codex owns all maintenance domains; role divisions allocate capabilities and capacity without inventing personal accounts. Local ownership files do not prove remote GitHub branch rules are enabled. Retain independent review for high-risk changes. New behavior and bug fixes require actual RED before GREEN, with GoogleTest/GoogleMock for C contracts. Product tasks, recovery, secure updates, manufacturing keys and proprietary PCBs belong in external projects.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Iteration directions and admission conditions", "type": "status"}, "status/software-verification.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This page navigates existing execution records; it does not establish platform-software qualification for this documentation HEAD. Compare source, assembly, dependencies, tools, actual execution and the same ELF/BIN/map bytes together, rather than reading passed alone. The qualification contract maintains qualification rules. The following fields apply to successfully packaged formal sites and offline packages. For build previews, first inspect source, status and original command receipts in documentation-build.json. A preview has no formal documentation-manifest.json yet and does not establish release completion. After successful packaging, manifests for the public site and offline package derive from actual build inputs. Documentation commits may change Git HEAD; this page does not manually insert a rapidly drifting HEAD constant. An unpackaged working tree can run git rev-parse HEAD to check its checkout, but that command output alone is not a qualification receipt. The three indexes retain their original schemas, scopes, failed attempts, receipts, archives and member identities. Do not add test executions, ARM images or task counts, or calculate a project-wide completion rate. Later documentation commits only record these baselines. Current documentation checking, rendering, offline recovery and deployment validation each have independent records. The build-system records did not execute one complete 72-stage software master plan, OCI recipe reconstruction, CP311 offline installation, Windows/macOS integration or MCU HIL. Interpret independently executed scopes according to their original indexes; do not expand conclusions from aggregate status. Eight current unsigned software candidates combine empty/UART and baremetal/FreeRTOS for H743 HSI64/Cache off and U575 MSIS4/TZEN=0. Open the corresponding results index and confirm the canonical code commit, configuration and recorded scope. Locate the archive through the index and verify SHA-256 and bytes; retain the original identities of failed attempts. Locate members using original fields such as archive_relative_path and archive_member_relative_path, then verify each member\u2019s hash and bytes. Field names vary by original schema; interpret them according to that index. Inspect actual execution records, raw logs and the same artifacts. Software models, linking, independent consumption and two network-disabled rebuilds prove their own scopes; success in one does not replace another. Assess physical records separately according to HIL status. Original /workspace/... and /dev/shm/... paths locate the execution host; they are not public downloads elsewhere. The build-system index\u2019s evidence_navigation specifies candidate archives, candidate documents, ELF/BIN/map and complete member-inventory locations. Archives are currently retained locally as recorded; do not invent download URLs for unpublished remote evidence. Documentation packages include original JSON for checking; they do not copy large raw archives or re-sign historical bytes. Software and physical evidence separately bind exact source and execution scope FIG-11 expresses qualification relationships without generating receipts. See the diagram atlas for standards and source authorities, and the ledger views for original per-task exit conditions.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Software verification, source identity and evidence", "type": "status"}, "status/support.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This page helps determine whether an exact assembly is maintained. The original support records remain authoritative for complete support statements; this page only provides assessment entry points. The fact-derived matrix reads SoC/Board/routes, providers and software fixtures; it cannot substitute for PCB or physical acceptance. Do not infer all peripherals are usable because a chip belongs to Cortex-M. The platform-reference entry links chip-specific software slices; the selection guide explains F407, F470, G474, H743, U575 and CPU-only Runtime differences. Both reference original support records without creating new capability rules. Fix the full part number, Board and PCB provenance; check oscillator, power and wiring facts that remain unknown. Find the exact mode, controller and endpoint in the derived matrix; unlisted modes are not automatically supported. Use the actual TOML resolver; conflicts, unknowns and unsupported configurations must be rejected. Compare software-result source, configuration and artifacts; do not borrow the execution identity of a neighboring Board or mode. Obtain actual station records before claiming physical capabilities; all current MCU HIL remains unexecuted. Platform startup does not start the scheduler. Tasks, buffers, stacks, owner-service budgets, stop and recovery policies belong to external projects. Continuous-stream modes also do not promise lossless operation for arbitrary interrupt delays or consumer speeds. CPU Runtime, exact SoC and physical-qualification boundaries FIG-13 shows software-support layers; see the diagram atlas for complete applicability conditions.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "zh_CN", "status": "reviewed", "title": "Support scope and platform selection", "type": "status"}, "tutorials/async-io.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Run this host tutorial to observe the real public SPI API against a Native provider. It demonstrates software ownership and failure paths, not physical DMA timing. The complete executable fixture is the maintained GoogleTest target; its model controls stand in for external IRQ and wire progress only. GoogleTest/GoogleMock and C++17 are host-only; production firmware remains C11. First obtain the fixed source and recursive dependencies. Keep the source revision with your fresh execution reports. From the repository root, with host tools and pinned dependencies available: Run each binary without a filter and retain fresh XML in a new directory: All three commands must exit zero and report passing, nonzero test counts. These complete target executions are a tutorial scope, not the all-target commit TDD qualification. Remove the tutorial directory only after retaining needed reports. The first endpoint accepts its request. The caller keeps the request and buffers alive. The second endpoint returns NX_ERROR_BUSY while its request stays READY; that rejection retains no borrow. Stepping payload transfer does not publish SETTLED before the simulated wire-completion step. Service and IRQ progress remain explicit; a waiting client cannot assume a hidden worker. Cancel requests termination. With drain deliberately held, the request becomes QUARANTINED, stop returns BUSY and prepare rejects reuse. The model then supplies actual drain progress; service publishes SETTLED and result observation exposes the cancelled result and transferred prefix. Removing the model hold is a test control, not a generic production recovery API. The generated factory fixture also tests platform stop with a quarantined UART borrow. The UART owner fixture checks rejected submissions and retained payload against GoogleMock dependency ports. Read ownership before copying request storage into an application. Request admission, drain, quarantine and final settlement FIG-06 expresses current Core helper transitions with provider proof obligations. The I/O lifecycle matrix limits what these models establish. IRQ hints, stable slot epochs, observer detach and join require the OS shutdown guide.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Observe rejection, borrow, cancellation and drain", "type": "tutorial"}, "tutorials/index.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "Follow one fixed, verifiable path. Start with the external Source SDK consumer; use the request tutorial before adding asynchronous execution.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Tutorials", "type": "reference"}, "tutorials/source-sdk.html": {"actual_language": "en", "excerpt": "This tutorial verifies that an independent C and C++ project can consume a relocated Nexus Source SDK. It runs on a Linux host with Python 3.11+, CMake 3.31+, Ninja, a C/C++ compiler and Git. It does not flash or execute an MCU. First obtain the fixed source and recursive dependencies. The following commands run from that checkout, not an arbitrary default branch. Work from the repository root with the pinned recursive dependencies initialized. Keep output outside the source checkout. This exact test calls the production packager and maintained consumer template, moves the prefix into a path containing spaces, configures independent projects, builds both languages and runs the Native executables: Success is a nonempty unittest execution ending in OK, with exit code zero. The test\u2019s temporary directory is removed automatically. Failure preserves its actual command diagnostic; do not count a missing compiler as a passed consumer. The fixture deliberately packages a development snapshot and explicitly opts in; this validates consumption, not a publishable software candidate. The complete consumer is maintained in the production verifier rather than copied into this page. These literal inputs are the same templates exercised by the test: From an initialized, clean, committed checkout, choose a new owned output path: Move the entire prefix, including its identity and licenses, before consumption. The Source SDK contract defines version and interface epoch checks. Missing, extra, modified or symlinked payload files reject configuration. A package made with --development-fixture remains nonpublishable and requires NEXUS_ALLOW_SOURCE_SDK_FIXTURE=ON in its consumer. Never use that opt-in to bypass release qualification. The formal installed-prefix verifier requires the locked ARM compiler on PATH in addition to host tools. It builds Native C/C++ and the maintained MCU consumers: Check verification.json, every recorded exit code, Native runtime results, MCU ELF/BIN/map and resource reports. MCU linking does not execute hardware. The verifier\u2019s output is owned; an unrelated existing directory rejects replacement. Remove only outputs you created after retaining required evidence. Products keep assembly.toml, main, workers and private Board data in an external repository. They consume Nexus::Platform through nexus_add_firmware(); the helper does not generate an application or start a scheduler. Use the assembly reference to select reviewed bindings and the factory explanation to obtain faces. Public product examples live in nexus-examples. Source SDK preparation, relocation and independent consumer verification FIG-10 shows the current software consumption flow. Runtime and MCU link evidence remain separate from physical qualification.", "requested_language": "en", "source_language": "en", "status": "original", "title": "Build an independent Source SDK consumer", "type": "tutorial"}}, "requested_language": "en", "revision": "51e93508f22fc1eb540c4ce4dd8713a91cc429f6", "schema_version": 1, "version": "1.0.0"};
